// Wonder Lab animal data. Generated by tools/build_animals.py
// Written and fact-checked in review passes; do not hand-edit.
// 210 species, 2424 facts.
const ANIMALS = [{"id":"cheetah","name":"Cheetah","group":"mammals","blurb":"A long-legged spotted cat built like a sprinter, and the fastest runner on land.","size":"About 4.3 ft (1.3 m) long — but lean. A big one weighs around 99 lb (45 kg).","homes":["grassland","desert"],"stats":{"speed":65,"weight":99,"length":4.3,"life":10},"facts":[{"cat":"speed","text":"The fastest cheetah ever timed ran 100 meters in 5.95 seconds. The human world record is 9.58. She beat the fastest man alive by more than three seconds.","more":"Her name was Sarah and she lived at the Cincinnati Zoo. She was chasing a fluffy lure on a rope, and she hit a peak of 61 mph (98 km/h) on the run.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"speed","text":"At full sprint, one cheetah stride covers up to 23 feet (7 m). That is two small cars parked end to end. The cheetah takes four of those strides every second.","more":"Cheetah top speed gets exaggerated constantly. The highest reliably measured is 65 mph (104 km/h). A famous figure of 71 mph came from a faulty measuring method and was thrown out.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"Stand a cheetah next to a leopard and the cheetah looks bigger. Weigh them and it isn't. Small head, deep chest, legs like stilts: long and light, not heavy.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"A cheetah's claws never fully pull in. They have no protective sheath, so they stay out and blunt, biting into the ground like a sprinter's spikes.","more":"Nearly every other cat keeps its claws sheathed and razor-sharp for fighting. The cheetah's are permanently out and permanently dull. Grip matters more than sharpness when you are moving that fast.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"build","text":"The tail is a rudder. Watch a cheetah corner at speed and the tail swings the opposite way, flinging the body round the turn without a tumble.","more":"GPS collars on wild cheetahs in Botswana recorded hundreds of hunts and found they are won by grip and turning, not top speed. The average hunting run reached only 34 mph (54 km/h).","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"record","text":"Pound for pound, a cheetah picking up speed puts out about four times the power Usain Bolt makes at full sprint. That is double what a polo pony at a gallop puts out.","more":"Those same collars in Botswana carried motion sensors as well as GPS. They logged acceleration, braking and turning force. Those figures are the highest ever measured on a wild land mammal.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"A cheetah's retina carries a long horizontal band of sharp-vision cells. Nobody can look out through those eyes. But that band means the whole width of the grassland stays crisp at once. It works like a permanent panoramic camera.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"talk","text":"A cheetah cannot roar. It chirps like a bird. A mother calling her cubs makes a high yelping sound you would swear came from something with feathers.","more":"Cats fall into two camps: the ones that roar — lion, tiger, leopard, jaguar — and the ones that purr. The cheetah is firmly in the purring camp, and its purr is far louder than a house cat's.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It hunts in daylight, while lions and hyenas are sleeping off the heat. Speed is no use to you if something bigger simply walks over and takes your dinner.","more":"Stealing another animal's kill has a name: kleptoparasitism. Cheetahs lose kills to it constantly, so they eat fast and rarely stay with a carcass. After a flat-out run a cheetah has to lie panting for many minutes before it can even begin.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"build","text":"Huge nostrils, huge lungs, huge heart. A sprinting cheetah is essentially an air pump with legs.","more":"The nasal openings are so wide there is little room left in the skull for long tooth roots, so a cheetah's canines are small and conical. It kills by clamping the windpipe shut and holding on, not by stabbing.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Cheetah cubs grow a cape of long smoky-gray hair down the back and shoulders, shaggy enough to hide the spots underneath. Researchers call it a mantle.","more":"It thins out and disappears as the cub grows. Until then a cub looks almost nothing like the sleek animal it will become — more like a scruffy gray tuft with a cat's face.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"No two cheetahs are spotted the same way. Field researchers photograph faces and flanks. They identify each cat by its pattern, the way police use fingerprints.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Claws that never sheathe, a tail that steers, eyes that hold the horizon still — every separate part of this animal pointing the same direction."},{"id":"snow-leopard","name":"Snow leopard","group":"mammals","blurb":"A pale smoke-gray cat with an enormous tail. It lives higher up the mountains than almost any other large animal.","size":"About 3.6 ft (1.1 m) long and around 99 lb (45 kg), plus a tail nearly as long again as the rest of it.","homes":["mountain"],"stats":{"weight":99,"length":3.6,"life":15},"facts":[{"cat":"build","text":"The tail is the giveaway. Up to 3 feet (1 m) long — nearly as long as the rest of the cat, and thick as a fire hose with fur and stored fat.","more":"It works as a balance bar on ledges barely wider than a paw. At rest the snow leopard curls it over its own face like a scarf.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"For a big cat it is small: about 100 lb (45 kg). The paws and the tail look several sizes too big for the body they came with.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"A snow leopard cannot roar — its vocal folds are too short. Its greeting is a chuff: a puff of air pushed out through the nose, half snort, half soft sneeze.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Its paws are enormous and furred underneath. They spread its weight across snow like a pair of built-in snowshoes.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"build","text":"It breathes thin, freezing air at 10,000 feet (3,000 m) and higher. Its nasal cavity is unusually large, and the best read of all that extra space is air warmed on the way in, before it ever reaches the lungs.","more":"Its whole range sits between about 9,800 and 14,800 feet (3,000 to 4,500 m). Almost no other large predator goes that high. The air up there has a third less oxygen in it than air at the beach.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"food","text":"A snow leopard regularly kills prey heavier than itself. Wild sheep and ibex of 80 to 165 lb (36 to 76 kg) are normal. It brings them down where one slip means a very long fall.","more":"Its favorite prey, the blue sheep, lives on cliffs. The cat stalks from above, drops, and grabs. Then it stays with the kill for days, eating slowly, because the next meal might be a week away.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"The pounce is only the start. Carrying the momentum of that first leap, a snow leopard has been tracked chasing prey 980 feet (300 m) down a mountainside.","more":"That is the length of three football fields. The ground is loose rock, snow and drop-offs, taken at a run. The cat has already thrown itself off a ledge and cannot change its mind.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"Pale gray fur smudged with dark rosettes. Photographs of snow leopards on bare rock get passed around as puzzles, because people genuinely cannot find the cat.","more":"The pattern breaks the outline up. Instead of one cat-shaped thing you see scattered patches of light and shadow, which is exactly what a scree slope already looks like.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"It is called the ghost of the mountains, and not as a compliment to its manners. Researchers can work in snow leopard country for months and see nothing but tracks.","more":"Almost everything we know comes from camera traps and GPS collars, not from watching. Even the rosette patterns that tell one cat from another are read off automatic photographs.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Cubs are born blind in a rock den that the mother lines with fur pulled from her own belly. They do not step outside it for two to four months.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Its fur grows up to 5 inches (12 cm) long, deepest of all on the belly, so the cat can lie down flat on snow and stay warm.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"travel","text":"One cat may patrol a home range larger than a city. In mountains that steep, the distance is measured in climbing rather than walking.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Nobody is watching when that tail wraps over that face on a frozen ledge at 12,000 feet. It was made beautiful anyway."},{"id":"tiger","name":"Tiger","group":"mammals","blurb":"The largest cat in the world: a striped ambush hunter that works alone and swims for pleasure.","size":"A big male weighs around 485 lb (220 kg) — about as much as three grown men — and stretches over 6.6 ft (2 m) nose to tail.","homes":["forest","rainforest","grassland"],"stats":{"speed":35,"weight":485,"length":6.6,"life":13},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"No cat alive is bigger. A large male tiger weighs around 485 lb (220 kg), about as much as three grown men.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Shave a tiger and the stripes are still there. The pattern is in the skin itself, not only in the fur.","more":"Every tiger's pattern is its own. Littermates do not share it. A mother and cub do not share it. The left and right sides of one tiger do not even match. Researchers photograph both flanks and name a tiger from its stripes alone.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Its upper canine teeth are the longest of any cat alive. They reach up to 3 inches (7.6 cm). That is roughly the length of a grown-up's middle finger.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"A tiger can stalk for an hour, creep to within 30 feet (9 m), and still end the night hungry. Far more hunts fail than succeed — by some counts nine in ten.","more":"Which is why a kill matters so much. A tiger can eat 110 pounds (50 kg) of meat in one sitting. That is close to a quarter of its own body weight. It drags what is left into thick cover and comes back to it for days.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"Orange and black looks loud to you. To a deer it does not — deer cannot see orange. Nobody can look out through a deer's eyes, but the best read is that a tiger in dry grass shows up as nothing but broken lines of shadow.","more":"Deer see blues and yellows but not reds and oranges. The tiger's coat lands on them as roughly the same dull tone as the grass around it, cut into stripes that hide the shape of an animal.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"talk","text":"A tiger's roar carries about 2 miles (3 km) through forest. You would hear it long, long before you had any chance of seeing the animal that made it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Most cats loathe water. Tigers swim well and often, lying in rivers for hours on hot days and crossing open channels as wide as 5 miles (8 km).","more":"The Sundarbans mangrove forest is cut into pieces by tidal creeks. There, swimming is simply how a tiger gets to work.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Worked out from the eye itself, a tiger sees in near-darkness about six times better than you can. A mirror-like layer sits behind the retina. It bounces light back through the eye for a second pass. That same layer makes cat eyes flash in a flashlight beam.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"speed","text":"A tiger sprints about 35 mph (56 km/h), and not for long. All that weight is made for the ambush, not the chase: it has to be within a few bounds before it moves at all.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"build","text":"A tiger's tongue is covered in backward-pointing hooks stiff enough to rasp meat off bone. A few licks would take the skin off your arm.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Cubs are born blind and helpless. They stay with their mother for around two years, learning how to hunt.","more":"Everything a tiger knows about stalking, choosing prey and killing cleanly is learned in those two years. A cub that leaves early usually cannot feed itself.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"A tiger patrols alone. It marks the edges of its territory with scent and with claw rakes gouged deep into tree trunks. The message is left for cats it may never meet.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"The stripes run all the way down to the skin. Whoever made this animal did not stop at the part you can see."},{"id":"lion","name":"Lion","group":"mammals","blurb":"The only cat that lives in a family group — and by a wide margin the loudest.","size":"A big male's shoulder comes up to an adult's waist. He weighs around 419 lb (190 kg) — about as much as two and a half grown men.","homes":["grassland","desert"],"stats":{"speed":35,"weight":419,"length":6.2,"life":14},"facts":[{"cat":"talk","text":"A lion's roar carries about 5 miles (8 km) across open country. Standing close to one is like standing beside a chainsaw that has opinions about you.","more":"The low pitch is the trick. Deep sound bends around trees and rocks instead of bouncing off them, so it keeps traveling long after a higher, shriller noise would have faded to nothing.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"Stand beside a big male and his shoulder reaches your waist. He weighs around 419 lb (190 kg) — about two and a half grown men — and most of that is neck, chest and jaw.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Researchers stood life-sized model lions out on the Serengeti to find out what a mane means. Females walked up to the dark-maned dummy. Males avoided it and picked on the blond one.","more":"Mane color tracks a male's condition. Darker-maned lions carried more testosterone and were in better shape. The color is honest advertising, readable from a long way off, before anybody has to fight.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"Lions are the only cats that live in prides. A pride is a permanent family group of mostly related females. Grandmothers, mothers, sisters and daughters stay together for life.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"The lionesses do most of the hunting, and they do it as a team. Some circle wide to spook the prey. Others wait flat in the grass exactly where it will run.","more":"Hunting together lets a pride take on animals no single lion could handle, including buffalo. The males mostly hold the territory against other males. That is dangerous work of a different kind.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"A lion may rest 20 hours out of every 24. In the afternoon heat a whole pride lies flat on its back in the shade like a pile of dropped laundry.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"speed","text":"For all that power a lion is a sprinter, not a runner: about 35 mph (56 km/h) for a couple of hundred feet, and then it is finished. The hunt ends quickly or not at all.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Lion cubs are born covered in faint spots and rosettes that fade as they grow. For the first months a lion cub looks a little like a small leopard.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"Cubs of similar ages are raised together in a nursery group. A lioness will let cubs that are not her own nurse from her.","more":"This is genuinely unusual. Most mammal mothers feed their own young and nobody else's. In a lion pride the females are close relatives, and the cubs are raised more or less as one batch.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Every lion has its own pattern of whisker spots — the small dark dots the whiskers grow out of. Researchers use them to tell one lion from another in the field.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"A lion is the only cat with a tuft on the end of its tail. Cubs are born without one; it grows in over their first year.","more":"Hidden inside the tuft on some lions is a hard little spur of skin about 0.2 in (5 mm) long. Nobody has worked out what it is for.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Almost all wild lions live in Africa. One small population hangs on in the Gir Forest of India — the last wild lions anywhere else on earth.","more":"The Gir lions are slightly smaller, with sparser manes and a fold of skin along the belly. They live in dry teak forest rather than open savanna, sharing the ground with farms and villages.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Given a voice that carries five miles, and given a family worth using it on."},{"id":"fennec-fox","name":"Fennec fox","group":"mammals","blurb":"The smallest fox in the world: a pale, enormous-eared desert dog that comes out at night.","size":"About 1.2 ft (37 cm) long and only about 3 lb (1.4 kg) — light enough to lift in one hand.","homes":["desert"],"stats":{"weight":3,"length":1.2,"life":10},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"The smallest fox on earth, and the smallest wild dog of any kind. A full-grown fennec weighs about 3 lb (1.3 kg) — you could hold one in two hands like a loaf of bread.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Its ears are nearly 4 inches (10 cm) tall, on an animal no bigger than a kitten. No dog, fox or wolf anywhere has bigger ears for its body size.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"A fennec hunts by listening. It stops dead and swivels its head one way, then the other. Then it digs straight down into open sand. It comes up with something that was never visible at all.","more":"The prey is underground — beetles, grubs, lizards. The fennec is not guessing. Those ears pick up small movements beneath the surface, and the fox digs to the exact spot it heard.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Those same ears look like a cooling system. They are thin, with blood vessels running close to the surface, and the best read is that heat leaves the blood there and drifts away into the air.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"weird","text":"A fennec can live where there is nothing to drink. It takes its water from the bodies of the animals it eats, and goes without whenever it has to.","more":"Its kidneys are large and dense, holding water back instead of spending it, so very little is lost. Offer a fennec a puddle and it will happily drink — it simply does not need one to survive.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The soles of its feet are furred over. The best read is heat-proofing against sand hot enough to burn, with grip thrown in — snowshoes, for sand.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"home","text":"It digs. In firm ground a fennec's den can sprawl across 120 square meters — about the floor of a small house — with as many as 15 separate doorways.","more":"In loose sand the den is far simpler, often with a single entrance, because the walls will not hold. Underground the air stays cool and slightly damp all day while the surface above it bakes.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"It sleeps through the heat and comes out at dusk. Nearly everything a fennec does — hunting, digging, squabbling, playing — happens in the dark.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"Most foxes live alone. Fennecs live in family groups, share a den, and have been seen wrestling and chasing each other like puppies.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"It is astonishingly noisy for its size. It barks, yaps, squeaks and purrs. The voice belongs on an animal ten times bigger.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It eats nearly anything small: insects, lizards, small birds and their eggs, and rodents. It also eats fruit, leaves and roots, which count as dinner and drink at the same time.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"Its cream-colored coat matches the sand almost exactly. The same pale fur throws off sunlight during the day and holds warmth in through desert nights that drop close to freezing.","kind":"worked"}],"wonder":"The smallest fox in the desert was given the biggest ears — and everything else it needed to live where nothing drinks."},{"id":"arctic-fox","name":"Arctic fox","group":"mammals","blurb":"A small fox with deep fur. It lives farther north than almost any other land animal and stays out hunting all winter.","size":"About 1.8 ft (55 cm) long and 8 lb (3.5 kg), with a tail as thick as your arm.","homes":["polar","mountain"],"stats":{"weight":8,"length":1.8,"life":4},"facts":[{"cat":"build","text":"Turn one paw over and even the soles are furred, pads and all. Its scientific name, Vulpes lagopus, means \"hare-foot\" — no other wild dog has this.","more":"That fur does two jobs. It grips ice like a rubber sole, and it lifts the pads off frozen ground. Blood vessels in the legs run a countercurrent exchange, warming blood on the way back up. The feet sit just above freezing without draining heat from the body.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"An Arctic fox can curl up on open snow at -50 C (-58 F) and go to sleep. No burrow, no shelter. Its winter coat is among the best insulation ever measured on a mammal.","more":"The coat roughly doubles in thickness for winter, and the fox wraps that bushy tail over its face like a scarf. Everything else is tucked in too — short muzzle, short legs, small round ears. Less body sticking out means less heat leaking away.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"Twice a year it changes clothes. White from nose to tail in winter, then a patchy brown-gray for summer. No other wild dog swaps color with the seasons.","more":"The moult is triggered by changing daylight, not by snowfall. So in a late spring a fox can end up brilliant white on bare brown tundra for a few awkward weeks, visible to every eagle for miles. Coastal \"blue\" foxes skip white altogether and stay smoky gray all year.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"It hears a lemming moving under the snow and lines itself up. Then it dives headfirst. Its front feet punch through the crust and pin prey it never saw.","more":"Snow can bury a lemming completely, so the hunt is done entirely by ear. The fox tilts its head to pinpoint the rustle. It springs almost straight up and comes down nose-first. Both forefeet drive through the crust to trap whatever is moving below.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Arctic foxes raise some of the largest families of any meat-eater on earth. Five to eight cubs is normal. In a summer thick with lemmings, a den can hold more than a dozen.","more":"Litter size tracks the food supply. Lemming numbers rise and crash on a cycle of a few years. In a boom summer a mother can raise a dozen or more cubs in one den; in a crash year she may not breed at all.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"One young female walked from Norway to Canada. Her satellite collar logged 2,179 miles (3,506 km) in 76 days — more than a marathon a day, across drifting sea ice.","more":"She left Svalbard in late March, crossed to Greenland in 21 days, and reached Ellesmere Island in Nunavut by June. Her fastest single day covered 96 miles (155 km). Young foxes make journeys like this to find country of their own to live in.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Fox dens are handed down. Generation after generation digs the same sandy ridge until it has dozens of entrances — and the plants on top grow greener than anywhere around.","more":"Droppings, food scraps and urine build up in the soil over the years and fertilize it. An old den mound sits on gray tundra like a bright green island. Researchers spot dens from far off just by the color of the grass.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"In summer it caches. A fox carries goose eggs away one at a time and buries them in cold ground. Months later it digs them up, when the tundra has nothing left to offer.","more":"On Bylot Island in Canada, researchers watched foxes stash hundreds of eggs taken from snow-goose colonies. The frozen soil keeps the eggs cold, so they stay edible long enough to feed a fox through the leanest stretch of winter.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Follow the biggest hunter and you eat. Arctic foxes shadow polar bears across the sea ice for miles, waiting to clean up the seal the bear leaves behind.","more":"A polar bear eats the fat and abandons most of the meat. One kill can feed several foxes and a crowd of gulls. Foxes have been tracked far out on floating ice, well beyond sight of land. They live entirely on someone else's leftovers.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"It is smaller than it looks. About house-cat size, roughly 8 lb (3.5 kg) — and a good share of what you see is fur, not fox.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Fur on the soles of its feet, ears kept small to save heat, a coat that turns white before the snow arrives — every part of this fox is fitted for the cold it was made to live in."},{"id":"grey-wolf","name":"Gray wolf","group":"mammals","blurb":"The largest wild member of the dog family. It is a long-legged hunter that lives, hunts and raises its young as a family.","size":"As tall at the shoulder as a big German shepherd, but longer-legged and heavier-headed","homes":["forest","grassland","mountain"],"stats":{"speed":35,"weight":88,"length":4.9,"life":6},"facts":[{"cat":"teamwork","text":"A wolf pack is not an army. It is a family — a mother, a father, and their pups from this year and last. The \"alpha\" fighting his way to the top is a myth.","more":"That idea came from watching unrelated wolves crowded into a zoo enclosure, where strangers really do scrap over rank. In the wild the leaders are simply the parents. The biologist whose book spread the alpha story spent years afterwards asking people to stop repeating it.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"talk","text":"A howl carries about 10 miles (16 km) across open tundra and 6 miles (10 km) through forest. It is how a pack scattered over a hunt finds itself again.","more":"Wolves howl to regroup, to rally before a hunt, and to warn neighboring packs off their ground. Each wolf's voice is distinct enough that packmates can tell who is calling — and a chorus of three or four is very hard to count by ear.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"Wolves are built for distance, not sprints. A pack may cover 30 miles (50 km) in a day at an easy trot, patrolling a territory bigger than many cities.","more":"The trot runs about 5 mph (8 km/h), and they can hold it for hours. Territories range from around 50 square miles to several hundred. The pack works the boundaries as it goes. Scent marks tell other wolves the ground is taken.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Feast or famine. After a kill a wolf can eat 20 lb (9 kg) of meat at one sitting — then go days, sometimes over a week, with nothing at all.","more":"Most hunts fail. Wolves test many animals for every one they catch, so when food is finally in front of them they load up completely. That is where the phrase \"wolfing it down\" comes from, and a wolf leaving a carcass really does look bloated.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Behind the long canines sit the carnassials. These teeth are shaped like blades. They pass each other like scissor blades and shear straight through hide, tendon and bone.","more":"A wolf has 42 teeth. The canines can top an inch (2.5 cm) and are for gripping; the carnassials do the cutting. Wolves also crack open leg bones to reach the marrow inside, which is why a carcass they have finished with is used up so completely.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"A wolf hunts with its nose. Researchers following packs have watched them swing off course and head straight for a moose 1.5 miles (2.4 km) away, downwind and out of sight.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Pups are born underground, blind and deaf. Their eyes open at around two weeks, and the outside world waits a few weeks longer.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Adults eat at the kill, walk miles home, then bring it back up for the pups. The pups start it themselves, nipping and licking at the adult's mouth until it comes.","more":"It is the most efficient way to carry 10 lb of meat across rough country — inside you. Any pack member will do it, not just the mother, and the pups mob whichever adult walks into the clearing by the den first.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Young wolves strike out alone. One male collared in Oregon walked more than 1,000 miles (1,600 km) across two states, by himself, before he found a mate and settled.","more":"He was known only by his collar number, OR-7. Wolves like him cross highways, rivers and whole mountain ranges, looking for empty country and a partner. That is how wolves turn up where nobody expected them.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"A wolf sleeps outdoors in a blizzard. It curls nose to tail with that thick brush laid across its face. Then it simply lets the snow drift over it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"Put your hand inside a wolf track and your palm vanishes. A front paw runs roughly 4 by 5 inches (10 by 13 cm) — far wider than a coyote's, and wider than most dogs of the same height.","more":"Broad feet work like snowshoes on soft snow. In deep drifts a pack walks single file. Each wolf steps exactly into the prints of the one ahead. A line of six can leave a trail that looks like one animal passed through.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"The pack is a family before it is anything else — parents, pups, shared meals, and a voice that carries ten miles to call everyone home."},{"id":"polar-bear","name":"Polar bear","group":"mammals","blurb":"The largest bear on earth — a white-coated hunter that lives out on Arctic sea ice and hunts seals.","size":"Rearing up on its back legs, it stands about 10 ft (3 m), the height of a basketball hoop.","homes":["polar","ocean"],"stats":{"speed":25,"weight":992,"length":8.2,"life":18},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"Stand a big male on his hind legs and he reaches about 10 ft (3 m) — eye to eye with a basketball hoop. He weighs more than eight grown men put together.","more":"Males run 772-1,433 lb (350-650 kg) (770-1,430 lb); females are roughly half that. For all that bulk, a polar bear walks almost silently, because the soles of its feet are padded and thickly furred between the pads.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Polar bear fur is not white. Every guard hair is a clear, hollow tube — and the skin underneath is jet black. You can see the black on its nose and lips.","more":"The hollow core scatters sunlight in all directions at once. Because every color scatters equally, your eye adds them back together as white. It is the same reason crushed glass and fresh snow look white while a single piece of either is see-through.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"A polar bear can smell a seal from more than a kilometer (0.6 miles) away. It can smell one through a meter (3 ft) of packed snow. That is enough to find a seal pup sealed inside a snow lair.","more":"Ringed seals hollow out birth lairs under the snow on top of the ice, completely invisible from above. The bear works the wind, walks in carefully, then rears up on its back legs and crashes down through the roof with both front feet.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"One collared female swam for nine days without stopping. She crossed 426 miles (687 km) of open Arctic sea. The water was only a few degrees above freezing.","more":"A GPS collar and a temperature logger recorded the whole crossing: 232 hours in water of 2-6 C (36-43 F). She lost roughly a fifth of her body weight doing it. Polar bears swim with the front legs only, the back legs trailing behind as rudders.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Its front paw is up to 12 in (30 cm) across. The toes are slightly webbed for paddling, and the soles are covered in papillae, tiny bumps that grip ice.","more":"Wide feet spread an enormous weight across thin ice the way snowshoes spread yours across deep snow. Fur fills the gaps between the pads for warmth and extra grip, and the claws are short, thick and curved — ice hooks rather than knives.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"A polar bear eats the fat first. It can strip 100 lb (45 kg) of blubber off a seal in a single meal, then walk away and leave the red meat for foxes and gulls.","more":"Blubber is nearly pure energy. A bear living on the ice needs energy far more than it needs protein. Arctic foxes trail bears for exactly this reason. One seal kill can feed half a dozen scavengers after the bear has finished.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A newborn polar bear weighs about 21 oz (600 g). It is blind and thinly furred. It is born inside a snow cave in the depth of Arctic winter.","more":"By the time the family digs its way out in March the cubs weigh around 22 lb (10 kg), about twenty times their birth weight. Their mother's milk is roughly a third fat, among the richest milk of any bear.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"The mother digs a den into a snowdrift and shuts herself in for months. No food, no water, no daylight. She lives off her own fat while the cubs nurse.","more":"Inside, the den stays far warmer than the air outside, heated by the bear herself. A pregnant female may go up to eight months without a single meal. That runs from before she dens until she is back out on the ice hunting, with cubs at her heels.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"speed","text":"It can run 25 mph (40 km/h) — but only briefly, because that coat is so warm the bear overheats within minutes. The best read of its sit-and-wait hunting is that chasing simply costs too much heat.","more":"After a short chase, bears have been watched rolling in snow or lying spread out flat on the ice to cool down. Everything that keeps a polar bear alive at -40 works against it the second it starts to run.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"food","text":"The still-hunt: find a seal's breathing hole, lie down beside it, and wait — completely motionless, sometimes for hours. Most of the time the seal never comes up.","more":"Seals keep several holes open in the ice and surface at whichever one they choose, so the odds are against the bear on any single attempt. Patience is the entire technique. A bear that fidgets goes hungry.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"It is the only bear counted as a marine mammal. Its scientific name, Ursus maritimus, means \"sea bear\", and it can spend most of the year out on floating ice, far from any land.","kind":"record"}],"wonder":"Hollow hair, black skin, snowshoe feet with the grip built into the soles — a polar bear wears everything the Arctic asks of it."},{"id":"grizzly-bear","name":"Grizzly bear","group":"mammals","blurb":"A large brown bear of North America's mountains and salmon rivers. It has a hump of muscle on its shoulders and claws built for digging.","size":"Standing up, taller than a doorway; a big male outweighs three grown men","homes":["forest","mountain","grassland"],"stats":{"speed":35,"weight":551,"length":6.6,"life":25},"facts":[{"cat":"build","text":"That hump over its shoulders is solid muscle, and it powers the front legs. A grizzly can dig itself a hole to lie in through ground you would need a pickaxe for.","more":"The claws finish the job: 2-3.9 in (5-10 cm) (2-4 in) long and almost straight, shaped for turning over rocks and hauling out roots and ground squirrels. A black bear's claws are shorter and hooked for climbing, which is why black bears go up trees easily and grizzlies mostly do not.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"By late summer a grizzly goes into hyperphagia — \"over-eating\". It feeds up to 20 hours a day and can put on 3-7 lb (1.5-3 kg) (3-6 lb) of fat every single day.","more":"The signal that tells the body it is full gets switched off. A bear on a salmon river or a berry slope eats, dozes briefly, then eats again, around the clock. The fat it carries into the den is every calorie it will get until spring.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"In the den it does not eat, drink, urinate or pass droppings for five to seven months. It walks out in spring with nearly all its muscle still on it.","more":"A denning bear recycles its waste instead of dumping it. Urea is broken apart and the nitrogen rebuilt into protein. A human loses serious muscle after only weeks in bed. So doctors study bears closely, hoping to learn how they hold on to theirs.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Cubs are born in midwinter, deep in the den, weighing about 1 lb (450 g) — blind, nearly bald, and smaller than their mother's paw.","more":"She is in torpor, drowsy rather than fully asleep, and rouses to clean and feed them. Her milk is roughly a third fat, so by the time the family walks out into April light the cubs weigh several kilos and can follow her.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"A bear's world is smell. The olfactory bulb is the part of the brain that handles scent. A bear's is at least five times bigger than yours. Its nose lining has about a hundred times more surface.","more":"Inside the muzzle, thin sheets of bone are folded like a honeycomb to pack in all that surface. A grizzly lifts its nose, swings its head slowly, and reads the wind. That is why hikers are told to make noise rather than surprise one at close range.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"speed","text":"A grizzly runs about 35 mph (56 km/h) — faster than any human ever timed. And no, it does not slow down going downhill.","more":"One bear was clocked holding 25-28 mph for two miles. The old advice to run downhill from a bear is simply wrong; they are agile on steep ground. Rangers tell you not to run at all, because running is what turns you into something worth chasing.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"High in the Rockies, grizzlies climb above the treeline to eat moths. One bear can turn over rock after rock and lick up 40,000 army cutworm moths in a single day.","more":"The moths shelter from summer heat in cool piles of broken rock. Their bodies are so fatty that a day of licking them up carries a bear's whole energy needs. Bears walk to the same rock fields year after year. They feed close together without much argument.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"When salmon are thick in the river, a bear stops eating whole fish. It bites out the skin, brain and eggs — the fattiest parts — and drops the rest on the bank.","more":"Biologists call it selective feeding. It only happens when fish are so plentiful the bear can afford the waste. Gulls, eagles and smaller bears finish what is left. Carcasses dragged into the woods feed the trees along the riverbank.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Most years it digs a brand-new den. The spot is usually a steep north-facing slope where snow drifts deep. The sleeping chamber is often barely bigger than the curled-up bear.","more":"A tight den holds body heat. Snow piling over the entrance seals it like a blanket, and the bear may plug the tunnel with branches and grass. Many dens collapse in the spring thaw, which is one reason bears rarely move back into an old one.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"Inland grizzlies weigh around 440 lb (200 kg). Coastal brown bears with salmon rivers to fish reach twice that. Same animal — different dinner.","more":"Kodiak and Alaskan coastal bears can top 1,000 lb (450 kg). Salmon deliver enormous fat in a short season. Inland grizzlies live on roots, grasses, berries, moths and the occasional elk calf. That is plenty to live on, not enough to grow huge.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Bears leave messages on trees. A grizzly backs up to a trunk, rubs hard, and leaves behind hair and scent — and trail cameras show bear after bear visiting the very same tree.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"A whole winter with no food, no water and no waste, and the bear walks out in spring with its strength still on it."},{"id":"giant-panda","name":"Giant panda","group":"mammals","blurb":"A black-and-white bear from the cold mountain forests of China that eats almost nothing but bamboo.","size":"About 220 lb (100 kg), the weight of a big grown man, on much shorter legs.","homes":["forest","mountain"],"stats":{"weight":220,"length":4.9,"life":20},"facts":[{"cat":"build","text":"A panda has a spare thumb. It is not a finger at all but an enlarged wrist bone, the radial sesamoid, jutting out under the paw and pressing against the other five.","more":"That extra knuckle lets a panda hold a bamboo stem the way you hold a pencil. It turns the stem, strips the leaves, and feeds it into the side of its mouth. No other bear has one. It is why a panda can sit upright and eat with its hands for hours.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"An adult eats 26-84 lb (12-38 kg) (26-84 lb) of bamboo a day and spends 12-15 hours doing it. That is more than half of every single day, spent eating.","more":"Bamboo is poor fuel: a panda gets only about a sixth of the nutrition out of what it swallows. The only way to make that work is sheer volume, so a panda's life is essentially one long meal broken up by naps.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Watch one crunch a stem as thick as your thumb. Broad flat molars and jaw muscles anchored right across a wide skull turn the whole head into a pair of bolt cutters.","more":"The panda's famously round face is mostly muscle and heavy bone built for chewing. Its molars are wide and ridged for grinding tough fiber, nothing like the shearing blades of a wolf. The bite is strong enough to split a bamboo culm lengthways.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Bamboo is a grass — but a panda is not built like a grass-eater. It has the canine teeth and the short, simple gut of a meat-eater, and it sits in the order Carnivora alongside wolves.","more":"A cow ferments plant food for days in a many-chambered stomach full of microbes. A panda's gut is a plain tube, so bamboo passes through in well under a day, barely broken down. It gets around the problem by eating vast amounts and moving slowly to save energy.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Bamboo goes through a panda so fast it comes out still green and shredded. A panda leaves droppings dozens of times a day — and researchers count wild pandas by finding them.","more":"Wild panda surveys really are run this way. The chewed bamboo fragments in a dropping come out at different lengths from panda to panda, and DNA carried along with it can identify the individual. A whole national census, built out of droppings.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A newborn panda weighs about 3.5 oz (100 g) — pink, blind, hairless, and roughly one nine-hundredth of its mother. Almost no other mammal is born so tiny beside its own parent.","more":"Picture a stick of butter, 4 oz (113 g), next to a grown man. The mother cradles her cub almost constantly for the first weeks. Its eyes do not open for six to eight weeks. It will not walk properly for months.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"It bleats. A friendly panda greeting sounds like a goat kid, and pandas also honk, chirp, huff and bark. What no panda ever does is roar.","more":"Researchers have sorted panda calls into about a dozen types. A bleat is friendly. A honk means mild annoyance. Chirping between adults in spring is a male and female trying to find each other. The bamboo is so dense that neither can see more than a few meters.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"A male marks trees by doing a handstand — walking his back feet up the trunk so he can spray urine as high as he can possibly reach.","more":"A higher mark carries further on the breeze and lasts longer before rain washes it off. It also tells any panda passing roughly how big the animal that left it was. Pandas pick big trees with rough bark for the job, because rough bark holds the scent.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Pandas do not hibernate. When mountain winter closes in, a panda simply walks downhill to where the bamboo is still standing green, and carries on eating.","more":"Bamboo does not die back the way most plants do, so there is food all year. A panda could not store enough fat to sleep through winter anyway — its diet never leaves a surplus. Instead it has the coat for it: coarse, oily fur that sheds rain and snow.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"Between meals it sleeps two to four hours wherever it happens to be. Flat on its back, draped over a fallen log, or wedged into the fork of a tree.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"A panda cub climbs before it can fight. Cubs go straight up a trunk to escape trouble, and even adults, heavy as they are, can haul themselves into the branches.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"A bear that lives on grass, given the one extra thumb it needs to hold it."},{"id":"sloth","name":"Three-toed sloth","group":"mammals","blurb":"A shaggy, slow-moving leaf-eater of Central and South American rainforests. It spends almost its entire life hanging upside down in the canopy.","size":"About 1.6 ft (0.5 m) long and roughly 9 lb (4 kg), with very long arms.","homes":["rainforest","forest"],"stats":{"speed":0.15,"weight":9,"length":1.6,"life":25},"facts":[{"cat":"speed","text":"Top speed on the ground is about 0.15 mph (0.24 km/h). That is roughly 13 feet (4 m) in a minute. You cover that in two seconds.","more":"Slowness is the whole strategy. Leaves carry very little energy, so a sloth spends almost none. It has far less muscle than other mammals its size, and simply cannot move fast, not even to escape.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"Its arms are much longer than its legs, and each hand ends in three curved claws about as long as your finger.","more":"A sloth is built for hanging, not standing. Those long arms reach across gaps between branches without the animal ever letting go. The claws do the gripping, so a sloth can hang there and fall asleep.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It can take about a month for a single leaf to pass all the way through a sloth. That is the slowest digestion measured in any mammal.","more":"Leaves are tough, low in energy and often mildly poisonous. A sloth's four-chambered stomach is packed with microbes that ferment them for weeks. The contents alone can make up a third of the animal's body weight.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"A sloth climbs all the way down to the forest floor to poop, about once a week. It is the most dangerous thing it ever does.","more":"On the ground a sloth is nearly helpless, and many sloths that get eaten are caught on this trip. It digs a small hole at the base of a tree, goes, covers it over, then hauls itself back up.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"You have seven neck bones. A giraffe has seven. A three-toed sloth has nine, and can swivel its head around roughly 270 degrees.","more":"Extra neck bones let a sloth scan for harpy eagles and snakes without moving its body. A motionless sloth is a nearly invisible sloth. It can also reach leaves all around it without shifting its grip.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"Sloth fur is grooved, and green algae takes root in the grooves. In the wet season a sloth slowly turns the color of the tree it is sitting in.","more":"Each hair is cracked along its length, trapping rainwater for the algae to live in. Beetles, moths and other small creatures move in as well. One sloth can carry a whole miniature community around on its back.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"A sloth's hair grows the wrong way, from belly toward back, so rain runs straight off a body that spends its life upside down.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"travel","text":"Sloths swim about three times faster than they move on the ground. They cross rainforest rivers with a steady doggy-paddle, using the same long arms they hang by.","more":"Rivers cut sloth territory into pieces, so crossings matter. In water those arms finally pay off. On land they are useless for walking, and a sloth caught on the ground has to drag itself along by its claws.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"Wild sloths sleep about 10 hours a day, not the 20 you may have heard. The bigger number came from sloths kept in cages.","more":"Researchers fitted wild sloths in Panama with tiny brain-activity recorders. They measured 9.6 hours of sleep a day. Captive sloths, with nothing to do and nothing to watch for, slept roughly six hours longer.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"A sloth's eyesight is genuinely poor and its hearing is not much better. It works out which leaves to eat mostly by smell and by touch.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A baby sloth rides on its mother's belly for months. It learns which leaves are safe by taking chewed pieces straight from her lips.","more":"A poisonous mistake is expensive, so a youngster copies its mother rather than experimenting. It ends up feeding from the same handful of tree species she used. That is why two neighboring sloths can eat quite different menus.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"A sloth's body temperature drifts up and down with the weather. On a cool morning it climbs to a sunny branch and warms itself, the way a lizard does.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Hooked claws, grooved fur, a stomach in no hurry whatsoever. Not everything was made to be fast."},{"id":"naked-mole-rat","name":"Naked mole-rat","group":"mammals","blurb":"A nearly hairless, buck-toothed rodent from East Africa. It spends its whole life underground in a colony organized like an ant nest.","size":"About 3.6 in (9 cm) long — about as long as your finger.","homes":["grassland","desert"],"stats":{"weight":0,"length":0.3,"life":30},"facts":[{"cat":"teamwork","text":"A colony has one queen. Only she has babies. Everyone else digs, cleans, feeds and defends. Ants and bees live like this. Almost no mammals do.","more":"Colonies average 75 to 80 animals, and nests of more than 300 have been found. The queen shoves and head-butts the workers, and that pressure keeps the rest of the colony from breeding. When she dies, several females fight for the job.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"It is about as long as your finger and weighs around 35 grams, the same as a large strawberry. The queen is the only one who outgrows that.","more":"A queen passes 50 grams easily, and the largest on record reached 80. Everything else about the body is built for tunnels. It is tube-shaped, with loose wrinkly skin it can twist around inside. There is almost no fur, and the eyes are so small they are hard to find.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Its front teeth sit outside its lips. It can seal its mouth shut behind them, which means it can dig with its teeth without swallowing soil.","more":"About a quarter of a naked mole-rat's muscle is jaw muscle. You use roughly one per cent of yours on chewing. Each of the four front teeth also moves on its own, so the animal can work them like a pair of chopsticks.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Take the air away completely and a naked mole-rat carries on for about 18 minutes. A mouse in the same sealed jar dies in under a minute.","more":"With no oxygen, its brain and heart switch to burning fructose, a sugar no other mammal can use for fuel this way. The animal slows almost to a stop, then starts up again when air returns. Crowded burrows get stuffy, and this is how it copes.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"It is the only mammal that barely warms itself. A naked mole-rat's body temperature just matches its tunnel, so it huddles in heaps or shifts to a warmer level.","more":"Most mammals burn food constantly to hold one steady temperature. A naked mole-rat does not bother, which saves an enormous amount of energy. When it gets cold, the colony stacks up into a pile of bodies, or moves up to shallower soil warmed by the sun.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Chilli pepper does nothing to a naked mole-rat. Acid does not sting it either. Its skin nerves simply do not send that message to its brain.","more":"Burrow air is thick with carbon dioxide. That turns skin acidic and would leave any other mammal in constant discomfort. Naked mole-rats lack the chemical messenger that carries that particular signal, so life down there does not hurt. Pain researchers study them closely.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"A mouse this size lives about three years. Naked mole-rats have passed 30, and the oldest on record was over 37.","more":"Stranger still, their chance of dying in a given year barely climbs as they get older, which is not how ageing usually works. They also very rarely develop cancer. That combination is why laboratories all over the world keep colonies of them.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"One colony's tunnels can add up to 2 or 3 miles (3 to 5 km) of corridor. The diggers are animals the length of your finger, working mostly with their teeth.","more":"They dig in a chain. The animal at the front chisels away at the soil face. The ones behind sweep the loose dirt backwards with their feet. It passes down the line until someone at the end kicks it out of a hole at the surface.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"A naked mole-rat runs backwards exactly as fast as it runs forwards. In a tunnel with no room to turn around, that is the only sensible design.","more":"Fine hairs scattered over its otherwise bare skin tell it where the tunnel walls are, and its short tail works as a feeler behind it. Its eyes are tiny and nearly useless down there, so touch and smell do all the work.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"babies","text":"When a female becomes queen, her body gets longer. The gaps between her backbones stretch open to make room for the pups she will carry.","more":"That is odd, because she is already a fully grown adult and most mammals stop growing there. A litter of eleven or twelve pups is perfectly ordinary. Workers take over the pups almost as soon as they can walk.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Pups beg the queen for her droppings and eat them. It sounds revolting. It is how they pick up the gut microbes they need to digest tough roots.","more":"Naked mole-rats live on underground tubers, which are almost impossible to break down without help from the right bacteria. Those bacteria have to come from somewhere. Rabbits and several other plant-eaters do their own version of the same thing.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"They eat a root from the inside out. The root is hollowed into a shell, but the skin is left intact. The plant survives and quietly refills the larder.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Blind, bare and buried in the dark, and fitted out for every single thing that dark throws at it."},{"id":"pangolin","name":"Pangolin","group":"mammals","blurb":"The only mammal covered head to tail in hard scales. It is a shy night-time insect eater that curls into an armored ball when frightened.","size":"Body about as long as your arm, with a tail just as long again.","homes":["forest","grassland","rainforest"],"stats":{"length":2.6},"facts":[{"cat":"build","text":"Every scale is keratin, the same material as your fingernails. A pangolin is wearing several hundred overlapping fingernails. They make up roughly a fifth of its weight.","more":"The scales lie over each other like roof tiles and have edges sharp enough to cut. They keep growing and wear down as the animal digs and squeezes through burrows, exactly the way your nails do. A newborn's scales are pale and soft, and harden within days.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"Pangolins come in wildly different sizes. The giant pangolin far outweighs the smallest kind.","more":"The long-tailed pangolin is around 2 to 5 lb (2.5 kg) and lives up in the trees. The giant pangolin stays on the ground and can reach roughly 73 lb (33 kg). Same armor, same tongue, same trick of rolling up, on completely different scales.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Threatened, a pangolin rolls into a ball so tight that a big cat cannot get in. Predators have been filmed batting one around and eventually giving up.","more":"Its name comes from a Malay word meaning one who rolls up. Curled up, the tail locks over the head and only scale edges face outward. It can also lash out with that tail. This works beautifully against a leopard, and not at all against a human hand, which is the pangolin's tragedy.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"The biggest pangolins can push a tongue 16 inches (40 cm) out of their mouths, and it is barely half a centimeter thick. It is not anchored in the mouth at all.","more":"The root of the tongue sits deep in the chest near the breastbone, not up in the jaw like yours. When it is not in use it slides back into a sheath in there. Sticky saliva coats the whole length, so ants and termites simply stick and come back with it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"No teeth. None at all. A pangolin still puts away 5 to 7 ounces (140-200 g) of ants and termites in a day, without chewing a single one.","more":"Instead of teeth it has a muscular stomach lined with hard keratin spines, and it swallows small stones on purpose. Stomach, stones and spines grind the insects up together. A chicken's gizzard works on exactly the same principle.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"When it tears open a termite mound, a pangolin can close its nostrils and ears and shut a set of thick eyelids. Nothing angry gets in while it eats.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Cornered, a pangolin can spray a foul-smelling liquid from a gland under its tail. Skunks are famous for this trick. Pangolins do it without the publicity.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A pangolin pup rides on the base of its mother's tail. If she is alarmed and rolls up, the baby is folded away inside the ball with her.","more":"The pup is born with soft scales and stays pressed against its mother in a burrow for the first weeks. It rides along for around three months, which is also how it learns which mounds are worth ripping open.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"A pangolin walks on its knuckles with the front claws tucked under, keeping the digging edges sharp. Some species walk almost upright, tail out behind as a counterweight.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"The long-tailed pangolin has 49 tail bones, more than any other mammal. It can hang its entire body weight from that tail.","more":"All those joints make the tail grippy along its whole length, so it works like a fifth limb in the trees. The animal can dangle upside down from a branch with every foot free for digging into a nest.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"A pangolin sees badly and hunts almost entirely by nose. It can smell an ant colony through solid ground and dig straight down onto it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"The pangolin is the most trafficked wild mammal on Earth. More than a million were taken from the wild in a single decade, for scales that are only fingernail.","more":"Every pangolin species is now protected and international trade in them is banned. Rangers, sniffer dogs and rescue centers work on pangolins full time. Hardly any child has ever seen one in the wild, which is exactly why they are worth knowing about.","kind":"record"}],"wonder":"Armor plate, a tongue rooted in the chest, a stomach full of stones. One small animal, kitted out completely for a life of eating ants."},{"id":"platypus","name":"Platypus","group":"mammals","blurb":"An Australian river mammal with a soft duck-like bill, webbed feet and a beaver's tail. Only five kinds of mammal in the world lay eggs, and this is one of them.","size":"About as long as your arm from elbow to fingertips, and smaller than most people picture.","homes":["freshwater","forest"],"stats":{"weight":3,"length":1.5,"life":12},"facts":[{"cat":"senses","text":"A platypus hunts with its eyes, ears and nostrils all shut. Its bill carries about 40,000 sensors that pick up the faint electricity of a shrimp's twitching muscles.","more":"This is electroreception. Every muscle movement leaks a tiny electric field into the water. Packed in beside them are tens of thousands of touch sensors, and together they build a picture of a muddy riverbed the platypus never actually sees. It sweeps its head side to side as it swims, scanning.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A platypus lays eggs. Usually two, small and soft like leather, and the mother curls around them in a burrow for roughly ten days.","more":"The young hatch blind, bald and tiny. They stay underground for three or four months before they ever see the river, by which time they are close to full grown.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Platypus mothers have no nipples. Milk seeps out through the skin of the belly, pools in grooves in the fur, and the babies lap it up from there.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Male platypuses have a spur on each back ankle that delivers venom. It will not kill you, but people who have been spurred describe pain that morphine barely touches.","more":"Only the males have working spurs, and the venom runs strongest in the breeding season, which points at fights with other males rather than defense. There is no antivenom. Human victims have reported the hand aching for weeks, sometimes months, afterwards.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"Males are noticeably bigger than females. A male runs about 20 inches (50 cm) nose to tail-tip, against 17 inches (43 cm) for a female. He weighs close to twice as much.","more":"A big male is around 4 lb (1.7 kg) (nearly 4 lb) and a female around 2 lb (0.9 kg). Almost everyone imagines a platypus far larger than it really is. Held in two hands, it is about the bulk of a small cat.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"A platypus has no working stomach. Its gullet runs almost straight into its intestine, with no acid bag in between at all.","more":"It has no adult teeth either. It scoops food into cheek pouches, surfaces, and grinds the lot between rough horny pads in its jaws. Young platypuses do grow small teeth, but they fall out early and never come back.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Shine an ultraviolet light on a platypus and its plain brown fur glows cyan and green. Researchers noticed it on museum specimens, then found it on living animals too.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The front feet are the paddles. The webbing folds away under the palm when the platypus walks on land, so the claws can take over for digging.","more":"Underwater the web spreads out well past the claws and the animal rows with its front feet, steering with the back feet and tail. On land it walks on its knuckles with the web tucked back, which is why a platypus on a riverbank looks so awkward.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"That flat tail is not a paddle. It is a pantry. A platypus stores much of its fat reserve in its tail, and a well-fed one has a noticeably fat tail.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"You have two sex chromosomes. A platypus has ten.","more":"In nearly every other mammal, sex comes down to a single pair, X and Y. A male platypus carries five X and five Y chromosomes, which link up in a chain when its cells divide. No other group of mammals does it this way.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"A nesting mother digs a burrow into the riverbank. It can run 100 feet (30 m) back. Then she plugs it behind her with packed soil.","more":"The plugs keep the temperature and humidity steady inside and keep snakes and goannas out. She has to break through her own wall every time she leaves to feed, and rebuild it when she returns.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"One dive lasts about 35 seconds. A platypus hunting through the night may make well over a thousand of them, and eat around a fifth of its own body weight.","more":"It works mostly at night along the river bottom, taking insect larvae, worms and small freshwater shrimp. Each dive is a quick sweep of the bill through the silt, then back to the surface to chew and breathe before going down again.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"A bill that reads electricity, fur that glows, eggs, venom, and milk that arrives like sweat. Some things were clearly made without a category in mind."},{"id":"echidna","name":"Echidna","group":"mammals","blurb":"A spine-covered, ant-eating Australian mammal. It lays a single egg into a pouch on its own belly. Only five kinds of mammal in the world lay eggs, and this is one.","size":"About 1.3 ft (40 cm) long and around 9 lb (4 kg), covered in spines.","homes":["forest","desert","grassland"],"stats":{"weight":9,"length":1.3,"life":10},"facts":[{"cat":"babies","text":"An echidna lays one leathery egg straight into a pouch on her belly. Ten days later a tiny baby hatches inside the pouch.","more":"The hatchling is called a puggle. It weighs about a third of a gram, lighter than a raisin, and is blind, hairless and pink. There is no nipple to find: milk comes out of a patch of skin and the puggle laps it up. It stays in the pouch two to three months.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The pouch is not permanent. It is a fold of muscle the mother tightens into a pocket only when she has an egg, and lets go of afterwards.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"An echidna's beak reads electricity. Around 400 tiny sensors in the skin pick up the faint electric flicker of termites moving through damp soil.","more":"A platypus has about a hundred times more of these sensors, because water carries electricity far better than dirt does. An echidna's work best when the ground is wet. That is one reason echidnas get busy after rain.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Frightened, an echidna does not run. It digs straight down where it stands, sinking until nothing shows but a shield of spines.","more":"Its legs are short and startlingly strong. On soft ground it can disappear in under a minute, and once wedged into a crevice it cannot be pulled out by a person. The spines are not quills but modified hairs, up to 2 inches (5 cm) long.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"An echidna has no teeth. It grinds ants and termites between hard pads at the base of its tongue and the roof of its mouth, then swallows the paste.","more":"The tongue is long, thin and coated in sticky saliva, and it flicks in and out of a mound faster than you can follow. A feeding echidna also swallows a lot of soil, which helps with the grinding.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"An echidna runs cooler than nearly every other mammal, about 86-90°F (30-32°C). Put your hand on one and it feels less warm than a dog.","more":"It can let that temperature fall much further. Echidnas go into torpor and full hibernation, cooling toward the soil around them, with the heart slowing to four to seven beats a minute. Yours is beating around eighty right now.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"When a bushfire comes through, an echidna goes underground and shuts down. Researchers tracking them find animals alive in burned country where almost nothing else made it.","more":"They shelter in burrows, logs and rock crevices and drop into torpor, which lowers how much oxygen and energy they need. They can hold that state for up to three weeks. Afterwards they come out into a landscape full of insects and very few competitors.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"An echidna's back feet point backwards, and one claw on each is extra long. That claw is a comb, and it is the only way to scratch through a coat of spines.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"babies","text":"In breeding season, as many as ten males will line up nose to tail behind one female and follow her for weeks. It is called a train.","more":"The train can trail her for up to four weeks, with males dropping out and new ones joining on the end. When she finally stops, the males dig a trench in a ring around her and shove each other out of it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Echidnas live across more of Australia than any other native mammal. Snowy mountains, bare desert, wet forest, the edges of cities. Very few animals cope with a spread like that.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"Echidnas can swim. They paddle along with only the tip of the beak above the surface, using it as a snorkel, and have been watched crossing rivers.","more":"The spines trap air and the short legs paddle well enough, so an echidna in water is far less clumsy than an echidna on a road. They have been recorded swimming in the sea as well as in rivers and dams.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"An echidna curls up to about the size of a rugby ball. It weighs anywhere from 4 to 15 pounds (2-7 kg), depending on where in Australia it lives.","more":"Tasmanian echidnas are the biggest and by far the furriest, with thick hair pushing up between the spines. Desert echidnas are smaller with barely any fur at all. Most wild echidnas live around ten years, though individuals have reached forty.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"It lays an egg, senses electricity, and waits out a fire underground. One small spiny animal holding an armful of surprises."},{"id":"red-kangaroo","name":"Red kangaroo","group":"mammals","blurb":"The largest marsupial alive. It is a long-legged grazer that crosses the dry heart of Australia by hopping.","size":"A big male stands about 5 ft (1.5 m) tall, chin height on a grown man, and weighs about 146 lb (66 kg).","homes":["grassland","desert"],"stats":{"speed":35,"weight":146,"length":4.6,"life":8},"facts":[{"cat":"speed","text":"Watch the back legs. In a short burst a red kangaroo hits about 35 mph (56 km/h), faster than any human sprinter who has ever lived.","more":"That is a sprint, not a cruise. Crossing open country it settles into a steady 15 mph (25 km/h) and holds that pace for miles, because at hopping speed the traveling costs it almost nothing extra.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Its legs are springs. Enormous Achilles tendons stretch as it lands and snap back as it launches, so most of the energy for the next hop is recycled from the last one.","more":"Measure a hopping kangaroo's oxygen use and something odd shows up: as it speeds up, it does not breathe harder. The tendons are doing work the muscles would otherwise pay for. A galloping horse gets no such bargain.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"Slow it right down and the tail becomes a fifth leg. Shuffling along while it grazes, a kangaroo plants its tail and pushes as hard as the front and back legs combined.","more":"Researchers had kangaroos walk over plates that measure force. The tail was not merely propping the animal up. It was doing about as much pushing work as one of your legs does when you walk.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A newborn red kangaroo is about an inch (2.5 cm) long, pink, blind and deaf. And it makes the climb to the pouch entirely on its own.","more":"Roughly 33 days after mating, the joey is born with its hind legs barely formed but its front arms already strong and clawed. It hauls itself up through its mother's fur, finds a teat, and stays attached for months.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"A mother kangaroo can press pause. When the rain fails, a tiny embryo simply stops developing and waits, sometimes for months, until the country is good enough to raise it.","more":"The pause is called embryonic diapause. A female can have a joey at heel, another in the pouch, and a third on hold, all at once. The third one restarts the moment the grass comes back.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Grass wears teeth down to stumps. So a kangaroo's worn front molars drop out and the teeth behind slide forward to replace them, the whole row creeping along like a conveyor belt.","more":"Each side of the jaw gets four grinding molars, arriving one at a time across a kangaroo's life. When the last one is worn flat there is no replacement behind it, and a very old kangaroo can no longer chew tough grass.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"On a scorching day a kangaroo licks its own forearms. The skin there is thin with blood vessels close beneath, so evaporating spit chills the blood on its way back inside.","more":"It is the same trick as a wet cloth on the back of your neck, done deliberately and over and over. Kangaroos also lie up in shade through the worst hours and do most of their feeding at dusk and dawn.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Red kangaroos live where the creeks stay dry for months at a stretch. They can go a long time without drinking, pulling most of the water they need straight out of green plants.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Males box, but the boxing is not the weapon. A kangaroo leans back on its tail like a tripod and drives both hind feet forward, claws first, with its whole weight behind them.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Its ears swivel separately, each one turning toward a different sound. The head stays down in the grass and carries on eating.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Danger gets announced with a foot. A kangaroo thumps a hind leg hard against the ground, and every head in the mob comes up at once.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"A kangaroo cannot hop backwards. Look at that heavy tail and those long feet: they only work in one direction, so to retreat it has to turn its whole body around first.","kind":"worked"}],"wonder":"Springs for legs, a fifth leg for a tail, and a pause button for the hard years. Every part of it fits the country it was put in."},{"id":"koala","name":"Koala","group":"mammals","blurb":"A thick-furred, tree-living marsupial from eastern Australia. It eats almost nothing but eucalyptus leaves. Not a bear.","size":"About 2.3 ft (70 cm) long and 18 lb (8 kg), with a big broad head and no tail to speak of.","homes":["forest"],"stats":{"weight":18,"length":2.3,"life":15},"facts":[{"cat":"food","text":"Eucalyptus leaves are loaded with oils and toxins that would make most animals sick. A koala's liver takes those toxins apart and flushes them out in its urine.","more":"Almost nothing else competes for this food, which is exactly why it works. The price is that the leaves are tough, watery and poor in energy. So a koala's entire day is arranged around not wasting any.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Inside the koala is a fermenting tank. The caecum, a blind pouch off the gut, runs about 6.5 ft (2 m), which is longer than the koala it lives inside.","more":"Millions of microbes live in there, slowly breaking down leaf fiber the koala's own body cannot touch. A meal can sit fermenting for days. That long, patient gut is how something cat-sized survives on something so poor.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"Up to 20 hours a day dozing or sitting perfectly still. That is not laziness. The best read is the food: eucalyptus gives back so little energy that a koala cannot afford to spend much.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"gross","text":"A koala joey's first solid food is a soft green paste called pap, produced in its mother's gut and eaten straight from her.","more":"Pap is not ordinary droppings. It comes from the caecum and is crowded with living microbes. Without that mouthful the joey could never digest eucalyptus at all, so its mother is handing over her gut bacteria along with the meal.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Shut your eyes near a bellowing male koala and you would guess at something enormous. He has an extra pair of vocal folds sitting where the nose and throat meet.","more":"Most mammals make sound with folds inside the voice box. The male koala has a second, far larger pair outside it. They are about three times longer and hundreds of times heavier. That is how an animal you could lift produces a bass roar.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Koalas have fingerprints. Real ones, with loops and arches and whorls, so close to human prints that under a microscope even experts can confuse the two.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Look at the hands. Each front paw has two thumbs instead of one, so the grip closes on a branch from both sides at once, like a pair of pliers.","more":"The back feet are stranger still. The second and third toes are joined inside a single sheath, with two claws poking out the end. The koala uses that double claw as a comb for its fur.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"When rain runs down a tree trunk, koalas lick it off the bark. Researchers watching wild koalas recorded them drinking that way for many minutes at a stretch.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"On a blazing day a koala presses itself flat against a tree trunk. Thermal cameras show the trunk can sit several degrees cooler than the air around it.","more":"Measurements have found trunks up to about 9 degrees F (5 degrees C) below air temperature. A koala hugging one is doing what you do lying on cool tiles. It can be the difference between surviving a heatwave and not.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"The joey is born blind, hairless and jellybean-sized, into a pouch that opens downward. A ring of muscle around the opening keeps it from dropping out.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"That big leathery nose does the choosing. A koala sniffs a leaf before it bites, and turns down far more than it accepts, even in a tree it feeds in daily.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"A koala from the cool southern forests can be roughly twice the weight of a northern one. That is about 26 lb (12 kg) against 14 lb (6.5 kg). Its fur is shaggier and grayer too.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"A whole animal built around one poisonous tree, right down to the gut bacteria a mother hands to her child."},{"id":"wombat","name":"Wombat","group":"mammals","blurb":"A barrel-shaped Australian marsupial. It digs long tunnel systems and comes out at night to graze.","size":"Knee-high and shaped like a barrel, about 57 lb (26 kg) of very solid animal.","homes":["forest","grassland","mountain"],"stats":{"speed":25,"weight":57,"length":3.3,"life":11},"facts":[{"cat":"gross","text":"Wombat droppings come out as cubes. Eighty to a hundred of them a night, and the wombat balances them on rocks and logs where other wombats will find them.","more":"Scientists who studied wombat intestines found the last stretch is not evenly stretchy. Stiff patches and soft patches squeeze the drying pellets into flat sides. A cube stays put on a rock. A ball rolls off. That is the whole point.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Its backside is armor: a thick pad of cartilage under tough hide and coarse fur, with barely any bone in it. A wombat reverses into its tunnel and blocks the doorway.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"One burrow can run 65 ft (20 m) underground, with side tunnels and sleeping chambers off the main shaft. About two-thirds of a wombat's life happens down there.","more":"Below ground the temperature barely moves, whatever the weather is doing above. That is how a thick-furred animal survives blazing summers. The wombat is carrying its own cool room around with it, dug by hand.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Grass, and a great deal of chewing. Digesting a single meal takes a wombat 8 to 14 days, one of the slowest digestions known in any mammal.","more":"Slow is the point. The longer food sits, the more water and nourishment gets wrung out of it. That matters when your diet is dry native grass and the nearest creek is nowhere. Wombat droppings come out remarkably dry.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Every one of a wombat's teeth keeps growing for its whole life. The best read is that grinding gritty grass and roots would otherwise wear them away to nothing.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"babies","text":"The pouch faces backwards. A digging wombat hurls dirt out behind itself. A forward-facing pouch would pack solid with soil and bury the joey inside it. Nobody had to watch that happen to work out why.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"speed","text":"It looks like a barrel on legs. But a wombat can hit 25 mph (40 km/h) in a short dash. That is quick enough to beat most people back to the burrow.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"Wombat tunnels get used by animals that never dug them. Cameras set at burrow entrances have filmed small mammals, lizards and birds going in and coming back out.","more":"The wombat does not invite them and mostly does not appear to mind. One animal's hard work ends up sheltering a whole neighbourhood, which is why researchers call the wombat an engineer of its landscape.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"It is the largest burrowing plant-eater on earth. Around 57 lb (26 kg) of muscle, claw and bone, built low to the ground for shoving through soil.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Weak eyes, superb nose and ears. A wombat works the dark by smell, including reading which wombat left which cube on which rock.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Cornered underground, a wombat does not turn and fight. It braces its legs and jams that armored rump up into the tunnel roof, and the doorway simply closes.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"The hot part of the day is spent asleep underground. A wombat surfaces around dusk and grazes for hours, sometimes traveling well beyond its own burrow.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Shovels at the front, armor at the back, and a gut that turns dry grass into a signpost."},{"id":"tasmanian-devil","name":"Tasmanian devil","group":"mammals","blurb":"The largest meat-eating marsupial alive. It is a stocky, black, night-roaming scavenger, found wild only in Tasmania.","size":"About 2.1 ft (65 cm) long and 18 lb (8 kg), standing only around a foot (30 cm) at the shoulder.","homes":["forest","grassland"],"stats":{"weight":18,"length":2.1,"life":5},"facts":[{"cat":"record","text":"For its size, no living mammal bites harder. Pound for pound a devil's jaws out-bite a lion's, and it uses them to crunch straight through leg bone.","more":"Scientists compare bites using a bite force quotient, which measures bite strength against body size. The devil scores around 181, while a lion comes in near 112. A devil's head is very nearly all jaw muscle.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Nothing gets left behind. A devil eats the skin, the fur, the organs and the bones, and a dead wallaby can vanish overnight with barely a stain on the grass.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Given half an hour alone with a carcass, a devil can put away 40% of its own body weight. For you that would be roughly 25 school lunches in one sitting.","more":"Biologists call it gorge feeding. Devils compete hard for whatever has died, so the first one there eats as much and as fast as it possibly can before the next one shoves in. A well-fed devil walks away visibly swollen.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A mother devil gives birth to 20 or 30 young at once. She has four teats. Only the first four to arrive will ever feed.","more":"The newborns must crawl through her fur into a backward-facing pouch and latch on. Those four stay attached for around 100 days, then wait in a den while she hunts.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"The name came from the noise. Devils have at least eleven distinct calls, including snorts, growls, coughs and a screech that carries a long way through dark bush.","more":"Most of the racket is bluff. A crowd of devils around a carcass screams, gapes and shoves, and the noise largely settles who eats first without anybody getting badly hurt. Loud is cheaper than injured.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"weird","text":"When a devil is excited or upset, its ears turn red. Blood floods the thin bare skin there, and its mood becomes visible from across a clearing.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"Satellite collars show devils covering about 5 miles (8.6 km) on an average night. They criss-cross their patch looking for anything that has died.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"A devil keeps its savings in its tail. A thick, fat tail means an animal in good condition; a thin, ropy one is the first sign something is wrong.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"It works the dark by nose and whisker. Long whiskers sweep out to about shoulder width, feeling the edges of things the devil cannot see.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Days are spent asleep in a den. That might be a hollow log, a cave, or a thicket of dense grass. Quite often it is a burrow some wombat dug and abandoned.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"This is the biggest meat-eating marsupial on earth. A male still averages only about 18 lb (8 kg). That is no heavier than a very large house cat.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Devils rarely chase down healthy adult prey. They find what has already died — and the best read is that this keeps the bush clean and stops flies and disease building up around carcasses.","more":"Farmers in Tasmania have long noticed something. A paddock with devils working it has far fewer rotting carcasses in it. The animal with the worst reputation on the island is mostly doing the tidying up.","kind":"worked"}],"wonder":"Nothing goes to waste in a forest that has a devil in it. The clean-up crew arrives with its own bone-cracking jaws."},{"id":"african-elephant","name":"African elephant","group":"mammals","blurb":"The largest animal that walks on land, and the only one with a trunk that works as both a nose and a hand.","size":"A big bull stands taller than a 10 ft (3 m) basketball hoop and can weigh 12,125 lb (5,500 kg).","homes":["grassland","forest","desert"],"stats":{"speed":16,"weight":12125,"length":19.7,"life":60},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"Nothing walking on land is bigger. A large bull stands over 10 ft (3.2 m) at the shoulder and can reach 13,000 lb (6 tonnes), which is roughly four cars.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"There is no bone anywhere in the trunk. Tens of thousands of separate muscle bundles run through it, pulling against each other. Your entire body contains about 600 muscles.","more":"That is what makes it a crane and a pair of tweezers at once. The same trunk lifts a fallen branch and then picks up a single blade of grass. At the tip, an African elephant has two finger-like points that pinch together.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"An elephant does not drink through its trunk. It sucks water up, holds around 2 gallons (8 liters) in there, then curls the trunk into its mouth and squirts.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"An adult puts away up to 330 lb (150 kg) of grass, bark and branches every day, and spends most of its waking hours doing it.","more":"It drinks 50 to 60 gallons (180 to 230 liters) a day as well. Feeding that body is close to a full-time job, which is why herds are always walking, and why the routes to water get remembered so carefully.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Elephant molars are the size of bricks, and an elephant gets six sets in a lifetime. As one wears down it is pushed forward and out, and a fresh one moves up behind.","more":"Only about four grinding teeth are in use at any moment. When the sixth and final set is worn flat, no replacement is coming, and very old elephants shift to soft marsh grasses that need less grinding.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Elephants talk in rumbles pitched too low for human ears. Those calls carry for kilometers across open country. You can stand right there and hear nothing at all.","more":"The sound travels through the ground as well as the air. In playback experiments, elephants given a rumble through the soil alone, with nothing audible above it, froze and turned toward it. They pick the vibration up through their feet and trunk.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Smell is its main sense, not sight. The African elephant carries roughly 2,000 working genes for detecting odor, about five times as many as you have.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"An African elephant has the largest ears of any animal alive, and they are radiators. Big blood vessels run just beneath that thin skin, and flapping them cools the blood on its way back inside.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"An elephant barely sweats. It has no sweat glands anywhere on its body except between its toes, so its skin is covered instead in millions of hair-thin channels that hold water.","more":"That is why a mud bath is not only play. The cracked surface traps water and mud instead of shedding it. One wallow keeps evaporating and cooling for hours. The coating also shields the skin from sun and biting insects.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Pregnancy lasts 22 months, longer than any other mammal. The calf is born at around 250 lb (115 kg), heavier than a grown man, and is up on its feet within hours.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"The herd follows its oldest female. Families led by older matriarchs proved better at telling a stranger's call from a friend's, so the group's memory really does live in her.","more":"In one severe drought, some herds were led by matriarchs who had lived through an earlier dry spell. Those herds walked out to distant water. They lost fewer calves than herds without that experience at the front.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"An elephant cannot jump. No adult has ever been recorded with all four feet off the ground at once. The leg bones stack straight down like pillars, with no spring in them.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"A nose with no bone in it that can shove over a tree, then lift a single seed. Strength and gentleness built into the same tool."},{"id":"giraffe","name":"Giraffe","group":"mammals","blurb":"The tallest animal alive. It is a spotted, long-necked browser that eats the treetops nothing else can reach.","size":"Tall enough to look in through an upstairs window","homes":["grassland","forest"],"stats":{"speed":37,"weight":2646,"life":25},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"A big bull stands about 18 ft (5.5 m) tall. Stand him beside a basketball hoop and his head is still more than two meters clear above the rim.","more":"Almost all of that is leg and neck. A giraffe's front legs alone are about as tall as a grown-up, and the neck adds nearly 6.5 ft (2 m) on top. Which is why, to drink from a puddle, it has to splay those front legs wide and fold itself down like a deckchair.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Your neck has seven bones in it. So does a giraffe's. Just seven — but a single one can be longer than your whole forearm.","more":"The bones are stretched, not multiplied. One giraffe neck vertebra can measure over 11 in (28 cm). That is why the neck bends in long smooth curves instead of coiling tightly the way a swan's does.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"A giraffe's blood pressure is roughly twice yours. It has to be — the heart is pushing blood two meters straight uphill to reach the brain.","more":"Doing that needs a very thick-walled left ventricle, the heart's main pumping chamber. Then the head swings down to drink. A mesh of fine vessels at the base of the skull, called the rete mirabile, soaks up the surge. The brain never gets flooded.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"The tongue is dark purple-blue and about 18 in (45 cm) long — long enough to reach in past the thorns and strip an acacia branch bare.","more":"It is prehensile, meaning it grips. The giraffe wraps it round a branch and drags the leaves off through its lips, thick saliva coating the thorns on the way down. A giraffe can put away 75 lb (34 kg) of leaves in a day.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Giraffes were long thought to be nearly silent. Then researchers left microphones running overnight and caught them humming — a low steady note, around 92 hertz.","more":"It took over 900 hours of night recordings, and the hums turned up only after dark. One idea is that it is a contact call for when it is too black to see each other. What it actually means, nobody has proved yet.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"A giraffe sleeps only about four and a half hours out of twenty-four. It does not take one long night. It sleeps in short scraps, usually standing up.","more":"Deep dreaming sleep barely happens at all. The bouts run roughly a minute and add up to a few minutes a day. The head curls right back onto its own hindquarters. Lying down takes time to undo, and a giraffe on the ground is a giraffe that cannot run.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"A giraffe's kick can kill a lion outright. There is a reason lions mostly go after the calves instead.","more":"The hoof is broad and it swings on a leg about 6.5 ft (2 m) long. Giraffes can kick forwards, backwards and sideways. A mother will stand over her calf and turn slowly on the spot, keeping the danger in front of her feet.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A giraffe is born while its mother is standing up, so its first experience of the world is a two-meter drop. Most are on their feet within the hour.","more":"The umbilical cord snaps in the fall. The calf arrives already about 6 ft (1.8 m) tall — as tall as a grown-up — and can run alongside the herd within a day of being born.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"No two giraffes wear the same spot pattern. It is as individual as your fingerprints. Researchers identify wild giraffes by photographing their sides.","more":"Calves inherit the shape and roundness of their blotches from their mothers, so family lines can look alike. And against dappled shade, that pattern breaks up the outline of an animal weighing around 2,650 lb (1,200 kg).","kind":"found"},{"cat":"speed","text":"A galloping giraffe reaches about 37 mph (60 km/h). At a walk it paces — both legs on the same side swing forward together, the way a camel walks.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Giraffes chew the cud. Watch a resting one and you can see the lump of half-chewed leaves traveling back up two meters of neck to be ground again.","more":"Like a cow, a giraffe has a stomach with several chambers. Food goes down, softens, comes back up as cud and gets rechewed. The lump climbing the neck is one of the strangest things you will ever see at a zoo.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Males fight by swinging their necks like clubs, landing the blow with the bony knobs on their heads. The knobs are called ossicones, and old bulls wear the hair right off them.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Made tall — and given everything tall requires: the pressure to climb the neck, and the mesh at the skull to make the way down safe."},{"id":"hippopotamus","name":"Hippopotamus","group":"mammals","blurb":"A river giant that spends its days sunk in the water and its nights out on land, mowing grass.","size":"Around 3,300 lb (1.5 tonnes), and about 11.5 ft (3.5 m) nose to tail","homes":["freshwater","grassland"],"stats":{"speed":19,"weight":3307,"length":11.5,"life":45},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"A big bull averages around 3,300 lb (1,500 kg) — a small car — and the heaviest on record pushed past two and a half tonnes. On land, only elephants and rhinos outweigh them.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Hippos are not really swimmers. In deep water they walk and bound along the bottom, shoving off and drifting up again in slow, low-gravity leaps.","more":"They are too heavy for their size to float easily, so they use the riverbed like a road. An adult surfaces to breathe about every five or six minutes, and its own weight keeps it down there without any effort at all.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Eyes, ears and nostrils sit in one line along the top of the skull. A hippo can see, hear and breathe with the whole rest of it hidden underwater.","more":"When it dives, the nostrils clamp shut and the ears fold closed. Nothing gets in. It is the same trick that lets a calf feed underwater without swallowing half the river.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Hippo skin oozes a thick red liquid that people used to call blood sweat. It is neither blood nor sweat. The chemistry says sunscreen.","more":"Chemists pulled two pigments out of it. Hipposudoric acid is red. Norhipposudoric acid is orange. Both soak up ultraviolet light, and one of them slows the growth of bacteria. That is sunblock and antiseptic, made by the animal's own skin.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"build","text":"A hippo's lower canine teeth reach about 20 in (50 cm) — arm's length — and never stop growing. They sharpen themselves every single time the jaws shut.","more":"The lower tusks grind against the uppers with each bite, keeping a chisel edge on them. And a yawning hippo is not sleepy. It is opening its mouth to something past a right angle to show you those teeth. It is a warning.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"For an animal that size the diet is oddly plain: grass. A hippo crops around 88 lb (40 kg) a night, walking three to five kilometers from the water to find it.","more":"They graze with their tough lips, not those tusks — the tusks are weapons, not tools. Hippos take the same route night after night, wearing deep double-grooved trails up the riverbanks that you can still see by day.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"A sleeping hippo can rise, breathe and sink again without ever waking. The surfacing happens by reflex, the way you swallow without deciding to.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Hippos call underwater as well as above it. A submerged hippo can answer another one without either of them lifting a head clear of the river.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Calves are often born in the water, and they can nurse underwater. Nostrils shut, ears folded. Every so often the calf lets go and bobs up for air.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Hippos are among the most dangerous large animals in Africa. The best read of the attacks is that they are not hunting people but defending water: a bull will charge anything standing between him and the deep.","more":"They are fiercely territorial in the river and much calmer out of it. Guides in hippo country teach one rule above all the others. Never put yourself between a hippo and the water, especially at night when the herd is out grazing.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"home","text":"A hippo marks its patch while it dungs. It spins its short flat tail like a propeller. The result gets flung over bushes, water and rivals alike.","more":"It is not only rude. Enormous amounts of that dung end up in the river, feeding insects and fish and carrying nutrients from the grassland into the water. Hippos fertilise the rivers they live in.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Skin too delicate for the African sun — and the sunscreen supplied with it, built right into the animal."},{"id":"rhinoceros","name":"White rhinoceros","group":"mammals","blurb":"The second-heaviest animal on land. It is a grass-mowing giant in thick gray hide, with two horns made of the same stuff as your fingernails.","size":"As heavy as two small cars, with a back as high as a tall grown-up","homes":["grassland"],"stats":{"speed":31,"weight":4409,"length":12.5,"life":40},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"Only elephants are heavier on land. A bull white rhino averages 4,409 to 5,071 lb (2,000 to 2,300 kg) (4,400 to 5,000 lb) — two small cars — standing on legs like tree stumps.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"That wide square upper lip is a lawnmower. The white rhino grazes short grass, head down, for roughly half of every day.","more":"Its neighbor the black rhino has a pointed, grabby upper lip instead. That lip pulls leaves and twigs off bushes. Same country, same size, completely different mouths. The two of them almost never want the same mouthful.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The horn is not bone and it is not attached to the skull. It is keratin — the same material as your fingernails and hair — and like a fingernail, it keeps growing.","more":"Rangers sometimes cut a rhino's horn off to make the animal worthless to poachers. It grows back, because the living base stays behind, the way your nail regrows from under the cuticle.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"The front horn is usually around 2 ft (60 cm). The longest ever measured was over 5 ft (150 cm) — taller than the child reading this.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"A rhino may not recognize you standing still thirty meters away. Its eyes are poor. Its nose is not — smell is how a rhino knows what is out there.","more":"Its hearing is sharp too, and the two ears swivel separately, each tracking a different sound. Stand downwind and stay quiet, and a grazing rhino may never work out that you are there at all.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"White rhinos share communal dung heaps called middens, and every visitor reads them. The dung says who has been by, how old, male or female, and who owns this ground.","more":"Researchers found there is even a seating plan. The territory-owning bull dungs in the middle of the midden; females and younger males use the edges. He also times his own deposit for when the midden is busiest, so the message gets read while it is fresh.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"speed","text":"Two tonnes of animal, and it can still hit 31 mph (50 km/h). That is faster than the fastest human being who has ever run.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A white rhino calf runs in front of its mother, not behind her. Black rhino calves do the exact opposite and follow.","more":"The mother steers from behind with her horn and her bulk, keeping the calf out in open view ahead of her where she can see it. A calf stays with her for two or three years, until the next one arrives.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"Oxpeckers ride on rhinos, picking ticks off the hide. They also hiss an alarm when a person approaches — and rhinos carrying birds notice people far more reliably.","more":"In one study, every rhino with oxpeckers aboard spotted an approaching human. Rhinos without birds often walked on unaware. The Swahili name for the oxpecker means, roughly, the rhino's guard.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Nobody actually knows why it is called the white rhino. It is gray — precisely the same gray as the black rhino.","more":"The popular story is that English speakers misheard a Dutch word meaning wide, describing that square lip. Researchers went hunting for that word in old Dutch and Afrikaans writing about rhinos and could not find it used that way at all. The name is still a mystery.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"home","text":"Southern white rhinos were once down to fewer than a hundred animals. Guarded and moved to safe reserves, they came back — there are now more of them than all other rhinos put together.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"build","text":"Look at the tracks: three broad toes on every foot, printing like a fat clover leaf. All that weight rides on a springy pad tucked in behind the toes.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"A horn that wears down against the world and simply keeps growing back — an edge that never runs out."},{"id":"camel","name":"Dromedary camel","group":"mammals","blurb":"The one-humped camel of hot deserts. It is built to carry a load across country with no water in sight.","size":"About as tall at the hump as a grown-up with one arm raised","homes":["desert","grassland"],"stats":{"speed":25,"weight":1213,"length":9.8,"life":40},"facts":[{"cat":"weird","text":"The hump is not a water tank. It is fat — up to about 80 lb (36 kg) of it — food the camel carries around on its own back.","more":"When a camel goes hungry, the hump shrinks and flops over sideways like a half-empty bag. Feed it up and the hump firms and stands tall again. You can read a camel's recent luck straight off the shape of its back.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"A big male stands around 6 ft 6 in (2 m) at the shoulder before you even count the hump, and weighs around 1,200 lb (550 kg).","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"A camel will chew a thorn bush that would shred your hands. The inside of its mouth is lined with tough cone-shaped spikes, called papillae, that grip the thorns and steer them down the throat.","more":"The papillae all point backwards, so a mouthful can only travel one way. A camel can eat the dry, spiny, salty plants nothing else in the desert will touch. That is exactly the food still standing when everything else is gone.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"A thirsty camel can drink more than 26 gallons (100 liters) in about ten minutes. That is fifty big water bottles, straight down, without stopping.","more":"Taking on water that fast would burst the red blood cells of most mammals. A camel's are oval instead of round, and stretchy enough to swell to more than twice their size without splitting. It refills at a rate that would kill a horse, then walks away as if nothing happened.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Three eyelids. Two ordinary ones, plus a thin third that sweeps sideways like a windscreen wiper — and it is see-through, so the camel can keep walking with it shut.","more":"Add two rows of long lashes and nostrils that squeeze down to slits, and a camel can keep going through a sandstorm that would blind you in seconds. Even the ears are lined with hair to keep the grit out.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"A camel lets its own body temperature drift across the day, from about 34°C to 41°C (93°F to 106°F). It does not have to start sweating until things are genuinely hot.","more":"Cool night, cool camel; by afternoon it has warmed through like a stone in the sun. Every degree it soaks up instead of sweating away is water it gets to keep. Your body would never permit a swing like that.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"A camel takes water back out of its own breath. The inside of the nose cools the outgoing air and pulls the moisture from it before it can leave.","more":"Overnight the nasal lining dries out and turns hygroscopic. That means it grabs moisture out of passing air. The camel breathes out air that is not even fully damp. Over a day that adds up to a serious share of the water it would otherwise lose.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"A camel can lose more than a quarter of its body weight in water and carry on walking. In most mammals, half that loss would be deadly.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Look at the feet. Two broad toes on a wide leathery pad that spreads under the weight like a snowshoe, so the camel presses onto the sand instead of down into it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Camels do spit, and it is not spit. It is stomach contents, brought up on purpose and thrown at whatever is annoying them. It smells exactly as bad as that sounds.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"In the mating season a male blows a pink sac out of the side of his mouth. It is the length of your hand and wobbles like a balloon. He gurgles at his rivals through it. The sac is called a dulla.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"speed","text":"A racing camel holds about 25 mph (40 km/h). The real trick is endurance. A working camel can cover 50 miles (80 km) in a day, then do it again the next day for a week.","more":"That is why camels crossed deserts no horse could cross. Not because they were fast, but because they simply did not have to stop.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"God gave the camel three eyelids, two rows of lashes and nostrils that shut — an entire face made ready for the sandstorm before it came."},{"id":"reindeer","name":"Reindeer","group":"mammals","blurb":"The deer of the frozen north — the only one whose females grow antlers, and the only one people have ever herded.","size":"About 5.9 ft (1.8 m) long and roughly 290 lb (130 kg) — waist-high to a grown-up","homes":["polar","mountain","forest"],"stats":{"speed":37,"weight":287,"length":5.9,"life":13},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"Reindeer are not big deer — a bull stands about waist-high to a grown-up. But he is dense with it, up to 330 lb (150 kg), the weight of two adults.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"A reindeer's eyes change color with the seasons. Gold in summer. Deep blue through the long winter dark.","more":"Behind the retina sits a mirror called the tapetum lucidum. In winter, pressure inside the eyeball squeezes its fibers closer together and the mirror turns blue. Less light bounces straight back out, so the eye has far more chance to catch what little there is. The trade is sharpness. The winter eye sees more, but blurrier.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Reindeer see ultraviolet light, which you cannot. Snow throws UV back; lichen, fur and urine soak it up. Nobody can borrow those eyes, but the reading is that the blank white world is covered in dark marks.","more":"Wolf fur shows as a dark shape against snow that looks empty to us. So does a patch of lichen worth digging for. Human eyes screen UV out to protect the retina; reindeer eyes let it straight through.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"build","text":"Reindeer are the only deer whose females grow antlers. The females hang onto theirs all winter, long after the males have dropped theirs.","more":"A pregnant female uses her antlers as a snow shovel and as a door. She can hold a feeding crater against bigger males who no longer have any headgear to argue with. She sheds hers in spring, around the time her calf is born.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"Some reindeer cover close to 3,000 miles (4,800 km) in a single year. That is the trip between winter range and calving ground. It is one of the longest journeys any animal makes on land.","more":"Satellite collars showed that the distance does not come from one straight march. It is a whole year of movement: north in spring, south in fall, and endless smaller shuffles in between, chasing food and running from insects.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Every step a reindeer takes clicks. A tendon slips over a small bone in the foot, and the sound carries. Nobody has proved what it is for. In fog or a blizzard, though, a herd can hear exactly where it is.","more":"The click cannot be switched off; it happens whether the animal wants it or not. Stand in a reindeer barn with your eyes closed and the whole room sounds faintly like popping corn.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"food","text":"Winter food is lichen — a crust on rock and ground that almost no other animal can digest. Reindeer carry gut microbes that break it down for them.","more":"They smell it through deep snow, then dig a crater with their hooves to reach it. Herders call it reindeer moss, though it is nothing of the kind: a lichen is a fungus and an alga living together as one thing.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The hooves change with the season. Soft and spongy in summer for wet tundra, they shrink back in winter to leave a hard sharp rim that bites into ice.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Reindeer hair is hollow. Every strand traps air, which holds the heat in and makes the animal float — so herds swim wide rivers and sea channels without much trouble.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"speed","text":"A reindeer calf can outrun you the day after it is born. Within a week it can keep pace with the whole running herd.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"The nose does the heating. Air arrives freezing and reaches the lungs at nearly body temperature, warmed on the way by scrolls of paper-thin bone inside the muzzle.","more":"Those same scrolls, called turbinates, strip the heat and moisture back out of the breath going the other way. So the reindeer is not throwing warmth and water into the Arctic every time it breathes out.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"Above the Arctic Circle the sun never sets in summer and never rises in midwinter. Reindeer drop their daily rhythm altogether. They feed and rest right around the clock.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Eyes that retune themselves for the polar night — the darkness was accounted for before the reindeer ever walked into it."},{"id":"moose","name":"Moose","group":"mammals","blurb":"The biggest deer on Earth. It is a lanky, hump-shouldered giant that wades through northern ponds pulling up water plants.","size":"A big bull stands about 6 feet (1.8 m) at the shoulder — eye to eye with a tall man, before you count the head and antlers","homes":["forest","freshwater"],"stats":{"speed":35,"weight":992,"length":8.9,"life":15},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"A big Alaskan bull can pass 1,500 pounds (680 kg) and stand six feet (1.8 m) at the shoulder. His back is higher than the roof of a car.","more":"Moose are the largest deer on Earth. Those long legs are working legs — for wading through chest-deep snow in winter and pond mud in summer. A moose can step over a fallen log that a horse would have to walk around.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Every summer a bull grows a whole set of antlers, then drops them in winter. At peak growth they can add close to an inch (2.5 cm) of bone a day.","more":"That makes antler one of the fastest-growing tissues in the animal world — a full rack in three to five months. While it grows it is wrapped in furry skin called velvet, packed with blood vessels feeding the bone underneath. Once the antler hardens, the velvet dries and the bull rubs it off against trees.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"The widest moose antlers ever recorded spanned close to 7 feet (2.1 m) tip to tip. That is wider than a standard doorway is tall.","more":"Big antlers are partly a signal. A cow can read a bull's condition off his headgear at a glance, and two rival bulls often size each other up and walk away without ever fighting.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"A moose eats 40 to 60 pounds (18-27 kg) of leaves, twigs and pond weed a day — a wheelbarrow of salad, gathered one mouthful at a time.","more":"Moose are browsers, not grazers: they strip leaves and twig-tips from shrubs rather than crop grass. In winter they live largely on frozen willow and birch twigs, and can spend eight hours of the day feeding.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"That vast nose is a valve. A moose can seal its nostrils shut, put its whole head under, and feed off the pond floor for half a minute at a time.","more":"Fatty pads and muscle inside the muzzle pinch the nostrils closed, so a feeding moose does not inhale pond water. Moose have been recorded diving more than 18 feet (5.5 m). That is deeper than the deep end of a swimming pool. No other deer regularly feeds completely underwater.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Moose hair is hollow. Every strand is a tiny air-filled tube, which traps warmth in brutal cold and doubles as a life jacket when the moose swims.","more":"Under the long guard hairs lies a dense wooly layer. Together they keep a moose comfortable in weather that would kill most animals — and buoyant enough to float while it feeds out in a lake.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"speed","text":"A moose runs 35 mph (56 km/h) through timber so thick you could not walk it. It also swims steadily for hours without ever touching the bottom.","more":"Moose have been recorded crossing lakes ten miles (16 km) wide, moving at around 6 mph (10 km/h). Water is not an obstacle to a moose — it is a highway, an escape route from wolves, and a salad bar all at once.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A newborn calf weighs under 30 pounds (13 kg) — less than a big dog. By its first winter that same calf can top 300 pounds (140 kg).","more":"Moose milk is far richer than cow's milk, and a calf puts on weight faster than almost any land mammal its size. It stays glued to its mother for a year — then she drives it off, just before the next calf arrives.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Forget the antlers. A moose's real weapon is its front feet. It rears up and strikes downward, and it can kick in any direction, including sideways.","more":"Wildlife officers in Alaska report that moose injure more people there each year than bears do. Almost always, somebody crowded a cow with a calf. Flattened ears and raised neck hair mean back away now.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"A moose's eyesight is poor — a person standing still can look like a stump. But each ear swivels on its own, and its nose is superb. It smells you first.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"The flap of skin swinging under a moose's throat is called the bell. Its shape changes as a bull ages, and the best read is that in fall it carries his scent through the forest.","more":"Nobody has fully settled what the bell is for. During the fall rut a bull digs a shallow wallow, soaks it in his own scent, and the dangling bell spreads that smell everywhere he walks.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"home","text":"Moose are built for cold and suffer in heat. On a warm day one will stand chest-deep in a pond for hours — which is exactly where photographers find them.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"God gave the moose a nose that seals like a valve, so a land animal could graze the bottom of a lake."},{"id":"pronghorn","name":"Pronghorn","group":"mammals","blurb":"A tan-and-white speedster of the North American plains. It looks like an antelope, isn't one, and is the finest long-distance runner on land.","size":"Shoulder about waist-high on an adult — roughly 3 feet (90 cm) — and about 110 lb (50 kg).","homes":["grassland","desert"],"stats":{"speed":55,"weight":110,"length":4.6,"life":10},"facts":[{"cat":"speed","text":"Nothing in North America is faster. A pronghorn hits about 55 mph (88 km/h) — and unlike a cheetah, it does not then have to stop and pant.","more":"A cheetah's sprint is over in well under a minute. The pronghorn's run has no stop button — it holds hard speed mile after mile, which is a completely different kind of fast.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"The endurance is the astonishing part. A pronghorn can hold 35 mph (56 km/h) for four miles (6.4 km) — a racehorse would be finished long before.","more":"The engine explains it. A pronghorn's heart, windpipe and lungs are all outsized for its body. It takes in oxygen several times faster than an animal that size normally could.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Pronghorn horns do something no other animal's do: the outer sheath slides off every year and grows back. And they are pronged — a forward hook plus the main spike.","more":"Underneath sits a permanent bony core. The sheath is keratin, the same material as your fingernails, formed from fused hair. Males drop theirs in late fall and have a complete new set by the end of the following summer.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"It is not an antelope. True antelope live in Africa and Asia. The pronghorn sits in a family entirely its own — there is nothing else quite like it anywhere on Earth.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Its eyes are huge for its skull, set high and wide on the head. A pronghorn watches roughly 320 degrees around itself — nearly a full circle — without turning its head.","more":"Out on open prairie there is nothing to hide behind, so spotting a problem two ridges away is the entire survival plan. A pronghorn can pick out movement a very long way off and simply leave early.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"One Wyoming herd walks over 150 miles (240 km) each way, twice a year, squeezing through a high mountain pass to reach its winter range.","more":"It is called the Path of the Pronghorn. At one point the route narrows to a tight bottleneck. Wyoming built wildlife overpasses across a busy highway there. The herd can keep using the road it has always used.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"A pronghorn can outrun almost anything, but a wire fence stops it cold. It does not jump — it drops flat and scrapes underneath, so a low bottom wire is a wall.","more":"Ranchers and biologists now build wildlife-friendly fence with the bottom wire raised and smooth, about 18 inches (45 cm) off the ground. Pronghorn slide straight under it; cattle still stay where they are put.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"When a pronghorn spots danger it flares the white hairs on its rump into a bright disc. Other pronghorn far across the prairie see the flash — and a scent goes with it.","more":"Muscles under the skin lift the long white hairs so the patch seems to double in size. A gland in that same patch releases a strong smell at the same moment, so one alarm travels by eye and by nose together.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A fawn is standing within half an hour and can outrun a human within its first week. Twins are normal, and the mother hides them far apart from each other.","more":"Two fawns stashed a hundred meters apart means one coyote finding one does not cost her both. She returns only to nurse, then leaves again — a still, nearly scentless fawn is safer than a guarded one.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Pronghorn eat sagebrush, a bitter, oily plant most grazers refuse. Through the dry months they pull much of the water they need straight out of what they are chewing.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"No burrow, no cliff, no cover. A pronghorn's whole defense is noticing trouble early and leaving before it arrives.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Eyes, lungs, heart and legs all sized to one purpose — every part of a pronghorn agrees with every other part."},{"id":"mountain-goat","name":"Mountain goat","group":"mammals","blurb":"A shaggy white climber of North America's highest cliffs. It is at home on ledges barely wider than a book.","size":"A big billy weighs up to 300 pounds (140 kg) — about as much as two grown men — with a shoulder hump of solid muscle","homes":["mountain"],"stats":{"weight":198,"length":4.9,"life":13},"facts":[{"cat":"build","text":"Look at those feet. Each hoof splits into two toes that spread wide for balance, with a rubbery pad in the middle that grips bare rock like a climbing shoe.","more":"The outer rim is hard, for biting onto tiny edges; the inner pad is soft and tacky, for friction on smooth stone. Behind each hoof, sharp dewclaws dig in as brakes on the way back down.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Mountain goats work slopes steeper than 60 degrees, far steeper than any staircase you have climbed. They can leap nearly 12 feet (3.6 m) from one ledge to the next.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"It isn't actually a goat. Zoologists file it with the chamois and the serows, in a group called goat-antelopes. Your farmyard goat is a different animal entirely.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"build","text":"The coat is really two coats: dense wool underneath for warmth, long hollow guard hairs on top to shed wind and snow. It can sit out a blizzard on an open ridge.","more":"In spring the whole thing comes off in ragged sheets and the goat looks like it lost a fight with a duvet. Tufts of shed wool end up caught on rocks and shrubs all over the summer range.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"In winter almost nothing grows up there. Goats paw snow off ledges for frozen grass, then eat lichen, moss and woody twigs that most animals would walk straight past.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Mountain goats crave salt. Their plant diet gives them almost no sodium, so they will travel miles and hang off a near-vertical mineral lick to lick the rock itself.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"In some national parks goats shadow hikers — not for sandwiches, but for the salt in human sweat and urine. Rangers ask visitors to pee well off the trail.","more":"A goat that learns people mean salt stops being wary of people. A wild animal with dagger horns standing next to a hiker is a problem for both of them. Some parks have moved habituated goats elsewhere.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Mountain goats do not head-butt like rams. Those black horns are barely a hand long but needle-sharp, and a fight means stabbing sideways at the other goat's flank.","more":"They carry armor for exactly that. The skin over a mountain goat's rump is unusually thick and tough. It protects the part most likely to get jabbed. Serious fights are rare, and threat displays usually settle the argument.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A kid is born on a ledge, stands within minutes, and is following its mother along cliff paths within days. There is no flat ground up there to practice on.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"The bosses are the females. Nannies outrank the much larger billies, push them off the best feeding ledges, and a big billy will back down rather than argue.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Mountain goats live above the treeline as high as 13,000 feet (4,000 m), in wind that can hit 99 mph (160 km/h) and cold near minus 50°F (minus 46°C).","more":"That is hurricane wind and freezer cold, on bare rock, all winter, with no shelter to speak of. A goat simply turns its back to the weather, tucks in behind a boulder if there is one, and waits it out.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The power is all up front. Worked out from the build: a humped shoulder and a heavy neck haul the goat upward. It is the way you would pull yourself over the top of a high wall.","kind":"worked"}],"wonder":"Feet, coat, shoulders and nerve, all fitted to one address — the last few hundred feet of the mountain, where nothing else can follow."},{"id":"chimpanzee","name":"Chimpanzee","group":"mammals","blurb":"A powerful, noisy, tool-making ape of African forests. It lives in big groups where everybody knows everybody.","size":"Standing, roughly 4 to 5 feet (1.2-1.5 m) — shorter than most adults — but built far heavier through the chest, shoulders and arms","homes":["rainforest","forest","grassland"],"stats":{"weight":88,"life":30},"facts":[{"cat":"record","text":"Pound for pound, chimpanzee muscle pulls about a third harder than yours. The difference isn't magic — it comes down to fiber type.","more":"Chimp muscle is roughly two-thirds fast-twitch fiber, the explosive kind that pulls hard and briefly. Human muscle leans toward slow-twitch fiber, the kind that keeps going for hours. Individual fibers are much alike; it is the mix that differs.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Chimps carry tool kits. In the Congo a chimp punches a hole into a termite nest with a stout stick. Then it fishes the termites out with a thinner stem, its tip chewed into a brush.","more":"The frayed brush tip catches far more termites than a plain stick does. Different communities make different designs and pass them to their young. That is why researchers talk about chimpanzee cultures: local ways of doing things.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"In West Africa chimps crack rock-hard nuts with a stone hammer on a stone anvil. It takes a young chimp years of clumsy practice before it actually works.","more":"Mothers leave nuts and stones set up correctly for their children. Sometimes a mother cracks one slowly right in front of her child. The youngster copies, misses, copies again. Meanwhile it eats the kernels its mother hands over.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"At Fongoli in Senegal, chimps snap off a branch, strip it, chew the end to a point, and jab it into hollow trunks to hunt sleeping bushbabies. Mostly females and youngsters do it.","more":"It is the first record of a non-human primate making a weapon to hunt with. It does not often succeed. In the first study, roughly one jab in twenty-two produced a catch. The tool is deliberately shaped every single time.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"A chimp drums on the flat buttress roots of a big tree. The boom travels over a kilometer (0.6 miles) through forest, and each drummer has his own rhythm.","more":"Researchers in Uganda's Budongo Forest learned to name the drummer from sound alone. One male hammered out fast, evenly spaced beats. Another used a stuttering pattern. Chimps drum most while traveling, as a way of saying where they are.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"A sick chimp will peel a shoot of a bitter plant it normally refuses and chew the pith. Within about a day, the parasite eggs in its droppings drop off sharply.","more":"The plant is Vernonia, and it is genuinely foul — healthy chimps will not touch it. It carries compounds that suppress a gut worm which can kill a chimp. One study found around 88% fewer worm eggs a day after a chimp chewed the pith.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"Chimps hunt monkeys as a team. Watch where each one goes. One drives the prey along a branch. Others move ahead to cut off the escape routes. They are splitting the job up.","more":"Afterwards the meat is shared, but not equally. Who gets a piece depends on who helped in the hunt, who is high-ranking, and who is a friend. Begging with an outstretched open hand often works.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A chimp baby rides its mother for years — belly-first at the start, then piggyback — and still nurses well past its third birthday. Some stay near her into their teens.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"Every evening, in about five minutes, a chimp bends branches into a springy bowl high in a tree and sleeps in it. The next night, it usually builds a fresh one.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"A chimp community can hold 15 to 150 members, but they are almost never all in one place. They split into small parties and rejoin — scientists call this fission-fusion.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Parties of males patrol the edge of their territory in single file. They go silent, hair standing on end, stopping to listen. Chimpanzee borders are taken extremely seriously.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"In a Japanese lab, young chimps beat adult humans at a memory test. Numbers flash on a screen for a fraction of a second, then vanish. The chimp taps them back in order.","more":"The numbers flashed up for less than a second. Then blank white squares hid them. The young chimps were faster than the university students tested against them, and more accurate too.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Hands made for gripping branches turn out to fish, hammer, drum and heal — one design doing a dozen different jobs."},{"id":"orangutan","name":"Orangutan","group":"mammals","blurb":"A huge, ginger, unhurried ape that spends almost its entire life up in the trees of Borneo and Sumatra.","size":"A big male weighs up to 200 pounds (90 kg), about as much as a grown man, with an arm span near 6 feet 7 inches (2 m).","homes":["rainforest"],"stats":{"weight":132,"life":35},"facts":[{"cat":"build","text":"Stretch your arms out wide. Now imagine them longer than you are tall. A male's arm span reaches about 6 feet 7 inches (2 m) while he stands only around 4.5 feet (1.4 m).","more":"The shoulder joint swings almost anywhere. The fingers hook like grappling irons. The feet grip as well as the hands do. An orangutan effectively has four hands, with hips loose enough to point its legs in any direction.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"Orangutans rarely jump. To cross a gap they climb a slender tree and rock it back and forth until it leans far enough to hand them across to the next one.","more":"Swaying costs far less energy than leaping, and it is far safer for an animal this heavy. A fall from the canopy would be serious, so an orangutan keeps at least two limbs holding on almost all of the time.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"An orangutan arrives at the right tree just as it fruits, over and over. The best read is that it knows hundreds of tree species and roughly when each one will fruit. It has to — this forest fruits unpredictably, and a wrong guess means a wasted climb.","more":"Favourites include figs and durian, a spiky fruit that smells like old socks and tastes like custard. In lean months orangutans fall back on bark, leaves and termites, and can lose noticeable weight.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"record","text":"No great ape waits longer between babies. A wild orangutan mother gives birth once every six to nine years, and the same infant is still with her for most of that time.","more":"The pay-off is that almost every infant survives. She is teaching one apprentice at a time. The lessons genuinely take that long: which tree is fruiting, how to open it, how to build a bed that holds.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"Every evening an orangutan builds a bed. A frame of thick bent branches, then a mattress of leafy twigs woven on top. Sometimes a pillow, and a roof against the rain.","more":"Researchers who tested the nests found a pattern. Orangutans pick stiffer branches for the load-bearing frame and thinner, springier ones for the mattress. It is the same idea as a bed base and a duvet. Then they build a brand new one the next night.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"A male called Rakus, cut in a fight, chewed the leaves of a vine and pressed the pulp onto the wound on his face, over and over. Within five days the wound had closed.","more":"The vine is called Fibraurea tinctoria, and people living nearby use it as medicine. Lab tests find it both antibacterial and pain-relieving. This was the first careful record of a wild animal treating a wound with a plant that actually works.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"When an orangutan is uneasy it makes a sharp kiss-squeak. Hold a handful of leaves in front of the mouth and the same squeak drops in pitch — making the caller sound far bigger than it is.","more":"Pitch normally gives away body size: the bigger the animal, the deeper the call. Leaves held to the lips drag the squeak lower, so a smallish orangutan can sound like a heavyweight. Only some populations have the trick.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"A big male throws his head back and roars for minutes at a stretch. The long call carries more than half a mile (about 1 km) through dense forest.","more":"The throat sac under his chin works as a resonator, the way the hollow body of a guitar amplifies a string. Females walk toward the call; rival males quietly walk the other way.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Only some adult males grow the huge cheek flanges. Others keep a slim face for years — and can start growing flanges later if the dominant male in the area disappears.","more":"The flanges are pads of fibrous and fatty tissue. They develop under the push of testosterone. Two adult males of exactly the same age can look like two entirely different animals.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"copied","text":"A Neesia fruit is stuffed with stinging hairs like fiberglass. In one Sumatran swamp, orangutans strip a twig and poke it into the fruit to flick the seeds out.","more":"Orangutans on the other side of the river have the same fruit and the same twigs, and do not do this. The trick is a local invention, passed along by watching somebody else. It is not something every orangutan is born knowing.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Orangutans are the only great apes in Asia, and they live wild in exactly two places: the islands of Borneo and Sumatra. The name means 'person of the forest' in Malay.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Most big apes live in groups. An adult orangutan usually travels alone. The food explains it. One fruiting tree rarely feeds a crowd, so the forest keeps them spread thin across the canopy.","more":"Mothers with young are the exception. Orangutans will also feed peacefully together in a heavily loaded tree. Daughters often settle near their mothers, so the ape in the next valley is quite often a relative.","kind":"worked"}],"wonder":"Four gripping hands, a bed built fresh each night, and a mother who teaches one child at a time — a life fitted exactly to the canopy."},{"id":"mandrill","name":"Mandrill","group":"mammals","blurb":"The biggest monkey in the world. It is a heavy, ground-walking forest primate with a scarlet-and-blue face you could pick out of a crowd from fifty meters.","size":"A big male weighs around 55 lb (25 kg) — about as heavy as a nine-year-old — walking on four legs","homes":["rainforest","forest"],"stats":{"weight":55,"length":2.6,"life":13},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"The mandrill is the largest monkey on Earth. A big male reaches 66 lb (30 kg) — about what a nine-year-old weighs, carried around on four legs.","more":"Males run 42 to 66 lb (19 to 30 kg); females only 22 to 33 lb (10 to 15 kg). Few primates differ that much between the sexes. A male can be more than twice the weight of the female sitting beside him, with long canine teeth she does not have.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"In Gabon's Lope forest, researchers counted a single mandrill horde of 845 animals. Apart from people, that is the largest group of wild primates ever recorded.","more":"They counted from film, frame by frame, because nobody can count that many moving animals by eye. Even an ordinary horde runs into the hundreds. When one crosses the forest floor, the ground itself seems to be moving.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"A male's scarlet nose stripe and blue cheek ridges brighten as he climbs in rank, and fade when he loses the top spot. Rivals read his color like a scoreboard.","more":"Researchers followed the same males for years. The brightest male is nearly always the alpha. Males compare color before anything else happens. That settles a great many arguments without a single bite being thrown.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"When a male opens his mouth wide at a rival, that is not a yawn. The best read is a display: he is showing canine teeth up to 1.8 in (4.5 cm) long — about the length of your thumb.","more":"Those canines are a weapon and an advertisement at once. Usually the advertisement is enough. A wide-mouthed stare across a clearing ends the disagreement. Every mandrill watching can see exactly what is on offer.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"weird","text":"A mandrill pulling its lips back to bare every tooth is not angry. Watch who gets that enormous grin and when, and the message comes out friendly — roughly: no trouble from me.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"build","text":"It packs fruit into stretchy pouches inside its cheeks until its head looks lopsided. Then it carries the load somewhere quieter. It pushes the food back into its mouth with a hand.","more":"Cheek pouches are standard equipment for the monkeys of Africa and Asia. They let a mandrill grab far more than it can chew while a fruiting tree is crowded and contested, then walk off and eat the lot in peace.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"A mandrill has a scent gland in the middle of its chest, and presses it against branches as it travels. The best read is that it is leaving a smell-message on the trail behind it.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"food","text":"Mandrills eat mostly fruit and seeds, plus insects turned up by rolling logs over. A big male will also chase down and eat a small animal when the chance appears.","more":"They spend the day working the forest floor: flipping leaf litter, cracking hard seeds, stripping fruit trees. Because they swallow seeds whole and then walk for kilometers, they end up planting a good deal of the forest they live in.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"A horde walks kilometers in a day, following the fruit as different trees ripen. Mandrills spend far more time on the ground than most monkeys ever do.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A newborn mandrill has a black coat and a bare pink face. It spends its first weeks slung underneath its mother, gripping her belly fur while she walks.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Mandrills live in the rainforests of west-central Africa and nowhere else in the wild. That means southern Cameroon, Gabon, Equatorial Guinea and parts of Congo.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"A whole language painted onto one face, and every mandrill in the forest can read it."},{"id":"ring-tailed-lemur","name":"Ring-tailed lemur","group":"mammals","blurb":"A cat-sized primate from the dry south of Madagascar. It is known everywhere by the long striped tail it carries straight up like a flag.","size":"About 1.4 ft (43 cm) long and around 5 lb (2.3 kg), with a tail longer than the rest of it","homes":["forest","mountain"],"stats":{"weight":5,"length":1.4,"life":16},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"Body about 16 in (40 cm) long; tail about 24 in (60 cm). The tail is longer than the lemur it belongs to — and it cannot grip a branch at all.","more":"Monkeys in South America hang by their tails. No lemur can. This tail is a balance pole for running along branches, and a banner held straight up so a troop can keep track of each other in long grass.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Count the rings. A ring-tailed lemur's tail carries around thirteen black bands and a dozen or so white ones, and the very tip always finishes black.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Males settle arguments by smell. A male drags his tail across scent glands on his wrists and shoulders, arcs it over his head and wafts it at his rival. It is called a stink fight.","more":"A stink fight can go on for an hour of standing and wafting, and the loser walks away without a scratch on him. The wrist glands produce a complicated mixture of chemicals, and the whole troop can smell how it went.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"A male also gouges saplings with a horny spur on his wrist, cutting into the bark before he scent-marks it. The best read is that the cut lets the smell soak in and last.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"talk","text":"The troop keeps different alarm calls for different dangers. One call means a hawk overhead, and everybody drops down out of the open. Another means a fossa on the ground, and everybody goes up.","more":"The fossa is Madagascar's largest predator. It is a long, muscular, cat-like hunter that climbs as well as any lemur. Getting the call right matters enormously. Go up a tree for a hawk and you are exposed. Go down for a fossa and you are lunch.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"The boss of a ring-tailed lemur troop is always a female. She eats first, she decides where the group goes, and fully grown males step out of her way.","more":"In most primate groups the biggest male is in charge. Here a female half a male's size can take food straight out of his hands and he lets it go. Males often live at the edge of the troop and move between troops.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"On a cold morning the whole troop sits upright with legs apart. Pale bellies turn to the sun and arms rest on knees. They warm up before the day's traveling begins.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"The kily, or tamarind tree, supplies a huge share of everything they eat. Its fruit, its leaves and its flowers all go down. It grows in the dry forests where they live.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"Of all the lemurs, this one spends the most time on the ground. It walks on all fours between one patch of trees and the next, striped tail hoisted.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A newborn rides slung under its mother's belly. After a few weeks it graduates to riding on her back. It sits up like a jockey while she leaps.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Wild ring-tailed lemurs live in one place on Earth. That is the dry southern end of Madagascar, from spiny scrub and gallery forest up into cold mountain country.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"A tail that grips nothing at all, yet balances, signals and settles fights — not one part of this animal is spare."},{"id":"tarsier","name":"Tarsier","group":"mammals","blurb":"A tiny night-hunting primate from the islands of Southeast Asia. Its eyes are so large they cannot move in their sockets. Its legs are built like springs.","size":"Small enough to sit in your cupped hands, with a tail twice as long as its body","homes":["rainforest","forest"],"stats":{"weight":0,"length":0.4},"facts":[{"cat":"senses","text":"Each eyeball is about 0.63 in (16 mm) across — as big as the tarsier's own brain, sometimes bigger. No mammal has larger eyes for its body size.","more":"Scaled up to your size, you would be walking around with eyes as big as grapefruit. Those eyes gather so much light that a tarsier can pick a moth off a leaf on a night with no moon at all.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"The eyes are locked in their sockets — a tarsier cannot roll them. So it turns its head instead, about 180 degrees each way, enough to look back over its own spine.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Tarsiers are the only primates that eat no plants whatsoever. No fruit, no leaves, no seeds — only animals they catch: insects, spiders, lizards, small birds.","more":"Chimpanzees eat meat and so do baboons, but both eat plenty of fruit as well. Every mouthful a tarsier takes was alive a moment before. It snatches insects out of the air in mid-leap and pins them with both hands.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"It can cross 10 ft (3 m) of open air between two trunks. That is more than twenty times its own body length. It lands feet-first and grips bark instantly.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The name comes from its ankles. Two bones in each ankle, the tarsals, are stretched extra long, which turns a hand-sized animal into a spring-loaded launcher.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"talk","text":"A tarsier can scream at a pitch you will never hear. Researchers recorded calls with a dominant frequency near 70 kHz — more than three times higher than human ears can catch.","more":"The same team tested tarsier hearing. Sensitivity reached about 91 kHz, among the highest measured in any land mammal. To a person standing under the tree, the animal just opens its mouth in complete silence.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Its ears crumple and unfurl constantly, each one moving separately from the other. They track a single insect through the dark by sound alone.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A newborn tarsier arrives finished. Its eyes are already open and its fur already grown. It can climb within a day of being born.","more":"She sometimes parks the baby on a branch while she hunts, and carries it in her mouth the way a cat carries a kitten. Within a few weeks it is catching its own insects.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"By day it clings upright to a thin vertical stem, deep inside a tangle of vegetation. It sleeps there. It returns to the same few stems night after night.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The underside of its tail is ridged and callused. The tarsier props on it like a third leg while clinging upright to a trunk.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Tarsiers do badly in captivity. A stressed one beats its head against the cage, and its skull is thin enough that this kills it. The honest answer to 'can I keep one' is no.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Wild tarsiers live only on certain islands of Southeast Asia. Different islands have different kinds.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Eyes too big to move, set in a head that turns almost the whole way round — one design answering another."},{"id":"vampire-bat","name":"Vampire bat","group":"mammals","blurb":"A small bat of Mexico, Central and South America. It lives on one food and one food only: the blood of sleeping animals.","size":"About 3.6 in (9 cm) long — you could hold one in one hand","homes":["cave","rainforest","forest"],"stats":{"weight":0,"length":0.3,"life":9},"facts":[{"cat":"food","text":"Three kinds of bat in the world drink blood, and this is the one that goes for mammals — cattle, horses, pigs, tapirs. It eats nothing else. Ever.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Pits around its nose read heat. Held close to a sleeping animal, they show it exactly where a blood vessel runs nearest the skin — and that is where it bites.","more":"The nerve endings inside those pits are tuned to warmth rather than touch, so the bat effectively sees a heat map of the animal in front of it. Some snakes have a similar sense. Almost no other mammal does.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"It does not suck. Razor-edged front teeth open a shallow cut, and the blood runs up grooves along the sides of its tongue — lapped, the way a cat drinks milk.","more":"The cut is so shallow and the teeth so sharp that a sleeping cow usually never wakes. A meal takes about twenty minutes of quiet lapping, with the bat crouched on the ground or clinging to a leg.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Its saliva carries an anticoagulant, a chemical that stops blood clotting. The tiny wound keeps flowing the entire time it feeds.","more":"One of those proteins was named draculin, which is exactly the joke you think it is. Without it, a cut that small would seal itself within a minute or two and the bat would go to bed hungry.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"copied","text":"Doctors took an interest in that saliva. A clot-dissolving drug called desmoteplase is built from a vampire bat protein. It has been tested on stroke patients, whose brains are damaged by clots.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"gross","text":"A 1.4 oz (40 g) bat can drink over 0.7 oz (20 g) of blood — half its own weight. Blood is mostly water, so it starts urinating within two minutes of starting, shedding water as it drinks.","more":"Otherwise it would be far too heavy to take off. Afterwards its kidneys switch to the opposite job, producing thick, concentrated urine so the bat keeps every drop of water it can from the meal.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"speed","text":"Most bats are helpless on the ground. This one runs, in a bounding gait where the wings do the pushing instead of the legs.","more":"It matters because this bat feeds on the ground, creeping up on a sleeping animal's foot. Being able to bound sideways and spring into the air the instant a hoof moves is worth a great deal.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"A vampire bat that fails to feed for about two nights running will die. So a well-fed bat brings up part of its own meal and gives it, mouth to mouth, to a hungry roost-mate.","more":"Researchers have followed named individuals for years. Bats tend to share with the same partners over and over, and one that has donated is more likely to be fed when its own night goes badly. They keep track of each other.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A pup drinks milk for months, then is fed blood mouth-to-mouth long before it hunts for itself. Some are still not fully weaned at nine months old.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Every bat's contact call is slightly different from every other. Roost-mates learn to tell one another apart by voice, in a dark cave full of chatter.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"It roosts in caves, hollow trees and old buildings, in groups of dozens to hundreds. They hang in the dark and groom one another for hours at a time.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Nose, tongue, teeth and kidneys — every one of them fitted for a diet of nothing but blood."},{"id":"flying-fox","name":"Flying fox","group":"mammals","blurb":"The largest bats on Earth. They are fruit-eating, dog-faced bats that hang by the thousand in noisy treetop camps. They never use sonar at all.","size":"Wings that open wider than you are tall — about 5 ft (1.5 m) in the biggest kinds","homes":["rainforest","forest"],"stats":{"speed":13,"weight":2,"length":1.0,"life":15},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"The biggest flying foxes stretch about 5 ft (1.5 m) from wingtip to wingtip. That is wider than you are tall. The body in the middle weighs around a kilogram (2 lb).","more":"Almost all that width is skin. A bat's wing is its hand. The four long fingers running through the membrane are the same four fingers you have. They are drawn out thin and joined by living skin with blood moving through it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Flying foxes do not echolocate. No clicks, no sonar. They find their food with big forward-facing eyes and an excellent nose, the way you would find a bakery.","more":"Bats that hunt insects need sonar because a moth is small, fast and moving. A fruiting tree stays where it is and smells ripe from a long way off, so eyes and a nose do the job. 'Blind as a bat' was always nonsense.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"It squeezes the fruit against the roof of its mouth, swallows the juice, then spits out a dry pellet of skin and fiber. The ground under a feeding tree is scattered with them.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"By day they hang in camps. Hundreds or thousands of bats crowd into one stand of trees. They squabble over the best branches, shuffle about, and are audible from streets away.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"It sleeps hanging by its feet, wrapped in its own wings like a folded umbrella. The toes lock shut under the bat's own weight — worked out from the tendons, hanging on costs it almost no effort.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"food","text":"Durian flowers open after dark. Island flying foxes visit them for nectar, and when researchers watched closely, the trees the bats visited went on to set more fruit.","more":"Durian is the spiky, powerfully smelly fruit sold across Southeast Asia. A bat shoves its face into a blossom for nectar and comes away dusted in pollen. It carries that pollen to the next tree, sometimes kilometers away.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"A flying fox may cover up to 30 miles (50 km) in a night. That is the trip out to a fruiting tree and home again. It is a longer commute than most people's.","more":"It can hold about 19.7 ft (6 m) per second, roughly 13 mph, for three hours at a stretch. Because they carry seeds and pollen that far, they replant forest as they go: a seed swallowed in one valley gets dropped in the next.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A mother has a single pup a year. For its first weeks it flies with her, hooked to her chest by thumbs and feet, until it grows too heavy and stays behind in the camp.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"That hook at the bend of each wing is a thumb, with a claw on it, left free of the membrane. Flying foxes carry a second claw on the index finger as well.","more":"Insect-hunting bats have the thumb claw only. A flying fox climbs with both, hauling itself hand over hand along a branch and holding food up to its mouth while it hangs by one foot.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"A camp is loud all day long. Flying foxes have a wide range of squabbling, warning and greeting calls, and a mother picks her own pup out of hundreds by call and by smell.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"On dangerously hot days they fan themselves with their wings and lick their wrists and chests, so the wet fur cools them as it dries.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"They eat the forest and replant it in the same night — feeding one thing feeds another."},{"id":"blue-whale","name":"Blue whale","group":"mammals","blurb":"The largest animal on Earth. It grows to about 82 ft (25 m) long. It lives almost entirely on some of the smallest creatures in the sea.","size":"About 82 ft (25 m) long and around 265,000 lb (120 tonnes)","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"length":82.0,"weight":264554,"speed":20,"life":80},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"Its heart weighs roughly 400 pounds (180 kg) — the largest heart of any animal alive.","more":"That figure comes from a real one. A blue whale washed ashore in Newfoundland, Canada, and a museum team cut the heart out, preserved it, and put it on display. It is still the only blue whale heart preserved anywhere in the world.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A newborn blue whale is already longer than a car — about 23 feet (7 m). Drinking its mother's milk, it puts on around 200 pounds (90 kg) every single day.","more":"The milk is roughly as thick as toothpaste and enormously fatty, so a calf puts on weight every single day. It nurses for six to eight months and does not stop growing the whole time.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It eats krill — shrimp-like animals about as long as your little finger. On a heavy feeding day a big blue whale can swallow several tons of them.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"No teeth at all. About 300 fringed plates of baleen hang from each side of its upper jaw. They are made of keratin, the same stuff as your fingernails. They strain krill from the water.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"One lunge takes in more water than the whale's whole body weighs. It swallows nothing yet: first it squeezes all that water back out through the baleen.","more":"The throat has deep grooves called pleats. They unfold like an accordion, so the mouth balloons into an enormous bag. The gulp-and-squeeze takes well under a minute. The whale may repeat it several times on a single dive.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Scientists once suction-cupped a heart monitor onto a wild blue whale. On a deep dive its heart slowed to two beats a minute, then raced to 37 back at the surface.","more":"Two beats a minute means nearly thirty seconds of silence between beats. Slowing that far saves oxygen while the whale is down. The tag held on with four suction cups, then fell off and was collected floating on the surface.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Its calls sit so low that much of the sound is at the very bottom of what people can hear. It is a rumble you feel in your chest more than hear.","more":"Low sounds travel enormous distances in deep water. Researchers listening on underwater microphones can pick up one blue whale from far off. They can follow it for days without ever laying eyes on it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"Its tongue alone weighs roughly 3 tons (2,700 kg).","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Blue whales swim in every ocean, but they are thinly spread and hard to find. Whaling once brought them close to vanishing; they are protected now, and in some seas their numbers are slowly climbing.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Blue whale droppings come out as a bright orange cloud, colored by the shells of all that krill. It is loaded with iron, and the best read is that it feeds the tiny drifting plants krill live on.","more":"The whale feeds deep and releases waste near the surface, carrying nutrients up into sunlit water where plankton can use them. Researchers scoop the floating clouds into fine nets to work out exactly what the whale has been eating.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"build","text":"Its blow is a column of misty breath that shoots up 30 feet (9 m). That is as high as a two-story house. You can smell it from the deck of a boat.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"The biggest heart God ever set beating, in an animal that lives on the smallest food in the sea."},{"id":"humpback-whale","name":"Humpback whale","group":"mammals","blurb":"A barnacle-crusted whale with enormous white flippers. It is famous for leaping clear of the water, and for the long songs the males sing.","size":"About 49 ft (15 m) long, with unusually long flippers","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"length":49.2,"weight":66139,"speed":15,"life":80},"facts":[{"cat":"build","text":"Its flippers are the longest of any animal — up to 16 feet (5 m), about a third of its whole body. Its scientific name, Megaptera, means big wing.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"copied","text":"The front edge of a humpback's flipper is lumpy, not smooth. Engineers copied those lumps onto wind turbine blades and fans, because they help a wing grip at steep angles.","more":"The lumps are called tubercles. They split the flow into channels and delay the moment a wing stalls and loses its lift. That grip is how a whale as heavy as twenty cars can turn in a tight circle underwater.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"Several whales dive beneath a school of herring and blow a spiralling curtain of bubbles. The fish will not swim through it. Then the whales rise up the middle, mouths open.","more":"It is called bubble-net feeding, and it is learned rather than automatic. Only some humpback populations do it, and whales appear to pick it up from one another. In a group they take set roles and lunge together on a signal call.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Only the males sing. One song can run from a few minutes to half an hour. He repeats it for hours. Every singing male in that stretch of ocean sings the same version.","more":"The song changes slowly through the season, and all the singers change with it. Sometimes a brand-new song spreads from one ocean population to the next. Whales thousands of miles apart end up singing the newcomer.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It eats krill and small schooling fish — herring, capelin, sand lance — taken in enormous mouthfuls. Almost all of a year's eating is packed into the cold feeding months.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"Some humpbacks swim more than 5,000 miles (8,000 km) between cold feeding seas and warm calving waters. It is one of the longest migrations any mammal makes.","more":"They feed hard through the cold months, then eat little or nothing in the warm breeding waters, living for months on the blubber they packed on. Mothers make the entire journey while nursing a calf.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Humpbacks have been recorded charging at hunting orcas and driving them off. Often the animal being rescued was a seal or a sea lion, not a humpback at all.","more":"Researchers gathered more than a hundred of these encounters from all over the world. The humpbacks bellow, slash with their tails, and put their huge flippers between the orcas and the prey. Why they bother is still an open question.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"The knobs on its head are not barnacles. Each one is a tubercle with a single hair growing out of it, wired to nerves — something like a cat's whisker.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"weird","text":"No two tails are alike. The black-and-white pattern under the flukes works like a fingerprint, so researchers photograph it and follow the same individual whale for years.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"A humpback weighing 30 tons can throw nearly its whole body out of the water and land with a crash you can hear a long way off.","more":"Breaching may knock off clinging hitchhikers. It may send a signal other whales can hear underwater. It may do both. Researchers have found humpbacks breach more often once the wind picks up and the sea turns noisy. That fits the idea that the splash is a way of being heard.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"It carries acorn barnacles cemented right into its skin — around the chin, the flippers and the tail. They are hitchhikers, not parasites, and their kind rides only on whales.","more":"Barnacle larvae drift in the water, settle on the whale's skin and grow a hard shell anchored in place. Riding a whale means a constant flow of food-rich water pushed past them. Goose barnacles often grow on top of the acorn barnacles — hitchhikers on hitchhikers.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A mother and her calf whisper. They swap quiet grunts and squeaks that carry only a short way. The best read is that a loud call would tell an orca exactly where the calf is.","more":"Researchers stuck recording tags on calves and found the calls were extremely faint. The calf stays tucked against its mother's side, and she often keeps her own body between the calf and the open sea.","kind":"worked"}],"wonder":"Given long white wings, a voice that carries for miles, and a habit of turning back for animals that are not its own."},{"id":"sperm-whale","name":"Sperm whale","group":"mammals","blurb":"The largest toothed animal on Earth. It is a square-headed hunter that dives into the pitch dark to catch squid.","size":"About 52 ft (16 m) long, with a huge blunt head","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"length":52.5,"weight":88185,"speed":20,"life":60},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"A third of a sperm whale is head. Inside is the spermaceti organ, a great case of waxy oil. Worked out from its shape and the beam of sound leaving the front of it, the case focuses and aims the whale's clicks.","more":"The case can hold hundreds of gallons of oil. Sound made in the whale's nose passages travels back through the case. It bounces off an air sac. It leaves the front of the head as a tight, powerful beam of clicks.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"record","text":"It hunts in total darkness half a mile down. A dive usually lasts about 45 minutes, but sperm whales have been recorded past 6,500 feet (2,000 m) and under for more than an hour.","more":"On the way down its lungs collapse under the pressure, which is exactly what they are built to do. Oxygen is stored in the blood and muscles instead of the lungs, and its muscle is almost black with the stuff.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"The loudest sounds any animal makes are sperm whale clicks. They are sharp cracks that carry for miles underwater. You can hear them right through a boat's hull.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"It hunts blind. A click goes out, an echo comes back off a squid, and the whale reads the darkness from sound alone. That is echolocation.","more":"It scans with slow clicks while searching, then speeds them into a buzz as it closes in — the faster the clicks, the more often the picture updates. Researchers listening in can tell from the buzz alone when a hunt is about to end.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It eats squid, mostly — a great many of them, and mostly ones no longer than your arm. Some sperm whales carry round scars left by the suckers of giant squid.","more":"Nobody has ever watched a sperm whale fight a giant squid; the evidence is the scars and the beaks. A single stomach can hold thousands of hard squid beaks, which is how researchers work out what the whale has been eating.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Its teeth are only in the narrow lower jaw — twenty to twenty-six cone-shaped teeth down each side, up to 8 inches (20 cm) long. They fit into sockets in the upper jaw.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"Sperm whales sleep standing up. A whole group will hang vertically near the surface, nose-up and completely still, for ten or fifteen minutes at a time.","more":"It was discovered almost by accident. A research boat drifted in among motionless whales, and they did not react until the hull touched one. They nap in short bursts and appear to sleep less than almost any other mammal.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A calf cannot follow its mother down into the cold dark. So it waits at the surface, and another adult from the family stays up there with it until she returns.","more":"Sperm whale families are mothers, daughters and young. Babysitting shifts get shared around, and calves sometimes nurse from females that are not their mother. Males leave the family when they grow up and head for colder water alone.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"It has the largest brain of any animal — around 17 pounds (8 kg), roughly five times the weight of yours.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"Families talk in codas: short rhythms of clicks, like knocking on a door. Different clans use different rhythms, and a calf spends its first years learning its own family's.","more":"Clans using different codas can share the same stretch of ocean and still keep to their own kind. Researchers can work out which clan they are listening to from a recording, without ever seeing the whales.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Squid beaks are too hard to digest. Now and then they bind into a waxy lump called ambergris, which drifts ashore and is worth a fortune to perfume makers.","more":"Fresh ambergris smells terrible. After a long time at sea it hardens into a gray, sweet-smelling lump that was used to make perfume last longer on skin. Pieces found on beaches have sold for staggering sums.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"build","text":"Its blowhole sits at the front left of its head, so the spout blows forward and off to one side. Whalers could name the whale from miles away by that crooked, bushy blow.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"A head built as a lens for sound, so an animal can find its way where light has never reached."},{"id":"narwhal","name":"Narwhal","group":"mammals","blurb":"An Arctic whale with a long spiral tusk growing straight out of its head — a real animal, not a legend.","size":"Body about 15 ft (4.5 m) long, plus a long spiral tusk","homes":["polar","ocean"],"stats":{"length":14.8,"weight":2205,"life":50},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"The tusk is a tooth — the narwhal's left canine, grown straight out through its upper lip. On a big male it reaches 10 feet (3 m) and weighs over 20 pounds (9 kg).","more":"Almost every tusk belongs to a male, though a few females grow one and the odd narwhal grows two. The tooth keeps growing for most of the whale's life. Narwhals have no other working teeth — their mouths are otherwise nearly bare.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"The tusk is not solid armor. It is porous, threaded with millions of tiny nerve channels, and seawater seeps in through the surface to reach them.","more":"Researchers fitted narwhals with heart monitors and washed the tusk with water of different saltiness. The heart rate changed. That is real evidence the tusk is a sense organ, feeling the sea the whale swims through.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Every narwhal tusk spirals the same way — counterclockwise. Even on the rare whale that grows two tusks, both turn in that same direction.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It has no chewing teeth, so it never chews. It swims up to a Greenland halibut or an Arctic cod and sucks the fish in whole, like a vacuum.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Drone cameras over the Canadian Arctic have filmed narwhals whacking cod with the tusk. It is a fast downward flick. It stuns the fish just long enough to swallow it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Few air-breathing animals dive deeper. Through the winter narwhals drop past 2,600 feet (800 m) again and again, and the deepest dives on record go well over a mile down.","more":"A deep dive lasts up to about 25 minutes, and a narwhal may make fifteen or more in a day. Much of that happens in winter under the ice — so every dive has to end at a crack or hole it can find again in the dark.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"It spends the whole winter under thick pack ice, in water as black as night. It breathes at cracks and holes. Every time it needs air, it has to find one again.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"There is no fin on its back — only a low bumpy ridge. Nobody has seen what a tall fin would do up there; the ice it lives beneath is the argument.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A calf is born a plain gray. As a narwhal grows it turns blotchy and mottled, and the oldest ones fade to almost pure white.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"When orcas arrive, narwhals go quiet. They press into shallow water near the shore. Or they push into ice packed too tightly for an orca's tall fin to follow.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"Narwhals return to the same summer fjord year after year, and to the same wintering water. The best read is that each one is following the route its mother took.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Narwhal tusks were once sold in Europe as unicorn horns. Buyers who had never seen the Arctic paid many times the tusk's weight in gold for them.","kind":"record"}],"wonder":"God gave the narwhal a tooth that can feel the sea it swims through."},{"id":"orca","name":"Orca","group":"mammals","blurb":"The ocean's top hunter. It is a black-and-white animal that lives in families and teaches its young how to hunt.","size":"Longer than a car and a half, with a back fin taller than a grown man","homes":["ocean","polar"],"stats":{"length":23.0,"weight":8818,"speed":30,"life":50},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"Despite the name killer whale, an orca is not a whale at all. It is a dolphin — the biggest one there is. A large male reaches 32 feet (9.8 m).","more":"A big male can weigh 11 tons, about the same as two elephants. Males outgrow females by a wide margin, and everything on them runs larger: the flippers, the tail, and above all the towering fin on the back.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"A grown male's back fin can stand 6 feet (1.8 m) tall, taller than most adults. Every fin is nicked and shaped a little differently, so researchers use them to tell individuals apart.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"Orcas live in families led by the oldest female. In some populations a son never leaves his mother — he swims beside her for his entire life.","more":"The family group is called a matriline: a grandmother, her daughters, her sons, and her daughters' calves. They hunt, travel and rest together. A whale that gets separated calls until it finds them again.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Each family has its own set of calls — a dialect. A calf learns its mother's calls, and researchers can tell which family is going past simply by listening.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Different orca families eat completely different food. Some eat nothing but salmon. Others hunt seals and never touch a fish. They share the same ocean and do not mix.","more":"The differences run deep. Separate calls, separate hunting methods, even differently shaped fins. A salmon-eating orca and a seal-hunting orca can pass within sight of each other and behave like total strangers.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"In Antarctica, orcas line up shoulder to shoulder and charge an ice floe together. The wave they make washes the seal on top of it into the water.","more":"They have been filmed doing a practice run first, and even nudging the ice around so the seal ends up on the side they want. If the first wave fails, they line up and go again.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Off South Africa, orcas have been filmed opening up great white sharks. They eat only the liver, the fattiest part. Then they leave the rest of the shark behind.","more":"Great white carcasses began washing ashore with a neat wound and no liver. Two particular orcas, known by their bent-over fins, are behind many of them. The white sharks now clear out of the area entirely when those two show up.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"It finds prey by echolocation, clicking and reading the echoes. But seal-hunting orcas often go almost silent. The best read is that they have to: a seal can hear those clicks coming.","more":"Fish hear orca clicks poorly, so salmon hunters chatter away constantly. Seals and porpoises hear them very well. So the mammal hunters work in near silence. They only start talking again once the hunt is finished.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"home","text":"Orcas live in every ocean on Earth, from the edge of the ice to the tropics. Few mammals anywhere are spread more widely.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Older females stop having calves but keep leading. In poor salmon years the grandmother swims out in front, and her grandcalves are the ones most likely to make it through.","more":"Researchers followed the same families for many years. When a grandmother dies, her grandcalves are far more likely to die in the couple of years afterwards. She knows where the fish are when the fish are hardest to find.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"In Patagonia, orcas rush right up onto the beach to grab a sea lion, then wriggle backwards into the sea. Mothers spend years teaching the young ones how, starting in safe shallow water.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Nothing hunts a healthy orca. It is an apex predator wherever it lives — and there is no confirmed record of a wild orca ever killing a person.","kind":"record"}]," wonder":"The oldest mother is her family's map and memory — the wisest kept, not set aside.","wonder":"The oldest mother is her family's map and memory — the wisest kept, not set aside."},{"id":"bottlenose-dolphin","name":"Bottlenose dolphin","group":"mammals","blurb":"A fast, clever sea mammal that breathes air and travels in groups. It hunts fish by firing sound into the water and listening to what comes back.","size":"Most adults run 6.5 to 13 ft (2 to 4 m) from nose to tail.","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"speed":18,"weight":551,"length":8.2,"life":40},"facts":[{"cat":"senses","text":"Click. Listen. Click again. A dolphin fires sound out of its forehead and reads the echoes coming back — echolocation — until it knows a fish's size, shape and direction without ever seeing it.","more":"The clicks are made in air passages under the blowhole and focused through a fatty lump in the forehead called the melon. Echoes return through the lower jawbone to the ear. In tests, dolphins picked out a target the size of a tennis ball from more than 328 ft (100 m) away — the length of a football field.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"A dolphin never fully falls asleep. Half its brain rests while the other half keeps watch and remembers to breathe — because for a dolphin, breathing is a decision, not a reflex.","more":"Blacking out underwater would be fatal, so the two halves of the brain take turns and then swap over. In one study, dolphins kept echolocating and reporting targets accurately for five days straight. They made none of the sloppy mistakes a sleep-starved human would.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Every dolphin invents its own whistle in its first months and keeps it for life. Other dolphins copy that exact whistle to call it over — the nearest thing to a name in the ocean.","more":"Researchers can identify individual wild dolphins by whistle alone. A mother and calf sometimes get separated in murky water. They trade each other's signature whistles back and forth until they meet up again.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"A dolphin cannot smell anything at all — it has no olfactory nerves, and no smelling lobe in the brain either. It hears astonishingly well instead, and reads the whole water column with sound.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"In Florida's shallow bays, one dolphin swims a fast tight circle, beating its tail to kick up a ring of mud. The trapped fish panic, leap, and land in the mouths waiting outside.","more":"It is called mud-ring feeding and it needs a cast: one ring-maker, several catchers holding position. Not every dolphin population does it. The trick runs in particular family groups, so dolphins a bay away may never have seen it done.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Some dolphins in Shark Bay, Australia, tear a cone-shaped sponge off the seafloor. They wear it over the snout like a glove. Then they poke through sharp rubble for hidden fish.","more":"The sponge guards the beak from scrapes and stings. Calves learn it by swimming beside their mothers and start carrying their own small sponge while still young. It is the best-documented case of tool use in a sea mammal.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Count the teeth and you get somewhere between 72 and 112, every one the same simple cone. They are for gripping, not chewing — a dolphin swallows its fish whole, head first.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A dolphin calf is born tail first and has to reach the surface for its first breath within seconds. Its mother nudges it up. Then it rides in her slipstream for years.","more":"Calves nurse for around eighteen months to two years and often stay with their mother for three to six. A dolphin has no lips to suck with, so the mother squirts milk into the calf's mouth using muscle around the mammary gland.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"speed","text":"Top speed is about 18 mph (29 km/h) — faster than any human has ever swum — but only in short bursts. Cruising, a dolphin ambles along at roughly walking pace.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"At speed a dolphin leaps clear of the water again and again. Air is thinner than water to push through, so the best read is that porpoising costs less than staying under.","more":"A boat or a big whale shoves a pressure wave along ahead of it. Dolphins ride that wave, coasting on someone else's effort without a single beat of the tail. Bow-riding dolphins can hold station there for a long time and barely work at all.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"build","text":"That hole on top of the head is its nostril, sealed by a muscular flap the dolphin opens on purpose. Underwater the flap stays shut. Not a drop gets in.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"A bottlenose dolphin's brain is bigger than yours — about 3.5 lb (1.6 kg) against a human's 3 lb (1.4 kg). Brain size on its own does not measure cleverness, but that is a lot of brain.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Half a brain resting, half on watch, night after night for a whole lifetime — a design that never has to stop breathing in order to rest."},{"id":"sea-otter","name":"Sea otter","group":"mammals","blurb":"A dog-sized sea mammal of the cold North Pacific. It floats on its back and cracks shellfish open on its own chest. It wears the thickest fur coat of any animal.","size":"Up to 5 ft (1.5 m) from nose to tail tip — about as long as a nine-year-old is tall.","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"weight":66,"length":4.3,"life":15},"facts":[{"cat":"record","text":"No blubber. None at all. Instead, up to a million hairs packed into a patch of skin the size of a postage stamp — the densest fur of any animal on Earth.","more":"The fur traps a layer of air right against the skin, so an otter is really wearing a coat of bubbles. That is why otters groom constantly. Fur that gets dirty or oiled lets the air out. A soaked otter cannot warm itself up again.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"A sea otter eats at least a quarter of its own body weight every single day. Scaled to you, that would be roughly 18 lb (8 kg) of food — around 80 apples — before bedtime.","more":"Staying warm in near-freezing water burns enormous energy, and the otter has no fat layer to fall back on. Its body burns fuel two to three times faster than a land mammal of the same size. So it dives and eats almost all day: urchins, crabs, clams, snails, mussels.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Under each front leg is a loose pocket of skin. The otter stuffs it with food it has collected — and often with one favorite rock, carried up and down between dives.","more":"Floating on its back, the otter sets the rock on its chest and hammers shellfish against it until they crack. Some individuals keep the same stone for a long stretch. Sea otters are among the very few mammals anywhere known to use tools.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"It hunts mostly by feel. Down on a dark, murky seafloor the otter sweeps sensitive paws and whiskers through loose rock and kelp instead of looking for its dinner.","more":"In laboratory tests, sea otters told rough surfaces from smooth faster than humans could, and were just as accurate using their whiskers as their paws. Where the light is bad and the water is stirred up, touch simply beats sight.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"Otters sleep floating, and they hate to drift. They wrap themselves in strands of kelp like a seatbelt, or hold paws with the otter beside them. A group of them is called a raft.","more":"Rafts range from a few animals to dozens. Males and females usually rest in separate groups. Holding paws also lifts those bare pads clear of the water. Paw pads are one of the few places on an otter with no fur to keep the heat in.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A newborn pup is so fluffy it physically cannot sink. It bobs on the surface like a cork while its mother dives, and it cannot follow her under for weeks.","more":"The baby coat holds so much air that diving is impossible. A mother often parks her pup wrapped in kelp so it will not drift off, and grooms it for hours every day. A pup that is not groomed gets wet to the skin, and a wet pup gets dangerously cold.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"A pup left alone on the surface screams. It is a thin, gull-like cry that carries a long way over open water. The pup keeps screaming until its mother's head pops up.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Eat enough purple sea urchins and it shows. The pigment from the urchins soaks into the otter, and its teeth and bones can end up stained purple.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Where otters hunt, kelp forests stand. Otters eat sea urchins, and urchins chew through the kelp's anchors. Remove the otters and the urchins mow the forest down to bare rock.","more":"Divers call that stripped rock an urchin barren. Surveys in Monterey Bay found kelp holding on exactly where otters were feeding hardest. One animal's appetite props up an entire underwater forest.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"copied","text":"Engineers have built rubbery, hairy materials copied from otter and beaver fur. They hold a layer of air underwater. That could lead to a warmer, lighter kind of wetsuit.","kind":"record"}],"wonder":"No blubber, no fire, no shelter — just a coat made so finely that a trapped skin of air keeps a warm animal alive in freezing water."},{"id":"walrus","name":"Walrus","group":"mammals","blurb":"An enormous Arctic sea mammal with two ivory tusks and a bristling mustache. It is built for hauling out on sea ice and vacuuming clams off the seafloor.","size":"Heavier than a small car — a big male can pass 2,600 lb (1,200 kg) and stretch over 10 ft (3 m).","homes":["polar","ocean"],"stats":{"weight":2646,"length":10.5,"life":30},"facts":[{"cat":"build","text":"Those tusks are teeth — two upper canines that never stop growing. On a big male they reach about 3 ft (1 m).","more":"Walruses hook them into the ice to drag themselves out of the water, which is where the genus name Odobenus, 'tooth-walker', comes from. They also chip breathing holes with them, and males square off and display them. The bigger set usually wins without a fight.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Count the whiskers: 400 to 700 of them, in thirteen to fifteen neat rows across the snout. In pitch-black water a walrus finds clams buried in mud entirely by feel.","more":"Each whisker is a vibrissa, rooted in its own bed of muscle, blood and nerve, and swept across the seafloor like fingers reading a page. Walruses feed far below the reach of light. Touch is not a backup sense for them — it is the entire hunt.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"A walrus does not chew a clam. It clamps its lips over the shell and yanks its thick tongue back like a piston, and the suction rips the animal straight out.","more":"The empty shells drop back to the seafloor and only the soft body goes down. A single walrus can take several thousand clams in one feeding bout, and it works over snails, worms and sea cucumbers on the same trip.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"A walrus can sleep in open water without drowning. It inflates two air sacs in its throat like built-in water wings and bobs there, head up, fast asleep.","more":"The pouches are pockets off the windpipe that hold many liters of air. Males use them for something else too: a deep, bell-like note produced underwater that carries far enough to be heard through the hull of a boat.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"A walrus changes color. After a long, cold dive its skin looks pale, almost white. Warm up in the sun on a beach and it flushes pink all over.","more":"In cold water the blood vessels near the surface clamp down to hold heat in the core, which drains the color out of the skin. On land they open wide again to dump heat. A pink walrus is a walrus letting off steam.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Walruses pile onto ice and beaches in their thousands, body pressed against body. It is warm and it is safe, but the herd spooks easily, and a stampede crushes the animals underneath.","more":"A haul-out can hold tens of thousands of animals shoulder to shoulder, and it is a loud place: grunts, roars, and the clatter of tusks on tusks. Young walruses often just climb up and rest on top of the adults rather than fight for floor space.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A walrus calf is born at 99 to 165 lb (45 to 75 kg) — already the weight of a grown man — and stays with its mother for two years or more.","more":"That is among the longest and strongest mother-and-baby bonds of any seal, sea lion or walrus. Mothers carry small calves on their backs in the water and defend them hard. A calf drinks milk for over a year while it learns where the clam beds are.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"Under that wrinkled hide sits a blubber layer up to 6 in (15 cm) deep. That is about the length of your hand. It is the only thing between a warm animal and near-freezing water.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"An old male's neck and shoulders are armored in thick, knobbly lumps of skin called bosses. In a shoving match, they blunt the point of another walrus's tusks.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Males sing under the ice. A courting walrus works through a long pattern of knocks, taps, whistles and that deep gonging bell, and repeats it for hours on end.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"Feeding dives usually last five to ten minutes in water shallower than 260 ft (80 m) — about as deep as a 25-story building is tall. A walrus goes where the clams are.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Whiskers that read the dark, a tongue that pulls like a pump, and a throat that turns into a life jacket — every piece of a walrus is fitted to a sea with no light in it."},{"id":"star-nosed-mole","name":"Star-nosed mole","group":"mammals","blurb":"A small, dark, mud-loving mole with twenty-two pink tentacles ringing its nose. It is the fastest eater ever measured among mammals.","size":"Palm-sized — about 8 in (20 cm) from nose to tail tip, and roughly the weight of a golf ball.","homes":["freshwater","forest"],"stats":{"weight":0,"length":0.7,"life":3},"facts":[{"cat":"senses","text":"Twenty-two pink tentacles ring its nose, and not one of them is for smelling. They are for touching. The star carries more than 100,000 nerve fibers — about six times what your entire hand has.","more":"The touch sensors are called Eimer's organs. About 25,000 of them cover a star only 0.4 in (1 cm) across, less than the width of your fingernail. The mole flicks the star from spot to spot roughly a dozen times a second. It builds a picture of the tunnel by feel alone.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Fastest eater on record. From first touch to swallowed, a star-nosed mole handles a worm in about a quarter of a second — and at its very quickest, barely a tenth.","more":"Its brain takes roughly 8 milliseconds to decide whether something is food — close to the physical speed limit of a nerve signal. Researchers needed high-speed video just to see it happen. To your eye, the mole twitches and the worm is simply gone.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"It can smell underwater. The mole puffs a bubble of air out of its nostrils onto something on the streambed. Then it sucks the same bubble straight back in and reads the scent it collected.","more":"It blows eight to twelve tiny bubbles a second — the same rhythm a mouse sniffs with on land. Only the star-nosed mole and a couple of water shrews are known to manage it. In tests, moles followed underwater scent trails to a worm with near-perfect accuracy.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Its eyes are tiny and barely work. In a black tunnel there is nothing to look at anyway, so the star does the seeing instead.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Other moles want dry ground. This one wants mud. It tunnels through soggy meadows, marshes and stream banks, and plenty of its tunnels open straight out into the water.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"It swims well, and it does not stop for winter. Star-nosed moles have been watched paddling beneath the ice of frozen streams and tunnelling their way through snow.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Its front feet are turned permanently outwards like a pair of shovels, and they are nearly as wide as they are long. Watch one dig and the shape explains itself: they are for shifting soil sideways, not for walking.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"food","text":"Dinner is worms, insect larvae and small water creatures, most of them smaller than a grain of rice. The best read is that its speed is what makes prey that small worth catching at all.","more":"A slower animal would burn more energy chasing a speck of food than the speck is worth, so it ignores them. The star-nosed mole eats a very large number of very small animals. The speed of its star is what makes that a living.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"babies","text":"One litter a year, usually four or five pups, raised in a nest of dry grass built above the waterline. A newborn is tiny, and its star is folded flat.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Almost everything eats it. Owls and hawks take them at the surface, skunks dig them out, and large fish snap them up while they are swimming.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Twenty-two tentacles, a hundred thousand nerve fibers, and a decision made in eight thousandths of a second. All packed into an animal about the weight of a golf ball."},{"id":"capybara","name":"Capybara","group":"mammals","blurb":"The largest rodent in the world. It is a calm, barrel-shaped, grass-eating animal of South America that lives with one foot in the river.","size":"Up to 4 ft (1.3 m) long and around 110 lb (50 kg) — heavier than most nine-year-olds, and built like a barrel.","homes":["grassland","freshwater","rainforest"],"stats":{"speed":22,"weight":110,"length":3.9,"life":4},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"It is a rodent, in the same great group as mice and squirrels, and it can outweigh you. A large capybara passes 130 lb (60 kg) and stands about knee-high to an adult.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Its front teeth never stop growing, and neither do its back ones. It grinds them down all day on tough grass — a mouth that wears out and rebuilds itself at the same time.","more":"That is the deal every rodent lives by. The incisors never stop growing, and must be worn down or they overgrow. The capybara takes it further than most. Grass is full of grit and silica, so the back teeth grind constantly too.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Look at the head. Eyes, ears and nostrils all sit high on the skull in one line. The capybara can float with its whole body hidden and still see, hear and breathe.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Trouble on the bank? The capybara walks into the river and vanishes. It can hold its breath for up to five minutes, and surfaces with nothing showing but two nostrils.","more":"Its feet are partly webbed, so it swims strongly rather than splashing about, and it can doze in the shallows with only its nose above water. Jaguars, caimans and anacondas all hunt capybaras. The river is the escape route, every time.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"A capybara eats grass. That is very nearly the whole menu — grass, water plants, some bark and fruit — several kilos of it a day, cropped short with those chisel teeth.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Grass is hard to unlock. So every morning a capybara eats its own droppings, a special soft kind. The whole meal runs through its body a second time.","more":"Rabbits and hares do exactly the same. The first trip through the gut lets bacteria break the tough fibers apart; the second trip is when the animal actually absorbs what those bacteria made. Skip it and you would starve on a full stomach.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Capybaras are chatty. They purr, whistle, click, huff and bark, and a grazing family keeps up an almost constant low churring. In grass that tall, the best read is that the churring is how they keep from wandering off.","more":"A sharp bark is the alarm: the whole group freezes, then heads for water. Pups purr steadily while they feed and mothers answer them. In grass taller than a young capybara, sound is how the herd keeps track of itself.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Pups arrive ready for work — eyes open, fur on, nibbling grass within about a week. In a big group the youngsters bunch into one crowd, and females will nurse pups that are not their own.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"They live in groups of roughly ten to twenty, swelling to fifty or more in the dry season when everyone crowds around the last water. Someone always has their head up while the rest eat.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Birds land on them and they simply do not mind. Caracaras and other birds ride around on a capybara's back picking off ticks. The bird gets a free meal and the capybara gets fewer parasites.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"speed","text":"Barrel body, stumpy legs, and still quick. A capybara can hit around 22 mph (35 km/h) in a short dash. That is faster than you can run. Then it reaches the water and dives.","more":"It is a sprinter, not a distance runner. Everything about the escape is short: burst across open ground, hit the river, submerge. On land a capybara is prey. In the water it is a strong swimmer that can hold its breath longer than anything chasing it wants to.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"A male carries a bare dark lump on top of his snout called a morrillo. He rubs it along plants and grass stems to leave a scent that marks whose stretch of riverbank this is.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Eyes, ears and nose set in one high line, feet half webbed, teeth that never wear out — a grass-eater fitted exactly to the edge of a river."},{"id":"beaver","name":"Beaver","group":"mammals","blurb":"A thickset, buck-toothed rodent that cuts down trees with its teeth and rebuilds whole streams into ponds.","size":"About 3.3 ft (1 m) long and 44 lb (20 kg), with a flat leathery tail shaped like a paddle.","homes":["freshwater","forest"],"stats":{"speed":5,"weight":44,"length":3.3,"life":12},"facts":[{"cat":"build","text":"A beaver's four front teeth never stop growing. It has to gnaw every day of its life, or they would grow right past its jaw.","more":"The front face of each incisor is hard, iron-rich enamel; the back is softer dentine. The soft side wears away faster, so every bite grinds the tooth back into a fresh chisel edge. A beaver's teeth sharpen themselves on the job.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"The longest beaver dam anyone has found runs 850 meters (2,790 feet) across a marsh in northern Alberta. That is longer than nine football fields end to end.","more":"It sits on the southern edge of Wood Buffalo National Park and was first spotted in satellite photographs. No single beaver built it. One family after another kept patching and extending the same wall until it ran clear across the wetland.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Beavers build because of a sound. Play a recording of running water next to a beaver and it will pile mud and sticks on top of the loudspeaker.","more":"A Swedish biologist named Lars Wilsson tested exactly this. He offered beavers a real leak they could see, running almost silently, and a speaker trickling away on dry ground. They ignored the leak and dammed the speaker. The trigger is the noise.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"In slow water a beaver builds its dam straight across; in fast water it builds a curve, bowed against the push of the river. Nobody has watched one decide — the shapes are the argument that it reads the current.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"senses","text":"A beaver has see-through third eyelids that slide across like swim goggles. Its lips shut behind its front teeth. It can chew a branch underwater without swallowing the pond.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Slap. A beaver whacks its tail flat on the water and the crack carries across the whole pond — the alarm that sends every beaver diving.","more":"Adults do not always fall for it. When a young beaver slaps at nothing much, the older ones often keep right on eating. A beaver can stay under for up to about fifteen minutes on one breath, so a false alarm costs it little.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"The front door is underwater. A beaver dives, swims up a tunnel and climbs out onto a dry shelf inside a dome of mud-plastered sticks.","more":"Towards winter the family smears wet mud over the outside. It freezes to the consistency of concrete and a wolf cannot dig through it. They leave the very top loose and airy on purpose — that patch is the chimney that lets everyone breathe.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Beavers eat no fish at all. They eat bark — mostly the soft living layer just underneath it, called cambium — plus buds, roots and pond plants.","more":"Wood is close to impossible to digest. A beaver's gut carries microbes that break down cellulose for it, the same trick a cow uses on grass. Willow, aspen and poplar are the favourites, and branches get stashed underwater as a winter larder.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Beavers mark their territory with mud pies. They scrape up a heap on the bank and dab it with castoreum, a goo from a gland under the tail that smells of vanilla.","more":"That is not a poetic description — castoreum genuinely smells sweet, and people have used it in perfume for a very long time. To another beaver the little mound is a signpost saying, plainly, this stretch of stream is taken.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"The beaver is the biggest rodent in North America, and the porcupine is second. A grown beaver weighs around 44 lb (20 kg) — about what a Border Collie weighs.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Kits are born furred, with their eyes already open, and can swim within days. They stay with their parents about two years, helping with dam repairs before setting off.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"A beaver pond is a whole neighbourhood. Flooding the trees makes still water, and frogs, herons, dragonflies, moose and trout all move in behind the beaver.","more":"This is why beavers get called ecosystem engineers. One family cutting willows reshapes a valley. Floods slow down. Water stays put through dry spells. A marsh spreads where there used to be nothing but a narrow creek.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Goggles, sealing lips, a mud trowel of a tail, teeth that sharpen themselves — everything a dam-builder needs, given before the first dam was ever built."},{"id":"porcupine","name":"Porcupine","group":"mammals","blurb":"A slow, shaggy, tree-climbing rodent with about thirty thousand sharp quills growing out through its fur.","size":"About 18 lb (8 kg), and it looks twice that size once the quills go up.","homes":["forest","mountain"],"stats":{"weight":18,"length":2.6,"life":10},"facts":[{"cat":"defence","text":"A porcupine cannot throw its quills. Not one, not ever. It turns its back, hunches up and reverses at you — the quills release only when something touches them.","more":"The myth probably starts with the tail. A porcupine lashes it sideways, and if the tail strikes a rock, loose quills can fly off and travel a short way. It looks like a launch. It is really just a hard swing.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"The barbs that make a quill so hard to pull out are microscopic. Every one of them is crowded into the top sixth of an inch (4 mm) of the quill.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"copied","text":"Engineers copied the quill. In the laboratory those barbs cut the push-in force roughly in half, and quadruple the force needed to drag the quill back out.","more":"Easy in, hard out is a strange combination. It is also exactly what surgeons want from a staple or a skin patch. A barbed quill slides into tissue more easily than a hypodermic needle of the same thickness. Laboratories now build needles with quill-shaped barbs.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"It is the second-largest rodent in North America, after the beaver. Every one of those quills is really a hair: hollow, stiff, and up to three inches (75 mm) long.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A newborn porcupine is called a porcupette, and it arrives with its quills lying flat and soft as wet paper. They dry and stiffen within hours.","more":"It is born big — eyes open, whiskered, able to climb a tree within days. Mothers raise a single baby a year, which is very few for a rodent. Then again, almost nothing wants to eat an animal made of needles.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Porcupines crave salt so badly they chew human things to get it. Axe handles, canoe paddles, plywood, outhouse seats, road signs.","more":"A diet of bark and leaves is desperately low in sodium, so a porcupine hunts it wherever it can. Anything a sweaty hand has gripped for years tastes salty to them. Backpackers learn to hang their boots and tools well out of reach.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"In winter a porcupine eats bark and conifer needles. The food is so poor that it loses weight all season long. It simply has to last until spring.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Quills are greasy, and the grease is antibiotic. Nobody has watched it save a porcupine, but the best read is that it protects the animal from its own weapon.","more":"Porcupines fall out of trees more often than you would guess. A falling porcupine lands squarely on its own points. The fatty acids coating each quill have antibiotic properties. They help stop those self-inflicted wounds turning septic.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"build","text":"Look at its hands. Long curved claws and rough, pebbly palms grip bark, and the stiff bristles under its tail dig in behind like a climber's spikes.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"You smell a porcupine before you see one. It carries a strong sour odor — best read as a warning broadcast on a second channel, in case you somehow missed the spikes.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"defence","text":"One hunter has an answer to quills. The fisher is a big weasel. It circles the porcupine's face and bites the bare nose until it can flip the animal over.","more":"The belly carries no quills at all, so the whole fight is about getting underneath. Fishers are agile climbers and will force a porcupine down out of a tree first, where it is far more vulnerable than it is up among the branches.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Thirty thousand needles, each one greased against infection — armor that also looks after the animal wearing it."},{"id":"skunk","name":"Striped skunk","group":"mammals","blurb":"A cat-sized black-and-white forager. It can fire a foul-smelling chemical spray from two nozzles under its tail.","size":"About 2.1 ft (65 cm) long and 7 lb (3 kg), with a bushy tail nearly as long as its body.","homes":["forest","grassland","backyard"],"stats":{"weight":7,"length":2.1,"life":3},"facts":[{"cat":"defence","text":"The spray is a last resort. First comes the deimatic display: the skunk stamps its front feet, hisses, arches its back, fluffs up, and hoists that tail like a flag.","more":"Deimatic means startling — a bluff meant to make a bigger animal back off. Everything about a skunk is a warning it would much rather you took. The stripe is a signpost; the stamping is a countdown. Spraying costs the skunk something real.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"It aims. Two nozzles under the tail swivel and fire, and a skunk can hit your face with real accuracy from three meters (10 feet) away.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"A skunk carries only about five or six shots — roughly a tablespoon in total. Once the glands run dry it can take up to ten days to refill them.","more":"Which is exactly why the warning dance matters so much. A skunk with empty glands still stamps and hisses and puffs up and hopes hard that the bluff works — because there is nothing at all behind it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"The stink is made of thiols, which are sulfur compounds. Your nose picks them up at about eleven parts per billion — roughly one drop spread through twenty-five full bathtubs.","more":"Sulfur is the thread connecting rotten eggs, struck matches and skunks. Human noses are extraordinarily tuned to it. We pick up thiols far more easily than almost anything else we can smell. That is the skunk's whole business model.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Skunk smell comes back. Part of the spray is a chemical with almost no odor until water touches it — then it turns into thiol and stinks all over again.","more":"That is why a washed dog smells fine when dry and dreadful in the rain. The culprits are thioacetates, hiding in the fur, waiting. Rinsing without neutralising them properly just sets up the next ambush.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Tomato juice does not work. It only tires your nose out. The real fix is chemistry: hydrogen peroxide, baking soda and dish soap, mixed fresh in a bucket.","more":"A chemist named Paul Krebaum worked it out. The peroxide turns the thiols into compounds that have no smell at all, and the soap breaks up the oil so the peroxide can reach it. Mix it and use it at once — never seal it in a bottle.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"A skunk will dig a wasp nest out of the ground and eat the grubs, stings and all. Thick fur and loose skin shrug off the defense.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"It is about housecat-sized, and the tail does a lot of the work. The tail fluffs out when the skunk is alarmed. An animal of roughly 7 lb (3 kg) suddenly looks far bigger.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"A skunk can barely see you. Its eyes cannot make out objects much beyond three meters (10 feet). That is why it stamps and postures at anything that comes close.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"defence","text":"One hunter is not put off in the slightest. The great horned owl is the skunk's only regular predator — it flies in silence, and it hardly smells a thing.","more":"Owls have a famously weak sense of smell, so the skunk's entire chemical defense means very little to them. Naturalists sometimes find great horned owl nests that reek of skunk, and owls that carry the scent on their feathers for weeks.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"Skunks do not truly hibernate. They drop their body temperature and doze underground through the cold — often several females to a den — and wander out to feed on mild nights.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Kits are born blind, deaf and barely furred. The stripe is already there, printed into the skin before the hair grows.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"A creature that would far rather be believed than fight, wearing its warning sign in its own skin."},{"id":"armadillo","name":"Nine-banded armadillo","group":"mammals","blurb":"A digging, insect-hunting mammal built inside a jointed shell of bony plates. It looks like a small armored car.","size":"About 2.5 ft (75 cm) long once you count the tail, and about 11 lb (5 kg).","homes":["grassland","forest"],"stats":{"weight":11,"length":2.5,"life":12},"facts":[{"cat":"babies","text":"Every litter is identical quadruplets. Nearly always four babies, all the same sex, all grown from a single fertilised egg that split into four.","more":"Almost nothing else on earth does this as a rule, only its close armadillo relatives. The four are as alike as identical twins. That is why medical researchers value them so highly: four subjects with exactly matching genes, raised in exactly the same burrow.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"It gulps air to swim. An armadillo swallows enough air to puff up its stomach and gut, turning a heavy armored animal into something that floats.","more":"The other option is stranger still. If the creek is narrow, it simply walks in, sinks, and strolls across the bottom holding its breath for up to six minutes. Either way it reaches the far bank.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Startle an armadillo and it launches straight up — about a meter (3 to 4 feet) into the air, all four feet off the ground at once.","more":"This works beautifully against a coyote and disastrously against a car. An armadillo low enough to pass safely under a vehicle jumps up into the bumper instead. That is why so many end up beside the road.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"It cannot roll into a ball. That is a different animal entirely. Only the three-banded armadillos of South America can close themselves up into a sphere.","more":"A nine-banded armadillo's shell has no room to fold that far. Its plan is to run, or to wedge itself deep into a burrow and arch its back so hard against the roof that nothing can drag it out.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Nearly blind, magnificent nose. An armadillo works a patch of ground by smell alone and can pin down a beetle grub twenty centimeters (8 inches) under the soil.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It eats mostly insects — beetles, termites, ants, grubs — plus millipedes, worms and the odd bird egg. Less than a tenth of its diet is anything other than animal.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Its teeth are just pegs. No canines, no enamel at all — a row of blunt little cylinders at the back and nothing whatsoever at the front to bite with.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The shell is real bone. Small plates called scutes grow inside the skin under a horny layer, with bands of soft flexible hide between them so the animal can still bend.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"It digs constantly. There are burrows for sleeping and burrows for escaping. The nest chamber is stuffed with a huge ball of dry leaves, raked in with its front claws.","more":"An armadillo sleeps up to sixteen hours a day underground and comes out around dusk. Abandoned burrows get taken over by snakes, rabbits, opossums and burrowing owls. A single digger ends up housing a whole crowd.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"An armadillo runs cool. Its body sits around 34°C (93°F), a few degrees below yours, and it suffers badly in a hard frost.","more":"That low temperature is also why armadillos matter to medicine. The bacterium behind leprosy grows best below normal human body heat. The armadillo is one of very few animals it can live inside. It is one good reason never to pick up a wild one.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"In the whole United States there is only one kind of armadillo: the nine-banded. Its range keeps pushing further north.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"Despite the name, the bands vary. Count the moveable bands around an armadillo's middle and you may find seven, or eleven — nine is simply the most common.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Armor, a nose that reads underground, and lungs that let it walk across a riverbed — one small animal issued three completely different toolkits."},{"id":"meerkat","name":"Meerkat","group":"mammals","blurb":"A small, sharp-eyed African mongoose. It lives in big underground families and posts a lookout while the others dig for food.","size":"From nose to rump about 12 in (30 cm), the length of a school ruler, with a thin tail nearly as long again.","homes":["desert","grassland"],"stats":{"weight":2,"length":1.6,"life":8},"facts":[{"cat":"teamwork","text":"Somebody is always watching. One meerkat climbs the highest thing around and scans the sky while the rest dig, then swaps out for the next volunteer.","more":"Sentries do not eat while on duty. They keep up a soft running murmur that researchers call the watchman's song. It is a steady all-clear. The diggers can keep their heads down and their minds on the sand.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Meerkat alarm calls carry two pieces of information at once. They say what the danger is, and how close it has already got.","more":"A call for an eagle sends everyone bolting for the nearest bolt-hole. A call for a snake or a jackal sends them bunching up instead. Urgency is coded separately, so a distant hawk gets a different call from one already diving.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Adults teach. They hand pups live scorpions with the stings bitten off, and as the pups get better they pass over prey that is less and less disabled.","more":"This is one of the clearest cases of genuine teaching known outside our own species. The adults listen to the pups' begging calls to judge how old they are, then raise the difficulty. A live, fully armed scorpion is the final exam.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"A meerkat disarms a scorpion before it fights it — one fast bite that snips the stinger clean off the end of the tail.","more":"Pups who get the timing wrong really do get stung, and they squeal about it. Adults are quicker and far more precise. An adult will strip the stinger off a scorpion before handing the rest to a pup that is still learning the job.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"A meerkat weighs about 730 grams (1.6 lb), roughly a big bottle of water. It props itself upright on its hind legs. The tail becomes the third leg of a tripod.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"A membrane sweeps across each eye like a windscreen wiper while it digs, and the ears fold shut to keep the sand out. The front claws are long, curved hooks.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"The burrow is a town. There are tunnels, sleeping chambers and around fifteen doorways. It is dug a meter and a half (5 feet) down and shared by up to thirty meerkats.","more":"The sand at the surface can get too hot to stand on. The chambers far below stay cool and steady all day long. Each family owns several burrows across its territory and moves between them. There is always somewhere to run.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"When the group goes foraging, some adults stay behind as babysitters. They go a whole day without food to guard pups too young to leave the burrow.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Meerkats hardly drink at all. Almost everything they need comes out of what they eat, with roots and tsamma melons supplying water in the driest months.","more":"Insects make up most of the diet: beetle larvae, crickets, spiders, centipedes. Lizards, eggs and the occasional small snake go down too. A meerkat forages nearly all day and stores almost nothing away for later.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The black patches around a meerkat's eyes cut glare. It is the same job as the black stripes an American footballer paints under his.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Meerkats will mob a snake. The whole group bristles up, packs tight together and advances on it as one loud, spitting, much-larger-looking animal.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"On a cold morning the family sits up and faces the sunrise. The belly fur is thin over dark skin, so they warm through like a row of small solar panels.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Lookouts, teachers, babysitters and a shared front door — a whole society packed into an animal lighter than a bottle of water."},{"id":"peregrine-falcon","name":"Peregrine falcon","group":"birds","blurb":"A crow-sized falcon that hunts other birds in mid-air. It dives faster than any other animal on Earth.","size":"About 1.5 ft (45 cm) from bill to tail, and about 2 lb (1 kg).","homes":["mountain","backyard","grassland"],"stats":{"speed":200,"weight":2,"length":1.5,"life":13},"facts":[{"cat":"speed","text":"A peregrine folds its wings and drops. In that dive — a stoop — it passes 200 mph (320 km/h), faster than any other animal alive.","more":"Level flight is a different story. Flapping along, a peregrine is no quicker than plenty of ordinary birds. All of that speed comes from falling: wings tucked, body stiffened, dropping like a thrown stone with a target at the bottom.","cat_note":null,"kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"A hunting peregrine can spot a pigeon a mile (1.6 km) away — a speck you would need binoculars to find at all.","more":"Deep in each eye is a pit called the fovea, packed with light-detecting cells far more tightly than anything in your eye. It works like a built-in telephoto lens. A third eyelid, clear as glass, sweeps across during the dive to keep the eye wet and clean without blinding the bird.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Peregrines hunt other birds, almost always in the air. Worldwide they have been recorded catching 1,500 to 2,000 different bird species — about a fifth of all the kinds there are.","more":"A peregrine usually strikes with a clenched foot rather than grabbing — a punch delivered at full dive speed. It then wheels around and catches the falling bird out of the air before it ever reaches the ground.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Look at the beak. There is a notch on each side near the tip, called the tomial tooth, and the falcon uses it to sever its prey's spine with one bite.","more":"Hawks and eagles kill mostly by crushing with their feet. A falcon catches with the feet and finishes with the beak, and that notch fits between two neck bones like a key into a lock.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"Peregrine pairs sometimes hunt as a team. One flies low to panic a flock up into the open; the other is already waiting above it, ready to drop.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Peregrines nest on skyscraper ledges, bridges and cathedral towers. To a falcon, a high-rise is just a cliff that happens to have people inside it.","more":"Cities suit them: tall perches, warm updrafts rising off the streets, and enormous flocks of pigeons. Many city pairs are watched on live nest cameras, so you can follow the chicks — called eyases — from a school computer.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"The name peregrine means wanderer. Some nest on Arctic tundra and spend the winter in South America, crossing the length of two continents twice every year.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Peregrines build no nest at all. The female scrapes a shallow dish into gravel or dirt with her chest and feet, and lays her eggs straight into it.","more":"The dish is called a scrape, and a pair will use the same ledge over and over. Chicks hatch covered in white down and are flying about six weeks later. Young birds make their first migration with no parent to follow.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"The female is the bigger one — up to about 30% larger than the male. Falconers call the male a tiercel, from an old word meaning a third.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"A peregrine pair will fly straight at an eagle, a fox or a person that comes too near the nest, screaming, and strike from behind.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Peregrines once vanished from the entire eastern United States. A pesticide called DDT made their eggshells so thin that the eggs cracked. The weight of the parent sitting on them was enough.","more":"The chemical built up in the small birds peregrines ate, growing stronger at every step. Once it was banned, people raised chicks by hand and released them from cliffs and city rooftops. The falcons came back, and now nest in cities that had lost them completely.","kind":"record"}],"wonder":"Eyes that can find a pigeon a mile off, and a body made to get there faster than anything else alive."},{"id":"bald-eagle","name":"Bald eagle","group":"birds","blurb":"A huge fish-hunting sea eagle with a snow-white head, found only in North America.","size":"Wings up to 7 feet 7 inches (2.3 m) across — wider than a tall man is high.","homes":["freshwater","forest","ocean"],"stats":{"speed":40,"weight":10,"length":3.0,"life":20},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"Stretch a bald eagle's wings out and they span up to 7 feet 7 inches (2.3 m) — wider than a tall man standing up.","more":"The female is the bigger one, averaging about 12 pounds (5.5 kg). That sounds heavy until you weigh the parts. One bald eagle was taken apart and weighed piece by piece. Its feathers came to more than twice the weight of the whole skeleton. The bones are hollow, braced inside with tiny struts.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Bald eagles build the largest tree nest of any bird. The biggest ever measured was 9 and a half feet (2.9 m) across and 20 feet (6.1 m) deep.","more":"A pair adds sticks to the same nest year after year until it becomes a tower. A big nest grows so heavy that the branch holding it sometimes snaps under the load.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"More than half of what a bald eagle eats is fish. It skims the surface and snatches one out with its feet, never getting more than its legs wet.","more":"The soles of its toes are covered in rough spikes called spicules. They hold a wet, thrashing fish the way sandpaper holds wood. Eagles also take ducks, gulls and rabbits, and a great deal of carrion. An eagle is perfectly content with a dead salmon on a riverbank.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Bald eagles are champion thieves. An eagle will chase an osprey through the air until the smaller bird drops its fish, then catch the fish before it hits the water.","more":"There is a word for stealing a meal somebody else caught: kleptoparasitism. Benjamin Franklin disliked the eagle partly for this habit. He wrote that it is \"a Bird of bad moral Character.\"","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A bald eagle's head is not white until its fifth year. Young ones are blotchy brown all over and get mistaken for golden eagles constantly.","more":"Each year the brown fades and more white comes through, so you can roughly age a young eagle by how patchy its head and tail still look. The beak shifts from dark gray to bright yellow over the same stretch.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"That fierce scream you hear in films is not an eagle. A bald eagle's real voice is a thin, chirping whistle, a bit like a squeaky gull.","more":"Film-makers almost always dub in the cry of a red-tailed hawk instead, because the eagle's own call sounds too weak for the way the bird looks. Once you know this, you will catch the swap in nearly every movie.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"An eagle's eyesight is about four times sharper than yours. It can pick out a fish just under the surface from high above a lake.","more":"Each eye has two foveas — the tiny pits where vision is sharpest — while you have one. That gives an eagle a straight-ahead view and a sideways view, both in full detail, at the same moment.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Its rear talon is about 2 inches (5 cm) long, longer than your thumb, and it lands with the whole weight of the bird driving it in.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"The name has nothing to do with baldness. Bald here comes from an old word meaning white-headed — the same sense that hides inside the word piebald.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"Courting pairs lock talons high in the air. They cartwheel toward the ground together, spinning. They let go only at the last second.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Bald eagles live in North America and nowhere else on Earth. Nobody has counted them all; surveys put Alaska alone at around 30,000.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"record","text":"Bald eagles nearly disappeared from the United States. The pesticide DDT thinned their eggshells until eggs broke under the parent sitting on them.","more":"The chemical washed off fields into rivers. It built up in fish. It concentrated in the eagles that ate those fish. DDT was banned and nests were given legal protection. Eagles returned so strongly that they were taken off the endangered species list.","kind":"record"}],"wonder":"Eyes to find the fish, feet to hold it, wings to carry it home — every piece of the eagle is fitted to the same job."},{"id":"barn-owl","name":"Barn owl","group":"birds","blurb":"A pale, heart-faced owl that catches mice in total darkness using nothing but its hearing.","size":"A yard (0.9 m) of wing from tip to tip, yet it weighs only about 1 lb (0.5 kg).","homes":["grassland","backyard","forest"],"stats":{"weight":1,"length":1.1,"life":4},"facts":[{"cat":"senses","text":"Put a barn owl in a room with no light at all — none whatsoever — and it will still catch a mouse. It does it on hearing alone.","more":"This was tested in a completely light-proof room, and the owl struck accurately every time. When the mouse dragged a rustling scrap of paper behind it, the owl aimed at the paper. It was hunting the sound, not the animal.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"One of a barn owl's ear openings sits higher on its head than the other. That tiny mismatch tells it whether a sound came from above or below.","more":"Your two ears sit level, so you judge left and right easily but up and down badly. The barn owl's lopsided ears give it height as well as direction. It hears in three dimensions, and can place a mouse under grass or snow it has never laid eyes on.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"That heart-shaped face is not decoration. It is a dish of stiff feathers that funnels sound into the ears, and the owl can change its shape to focus.","more":"Take those stiff face feathers away and the owl can still tell left from right but loses up and down completely. Try cupping both hands behind your ears and turning your head slowly — quiet sounds jump forward. That is roughly what a barn owl wears on its face all the time.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"copied","text":"A barn owl flies without making a sound. Comb-like teeth along the leading edge of its flight feathers break the rushing air apart before it can turn into noise.","more":"The back edge of the wing is fringed like a soft brush, and the whole surface is velvety, soaking up sound. Engineers have copied that comb onto fan blades and wind turbines to quieten them. The mouse never hears it coming — and neither does the owl, so its own wings never drown out the sound it is listening for.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"A barn owl swallows a mouse whole and head first — fur, bones and all. One owl eats three or four small mammals in a night.","more":"Over a year that comes to roughly a thousand rodents from a single owl, and far more from a pair feeding hungry chicks. Farmers put up nest boxes for exactly this reason. A barn owl family beats any trap ever built.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"It cannot digest fur and bone. Once or twice a day it coughs up a neat gray pellet. The pellet is packed with the skeletons of everything it ate.","more":"Pull one apart with tweezers and you can rebuild whole mouse skulls, jaws with the teeth still seated in them, and the hair-thin leg bones of shrews. Scientists count pellet bones to work out what lives in a field without setting a single trap.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Barn owls do not hoot. They make a long, ragged scream — the sound most people think belongs in a horror film, which is really a bird calling for its mate.","more":"The classic twit-twoo hooting belongs to tawny owls, not barn owls. A barn owl also hisses and clicks its tongue. A hungry chick makes a snoring noise loud enough to hear from outside the barn.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"An owl's eyes are not balls. They are tubes, locked in place by rings of bone, so it cannot swivel them at all — to look sideways it must turn its whole head.","more":"That is why an owl has 14 bones in its neck when you have 7. It can turn its head about 270 degrees, three quarters of a full circle, without shifting its body. The bony channels its neck arteries run through are roughly ten times wider than the arteries. There is slack to spare, so the twisting never cuts off blood to the head.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Barn owls live on every continent except Antarctica. Almost no other land bird is found across so much of the world.","more":"They roost in barns, church towers, hollow trees and cliff holes — anywhere dark, dry and high up. They build nothing. The female lays her eggs straight onto a bed of her own old pellets, trodden flat.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Barn owl chicks hatch days apart, so one nest holds a big fluffy chick beside one still tiny and pink. When food runs short, the older ones survive.","more":"The female starts sitting on the first egg immediately instead of waiting for the whole clutch. The eggs hatch in order rather than together. Researchers have watched older chicks pass food down to their smaller siblings. Very few birds have ever been recorded doing that.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"Pick one up and the weight is a shock. A bird a yard (0.9 m) across the wings weighs only about 1 lb (0.5 kg).","more":"Light bones and enormous soft feathers make a big, slow wing that costs almost nothing to carry. It lets a barn owl drift over a field at walking pace, listening, instead of having to keep its speed up just to stay in the air.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"A face shaped like a dish, ears set crooked on purpose, wings that make no sound — every part of a barn owl is tuned to one job: hearing a mouse in the dark."},{"id":"snowy-owl","name":"Snowy owl","group":"birds","blurb":"A huge white owl of the Arctic that hunts in broad daylight and nests on bare ground.","size":"Wings about 5 feet (1.5 m) across — wider than you are tall.","homes":["polar","grassland","ocean"],"stats":{"weight":4,"length":2.0,"life":10},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"This is the heaviest owl in North America. A big female weighs around 4 and a half pounds (2 kg) — like carrying a large bottle of water on your arm.","more":"A lot of that weight is insulation. Snowy owls are among the most thickly feathered of all owls, covered right down over the toes. They sit out cold that would finish an unprotected animal in minutes.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Its toes are buried under feathers averaging an inch and a third (33 mm) long. The next-best owl, the great horned, manages barely half an inch. It walks on snow in built-in boots.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"Most owls hunt at night. In an Arctic summer the sun never sets, so a snowy owl hunts in full daylight — and keeps the habit all year round.","more":"Above the Arctic Circle there is no darkness at all for weeks, so a night hunter would simply starve. Snowy owls perch on a hummock or a fence post in bright sun, sitting perfectly still for hours, and watch.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"A snowy owl eats mostly lemmings, small round Arctic rodents. An adult swallows three to five in a day, and in a hard freeze may need seven just to stay warm.","more":"Over a year that runs well past a thousand lemmings for one owl. Small ones go down whole and the bones come back up later as a pellet. When lemmings are scarce, the owls switch to ptarmigan, hares, ducks, and even other birds of prey.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"How many eggs a snowy owl lays depends on how many lemmings there are. In a lean year, five. In a lemming boom, eleven.","more":"Very few birds size their family to the food supply like this. In the worst years a pair may not nest at all. The eggs hatch days apart, so one nest can hold a chick the size of your fist next to one nearly ready to fly.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"There are no trees on the tundra, so a snowy owl nests on the ground. The female scrapes a hollow on a low rise where she can see anything coming.","more":"The rise matters twice over — it drains meltwater away from the eggs and gives her a clear view in every direction. She sits so tightly that snow can drift across her back without moving her.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Both parents attack anything that approaches the nest, including Arctic foxes. The male dive-bombs from above while the female screams and lunges from the ground.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"Geese and other tundra birds nest close to a snowy owl. The owl drives every fox off the hill. Their eggs sit safe inside its bad temper. The geese pick the spot for exactly that reason.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"Males grow paler as they age; females keep dark brown flecks and bars. On open snow, an old male can be almost impossible to see until he moves.","more":"A female on the nest is barred brown and gray, which hides her against rock and lichen rather than snow. The palest old males end up looking nearly pure white from a distance.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"A snowy owl will sit on one low mound for hours without twitching, watching an empty white plain. The instant a lemming shows itself, it drops in one silent glide.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"Some winters, snowy owls pour south in enormous numbers and turn up on farm fields, beaches and airport runways far from home. These sudden arrivals are called irruptions.","more":"An airport runway is flat, open and treeless. For hundreds of miles it is the closest thing to tundra, so the owls settle there. Bird crews trap them and move them safely away from the planes. Irruptions have carried snowy owls as far south as Texas and Georgia.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Snowy owls do not always spend winter on land. Some sit out on floating sea ice for months, hunting ducks and seabirds over open ocean.","more":"Tracking has followed birds far out on the northern seas, well away from any shore. They perch at the edges of leads — long cracks of open water in the ice — where sea ducks come up to breathe, and take them there.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Boots made of feather, eyes built for daylight, and a family size that rises and falls with the lemmings — the snowy owl is fitted for a place almost nothing else can live."},{"id":"wandering-albatross","name":"Wandering albatross","group":"birds","blurb":"A giant seabird of the Southern Ocean with the longest wings of any living bird. It can spend years at sea without touching land.","size":"The longest wings of any bird alive — up to 11 feet (3.5 m) tip to tip.","homes":["ocean","polar"],"stats":{"speed":50,"weight":19,"length":3.9,"life":30},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"No living bird has longer wings. A wandering albatross measures up to 11 feet (3.5 m) from tip to tip — lie down twice, head to toe, and you would still fall short.","more":"The longest wing ever properly measured on one was about 12 feet (3.7 m). Longer spans get reported from time to time, but not one has ever been verified. An ordinary bird, around 10 feet (3 m), still spans more than most doorways are wide, twice over.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"An albatross can cover 600 miles (1,000 km) in a day without flapping once. It does not really fly — it falls forwards through the wind, over and over.","more":"The trick is called dynamic soaring. Wind moves slower right at the water's surface than it does higher up. The bird climbs into the fast air, turns, and dives back down into the slow air. Every swap steals it a little speed. It can keep this going for hours.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"A sheet of tendon locks its shoulder when the wing is fully out, so the wing stays open with no muscle working at all. Gliding costs it almost nothing.","more":"Heart rates measured on gliding albatrosses sit barely above resting. Holding those enormous wings open is the hard part, and the lock does it for free. Crossing an entire ocean is closer, in effort, to sitting still than to flying.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"It drinks seawater. A gland above each eye strains the salt out and dribbles it down grooves in the beak, so you can watch brine drip off an albatross's nose.","more":"The liquid it sheds is saltier than the sea it drank. Albatrosses are tubenoses, named for the two tubes running along the top of the bill. Petrels, shearwaters and fulmars share the same tube-nosed build.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"An albatross finds its food by smell rather than sight. It can pick up a dead squid floating on the water from several miles downwind.","more":"The scent it follows is dimethyl sulfide, a gas released wherever tiny sea creatures are being eaten. It is the smell of the sea itself. Where that gas rises, food is happening, and the albatross flies the trail upwind until it arrives.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Squid is the main meal, with fish and crustaceans besides. An albatross takes most of it right at the surface, often at night, sitting on the water and seizing what floats up.","more":"It cannot dive deep. So it feeds off the top few feet of an ocean that runs miles down. The whole problem is finding the exact patch of sea where food has come up, which is why the nose matters more than the eyes.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"A parent flying home from a thousand miles out does not carry a fish. It carries a rich orange oil in its own stomach, and pours it straight into the chick.","more":"Tubenoses turn what they eat into concentrated stomach oil. That is far more energy for far less weight. It is the only way a meal survives a journey that long. The chick gets a few enormous deliveries instead of many small ones, and can wait days between them.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A pair raises one chick, and only one. It takes so long — the chick sits on the nest for close to nine months — that the parents cannot breed again the following year.","more":"The egg alone takes about 11 weeks to hatch. Then the chick sits alone on a windswept island right through the Antarctic winter. Both parents range thousands of miles for food. They come back every few days to fill it up.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"A well-fed albatross chick can end up heavier than either of its parents — then it thins back down before it can fly.","more":"Chicks have been weighed at over 35 pounds (16 kg), heavier than any adult. The extra is stored fat that keeps it alive when a parent is late back. It burns that off while its flight feathers finish growing in.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"A young albatross leaves its island and does not touch land again for years. It lives entirely on the wing and the water until it returns to find a mate.","more":"Wandering albatrosses do not begin breeding until they are somewhere between eleven and fifteen years old. Many live past 50. A bird soaring over the Southern Ocean right now may have been at sea since before you were born.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"Pairs stay together for life. Meeting again after months apart, they dance — bills clacking, wings thrown wide, heads tipped back, screaming at the sky.","more":"Young birds spend years practising this dance with different partners before settling on one. The routine has set moves, and each pair works out its own version. Once they choose, they may raise chicks together for decades.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"Tracking tags have followed single albatrosses right around Antarctica. Some circle the whole Southern Ocean three times in one year — over 75,000 miles (120,000 km).","more":"One tracked bird covered 3,700 miles (6,000 km) in twelve days, wider than the Atlantic Ocean, without once setting foot on anything solid. The wind down there blows the same way around the whole globe, and the albatross simply leans on it.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"God set the albatross in the emptiest place on earth and gave it wings that lock open, so the wind does nearly all the work."},{"id":"bar-tailed-godwit","name":"Bar-tailed godwit","group":"birds","blurb":"A long-billed wading bird. It holds the record for the longest non-stop flight of any animal.","size":"About 1.3 ft (40 cm) long and just under 1 lb (400 g), with long legs and a long bill.","homes":["polar","ocean","grassland"],"stats":{"weight":0.9,"length":1.3,"life":20,"speed":35},"facts":[{"cat":"record","text":"One godwit flew from Alaska to Tasmania without landing once: 8,435 miles (13,575 km) in 11 days. No stopping, no feeding, no sleeping on the ground.","more":"The bird was tracked by a tiny satellite tag. It was a juvenile, making the journey for the first time, with no adult to follow.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Before setting off, a godwit shrinks its own stomach, liver and kidneys to save weight, and grows its flight muscles instead.","more":"It cannot eat on the way, so digestive organs are dead weight. Roughly half the take-off weight is fuel — fat packed on before departure.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"It waits for a storm. Godwits launch on the tail of a weather front and ride the wind south, letting the sky do some of the work.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"They fly the whole way over open ocean. There is nowhere to rest, and nobody has watched what becomes of one that comes down — the best read is that it does not get up again.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"senses","text":"The bill tip is packed with pressure sensors, so a probing godwit can feel a buried worm move without seeing it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"On the mudflats it eats worms, clams and shellfish, feeding almost around the clock to build up fat before migrating.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"They nest on open Arctic tundra in a simple scrape. Chicks feed themselves within hours of hatching.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"It summers on Arctic tundra. It winters on the mudflats of New Zealand and Australia. Those are two places about as far apart as land allows.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"Females are noticeably larger than males and have longer bills. The best read of the difference is that a pair can then feed at different depths in the same mud.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Flocks keep in contact with a sharp kew-kew call, which is how a scattered group re-forms after being flushed.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Eleven days over open ocean, on a first attempt, with nobody to follow.","_units":"imperial"},{"id":"arctic-tern","name":"Arctic tern","group":"birds","blurb":"A small silver seabird that follows summer from one end of the planet to the other, every single year.","size":"Smaller than a pigeon, with long narrow wings and a deeply forked tail.","homes":["polar","ocean"],"stats":{"weight":0.24,"length":1.1,"life":30,"speed":25},"facts":[{"cat":"record","text":"The Arctic tern migrates farther than any other animal. Tracked birds cover about 44,000 miles (71,000 km) in a year, following a looping path rather than a straight line.","more":"It breeds in the Arctic summer, then flies to Antarctica for the southern summer. Over a 30-year life that adds up to further than three round trips to the Moon.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Chasing summer at both ends of the world, an Arctic tern sees more daylight in a year than any other creature alive. Nobody has followed one with a light meter; the tracked route is the argument.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"food","text":"It hunts by hovering, then plunging headfirst into the sea. It takes small fish and krill. Often it comes up with the catch crosswise in its bill.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Terns defend the nest by dive-bombing intruders and striking the top of the head with that sharp red bill. Arctic researchers wear hats for a reason.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Nests are just a scrape on gravel or sand. Chicks are speckled and sit still when a parent calls a warning, vanishing against the stones.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"Terns ride prevailing winds rather than flying straight, taking a huge S-shaped route down the Atlantic. It is longer in miles, and worked out from the winds it is the easier road.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"home","text":"They breed in colonies right around the Arctic, on coasts, islands and tundra. Other seabirds often nest alongside them.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Colonies are extremely loud. There is a constant harsh kee-yah. It rises to a roar when any predator approaches.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"Wings span up to 30 in (76 cm) on a bird weighing under 4 oz (110 g), which is what makes such long-distance gliding possible.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"When one tern sounds the alarm, the entire colony lifts at once — hundreds of birds mobbing a single fox or gull.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"A bird that weighs less than a cup of water, and spends its life following the light.","_units":"imperial"},{"id":"emperor-penguin","name":"Emperor penguin","group":"birds","blurb":"The largest penguin alive, and the only bird that breeds through the Antarctic winter.","size":"Chest-high on a nine-year-old — about 3 ft 9 in (1.15 m) standing up.","homes":["polar","ocean"],"stats":{"weight":84,"length":3.8,"life":20,"speed":6},"facts":[{"cat":"babies","text":"The father holds the single egg on his feet, under a fold of belly skin. He does this for about two months of Antarctic winter. He does not eat the whole time.","more":"The mother walks to the sea to feed as soon as she has laid. He stands through blizzards, losing up to half his body weight, and only then does she return to take over and feed the chick.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"Thousands of huddling males shuffle in a slow spiral so nobody stays on the freezing outside edge for long. Everyone gets a turn in the middle.","more":"Filmed from above, the huddle moves in tiny waves. Each bird steps forward a few inches every 30 seconds or so. That ripples through the whole crowd.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Emperors dive deeper than any other bird. One was recorded at 1,854 ft (565 m) down — deeper than four Statues of Liberty stacked up.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"They can stay underwater for over 20 minutes on a single breath. Their bones are solid rather than hollow, and the best read is that the extra weight keeps them from bobbing back up.","more":"Most birds have air-filled bones to save weight in the air. An emperor does not fly at all, so heavy bones cost it nothing and buy it an easier trip down.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"home","text":"Emperor penguins breed on sea ice in temperatures down to −40°F (−40 °C), in wind that would knock you flat.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Every penguin's call is unique. A returning parent finds its own chick by voice alone, in a crowd of thousands.","more":"The chick answers, and the two zero in on each other. Researchers have played recordings to test it — chicks respond to their own parent's call and ignore all the others.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Their feathers are the densest of any bird, packed about 100 to the square inch. They are layered like roof tiles over a thick underlayer of down.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Their eyes work better in dim blue light than in daylight — useful when you hunt hundreds of feet down where almost no sun reaches.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"They eat fish, squid and krill. A day of hunting can put away several pounds. Everything goes down whole, underwater.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"To reach the breeding colony they walk, and sometimes toboggan on their bellies. The trip runs up to 75 miles (120 km) across the ice.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"A chick left alone is fair game for a giant petrel. So unattended chicks bunch into their own small huddles, called crèches.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"A father standing still in the dark for two months, with everything that matters balanced on his feet.","_units":"imperial"},{"id":"ostrich","name":"Ostrich","group":"birds","blurb":"The biggest and fastest bird on Earth — far too heavy to fly, and built entirely for running.","size":"Taller than a grown man: up to 9 ft (2.7 m) to the top of the head.","homes":["grassland","desert"],"stats":{"weight":287,"length":9,"life":40,"speed":43},"facts":[{"cat":"speed","text":"An ostrich can run at 43 mph (70 km/h) and hold about 30 mph (48 km/h) for half an hour. No other two-legged animal comes close.","more":"A single stride can cover 10 to 16 ft (3 to 5 m), all in one step.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"It runs on two toes, and really on one. The big toe carries almost all the weight and even has a hoof-like nail, like a built-in running shoe.","more":"Every other bird alive has three or four toes. The ostrich is the only one with two, and that pared-down foot is part of what makes the stride so efficient.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Ostriches do not bury their heads in sand. That story comes from watching them turn eggs in a shallow nest scraped into the ground.","more":"From a distance, a bird lying flat with its head down on the ground does look headless — which is probably where the rest of the myth came from.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"An ostrich egg weighs about 3 lb (1.4 kg) — as much as two dozen chicken eggs. The shell is strong enough for a grown adult to stand on.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Its eye is about 2 in (5 cm) across, the largest eye of any land animal, and bigger than its own brain.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"A kick forward from those legs can be genuinely dangerous, and each foot carries a claw up to 4 in (10 cm) long.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Ostriches swallow pebbles on purpose. The stones sit in the gizzard and grind up tough plants, doing the job of teeth they do not have.","more":"A single ostrich may carry around 2 lb (1 kg) of stones inside it. Chickens do the same thing on a much smaller scale.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Several females lay in one shared nest. The main female arranges the eggs so hers sit in the safest middle spot.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"They live in African grassland and semi-desert, often alongside grazing zebra and antelope. The grazers are borrowing those extraordinary eyes.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Males boom. It is a deep, hollow, three-note call that carries for miles and sounds far more like a lion than a bird.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Wings that gave up flying and became rudders instead, on the fastest two legs in the world.","_units":"imperial"},{"id":"emu","name":"Emu","group":"birds","blurb":"Australia's tallest bird. It is a shaggy, flightless wanderer, second in size only to the ostrich.","size":"About head-height on an adult, up to 6 ft 3 in (1.9 m).","homes":["grassland","forest","desert"],"stats":{"weight":121,"length":6.2,"life":15,"speed":31},"facts":[{"cat":"babies","text":"The father does all of it. He sits on the eggs for about eight weeks barely eating or drinking, then raises the chicks for up to 18 months.","more":"He can lose a third of his body weight sitting. The striped chicks follow him everywhere, and he will see off anything that comes near them — including the mother.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Emu feathers grow two shafts from a single root, giving a loose shaggy coat. The best read is that it lets heat escape instead of trapping it.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Females make a deep drumming boom you can hear over a mile away. An inflatable pouch in the neck produces it.","more":"Males make a lower grunting sound instead. The booming pouch is a stretchy sac in the windpipe that the bird fills with air like a drum.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"Emus follow the rain. Flocks have been tracked moving hundreds of miles across Australia, searching for food and water.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"speed","text":"An emu sprints at 31 mph (50 km/h). It can change direction mid-run without slowing much. The credit goes to enormous calf muscles.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Emu eggs are dark green, almost black, and about 5 in (13 cm) long. The color hides them in grass and leaf litter.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"They eat seeds, fruit, insects and shoots — and swallow stones to grind it all up. Emus also eat charcoal, and nobody is quite sure why.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Emus live across most of mainland Australia, from forest edges to dry inland plains. They avoid only the thickest rainforest.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"An emu's main defense is running, but it can also kick forward hard enough to tear through a wire fence.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Emus can swim, and will cross a river with their body low in the water and only the head and neck showing.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"A father who spends two months without a proper meal so a clutch of dark green eggs can hatch.","_units":"imperial"},{"id":"kiwi","name":"Kiwi","group":"birds","blurb":"A flightless, chicken-sized bird from New Zealand that hunts at night by smell, poking a long beak into the ground like a probe.","size":"Stands roughly 16 in (40 cm) tall, and weighs about 6 lb (2.7 kg)","homes":["forest","grassland"],"stats":{"weight":6,"length":1.3,"life":30},"wonder":"Not every bird was given wings that work. This one was given a nose instead — and it is enough.","facts":[{"cat":"senses","text":"The kiwi is the only bird on Earth with its nostrils at the very tip of its beak. It sniffs the ground, then drives that beak straight down after a worm.","more":"Most birds have their nostrils up by the face, where the beak meets the head. The kiwi's sit at the far end, right where the digging happens — and when soil clogs them, the kiwi sneezes it out with a snuffly little blast.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Most birds have hollow bones full of air. A kiwi's are heavy and packed with marrow, like yours — one reason it will never leave the ground.","more":"Flight costs weight, so a flying bird keeps its skeleton light and airy. The kiwi is built the opposite way: dense bones, thick muscular legs, and a whole body arranged for walking, digging and probing instead of the air.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"size","text":"A kiwi egg can weigh up to a fifth of the mother's entire body. Imagine carrying a fifth of your own weight inside you.","more":"A brown kiwi egg can reach 1 lb (450 g), about six times a chicken's. By the last few days it fills so much of her that there is no room left for her stomach. She has to stop eating for two or three days.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"In brown kiwi the father does the sitting — seventy-five to ninety days on the egg, close to three months. Most garden birds are finished in two weeks.","more":"The egg carries nearly twice the yolk of most bird eggs its size. That is the chick's packed lunch. It hatches fully feathered, a miniature of its parents, and can live off the yolk still in its belly for well over a week.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"No kiwi chick is ever fed by its parents. About five days after hatching it walks out of the burrow and starts finding its own dinner.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"For its body size, the kiwi has the smallest eyes of any bird. It hardly needs them — it works the night shift entirely by nose, ear and whisker.","more":"Stiff whisker-like feathers ring the base of the beak and feel the way ahead in the dark. Its ear openings are large and open, and a kiwi will cock its head toward a sound just as you do. Wild kiwi that are completely blind have been found living perfectly well.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Kiwi feathers are long, loose and shaggy. They are far closer to coarse hair than to the sleek, zipped-together feathers of a bird that flies.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Somewhere under all that shag is a wing about 1 in (3 cm) long — shorter than your thumb — with a small cat-like claw on the end.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"A kiwi runs cooler than most birds — about 38°C (100°F), close to your own temperature. Most birds idle nearer 40°C (104°F).","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It feeds by tapping slowly along the ground. Then it punches its whole beak down into the leaf litter. It is after earthworms, beetle grubs, centipedes and big crickets called wētā.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"A kiwi carries a strong, musty, mushroomy smell. Trained conservation dogs use it to find them in the bush. Unfortunately, so do introduced predators.","more":"Kiwi also mark their patch with pungent droppings. Conservation teams now work with certified kiwi dogs. The dogs locate birds by scent without ever touching them. Chicks can then be found, checked and protected.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"The name matches the noise. A male gives a shrill rising whistle, repeated fifteen to twenty-five times through the dark; the female answers slower, lower and throatier.","kind":"found"}]},{"id":"bee-hummingbird","name":"Bee hummingbird","group":"birds","blurb":"The smallest bird in the world — a Cuban hummingbird so tiny that people who see one often think a large bee has just gone past.","size":"Thumb-length, and lighter than a US penny","homes":["forest","backyard"],"stats":{"weight":0,"length":0.2,"speed":26,"life":7},"wonder":"Two grams. And inside those two grams, every single thing a bird needs — nothing left out, nothing forgotten.","facts":[{"cat":"record","text":"No bird in the world is smaller. A male weighs about 1.95 grams — less than a US penny — and measures 2 in (5.5 cm) from beak tip to tail tip.","more":"Females are a little bigger, around 2.6 grams and 2.4 in (6.1 cm). Most of that length is beak and tail; the actual body is roughly the size of the end of your thumb. Bees have been mistaken for these birds, and these birds for bees.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"The nest is about 1 in (2.5 cm) across — a cup that would sit on a coin. The eggs inside are the size of coffee beans.","more":"She weaves it from cobwebs, bark and lichen up in a tree, and lays one or two eggs. They hatch after about twenty-one days, and the chicks are flying eighteen days after that.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"A bee hummingbird may visit around 1,500 flowers in a single day, and eat up to half its own body weight.","more":"An engine that small is expensive to run. Nectar is nearly pure sugar — instant fuel — and the bird tops up with tiny insects and spiders for protein. Pollen sticks to its head at every flower and rides to the next one, so it is planting as it feeds.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"speed","text":"Its wings beat about eighty times a second — far too fast for your eye to separate. In a courtship display a male drives them past two hundred.","more":"That hum is where the whole family gets its name, and it is not a voice at all — it is the sound of air being hammered by wings. A camera has to shoot in slow motion before the wing looks like a wing.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The wing pivots right at the shoulder and traces a figure-eight, pushing air down on the forward stroke and the backward one. That is how it hangs still in midair.","more":"Almost every other bird gets lift only on the downstroke. A hummingbird's shoulder swivels so the wing flips over and works both ways — which also lets it fly backwards, straight up, and briefly upside down.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The male's flaring pink-red head is not colored with pigment. The feathers are built in microscopic layers that split light, so the color blazes and vanishes as he turns.","more":"Tilt a soap bubble in sunlight and you see the same trick. There is no pink dye in those feathers at all, only structure. That is why a head that looks like fire from one angle can look flat black from two steps sideways.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"speed","text":"For something lighter than a penny it really moves. Ordinary level flight runs around 25-30 mph (40-48 km/h).","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"On a cold night it powers down. Its body cools, its heart slows to a small fraction of its daytime rate, and it burns almost nothing until dawn.","more":"This is called torpor, and metabolism can fall to roughly a fifteenth of normal. A bird in torpor is cold and stiff and takes several minutes of shivering to restart. Without it, an engine this small would run dry overnight.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"The entire species lives on Cuba and one island beside it, and nowhere else on the planet. That is the only place on Earth to see the smallest bird.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Small is not the same as timid. A male claims a patch of flowers and chases off intruders, including hummingbirds considerably larger than himself.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Its feet are almost useless for walking. It can grip a twig and shuffle sideways along it, but it cannot stride — everything else, it flies to.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"The father takes no part. The female builds, incubates and feeds entirely alone, and about eighteen days after hatching the chicks fly out of that coin-sized cup.","kind":"found"}]},{"id":"ruby-throated-hummingbird","name":"Ruby-throated hummingbird","group":"birds","blurb":"The little green hummingbird of eastern North America. It is the one that hangs at a garden feeder. It is also the only hummingbird that nests right across that half of the continent.","size":"Not much longer than your thumb, and lighter than a five-cent coin","homes":["forest","backyard"],"stats":{"weight":0,"length":0.3,"speed":30,"life":4},"wonder":"Nobody teaches a young ruby-throat the way south. It hatches already knowing.","facts":[{"cat":"travel","text":"Nobody has followed a three-gram bird out over the Gulf of Mexico. The radar and the arrival dates say many cross in one go. That is about 500 miles (800 km) of open water. No land, no rest, roughly twenty hours of flying.","more":"There is nothing to land on for the entire crossing, and no plan B if the weather turns. Not every bird takes the sea route; plenty go the long way round the Texas coast. But the ones that go straight across do it non-stop, weighing three grams.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"food","text":"Before it goes, it eats and eats. Weighed at banding stations, a ruby-throat can roughly double the fat it carries — and that works out to just about exactly what one flight over water would cost.","more":"Fat is the densest fuel a body can carry. It is the only way a three-gram bird can afford twenty hours over the sea. It arrives on the far shore thin and exhausted. Then it immediately starts looking for flowers.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"speed","text":"Its wings beat more than fifty times every second while it hovers. Your eye cannot follow that at all — it registers only a blur and a low hum.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The tongue is forked, and it does not suck. It flicks into the nectar, splits open, then snaps shut around the liquid and hauls it back — a pump, not a straw.","more":"People long assumed nectar simply wicked up the tongue the way water climbs a paper towel. High-speed film showed something better: the two halves of the tongue spring apart inside the liquid and clamp closed as they leave it, trapping nectar in the grooves. It happens many times a second.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The male's throat is not painted red. It is a stack of microscopic layers that fling red light back at you — and from the wrong angle it goes flat black.","more":"This is iridescence: structure rather than pigment, the same reason a soap bubble has colors. He can aim it. During a courtship dive he angles that throat squarely at the female so it detonates into scarlet at the right instant. Females and young males have plain pale throats.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"The nest is thimble-sized, made of plant down and lichen and bound with spider silk. The cup stretches as the chicks grow instead of splitting, and the elastic silk is the best explanation for how.","more":"Spider silk is the trick, because it is elastic. A cup that starts barely wide enough for two pea-sized eggs can expand around two fast-growing chicks without ever being rebuilt. The lichen glued to the outside makes the whole thing look like a knot on the branch.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"food","text":"It is not living on sugar alone. Ruby-throats hunt small insects and spiders, and will even pick prey out of spider webs. That is where the protein comes from.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"On a cold night it shuts down completely. Body temperature falls, the heart slows enormously, and the bird hangs on its twig cold and unresponsive until morning warms it.","more":"This is torpor. A ruby-throat's heart can run past 1,200 beats a minute by day. In torpor it drops to a small fraction of that, and metabolism to roughly a fifteenth of normal. Waking takes several minutes of shivering before it can fly.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"A ruby-throat weighs about three grams. Put eight of them on a scale together and you would only just match one AA battery.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"It is one of very few birds on Earth that can fly properly backwards — and hold a dead-still hover in a breeze while it drinks.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"There are no flocks and no guides. Adults leave weeks before the young birds do, so a young ruby-throat that has never made the journey must be finding Central America without being shown.","more":"The route is not taught. Adults leave weeks before the young birds do. So the first migration is made by a bird a few months old, traveling solo. It navigates by something it was born already carrying.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"home","text":"Ruby-throats come back to the same gardens year after year. Often it is the very same feeder. They arrive within a few days of the same date.","kind":"found"}]},{"id":"kea","name":"Kea","group":"birds","blurb":"A big olive-green parrot that lives in New Zealand's snowy mountains. It is the world's only alpine parrot. It is also one of the cleverest and most destructive birds alive.","size":"Crow-sized but heavier — about 19 in (48 cm) long and roughly a kilogram (2 lb)","homes":["mountain","forest"],"stats":{"weight":2,"length":1.6},"wonder":"A parrot given snow instead of jungle — and a mind restless enough to enjoy it.","facts":[{"cat":"home","text":"Almost every parrot on Earth lives somewhere warm. The kea breeds in New Zealand's Southern Alps at 5,200 ft (1,600 m) and higher — about a mile up, in snow.","more":"Picture a parrot: bright, tropical, sitting in a palm. Now picture that same bird walking across a snowfield in a mountain blizzard, digging through ice for roots. That is a kea, and no other parrot lives this way.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"Heavier than it looks. A big male runs to about 2 lb (1 kg) and roughly 19 in (48 cm) from beak to tail.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Kea take cars apart. They pick the rubber seals out of windscreens, strip wiper blades and unzip backpacks in ski-field car parks. There is no food in any of it, which is the strongest argument that they do it purely to find out what happens.","more":"This is genuine curiosity and it is relentless. Hand a kea an object it has never met and it will bite it, lever at it, pull it apart and flip it over. That same instinct is exactly why kea are so good at solving problems.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Hidden cameras in a South Island valley caught kea breaking into baited stoat traps. More than three-quarters of the traps in that area had been set off.","more":"The traps are steel and spring-loaded, built so a stoat cannot beat them. The kea worked out how to reach the bait anyway. Conservation staff had to redesign the trap boxes to keep the birds out and the stoats catchable.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"A kea named Bruce lost the top half of his beak. At the wildlife park where he lives he learned to wedge a pebble against his tongue and comb his feathers with it.","more":"Without an upper beak, preening should be impossible. Keepers and researchers watched him pick pebbles of a particular size. He held them in place and used them. When he dropped one, he went after it to carry on. He appears to have worked the trick out entirely on his own.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"A kea eats more than forty kinds of plant, digs fat beetle grubs out of rotting wood, and will strip flesh and marrow off a dead animal. Very little is off the menu.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Kea have been filmed landing on live sheep and using that hooked beak to cut through the wool and feed on the fat underneath.","more":"It is rare, and most kea never do it, but it is real — farmers argued about it for generations before video settled the question. Kea were shot under a government bounty because of it. They are fully protected now, and only three to seven thousand remain.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The upper beak is long, narrow and heavily hooked — closer to a crowbar than a nutcracker. It works as chisel, lever, and a third foot for climbing.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Kea nest underground, in rock crevices and tunnels under tree roots. Some of those tunnels run six meters (20 ft) back into the mountainside — longer than a car.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Play a recording of the kea's warbling call to wild birds. They break off what they are doing and start to play. They chase, tumble and throw things about.","more":"Researchers left the recording running for several minutes. Birds that heard it went off and played, often alone rather than with each other, and kept going after the sound stopped. Young kea, who roam in loose gangs of up to thirteen, play hardest of all.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"A kea will sit and wait as long as 160 seconds — nearly three minutes — for a better reward rather than take a worse one immediately. Very few animals hold out that long.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"On the ground a kea is drab olive-green and easy to miss against rock and scrub. Then it opens its wings and the undersides flash brilliant orange.","kind":"found"}]},{"id":"new-caledonian-crow","name":"New Caledonian crow","group":"birds","blurb":"A glossy black crow from one Pacific island. It makes hooked tools out of twigs. Very few animals besides people manufacture a hook.","size":"About as long as your forearm — roughly 16 in (40 cm) beak to tail","homes":["rainforest","forest"],"stats":{"weight":1,"length":1.3},"wonder":"A mind that can look at a plain forked twig and see a hook that isn't there yet.","facts":[{"cat":"record","text":"This crow snips a forked twig off a branch, strips the leaves, and carves the fork down into a neat little hook. Then it goes fishing with it.","more":"Making a hook is a serious business. It means the bird is not just picking up a handy stick — it is reshaping an object for a job it has not started yet. Outside of humans, almost nothing on Earth manufactures a hook.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"What it is fishing for is a fat beetle grub jammed deep inside rotting wood. The crow pokes the hook in, annoys the grub until it bites the stick, then pulls it out still holding on.","more":"The grub's own jaws do half the work. A crow will spend a long time on a single hole, jiggling, twisting, listening. One big grub is worth a great deal of energy, so the patience pays.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The pandanus plant has tough, saw-edged leaves. From them a crow cuts long barbed strips. It cuts in steps, wide at the base and narrow at the tip, so the tool tapers.","more":"The strip is torn out so precisely that it leaves a matching hole behind in the leaf. Researchers can walk the forest reading those holes like signatures. They count how many tools were made and what shape each one was, without ever seeing a bird.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Young crows are hopeless at first. They watch their parents, steal their parents' tools, and fumble with twigs for the better part of a year before they can make a good one.","more":"Even a young crow raised without ever seeing an adult will start poking sticks into holes. The urge comes built in. But skill takes practice, and tool designs differ across the island. Crows in one district cut one pattern and crows in another cut a different one. Each youngster grows up copying the local shape.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Most birds have eyes on the sides of the head and a curved beak. This crow has an unusually straight bill and unusually forward-facing eyes. Nobody can ask it why; the shape is the argument, and it lines the tool tip up right where both eyes can watch it.","more":"Hold a pencil in your fist and look down at the point — you need both eyes to judge exactly where it is. The crow's straight bill lines the tool up along its line of sight, and its wide overlapping field of view lets it aim the tip into a hole.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"weird","text":"A crow that has made a good tool does not drop it. It tucks it under a foot or wedges it into a hole while it eats, then takes it to the next log.","more":"Good tools take real time to make, and one dropped from high in a tree is gone. Crows are noticeably more careful about stowing a tool when they are working up in the canopy than when they are down on the ground.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"A captive crow named Betty was given a straight piece of wire. Food sat in a bucket at the bottom of a tube. She bent the wire into a hook and lifted the bucket out.","more":"Wire is not something a crow meets in a forest. She jammed one end against something solid and pulled the other into shape. Researchers later found wild crows bending twigs into hooks too — so she was not inventing hooks from nothing, but she applied the idea to a brand-new material.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Show one a tube of water with a floating treat out of reach, and a pile of stones. It drops stones in until the water carries the treat up to it.","more":"On this puzzle the crows scored about level with a five- to seven-year-old child. They also sorted which objects sink and which float, and preferred a narrow tube to a wide one because the water rises faster. Some versions still beat them — but the core idea is clearly there.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Some puzzles need three moves in order: use a short stick to reach a longer stick, then use the long stick to reach the food. Crows have solved these on the first attempt.","more":"The first move earns no reward at all — only a better tool. To make it, the bird has to hold the whole sequence in mind before it starts. Six of seven birds in one test got it right the very first time, with nobody training them through the steps.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"In one experiment crows were given short hollow rods and thin sticks, none long enough alone. Several slotted the pieces together into a pole long enough to reach the food.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"It is not a big bird — forearm-length, about 10 oz (300 g), lighter than a can of soup. Every tool it makes, it makes with a beak and two feet.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"The whole species lives on New Caledonia, a group of islands in the South Pacific, mostly in old undisturbed forest. There is nowhere else on Earth to find one.","kind":"found"}]},{"id":"african-grey-parrot","name":"African gray parrot","group":"birds","blurb":"A dust-gray parrot from the forests of central Africa. It has a scarlet tail and a mind that solves problems most animals cannot.","size":"About as long as your forearm, and light enough to sit on your hand all afternoon without tiring it","homes":["rainforest","forest"],"stats":{"weight":1,"length":1.1,"life":23},"facts":[{"cat":"talk","text":"A gray parrot named Alex learned more than a hundred English words. He used them to answer questions he had never been asked, about objects he had never seen.","more":"Alex worked with the scientist Irene Pepperberg for thirty years. Shown a tray of objects, he could say how many were green, name the material a key was made of, and pick the larger of two things. Not tricks on cue. New questions, new answers.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Parrots have no vocal cords. A gray shapes sound in its syrinx — a voice box down where the windpipe forks — then moves its thick tongue to sculpt it, exactly as you do.","more":"That tongue is the difference. Most birds cannot move theirs much. A parrot's is muscular and blunt. Shifting it forward or back changes the shape of the space the sound travels through. A raw buzz turns into a word.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Nobody taught Alex a word for zero. Asked about an amount that simply was not in front of him, he borrowed a word he already had and answered: none.","more":"That is harder than counting. Counting means matching a word to things you can see. Saying none means naming an amount that is not there at all — and Alex was never trained to do it. He carried the word over from another task himself.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"In one experiment, a gray parrot handed metal tokens through a hole to a neighboring parrot so the neighbor could buy a walnut. The helper got nothing, and kept handing them over.","more":"The birds could not eat the tokens. They were only useful traded to a researcher for food. When the neighbor had no way to spend them, the helping mostly stopped. The parrots seemed to understand what the tokens were actually for.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"A gray parrot's brain is smaller than a walnut. Counted under a microscope, the neurons inside are packed far tighter than in a mammal brain the same size. That is the best answer anyone has for how so much thinking fits in so little room.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"senses","text":"A gray parrot learns which human voice belongs to which human face, and stares longer when the wrong voice comes out of the wrong person. That stare is the whole evidence, and the best read of it is a bird tracking individuals, not just sounds.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"build","text":"Look at the feet: two toes forward, two toes back. That grip is called zygodactyl, and it turns a foot into a hand. Grays lift food to the bill one-footed, and most favor the same foot every time.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Fruit, nuts, seeds and oily palm fruit, plus bark, flowers and the occasional snail. That bill can crack a shell you would need a hammer for, then peel out the kernel without crushing it.","more":"The upper bill hinges against the skull instead of being fixed to it, so the whole tip swings. The bird can bear down hard for the crack and then work delicately with the very point — one tool, two settings.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Three to five white eggs in a hole high in a tree. Thirty days of sitting, then about twelve weeks before the chicks leave — far longer than most birds stay home.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"They roost in noisy flocks of hundreds in tall forest trees. At dawn they break into small groups. They fly out along the same routes to the same feeding trees.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Shake two closed cups in front of a gray parrot. Only one rattles, and the parrot picks it. Shake the empty one instead, and it picks the other — the silent one.","more":"This is called inference by exclusion, and very few animals manage it. The parrot never sees the food. It works out where the food must be from where the food is not, on sound alone, without looking inside anything.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Nobody has followed a wild gray from hatching to death. Worked out from birds of known age, a wild life comes to around twenty-three years. Kept safe and well fed, one can reach sixty — old enough to outlive the child who first met it.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"home","text":"Gray parrots are Endangered. So many were trapped in the wild for the pet trade that the international wildlife treaty stepped in. It banned commercial trade in wild-caught birds altogether.","kind":"record"}],"wonder":"A bird that answers a question nobody taught it, and hands a token to a neighbor it gains nothing at all from helping."},{"id":"lyrebird","name":"Superb lyrebird","group":"birds","blurb":"A chicken-sized Australian songbird. It copies the sounds of other birds so exactly you cannot hear the join.","size":"About 3.3 ft (1 m) long in all; the tail alone is 28 in (70 cm) of that","homes":["forest","rainforest","mountain"],"stats":{"weight":2,"length":3.3},"facts":[{"cat":"copied","text":"Seven or eight out of every ten sounds a male makes are borrowed from other birds. He is not really singing his own song. He is playing back the forest.","more":"He does not stitch them into mush, either. He runs one species cleanly, stops, and starts another. Kookaburra, whipbird, rosella, currawong. Each one is held long enough to be recognized, then dropped for the next.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"copied","text":"At the moment of mating the male makes a sound no single bird should be able to make. It is the panicked racket of a whole flock mobbing a predator. Several species at once, out of one throat.","more":"Researchers recorded it and lined it up against real mobbing flocks. It matches closely enough to fool the ear. Males produced it at two moments only — when a female tried to leave the display, and during mating itself.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"Songbird is a real scientific group, and this is one of its giants. Most songbirds fit in your palm. This one is chicken-sized, and the tail doubles it again.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The lyre-shaped tail is sixteen feathers. Two curved outer ones look like the arms of a harp. Twelve fine silvery filaments sit between them. Two thin ribbons run down the middle.","more":"The whole thing can reach 28 in (70 cm) — longer than the bird's own body. Females have a tail too, but plainer and shorter. Only the male grows the full instrument, and he sheds it and grows it again.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"During the display he throws the entire tail forward over his head. He vanishes under a shimmering silver canopy, and only the sound comes out.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Each male scrapes together a low mound of bare earth on the forest floor, climbs on top of it, and sings from there. He built the stage himself, and he comes back to it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"In midwinter, when most of the forest has gone quiet, a male may sing for four hours a day — nearly half the daylight there is.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"He rakes the forest floor with his feet all day long. On a patch of forest the size of two football fields, lyrebirds turn over as much as 220 tons (200 tonnes) of soil and leaf litter in a year.","more":"That is the weight of roughly thirty elephants, shifted by birds using nothing but their feet. The scratching mixes dead leaves down into the soil and clears away the loose dry litter that lets a bushfire run.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Worms, grubs, beetles, spiders, centipedes. He hooks them out from under the leaf litter with long strong claws, which is exactly why he does all that raking.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"One egg. Just one, once a year. The female builds the domed nest, sits on it for up to seven weeks — a very long wait for a songbird — and raises the chick entirely alone.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"copied","text":"Females copy too, which people missed for years. They use borrowed calls while foraging, and again when driving a predator away from the nest.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"It barely flies. It runs, hops, and scrambles up branch by branch to roost at night, then launches off in the morning and glides down like a paper plane.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"One throat given every voice in the forest, and one foot that turns the whole forest floor over while it sings."},{"id":"peacock","name":"Indian peafowl","group":"birds","blurb":"The blue-and-green bird of India whose male grows a fan of shimmering eyespots taller than a doorway.","size":"With the train trailing behind him, a male runs 79-91 in (200-230 cm) (6.5-7.5 ft) — longer than a doorway is tall","homes":["forest","grassland"],"stats":{"weight":10,"length":7.2,"life":15},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"That fan is not his tail. It is a cape of hugely overgrown feathers that grow above the tail. The real tail is short, stiff and plain brown. It hides behind the fan and props the whole thing upright.","more":"The train feathers are called upper tail coverts. On most birds, coverts are the small tidy feathers covering the base of the tail. On a peacock they have run away with themselves, and the actual tail is left doing the shoving.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"There is no blue or green dye anywhere in that train. The color is built, not painted: stacked microscopic rods inside each barbule bounce back only certain wavelengths of light.","more":"It is the trick of a soap bubble, or an oil slick on a puddle. Crush a train feather and the color dies with it. What is left is brown. The color lived in the shape, and you have just destroyed the shape.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"When he shivers the train it rattles about 25 times a second. High-speed film shows the eyespots holding almost still while every loose barb around them blurs.","more":"Each eyespot sits on its own shaft, its barbules hooked tightly together into one stiff disc. The looser feathery barbs surrounding it swing much further. The result is a field of steady eyes floating on a shaking sea of color.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Cameras that track where a peahen actually looks found she mostly ignores the top of the fan. Close up she watches the bottom edge; only from a distance does she take in the whole thing.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Guides tracking tigers in India listen for peafowl. A peacock that has spotted a big cat screams from its perch, and every animal within earshot learns where the tiger is.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"He drops the entire train every year after the monsoon and grows a whole new one by spring. For a few months, the most spectacular bird in India looks like a plain blue chicken.","more":"Growing that much feather takes real food and real time. The train is not fully grown until he is about four, so a young male spends his first seasons displaying with a stubby, unimpressive version of it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"Yes, he can fly. Dragging all of that, he still hauls himself up into a tall tree every evening to sleep — slow, deliberate wingbeats, no gliding.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Grain, seeds, flower buds, berries, insects — and small reptiles. A peafowl will take a lizard without hesitating. That elegant bird is a hunter when it wants to be.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"That famous scream, a harsh may-awe, is only one of his sounds. Researchers listening carefully sorted out about seven different calls, plus six more used only for alarm.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Three to eight eggs in a scrape on the ground, sat on for twenty-eight to thirty days. The chicks can walk and follow their mother within hours of hatching.","more":"Birds that hatch ready to run are called nidifugous — nest-fleeing. It is a very different childhood from a parrot's, where chicks sit blind in a tree hole for weeks. Peachicks are up and after their mother almost immediately.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Wild peafowl belong to India and Sri Lanka: dry forest, farmland edges, riverbanks. India chose it as the national bird. In many villages it simply walks about among people.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"copied","text":"Engineers study those feathers on purpose. Color made from structure never fades the way dye does, so labs build peacock-style nanostructures for inks and paints meant to keep their color for good.","kind":"record"}],"wonder":"Not one drop of dye in the whole train. The color is written into the shape of the feather itself."},{"id":"flamingo","name":"Greater flamingo","group":"birds","blurb":"A tall pink wading bird that eats with its head held upside down in water too salty for almost anything else.","size":"Up to 5 ft (150 cm) tall — taller than you are — yet no heavier than a house cat","homes":["freshwater","ocean"],"stats":{"speed":35,"weight":7,"length":4.3,"life":35},"facts":[{"cat":"food","text":"It feeds with its head upside down. The bill is a strainer. The bird sweeps it through the water and the fat tongue pumps like a piston. Comb-like ridges called lamellae hold back the shrimp and algae.","more":"In almost every animal, the lower jaw is the one that moves. A flamingo's upper jaw swings instead — which is why the bill looks like it was put on backwards. Turned upside down in the water, it is exactly the right way up.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The pink is eaten, not born. Brine shrimp and algae carry orange pigments called carotenoids; the flamingo digests them and lays the color into growing feathers. Fed the wrong food, a flamingo goes white.","more":"They are the same family of pigments that make carrots orange. Before nesting, a flamingo goes further. It rubs oil from a gland near its tail all through its feathers, pigment and all. It is brightening itself on purpose, like putting on make-up.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Both parents make milk. Not from a chest — from glands lining the throat and gullet, and it pours out bright red. It is loaded with fat and protein.","more":"Making it drains the parents' own color straight out of their feathers. So a flamingo raising a chick fades to pale while the chick gets the pigment. Fathers produce it just as mothers do, and both feed the chick.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Standing on one leg costs a flamingo almost nothing. The body settles and locks over the joint. A flamingo that has died can be balanced on one leg and it will stay standing on its own.","more":"Scientists tested exactly that, then measured living birds too — and found they sway less on one leg than on two. So the strange pose is the restful one. It is standing on both legs that takes the effort.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"That backwards-bending knee is not a knee. It is the ankle. The real knee is tucked up under the feathers against the body. A flamingo is standing on its toes, on very long feet.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Chicks hatch gray, with a straight bill. The famous downward kink only develops over the following weeks, and until it does they cannot filter-feed for themselves at all.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"At about two weeks old the chicks leave the nest and gather in a crèche. That nursery mob can swell to thousands of gray chicks. A handful of adults watch over the lot.","more":"A parent returning with food has to find one chick in that crowd. It does it by voice: calling, listening, answering back, until the right chick pushes its way through. Both learn each other's call in the first days after hatching.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"A flamingo cannot simply jump into the air. It runs — several long splashing strides across the surface with wings already beating — before it finally lifts off.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"It lives in salt pans and briny coastal lagoons that most animals cannot drink from, let alone live in. The tiny creatures it eats swarm there, and the best read is that they manage it precisely because so little else can.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Before nesting, a whole colony performs together. Hundreds of birds pack shoulder to shoulder, snap their heads side to side in time, and flash their black-and-red wings open on cue.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"The nest is a chimney of mud, built up with the bill until it stands clear of the water, with a hollow scooped in the top. One chalky white egg goes in it. Just one.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Thirty to forty years is normal in the wild. One greater flamingo at a zoo in Australia reached at least 83 — older than almost any bird ever recorded.","kind":"record"}],"wonder":"A bill that works upside down, a leg that rests while standing, and a color it feeds to its chick. Nothing here is spare."},{"id":"toucan","name":"Toco toucan","group":"birds","blurb":"A crow-sized South American fruit-eater. Its huge orange bill takes up a third of its whole length.","size":"About as long as your arm — and a third of that is bill","homes":["forest","grassland"],"stats":{"weight":1,"length":1.8},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"The bill runs about 8 in (20 cm) — close to a third of the whole bird. Only one bird beats that for proportion: the sword-billed hummingbird, whose bill is longer than the rest of it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Pick it up and it weighs almost nothing. Under a thin shell of keratin — the stuff of your fingernails — the bill is a foam of bony struts and air pockets.","more":"That is a very good way to build something big and light: a stiff skin outside doing the hard work, a spongy honeycomb inside stopping it from crumpling. The whole bill is mostly empty space.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"The bill is a radiator. It is packed with blood vessels. The toucan opens them wide to dump heat, or shuts them down to hold it. Infrared cameras catch the bill glowing hot, then going cold.","more":"Between a third and a half of the bird's entire skin surface is bill. Sitting in still air it sheds only a quarter of the heat the resting bird makes; in a breeze it can shed four times that heat. No sweating — birds cannot sweat at all.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"Going to sleep, it swings its head right round. It lays the bill down its back, under the wing feathers. Then it flips its tail up over the top. The result is a ball of feathers with no bill in sight.","more":"Its tail bones have a joint that lets the tail fold forward almost flat onto its own back. Tucking the bill away is not just tidiness: it shuts the radiator down, and the bill cools within minutes of the bird settling.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"The tongue is nearly as long as the bill, flat, and frayed along both edges like a narrow feather. The toucan plucks a berry at the tip, then flicks its head back to throw it down its throat.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Perched on a branch strong enough to hold its weight, a toucan can still stretch out and take fruit from twigs far too thin to stand on. Nobody can ask the bird what the bill is for — but that reach is the strongest argument on offer.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Mostly fruit, swallowed whole. But also insects, lizards, and the eggs and nestlings of other birds — that cheerful-looking bill is a long-reach tool for raiding nests.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Two toes forward, two toes back: zygodactyl feet, made for gripping a branch rather than walking along it. Toucans hop sideways down a branch. They do not stroll.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"It is a poor flier. Watch one cross a clearing — a burst of heavy flapping, then a glide, then more flapping — a bobbing, up-and-down line, and it will not go far.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Two to four white eggs in a hollow tree trunk. Both parents sit, for seventeen or eighteen days, and the chicks hatch naked, blind, and with no bill worth the name.","more":"There is no lining in the hole, just bare wood and old seeds. So toucan chicks come equipped with thick pads of skin on their heels, studded with small spines. They can squat on that rough floor for weeks without being rubbed raw.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"For the biggest toucan there is, the voice is a let-down. It is a dry, croaking grunt. It is repeated over and over for minutes at a time from the top of a tree.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"It likes edges — the seam where forest meets open ground, palm groves, riverbanks, savanna with scattered trees. It is not a bird of the deep unbroken jungle.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"One bill doing three jobs at once: picking fruit, reaching what the feet cannot, and cooling the whole bird down."},{"id":"woodpecker","name":"Pileated woodpecker","group":"birds","blurb":"A crow-sized black woodpecker with a flaming red crest. It chisels rectangular holes into dead trees to hunt the ants living inside them.","size":"About 1.5 ft (45 cm) from bill to tail, about the length of your forearm.","homes":["forest","backyard"],"stats":{"length":1.5,"weight":1,"life":9},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"Up to 19 in (49 cm) from bill-tip to tail-tip, with a wingspan reaching 30 in (75 cm) — about the width of a doorway. A crow with a chisel on the front.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Most woodpeckers drill neat round holes. This one gouges rectangles — long squared-off trenches down the trunk, sometimes deep enough to snap a small tree clean in half.","more":"The rectangular hole is a signature. Find one in the woods and you can name the bird that made it without ever seeing the bird. Fresh chips at the base of the tree, pale and sharp-edged, mean it was working there recently.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It isn't eating the wood. It's mining. Carpenter ants tunnel galleries inside dead trees, and the woodpecker follows those tunnels like a map to the nest.","more":"Carpenter ants are the largest part of what a pileated woodpecker eats. Wood-boring beetle grubs come close behind. It also takes berries and nuts, including poison ivy berries, which do it no harm at all.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Its tongue is too long to fit in its head the ordinary way. The bones supporting it curl up around the back of the skull and anchor near the nostril.","more":"That looping structure is the hyoid apparatus. When the bird pushes its tongue out, the whole apparatus slides forward and the tongue shoots far past the bill tip. It is barbed at the end and coated in sticky saliva, made for hooking ants out of a tunnel it cannot see into.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"A split second before every strike, a third eyelid sweeps sideways across the eye. Sawdust flying, eye shut, bill still swinging.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Two toes forward, two toes back, and a tail of stiff spiny feathers braced hard against the bark. Feet and tail make a tripod, so the bird is its own ladder.","more":"Watch one closely and you will see the tail doing real work. It is shoved against the trunk like a third leg, taking the push-back from every blow. The central tail feathers are noticeably stiffer and stronger than the rest, for exactly this job.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"It doesn't sing to claim its patch of woods. It drums — a deep rolling burst lasting about three seconds — and it picks a hollow dead tree. Hollow wood carries further, which is the best read of the choice.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"It carves a fresh nest cavity nearly every year and almost never reuses the old one. Owls, wood ducks, swifts, squirrels and bats move into the leftovers.","more":"This makes the pileated woodpecker a kind of landlord for the whole forest. Plenty of animals need a hole in a tree and have no way of making one. Take the woodpecker out of a woodland and you quietly take the housing supply with it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Both parents take turns sitting on the eggs through the day. But the male takes every single night shift, alone in the dark of the cavity, for about two weeks.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"It sleeps standing up inside a hollow tree. Roost trees often have several entrance holes, which reads as more than one way out if something comes in.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"home","text":"Pairs hold the same territory all year and stay together on it. They hammer away within earshot of each other through winter. Most other birds have long since left.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Feet, tail, tongue, eyelid and skull all doing one job together — a bird made whole for the work it was given."},{"id":"sociable-weaver","name":"Sociable weaver","group":"birds","blurb":"A small brown desert sparrow of southern Africa. It builds the biggest nest of any bird on earth. The nest is a giant grass haystack shared by a hundred families at once.","size":"About 5.5 in (14 cm) long, about the length of your hand, and only about 1 oz (28 g).","homes":["desert","grassland"],"stats":{"length":0.5,"weight":0},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"The bird itself is nothing much: 5.5 in (14 cm) long and around 1 oz (28 g). Then you look up at what it built.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Sociable weavers build the largest nest of any bird in the world — a shaggy grass haystack draped over a camelthorn tree. The biggest could shade a parked car.","more":"It looks less like a nest than like a haystack that fell out of the sky and got stuck. From below you can see dozens of dark round doorways in the underside. The birds never stop working on it; they add grass their whole lives.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Some of these nests grow so heavy that the branch holding them tears loose, or the whole tree comes down under the weight.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Each pair gets a private room off its own entrance tunnel. The room runs up to 10 in (25 cm) long and about 3 in (7 cm) wide. It is lined with soft fur and plant fluff.","more":"The birds jab sharp straw spikes downward around each doorway, points facing out. A snake climbing up into a chamber has to push past a mouthful of them. Over a hundred pairs may live in one nest mass, which can mean several hundred birds under a single roof.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"The Kalahari swings from near-freezing nights to scorching afternoons. Outside air ran from 16°C to 33.5°C (61°F to 92°F). Inside the occupied chambers, temperatures moved only 7-8°C.","more":"Thick packed grass is a superb insulator, the same reason a straw bale holds heat in. In a place with no shade and no shelter, the birds are carrying their own weather around with them, built out of dead grass.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"The chambers deep in the middle of the mass stay warmest, so those become the night bedrooms. The outer rooms stay cooler, so those are where birds wait out the worst of the afternoon.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"A nest is not built and abandoned. Some have been lived in without a break for more than a hundred years, handed down from parents to chicks to grandchicks.","more":"No single bird lives anything like that long. The building outlasts everyone in it, each generation repairing and extending what it inherited. It is the closest thing in the bird world to a family home.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Grown-up birds who aren't raising their own chicks that year help feed somebody else's. Older brothers and sisters often stay on as helpers at the nest.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"African pygmy falcons are actual birds of prey. They move into spare chambers and live inside the weaver colony. Nobody has found the southern birds nesting anywhere else. Almost the whole population there depends on weaver nests for somewhere to sleep.","more":"It is an uneasy arrangement. The falcons mostly hunt lizards and insects rather than their landlords, but nestling weavers are taken often enough that the birds squabble with them. The weavers put up with it anyway.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"food","text":"They eat insects and seeds, and get all the water they need straight out of their food. In a desert with no drinking water for miles, that is the whole trick.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Cape cobras and boomslangs climb the tree and work through the chambers one at a time, emptying them. In one study, 70% of all the clutches laid were eaten.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"A bird weighing about 1 oz (28 g), raising the only apartment block in the desert."},{"id":"bowerbird","name":"Satin bowerbird","group":"birds","blurb":"An Australian forest bird. The males build a decorated stage out of sticks. They spend their lives collecting blue objects to put in front of it.","size":"About 12 in (30 cm) bill to tail, the length of a school ruler.","homes":["forest","rainforest"],"stats":{"length":1.0,"weight":0,"life":8},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"About 12 in (30 cm) long — a school ruler, beak to tail. The adult male is glossy blue-black with violet eyes; the female is olive-green and scaly-patterned, with the same startling blue stare.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The male builds an avenue. Two parallel walls of upright sticks are pushed into the ground about a hand's width apart. A cleared court sits at each end.","more":"He lines the avenue up roughly north to south, so the sun falls along the court rather than into it. He weeds the court bare, then arranges his collection on the ground so the female — standing inside the avenue — looks straight at it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"The bower is not a nest. No egg is ever laid in it, no chick is ever raised in it. It is a stage, built purely to be looked at.","more":"This confused the first naturalists who found bowers and went hunting for eggs inside them. They aren't houses. They're closer to an art gallery, and the male is both the artist and the gallery owner.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"The female does the actual nesting alone. She builds a shallow cup of twigs high in a tree, well away from the bower, and raises the chicks with no help at all.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"He collects blue above all. Blue parrot feathers, flowers and berries. Near towns, blue bottle caps, straws, clothes pegs and pen lids. Yellow leaves and anything shiny go on the pile too.","more":"Young males start with a jumble of colors and shift steadily toward blue as they mature. Blue is genuinely uncommon on an Australian forest floor. A male holding a big pile of it has been searching a long time and guarding it hard.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"He chews leaves and fruit into a pulp. Then he picks up a wad of bark and holds it in his bill like a paintbrush. He paints the inner walls of his avenue with it.","more":"That wad of bark is a tool — an object picked up and used to do a job the beak alone can't do well. Very few animals use tools at all, and fewer still make one for decorating something.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"A male needs seven or eight years to finish turning blue-black. Until then he looks like a female, and spends those years building clumsy practice bowers that impress nobody.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Mid-display he throws in impressions of other birds: kookaburras, currawongs, whipbirds. He mixes them into a strange mechanical buzzing. It sounds nothing like a songbird.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Males raid each other. Given ten seconds alone at a rival's bower, a male will wreck the walls and make off with the best blue pieces.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"He watches the female constantly and dials his display up or down depending on how she reacts. Push too hard and she leaves; the males who read her best do best.","more":"Researchers tested this with robotic female bowerbirds that could be made to crouch or not crouch. Males who kept the intensity high when the female was not crouching startled her away. The successful males were the ones paying attention.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Mostly fruit, with leaves, seeds and insects on the side. Chicks get a diet of beetles, grasshoppers and cicadas until they leave the nest.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"One wild satin bowerbird was marked with a leg band. It was still alive twenty-six years later. That is the greatest age ever recorded for a banded songbird.","kind":"record"}],"wonder":"Nothing about the bower feeds him or shelters him. He builds it because beauty is worth the trouble."},{"id":"frigatebird","name":"Magnificent frigatebird","group":"birds","blurb":"A huge black seabird with an eight-foot wingspan and a scarlet throat balloon. It cannot swim and cannot walk well. So it almost never stops flying.","size":"Wingspan wider than a doorway is tall, but weighs less than a house cat","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"length":3.3,"weight":3},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"The wings span up to 8 ft (2.44 m) — wider than a doorway is tall. The whole bird weighs about 3 lb (1.4 kg), roughly a big bottle of water.","more":"That ratio is the entire story of this animal. Enormous wing, almost no body. No other bird alive has more wing for its weight. That is why it can hang in the air for hours without a single flap.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Its skeleton makes up only about a twentieth of its body weight. Every bone it owns, put together, weighs less than its feathers do.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"A seabird that must never touch the sea. Its feathers aren't properly waterproof and its legs are tiny, so a frigatebird that lands on water may not get off it again.","more":"So it feeds without stopping. It dips down at speed and plucks a fish off the surface with the hooked tip of its bill. Or it snatches flying fish out of the air as they burst clear of the waves. Everything happens on the wing.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"food","text":"It also robs. It chases boobies and terns until they give up their catch, then twists round and takes the falling fish out of mid-air before it hits the water.","more":"Sailors named the bird after the frigate, a fast warship built for chasing other ships down. Stealing is only part of its diet, not all of it. But it is very good at it. It will pull a booby by the tail to make its point.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"The male has a bare red throat pouch he blows up like a scarlet balloon under his chin. Filling it can take him around twenty minutes, and he then sits holding it for hours.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"A tree full of displaying males rattles. They clatter their bills, drum on the inflated pouch and call as females circle overhead, deciding.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"Frigatebirds fitted with trackers have stayed airborne for weeks at a stretch — no land, no water, no rest at all. They feed, travel and sleep on the wing.","more":"They ride thermals, the invisible columns of warm rising air. They spiral upward as high as 8,200 ft (2,500 m) without flapping. Then they glide slowly back down over the ocean until they find the next one. It costs them almost nothing.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"Tracked frigatebirds sleep on the wing in brief scraps. Often only one half of the brain shuts down at a time. It all adds up to well under an hour a day at sea.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A frigatebird chick is fed by its mother for close to a year after it has learned to fly. That is one of the longest childhoods of any bird.","more":"The chick takes so long that a female usually cannot raise another one the next year. She is still feeding the last one. Frigatebirds breed slowly. That is exactly why a colony that gets wiped out takes so long to come back.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"They nest in colonies in low mangroves and scrub on tropical islands, packed close together. The warm waters they use run from the southern United States down to Brazil and out to the Galápagos.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"They can barely walk. Those tiny legs are almost useless on the ground, and a frigatebird nests where it can drop off the edge straight into open air. Nobody has watched one weigh it up; the legs are the obvious reason.","kind":"worked"}],"wonder":"Bones lighter than feathers, wings like sails — built to live where nothing else can stay."},{"id":"pelican","name":"Brown pelican","group":"birds","blurb":"A big gray-brown coastal seabird. It hunts by folding its wings and smashing head-first into the ocean from sixty feet up.","size":"Wingspan wider than a car, and a bill longer than your forearm","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"length":4.1,"weight":8,"life":15},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"Wings spanning up to 7.5 ft (2.28 m) — wider than a car — and a bill reaching nearly 14 in (35 cm), which is longer than most people's forearms.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"The stretchy pouch slung under that bill can hold around 3 gallons (11 liters) of water. That is two or three times what the pelican's own stomach holds.","more":"The pouch is a net, not a lunchbox. It scoops, then it drains. Cartoons show pelicans carrying fish around in there. They do not. The fish goes down the throat within seconds, and the pouch goes flat again.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It hunts by falling. From 59-69 ft (18-21 m) (60-70 ft) it half-folds its wings and drops bill-first, hitting the water hard enough to stun small fish just under the surface.","more":"Of the world's eight pelican species, only the brown pelican and its close relative the Peruvian pelican hunt this way. All the others feed sitting on the water, swimming and scooping together. The dive is this bird's signature.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Air sacs sit under the skin of its chest and neck. Nobody has been inside a diving pelican to watch them work. But they are the best explanation for two things. The bird takes the smack of the dive. Then it pops back up to the surface like a cork.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"food","text":"Surfacing, it points the bill down and lets the water pour out of the sides of the pouch before tipping its head back to swallow. Eleven liters of seawater is heavy.","more":"Those few seconds of draining are the pelican's weakest moment. Laughing gulls know it. They shadow feeding pelicans and snatch fish straight out of the open pouch. Sometimes they stand on the pelican's head to do it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"It flies 65 ft (20 m) above the water and picks out one fish. The surface below is heaving, full of glare and chop. The target is only a few centimeters long.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"Pelicans fly in single file just above the waves, wingtips almost brushing the water. A wing that low gets extra lift out of the air squeezed underneath it — worked out from the physics, and it makes the travel close to free.","more":"Air squeezed between a wing and the surface just below it gives extra lift for no extra effort. Aircraft do the same thing on landing. Watch a pelican line skim a swell and you'll see them rise and fall to stay in that cushion.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Chicks feed by shoving their whole head down inside the parent's throat to reach the fish. A big chick can vanish to the shoulders doing it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Brown pelicans nearly disappeared. A pesticide called DDT worked up the food chain into the fish. It thinned pelican eggshells. Parents settled onto their nests and crushed their own eggs.","more":"In some places every single nest failed, year after year. Then the chemical was banned, the fish cleaned up, and the eggshells thickened again. The pelicans came back — off the endangered list entirely. It is one of the plainest proofs anywhere that damage done can be undone.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"weird","text":"To cool off on a hot day it flutters the bare skin of its pouch rapidly, like a dog panting. The moving air carries heat away through the thin skin.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Adults are nearly silent — the odd low croak and nothing more. The nesting colonies are still deafening, because the chicks scream constantly.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"They nest in noisy crowds on islands and in mangroves along warm coasts. The colonies run from the United States down to Brazil and Peru. Some are out on the Galápagos.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Twenty meters up, through glare and moving water, it finds one fish in a whole ocean — then folds itself into an arrow to reach it."},{"id":"puffin","name":"Atlantic puffin","group":"birds","blurb":"A small black-and-white seabird with a striped, brightly colored bill. It spends most of its life far out on the open ocean.","size":"Stands about 8 inches (20 cm) high on land — roughly the length of a brand-new pencil.","homes":["ocean","polar"],"stats":{"speed":50,"weight":1,"length":1.0,"life":20},"facts":[{"cat":"food","text":"A puffin can hold more than a dozen small fish crosswise in its bill at once. It still opens its mouth to catch the next one. Nothing falls out.","more":"The trick is inside the mouth. A puffin's tongue and the roof of its mouth are lined with backward-pointing spines. It pins each fish against the roof with its tongue, then reaches for another. Fish can slide in. They cannot slide out.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"A puffin flies underwater, rowing with half-folded wings while its feet steer behind it like a rudder. Nobody can ask a bird what its wings are for — but the best read is that they are shaped for water first and air second.","more":"Wings small enough to work against water are almost too small for air. Watch a puffin leave the sea and you will see it sprint across the surface first, feet slapping, because it cannot simply lift off.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"speed","text":"In the air it reaches 50 mph (80 km/h). It pays for that by beating its wings up to about 400 times a minute. They blur into a smudge.","more":"Four hundred beats a minute is nearly seven every second. Your eyes cannot separate wingbeats that fast. So a flying puffin does not look much like a bird. It looks like a football with a beak going past.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"That famous orange-and-blue bill is temporary. The colored outer plates fall off after breeding season. The puffin spends the winter with a smaller, duller, gray-brown beak.","more":"The bright bill is breeding gear, worn in spring and summer when puffins are ashore choosing partners, then shed in a partial moult. A winter puffin at sea looks so different that people once took it for a completely different bird.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"For most of the year there is no land in a puffin's life at all. It rides the open Atlantic, sleeping on the water, far from the nearest rock.","more":"Puffins come ashore only to breed. The rest of the year they are pelagic — creatures of the open sea — feeding, moulting and resting on the waves. Some winter so far out that for months nobody knows exactly where they are.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"One egg. That is the whole year's effort — a single white egg, laid at the end of a burrow the parents dig themselves with beak and feet.","more":"Both parents take turns sitting on it for about six weeks, then both feed the chick, which is called a puffling. When it is ready it leaves the burrow alone, at night, and walks or flutters straight out to sea without its parents.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Puffins drink seawater. They can, because a gland above each eye strips the salt out of their blood and drips it back out through their nostrils as brine.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"A puffin can spend a whole winter out on the ocean. In spring it returns to the same burrow on the same clifftop. Often it returns to the same partner too.","more":"Colonies honeycomb the soil with burrows three to seven feet (1 to 2 m) long. Birds wearing leg rings come back to the same hole year after year. Puffins are long-lived for their size. Twenty years is ordinary, and some have passed thirty.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Puffins nest on cliffs and offshore islands, where no fox can reach them — and the best read is that this is exactly why. The danger arrives from above instead.","more":"Great black-backed gulls and skuas hunt puffins in the air, and gulls will mug a returning parent in mid-flight for its beakful of fish. That is partly why puffins come in low, fast and straight, and wheel around the colony in big flocks before landing.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"size","text":"For all the fame, a puffin is small. It weighs 350 to 500 grams, about a full can of soup. It would sit comfortably in your two cupped hands.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Look closely at the eye. Those aren't feathers: in breeding season a puffin grows small horny plates above and below it, which make a round eye look triangular.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"One egg, one burrow, one partner — and a bird you could hold in two hands, carried safely all winter across an ocean with no landmarks on it."},{"id":"king-cobra","name":"King cobra","group":"reptiles","blurb":"The longest venomous snake in the world — a tall, sharp-eyed forest snake that hunts and eats other snakes.","size":"Big ones pass 13 feet (4 m), and the longest ever recorded reached 19 feet (5.85 m).","homes":["rainforest","forest","grassland"],"stats":{"length":13.1,"weight":13,"life":20},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"The longest venomous snake there is. Most adults run 10 to 13 feet (3.2 to 4 m) — about three times your own height — and the record reached 19 feet (5.85 m).","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"King cobras hunt rat snakes, pythons, kraits and other cobras. They will even eat other king cobras. Their scientific name, Ophiophagus, means snake-eater. That is exactly the job.","more":"A snake is an awkward meal for most predators and an easy one for a king cobra: long, smooth, and swallowed head-first like a strand of spaghetti. It will take venomous species as readily as harmless ones.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"babies","text":"The king cobra is the only snake in the world that builds a nest. She rakes dead leaves into a mound with her own coils and lays her eggs inside it.","more":"The mound can stand nearly two feet (55 cm) tall and spread four and a half feet (140 cm) across, with a chamber hollowed out for the eggs. Damp packed leaves hold warmth in, and she coils on top of it and guards, for weeks.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"A king cobra does not hiss like other snakes. It growls — a low, dog-like sound that comes out well below the pitch of an ordinary snake's hiss.","more":"The sound comes from pouches branching off the windpipe, called tracheal diverticula. They work as resonating chambers. A guitar's hollow body does the same thing to a plucked string, making it louder and deeper. A king cobra's growl peaks near 600 Hz. An ordinary snake's hiss peaks near 7,500.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Threatened, a king cobra lifts the front third of its body straight off the ground. A big one can look a grown adult in the eye. Then it moves forward at that height.","more":"The hood is not loose skin alone. Long ribs in the neck swing outward and stretch the skin flat, turning a narrow snake into a broad warning sign. It is a display first: the entire point is to be seen and given room.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"One bite can deliver 400 to 600 mg of venom — among the largest doses any snake gives. Antivenom for it exists, and it works.","more":"The venom is neurotoxic. It jams the signals traveling from nerve to muscle. That is why breathing is the thing that fails first. Hospitals treat the bite with antivenom. If needed, a ventilator breathes for the patient until the venom clears.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"No heat pits. A python or a rattlesnake has face holes that sense warmth. A king cobra has none of them. It finds other snakes by scent, and by unusually good eyesight.","more":"The forked tongue does the smelling, carrying scent particles back to the Jacobson's organ in the roof of the mouth. But a king cobra also watches. It will follow a moving snake with its eyes across open ground, which most snakes barely bother to do.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Its fangs are short and fixed — they do not fold. The best read is that it does not need long ones: a king cobra bites and hangs on, chewing the venom in rather than letting go.","more":"Cobras are elapids, with short permanently erect fangs at the front of the mouth. Vipers are built the other way round: long hinged fangs that fold flat when the mouth shuts. An elapid trades fang length for grip.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"home","text":"Look up. King cobras climb well and swim well. A snake this long can be draped along a branch high above the forest floor. It can also be out crossing a river.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"She guards that nest for weeks, which almost no other snake does. Then, shortly before the eggs hatch, she leaves — and the hatchlings are on their own from the first minute.","more":"A clutch runs from 7 to 43 eggs and takes two to three months to develop. Hatchlings emerge around 20 inches (50 cm) long, banded black and pale, and fully venomous immediately. Nobody teaches them how to hunt.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Of all the thousands of snakes in the world, one builds a house for its eggs and sits on it. Just the one."},{"id":"reticulated-python","name":"Reticulated python","group":"reptiles","blurb":"The longest snake in the world. It is a heavy, net-patterned constrictor from the forests and rivers of Southeast Asia.","size":"Longer than a car. The longest wild one ever properly measured, 22 feet 10 inches (6.95 m), would stretch right across a classroom.","homes":["rainforest","forest","freshwater"],"stats":{"length":16.4,"weight":110,"life":20},"facts":[{"cat":"record","text":"The longest snake on Earth. A wild one caught in Borneo and measured properly, with a tape, came to 22 feet 10 inches (6.95 m) — long enough to stretch across a classroom.","more":"The captive record-holder, a python named Medusa in Missouri, was verified at 25 feet 2 inches (7.67 m) and 350 pounds (158.8 kg). Far longer claims turn up online constantly. Almost none of them survive an actual tape measure.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"Reticulated means net-like. That gold-and-black web looks impossible to miss on a page. In dappled forest light it disappears completely.","more":"The pattern breaks up the snake's outline instead of matching a background. Sunlight coming through leaves falls in exactly that kind of broken patchwork. So an enormous animal lying still on the forest floor reads as leaf shadow. Then it moves.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Along its lips, top and bottom, runs a row of deep pits. They are not nostrils. Each one reads heat, so the python can find a warm rat in total darkness.","more":"These labial pits are lined with heat-sensitive tissue. Several of them are spread along both jaws, so the snake gets a direction as well as a signal. That is enough to aim a strike at an animal it has never seen.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"A python does not crush bones and it does not smother. It squeezes hard enough to stop the blood moving, and its prey goes unconscious in seconds.","more":"Researchers measured what happens inside constricted prey. Blood pressure collapses almost at once. The heart cannot pump against the squeeze. The brain loses its supply long before the animal could run out of air. Every time the prey breathes out, the coils take up the slack.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"A python does not unhinge its jaw — that would break it. The two halves of its lower jaw simply are not joined at the front, and a stretchy ligament lets them spread apart.","more":"Add a quadrate bone at the back that swings like a double hinge, plus skin that stretches, and the mouth opens far wider than the head. The snake then walks its jaws forward over the meal, one side at a time, dragging it in.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Swallowing something large takes hours. So the python shoves its windpipe forward out of the floor of its mouth, like a snorkel, and breathes around its dinner the whole time.","more":"The opening is called the glottis. In snakes it sits far forward and can be pushed out to one side of the prey. Without that, anything that took more than a few minutes to swallow would suffocate the snake doing the swallowing.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"She lays somewhere between 15 and 80 eggs. Then she wraps herself around the whole pile and stays. She barely moves and does not feed until they hatch.","more":"Guarding is the entire job, and it costs her a great deal of body weight over the three months or so the eggs need. The hatchlings break out around two feet (60 cm) long and are completely independent from that moment.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"Reticulated pythons are strong swimmers. They cross rivers, they swim in the sea, and they have turned up on small islands a long way offshore.","more":"A big snake is close to weightless in water. Swimming costs it far less effort than hauling its own bulk over the ground. That is why the species turns up on so many islands of Indonesia and the Philippines.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It can go months between meals. The record-length wild python had eaten nothing for nearly three months when researchers measured it — and still weighed 130 pounds (59 kg).","more":"Pythons run on remarkably little. Between meals the gut actually shrinks down; when food finally arrives, the digestive organs ramp back up within a day or two. A meal that would last you an afternoon can last a python weeks.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Every tooth curves backwards, like the barb on a fishing hook. They are not for chewing — a python swallows everything whole. The best read is that they are there to make sure nothing reverses out.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"size","text":"A big reticulated python can top 165 pounds (75 kg) — as heavy as a grown man — and be thicker through the middle than your own thigh.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Heat pits, a jaw in two halves, backward teeth, a windpipe that slides aside — four separate pieces of equipment, all built for one job: swallowing something wider than your own head."},{"id":"gaboon-viper","name":"Gaboon viper","group":"reptiles","blurb":"A huge, heavy African viper that lies motionless in dead leaves. It wears the best camouflage of any snake in the world.","size":"About 4.6 ft (1.4 m) long, and as thick through the middle as your forearm.","homes":["rainforest","forest","grassland"],"stats":{"length":4.6,"weight":18},"facts":[{"cat":"record","text":"The longest fangs of any snake alive — up to 2 inches (5 cm). That is the length of your thumb, folded away inside a closed mouth.","more":"They lie flat against the roof of the mouth and swing forward like a switchblade as it opens. A viper this size has no choice but to fold them: a fixed fang two inches long would punch straight through its own lower jaw.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"Lying in leaf litter, a gaboon viper is not there. The pale and dark geometry down its back matches dead leaves so exactly that people walk over one without ever seeing it.","more":"Then add stillness. Gaboon vipers hunt by ambush. An ambush hunter can hold the same position for hours or days. It waits for a rat or a ground bird to wander into range. The camouflage works because the snake refuses to move.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Drop for drop its venom is not the strongest. It does not need to be — a gaboon viper injects more venom in a single bite than any other snake.","more":"A single bite has been measured at 5 to 7 milliliters, more than a teaspoonful. The largest recorded milking was close to 10 milliliters. Antivenom exists and works well. Even so, every bite is a genuine emergency and belongs in a hospital immediately.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Most vipers strike, release, and track the prey down afterwards. A gaboon viper does the opposite: it bites and hangs on, fangs in, until the animal stops moving.","more":"Holding on lets it keep pumping venom in, and it means the meal never gets away to die somewhere it cannot be found. This snake is heavy and slow — chasing anything is off the table — so the first bite has to finish the argument.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"One of the heaviest venomous snakes alive. One just under six feet (1.8 m) long was weighed at 25 pounds (11.3 kg) on an empty stomach — about what a car tire weighs.","more":"The head alone can be almost 5 inches (12 cm) across: broad, flat and triangular, sitting on a neck a third that width. Lifted, a big gaboon viper feels less like a snake and more like a fire hose full of wet sand.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Watch it move and something looks wrong. It travels in a straight line — no S-curves at all — creeping forward on its belly scales like an enormous caterpillar.","more":"This is called rectilinear locomotion. Muscles pull groups of belly scales forward, plant them, and haul the body over the top. Very heavy snakes use it — gaboon vipers, big pythons, boas — and the great advantage is that it is almost silent.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"She does not lay eggs. She gives birth to live young — as many as fifty or sixty at once — each one already patterned and already fully venomous.","more":"Fifty or sixty is the top of the range; smaller broods are more usual. The newborns arrive ready for work: marked exactly like the adults, fanged, and carrying working venom from the first minute. Nothing is taught to them afterwards.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"There is a pair of tiny horns on its snout, right between the raised nostrils. On the gaboon viper they are small bumps; on its close relative the rhinoceros viper, they are unmistakable.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"For something this dangerous it is astonishingly reluctant. A gaboon viper will usually flatten its head and hiss like a kettle rather than strike at all.","more":"Reluctant is not the same as safe. The strike, when it finally comes, is over before you have registered that it started, and the snake was invisible right up to that moment. In gaboon viper country, people watch the ground.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It eats whatever walks past: rats, ground birds, hares, and occasionally something as large as a small antelope. No stalking, no chase — just whatever chose the wrong path.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Gaboon vipers live on the floor of African rainforests. They also use thicker patches of savanna. It is always somewhere with deep leaf litter to vanish into.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"That pattern is beautiful, and almost nobody was ever meant to see it — beauty laid down where only the leaves would notice."},{"id":"rattlesnake","name":"Western diamondback rattlesnake","group":"reptiles","blurb":"A thick-bodied American desert snake with diamonds down its back and a warning rattle on the end of its tail.","size":"Usually around 4 feet (1.2 m) long.","homes":["desert","grassland"],"stats":{"length":3.9,"weight":4,"life":15},"facts":[{"cat":"talk","text":"The rattle is not really a threat. Nobody can ask a snake what it means by it. The best read is that it is an announcement to anything big enough to tread on a snake: I am here, and I would rather you went around.","more":"The muscles that shake it are among the fastest known in any animal. They fire about 50 times a second. A rattlesnake can keep that up for as long as three hours. Nothing inside actually rattles. The hollow segments simply knock together.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"build","text":"Every time it sheds its skin, a rattlesnake adds one new segment to the rattle. But you cannot count the segments to find its age — that part is a myth.","more":"A snake may shed several times in a good year and hardly at all in a lean one, so the segments count meals more than birthdays. Worse, the end ones snap off constantly against rock. A long rattle mostly means a careful snake.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Between each eye and each nostril is a small hole. It is a heat detector, and it can register a temperature change of three thousandths of a degree.","more":"The pit is a thin membrane stretched across a chamber. It is wired into the same part of the brain that handles vision. So a rattlesnake does not merely detect warmth. It gets a heat picture laid over the ordinary one. That is how it strikes accurately in complete darkness.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"A rattlesnake bites a rat and immediately lets go. Then it waits, tongue flicking, and follows the trail of that one animal to wherever it finally fell.","more":"Hanging on to a struggling rodent is a good way to get bitten back, so the snake gets clear and lets the venom do the work. Remarkably, it can pick the trail of the rat it bit out from every other rat trail crossing it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Rattlesnakes do not lay eggs. The young are born alive, about a dozen at a time, each one up to a foot (30 cm) long and already venomous.","more":"A newborn has just one hard button on its tail — a single segment with nothing to knock against, so it cannot rattle at all. It has to shed at least once before it can make a sound. Until then it defends itself in silence.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The fangs are hollow, hinged, and disposable. A rattlesnake swaps them for a fresh pair every six to ten weeks, with at least three spare pairs already waiting in line behind them.","more":"They fold flat against the roof of the mouth and swing forward as the jaws open. Because they are hollow, like a hypodermic needle, venom travels down the inside of the tooth instead of running along a groove on the outside.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"The flicking forked tongue is not smelling the air. It is collecting it — the tips carry scent particles back to two pits in the roof of the mouth called the Jacobson's organ.","more":"Two tips means two samples at once, from slightly different places. If the left tip brings back more of a scent than the right, the snake knows which way to turn. It is smelling in stereo, the way two ears let you hear which direction a sound came from.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Its venom is hemotoxic — it works on blood and tissue rather than on nerves. An average bite delivers 250 to 350 mg, and the antivenom for it is effective.","more":"Western diamondbacks are behind a large share of serious snakebites in the American southwest. They live where people live. They also stand their ground rather than retreating. Nearly everyone who is bitten and reaches a hospital recovers.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"In winter they gather. Diamondbacks pack into rock crevices and abandoned burrows to sit out the cold. A single den can hold dozens of snakes, and other species share it too.","more":"It is called brumation, and it is not quite hibernation. The snake is cold and barely moving, but it will still drink. On a warm winter afternoon it may come to the mouth of the den to lie in the sun. The same dens get used year after year.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Look at the tail just above the rattle: four to six crisp black and white rings, like a raccoon's. That banded tail is one of the marks people use to name this snake in the field.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"Most are about 4 feet (1.2 m) long — roughly your own height — and weigh 3 to 6 pounds (1.2 to 2.7 kg), about a house brick. The largest reliable measurement was 7 feet (2.1 m).","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"A hunter that announces itself before it strikes — a warning built into the end of the tail, so that most meetings end with nobody hurt."},{"id":"komodo-dragon","name":"Komodo dragon","group":"reptiles","blurb":"The biggest, heaviest lizard alive. It is a slow-walking hunter from a handful of Indonesian islands. It brings down animals as large as deer.","size":"Longer than a canoe and heavier than a grown man — up to 10 feet (3 m) from nose to tail tip.","homes":["grassland","forest"],"stats":{"speed":13,"weight":176,"length":9.8,"life":30},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"The largest one ever properly measured stretched 10 feet 3 inches (3.13 m). It weighed 366 pounds (166 kg), and that was with a full stomach.","more":"That record weight included a whole undigested meal, so the dragon itself was lighter. Ordinary adults run about 8 feet (2.5 m) and 175 pounds (80 kg). Even the ordinary ones are enormous — nothing else with scales and four legs comes close on land.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Heaviest lizard on Earth, with no real rival. A few monitor lizards grow nearly as long, but they are slim and light. None of them carry this much bulk.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"It smells with its tongue. That yellow fork flicks out, catches scent particles from the air, and wipes them onto the roof of its mouth — the vomeronasal organ.","more":"Because the tongue is forked, each tip samples a slightly different patch of air. More scent on the left tip than the right, and the dragon turns left. It is smelling in stereo, the way you hear in stereo. Downwind, a dead animal can be picked up from about 2.5 miles (4 km) away.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"One meal can be 80 percent of its own body weight. Imagine eating a lunch that weighs almost as much as you do — then not needing another for weeks.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"About sixty curved teeth, every one edged like a steak knife. The tips are reinforced with iron — and the best read is that this is what keeps the serrations from wearing blunt.","more":"You can actually see it. The cutting edge carries a faint orange stain. Researchers checked museum specimens and tested the enamel. Iron really is concentrated right along the serrated edge. The teeth are also replaced over and over throughout the dragon's life. A worn one is never a problem for long.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"weird","text":"A Komodo dragon's bite does more than cut. Glands in its lower jaw release an anticoagulant — a chemical that stops blood clotting, so the wound keeps bleeding.","more":"For years people said the dragon's mouth was simply filthy, and that infection did the work. Then researchers scanned the jaw and found real venom glands. The venom also drops the victim's blood pressure, which is why bitten prey grows weak and slow.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Under its skin, a full-grown dragon wears chain mail. Thousands of small bones called osteoderms are set into the head, neck and body, in shapes like flowers and branches.","more":"A scan of an adult found the bony armor nearly everywhere except around the eyes, nostrils and mouth. Hatchlings have none of it at all — it builds up as the animal grows. The only thing that seriously threatens a big Komodo dragon is another big Komodo dragon.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Some female Komodo dragons have laid eggs that hatched without any male involved at all. Every hatchling was a son.","more":"Two English zoos recorded it. The process is called parthenogenesis. In Komodo dragons the chromosomes pair up a particular way. An egg produced like this can only ever become a male.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Young dragons roll in dung and in the emptied guts of a carcass. Nobody has watched an adult turn one down over the smell. The best read is that it makes a youngster unappetizing. Grown dragons would otherwise eat it.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"home","text":"Hatchlings head straight up a tree and stay there. On the ground the adults treat them as food, so young dragons spend their first years in branches, hunting insects and geckos.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"They swim well but they hardly go anywhere. Radio tracking showed most adults spend their whole lives inside a single valley just a few miles across.","more":"Researchers moved some dragons to other valleys to see what would happen. The dragons walked home. Several crossed miles of rough ground and steep ridges to get back to the exact valley they started in.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"speed","text":"For a heavyweight it is quick — short bursts of 13 mph (20 km/h), roughly a bicycle's pace. But it cannot hold that for long, so it prefers to wait in cover and ambush.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Chain mail under the skin, iron along the teeth, a tongue that smells in stereo — nothing here was left half-finished."},{"id":"veiled-chameleon","name":"Veiled chameleon","group":"reptiles","blurb":"A tall-helmeted tree lizard from the mountains of Yemen. Its eyes swivel independently, and its tongue fires like a spear.","size":"A big male is about 2 feet (60 cm) long, and about half of that is tail.","homes":["mountain","forest"],"stats":{"weight":0,"length":2.0,"life":5},"facts":[{"cat":"build","text":"That tall ridge on its head is called a casque. Water that settles on it in the night fog runs down the grooves toward the corners of the chameleon's mouth.","more":"Veiled chameleons come from the dry mountains of Yemen and Saudi Arabia, where rain is rare but night fog is not. On a big male the casque stands about 2 inches (5 cm) tall. It does other jobs too — males use it in displays — but a lizard that can drink off its own head is doing well in a dry place.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Each eye moves on its own. One can track a wasp behind while the other watches a branch in front — very nearly a full circle of vision from one still head.","more":"The eyelid is fused into a cone with a single pinhole for the pupil, so the whole turret swivels as one piece. The instant the chameleon finds something worth eating, both eyes swing forward onto the same target. That tells it exactly how far away the meal is, and how hard to fire.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"The tongue launches from a standstill and lands on the target in roughly the time it takes you to blink. It reaches out to about twice the chameleon's own body length.","more":"It is not thrown like a rope. A tube of muscle squeezes down over a stiff spike of bone and cartilage. Think of pinching a wet watermelon seed between two fingers. At the same moment, elastic tissue that has been stretched tight lets go. Every bit of stored energy leaves at once.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"The tongue tip is coated in mucus around 400 times thicker and stickier than your spit. Once it touches a cricket, the cricket is coming back with it.","more":"Researchers measured the viscosity directly. Viscosity means how much a liquid resists flowing. Water is thin and honey is thick. Chameleon tongue mucus sits closer to the honey end. The tip also folds around the prey as it lands. A struggling insect has nothing to push against.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Chameleons do not match the wallpaper. They change color to signal mood and temperature — a calm veiled chameleon is green and banded, an angry male goes bright and blotchy.","more":"Color is a language first and camouflage second. A male facing a rival flares yellow and turquoise. A female carrying eggs turns dark with pale spots, which tells males to keep away. Cold chameleons darken to soak up sun; overheated ones go pale to reflect it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The colors are not paint. Under the skin sits a lattice of tiny guanine crystals, and the chameleon shifts color by changing the spacing between them.","more":"Spread the crystals further apart and the skin throws back yellow and red; squeeze them closer and it throws back blue. Green is blue crystal-light shining up through a yellow pigment layer above. Chameleons studied closely also have a second, deeper crystal layer that bounces away infrared — heat — like a built-in sunshade.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Its feet are zygodactyl — five toes bundled into two opposing groups, two on one side and three on the other, so every foot is a pair of pliers. The tail grips too.","more":"The tail coils and holds like a monkey's, which makes it a fifth limb. Unlike most lizards, a chameleon cannot shed its tail to escape and grow a new one, so it takes care of the one it has.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"One female can lay up to 85 eggs in a single clutch. She digs a tunnel, lays them at the bottom, fills it in — and never sees them hatch.","more":"The eggs sit underground for months. A hatchling comes out barely longer than your thumb and gets no help whatsoever. It walks off into the branches and starts hunting insects on its first day.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"Watch it walk and it rocks forward and back, one slow step at a time, like a leaf shivering in wind. Predators hunting for movement often slide straight past it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Mostly insects: crickets, grasshoppers, flies. Veiled chameleons also eat leaves and flowers, which is unusual among chameleons. The likely reason is the water locked inside them.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"It has no ear opening and no eardrum. A chameleon is not deaf, but it hears poorly and in a narrow range — those eyes are doing nearly all the work.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"For all that reach and drama, a big male weighs only 5 ounces (150 g). He would sit on one of your hands with room to spare.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Turret eyes, an elastic tongue, crystal skin — a hunter that never needs to hurry and never needs to chase."},{"id":"thorny-devil","name":"Thorny devil","group":"reptiles","blurb":"A small, spiky, ant-eating lizard of the Australian desert. It drinks by pulling water across its own skin.","size":"About 8 inches (21 cm) nose to tail, about the length of a pencil, and light enough to sit in your open palm.","homes":["desert","grassland"],"stats":{"length":0.7,"life":15},"facts":[{"cat":"weird","text":"It drinks with its feet. Water touching any part of a thorny devil is pulled right across the body. Microscopic grooves between the scales deliver it to the corners of its mouth.","more":"The grooves work by capillary action — the same force that pulls water up a paper towel dipped in a puddle. Stand the lizard in a shallow puddle and the water climbs its legs, crosses its back and reaches its mouth, traveling uphill the whole way. Then it works its jaws to swallow.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"It shovels damp sand onto its own back. The sand sits there, the skin channels wick the moisture out of it, and the lizard drinks the sand dry.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It eats one thing: ants. A thorny devil parks beside an ant trail and picks them off one at a time — well over a thousand in a single day.","more":"It works at up to 45 ants a minute, flicking them up with a sticky tongue without breaking the line or panicking the colony. Small black ants are the favourites. A diet of nothing but ants sounds thin, but it never runs out.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"On the back of its neck sits a spiny knob shaped like a head. When something threatens, the devil tucks its real head down between its front legs. That leaves the knob showing. Nobody has watched a predator bite the wrong one. The shape and the posture are the argument that it is a spare head.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Every one of those cones is a spine. They point in every direction at once. The best read is that this is what they are for. A snake or a bird trying to swallow a thorny devil finds no good angle to do it from.","more":"It can also puff itself up with air when something threatens, which makes it wider and rounder and harder still to fit into a mouth. The spines are not a weapon — the lizard never attacks with them. They simply make it an awkward parcel to swallow.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"Its browns and tans match the sand exactly. When something approaches, it freezes flat. Most predators walk past a motionless thorny devil without ever registering it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"It moves in a strange stop-start rock — a step, a pause, a sway back and forth, another step. Nothing about it moves the way an animal moves, and the best read is that this is exactly the point.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"build","text":"It changes color with the weather. Pale through the heat of the day, darker when the desert turns cold, because dark skin soaks up more sun.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"The female digs a burrow about a foot (30 cm) deep, lays three to ten eggs at the bottom, fills it back in and walks away. The young dig themselves out months later.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"It looks ferocious and it is tiny. A full-grown thorny devil is shorter than a school ruler and sits comfortably in two cupped hands. The spines are armor and bluff — not a weapon.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"copied","text":"Engineers study that skin. It moves water sideways and uphill with no pump and no power. Dry countries need exactly that. This lizard has been doing it all along.","more":"The channels between the scales are shaped like tiny half-pipes. Once it is wet, the surface turns superhydrophilic. That means water spreads flat across it instead of beading up. Labs have copied the pattern to move liquid around without a motor.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"It lives in the dry interior of Australia, in spinifex grass and sandy scrub. A year there can pass with almost no rain. It is the hardest place on the continent to find a drink.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"In a country where it may not rain for a year, this lizard was given skin that finds water and carries it home."},{"id":"gecko","name":"Tokay gecko","group":"reptiles","blurb":"A loud, blue-and-orange-spotted gecko from Southeast Asia. It walks up glass and across ceilings, and it shouts its own name at night.","size":"About as long as your forearm, elbow to fingertips — up to 14 inches (35 cm) counting the tail.","homes":["rainforest","forest","backyard"],"stats":{"weight":0,"length":1.1,"life":8},"facts":[{"cat":"build","text":"There is no glue and no suction cup anywhere on a gecko. Each foot carries roughly half a million hairs called setae, and every hair splits at the tip into hundreds of finer pads.","more":"A single seta is about a tenth of a millimeter long — you would need a microscope to see one. Its split ends are finer still, around 200 nanometres across, smaller than the wavelength of visible light. The gecko's grip lives at a scale nobody could even look at until fairly recently.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The grip comes from van der Waals attraction — the faint pull between any two things whose molecules get close enough. One hair is nothing. Half a million at once, and the gecko hangs off glass.","more":"It is not stickiness in any ordinary sense. Nothing wet, no residue left behind, nothing that wears out or gets used up. A gecko's foot works in a vacuum, and on a pane so smooth and clean that sticky tape would slide right off it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Measured in a lab, two front feet gripped hard enough to hold 20 newtons. That is about the pull of a full 2-liter bottle hanging off them. It is many times what the gecko itself weighs.","more":"A climbing gecko uses only a fraction of what its feet can actually do. That huge margin to spare is why it can sprint straight up a vertical sheet of glass without ever pausing to test its footing.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"The hard part is not sticking. It is letting go. The gecko peels each toe backwards from the tip, which breaks the contact in about 15 thousandths of a second — faster than you can notice.","more":"That is why gecko toes curl upward and away as the foot lifts, which looks very odd if you slow the film down. Pull straight off and the hairs would fight you the whole way. Peel from the tip and they release instantly.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"copied","text":"Engineers copy those hairs. Pads covered in tiny synthetic wedges have carried climbing robots up glass walls and let a person haul themselves up a sheer pane.","more":"The grip leaves no residue and needs no power. So the same idea is being tested for catching satellites and space junk, where ordinary glue would freeze solid, boil away, or drift off.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"It is named after the noise it makes. Males bark a loud, rasping 'to-KAY! to-KAY!' from a wall after dark, over and over, and it carries a long way.","more":"The call is a territory notice and an advertisement for a mate, rolled together. A gecko usually warms up with a burst of dry cackling. Then it delivers the full call several times running. Across Southeast Asia it is an ordinary night sound, like a dog barking down the street.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"senses","text":"It has no eyelids at all. A clear scale covers each eye like a windscreen, and the gecko keeps it clean with a swipe of its tongue.","more":"The eye carries three light-catching pigments. One of them is tuned to ultraviolet, a band of light your eyes cannot register at any brightness. Geckos built like this can still tell colors apart by moonlight. Your own color vision switches off in near-darkness.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"This is one of the largest geckos there is. A big male stretches from your elbow to your fingertips, and he is heavy enough that you feel him land on your hand.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Grab a tokay by the tail and it hands the tail over. The tail keeps thrashing on the ground for minutes, holding the predator's attention while the gecko walks away.","more":"The break happens along a weak line that is already built into the tail. Muscles clamp the blood vessels shut, so it barely bleeds. A replacement grows in around cartilage instead of bone. It usually comes back a different shape and color from the original.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"It is one of very few geckos that fights back. A cornered tokay gapes its mouth wide, hisses, and lunges — and once it has hold of something, it does not let go.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A female lays just one or two eggs at a time and glues them to a wall, a tree trunk or the underside of a roof. They set rock hard and cannot be prised off.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It hunts insects — cockroaches, crickets, moths drawn to a lamp. A big tokay will also take small lizards, including other geckos, and now and then a mouse.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Half a million hairs on every foot, and each one splits again at the tip — detail on a scale nobody could see until we had microscopes strong enough to look."},{"id":"basilisk-lizard","name":"Common basilisk","group":"reptiles","blurb":"A crested lizard of Central American riverbanks. It escapes danger by rearing up and sprinting across the surface of the water.","size":"Up to 2.5 feet (76 cm) long, but three-quarters of that is tail.","homes":["rainforest","freshwater"],"stats":{"weight":1,"length":2.5},"facts":[{"cat":"record","text":"It runs on water. Not skimming, not swimming — up on its back legs, sprinting across the surface of a river. And it can do this from the day it hatches.","more":"In Central America people call it the Jesus lizard for exactly this reason. A basilisk crosses roughly 15 feet (4.5 m) of open water before it sinks in and swims the rest. The lightest ones — the hatchlings — stay up on top the longest.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Every footfall is three moves: slap, stroke, recover. The slap drives the foot down and traps a pocket of air. The stroke pushes backwards against that pocket. Then the foot lifts clear.","more":"The whole thing depends on getting the foot out fast enough. If the water collapses around the leg first, the lizard has to drag its foot up through water instead of air. That costs far more energy than it has. Speed is not showing off here. It is the only thing keeping the animal on top.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"The three outer toes on each hind foot carry a fringe of loose scales. On land they fold flat and stay out of the way. Hit water and they spread wide, widening the foot.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"A big male is longer than your arm, but you could hold him in one hand. Nearly all that length is a whip-thin tail he swings for balance while running.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Startle a basilisk and it drops. It flings itself off the branch, hits the water, and is already gone across the surface before the splash has finished.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"If running fails, it goes the other way. A basilisk can stay underwater for up to half an hour. It sits motionless on the bottom until whatever was chasing it loses interest.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Males carry three crests — one on the head, one down the back, one along the tail. Females have only the head crest, so you can tell them apart at a glance.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It eats almost anything it can catch or reach: beetles, dragonflies, flowers, small snakes, birds' eggs, fish. Very few lizards are that flexible about dinner.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"It never goes far from water. Basilisks live along rainforest streams and rivers, usually within a few steps of the bank. The best read on why is the water itself. It is the escape route.","more":"A basilisk that wanders inland loses its best trick. Out on dry ground it is just another lizard being chased by a snake. Stay near the bank and there is always somewhere to run that nothing following can follow across.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A hatchling weighs 2 grams — two paperclips — and is barely longer than your finger. No parent sticks around, but it can already run on water, which is most of what it needs.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"speed","text":"Across the surface it moves at about 5 feet per second (1.5 m/s). That is a brisk walking pace for you. The difference is that there is nothing underneath it but river.","more":"The legs are working far harder than that pace suggests. Each foot has to punch down, push back and pull clear again before the hole it made fills in, over and over, the whole way across.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"copied","text":"Engineers have built water-running robots by copying the basilisk's stroke. They are hunting for a machine that can cross a flooded surface without a boat.","more":"The hard part turned out to be the same problem the lizard already solves. The foot has to come back out of the hole it just punched. It has to happen before the water rushes in to fill it. Get that timing wrong and the robot goes straight to the bottom.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"It weighs two paperclips the day it hatches, and already knows how to cross a river without swimming."},{"id":"frilled-lizard","name":"Frilled-neck lizard","group":"reptiles","blurb":"An Australian tree lizard, slim and gray and almost invisible on bark. A huge collar of skin sits folded around its neck. It snaps open when the lizard is frightened.","size":"About 3 ft (90 cm) long — and more than half of that is tail.","homes":["forest","grassland"],"stats":{"length":3.0,"weight":1,"life":6},"facts":[{"cat":"defence","text":"Corner it and the show starts. A collar of skin snaps open around its head, over 12 in (30 cm) across — wider than a dinner plate — while the mouth gapes bright pink and it hisses.","more":"None of it is a weapon. The frill makes the lizard look several times bigger than it is, and most of the time that is enough. Whatever was hunting it backs off before anything has to be settled with teeth.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The frill has no muscles of its own. Rods of bone and cartilage in the throat swing it wide open, so the frill flies out at the same instant the jaws drop.","more":"The rods run off the hyoid — the same set of throat bones that works your tongue when you swallow. Shut the mouth and the whole frill folds flat along the shoulders like a closed umbrella, and the lizard is a plain gray stick again.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"speed","text":"When bluffing fails, it runs — on two legs. The front feet lift clear of the ground, the tail swings out as a counterweight, and it sprints flat out for the nearest tree.","more":"It doesn't stop at the trunk. It goes straight up, then shuffles round to the far side and freezes with the wood between itself and whatever was chasing. Walk around the tree and it walks too, staying exactly opposite you.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"Open, the frill is wider across than the lizard is long from nose to hips. All of that folds away into a neck you could close one hand around.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"It spends more than nine-tenths of the day up a tree, pressed flat against the bark, gray on gray, not moving. Most people walk straight past one.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Termites, by the thousand. Ants and centipedes too, and in the wet season fat moth caterpillars. Almost everything it eats is smaller than your thumbnail.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"Through the long dry season it nearly switches off. It sits in one tree for weeks, barely eating and slowly losing weight. It is waiting for the rain.","more":"In northern Australia the dry season can run half the year. Researchers tracking frilled lizards found individuals that shifted only a few trees in months. When the monsoon finally breaks, they come back on, feeding hard and putting the weight straight back.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"The frill is not only for enemies. Males flare it at each other over territory, heads bobbing. The argument usually ends right there. It is loud, but nobody gets bitten.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"She digs a burrow in warm sandy soil, drops in anywhere from four to twenty-odd eggs, fills it in and walks away. No parent ever comes back to check.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Most of its teeth are fused straight onto the top edge of the jawbone. They do not sit in sockets. Once they wear down, an adult grows no replacements.","more":"Biologists call this acrodont dentition. It makes a jaw that grips hard and doesn't lose teeth in a struggle, but there is no spare set coming along behind. An old frilled lizard is chewing termites with worn-down stumps.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Australia liked it enough to put it on money. The frilled lizard, frill up, sat on the country's two-cent coin for twenty-five years.","more":"Then Australia stopped making one- and two-cent pieces altogether, and the coin was retired. Millions of them are still sitting in drawers and jars around the country. Each one has a small furious lizard on the back.","kind":"record"}],"wonder":"Folded away it is nothing at all — a small gray lizard on a gray branch. God tucked a whole umbrella into that neck and left no seam showing."},{"id":"galapagos-tortoise","name":"Galápagos tortoise","group":"reptiles","blurb":"The largest tortoise on Earth. It is a slow, plant-eating giant. It lives on a scatter of volcanic islands in the Pacific Ocean.","size":"Shell about 4.3 ft (1.3 m) long, and the biggest ones weigh around 551 lb (250 kg).","homes":["grassland","desert","mountain"],"stats":{"weight":551,"length":4.3,"life":100,"speed":0.2},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"The heaviest one ever weighed came in at 919 lb (417 kg) — heavier than five grown men, walking about on four legs like tree stumps.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Passing a hundred years old is ordinary here. The oldest well-documented tortoise was kept in captivity and lived past 170. It was alive before cars, planes or electric light.","more":"A hatchling breaking out of its egg this morning could still be grazing that same hillside when the children alive today are grandparents. And be out there again, chewing the same grass, when their grandchildren are old.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"build","text":"Look at the front of the shell. On the wet green islands it curves down like a helmet. On the dry islands it lifts at the front like a saddle.","more":"The saddle-shaped ones have long necks and long legs. They live where the food grows high, on cactus pads well above the ground. The helmet-shaped ones live in the humid highlands. Thick low plants grow underfoot there, so they never need to reach for anything.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"A Galápagos tortoise can go more than six months without a drink, and up to a whole year with no food and no water at all.","more":"It carries its own supply. Water is stored in the bladder and around the base of the neck, and fat under the shell is the fuel. On islands where the rain sometimes simply doesn't come, that is the difference between a hard year and a dead one.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"A tortoise stands up tall, stretches its neck right out and holds still, and small finches hop over and pick ticks off the skin it just exposed. Nobody can ask the tortoise what it means by the pose; the best read is that it is an invitation.","more":"Both sides win. The birds get a meal, and the tortoise gets cleaned in the folds it could never possibly reach. The pose is deliberate: the tortoise strikes it, and the finches come because they know exactly what it means.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"speed","text":"Top speed is roughly 0.2 mph (0.3 km/h). Just strolling to the kitchen, you are moving more than ten times faster than a Galápagos tortoise can run.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"It sleeps up to sixteen hours a day. After dawn it lies in the sun for an hour or two, doing nothing whatsoever. A cold body cannot digest breakfast.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"Some walk the same route up a volcano and back down every single year, climbing to the green highlands when the lowlands dry out. The paths are worn into the ground.","more":"Researchers fitted tortoises with GPS tags and watched them climb hundreds of meters, the biggest adults making the trip while smaller ones stayed put. Nobody teaches them the route. The same animals walk the same lines, season after season.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"She digs a hole with her back feet, drops in a clutch of round eggs — often fewer than a dozen — seals it over with mud and leaves. Warm nests hatch female; cooler nests hatch male.","more":"The hatchlings dig their way up months later, each about the size of your fist, and are on their own from the first minute. Nearly everything on the island is bigger than they are, so they hide, and they grow slowly, and they wait.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Threatened, it pulls head and legs in and lets the shell do the work. That shell isn't luggage it carries — it is skeleton, ribs and backbone grown into plates of solid bone.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Cactus pads, grass, leaves, lichens and fallen fruit. A young tortoise can work through around a sixth of its own body weight in dry plant food in one day.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"An animal that can go a year with no food and no water, on islands where the rain sometimes just doesn't come. Provided for, long before it ever needed it."},{"id":"leatherback-turtle","name":"Leatherback sea turtle","group":"reptiles","blurb":"The biggest sea turtle in the world, and the only one with no hard shell. Its back is thick rubbery skin stretched over a mosaic of tiny bones.","size":"About 6.6 ft (2 m) long and up to 882 lb (400 kg).","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"length":6.6,"weight":882,"speed":22},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"Grown leatherbacks run 5.9–7.2 ft (1.8–2.2 m) (6–7 ft) nose to tail and weigh 551–1,543 lb (250–700 kg) (550–1,500 lb). One would not fit through your front door.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Every other sea turtle wears a hard shell of plates. This one doesn't. Its back is leathery skin over thousands of tiny bones, ridged with seven long keels like the hull of a boat.","more":"The little bones are called osteoderms. Because they sit packed together instead of fused into one rigid plate, the whole back can flex. A stiff shell would be crushed at the depths this animal swims to. A flexible one simply gives.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"One leatherback was recorded diving to 4,200 ft (1,280 m). That is nearly three Empire State Buildings stacked end to end, straight down into black water.","more":"Deeper than most whales go. Dives of up to 70 minutes have been recorded, in cold and total darkness. Down there the pressure is more than a hundred times what you feel standing on the beach. Then the turtle surfaces and breathes like nothing happened.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It eats jellyfish, and almost nothing else. A big leatherback can get through around three-quarters of its own body weight in jellyfish in a single day.","more":"Jellyfish are mostly water. That is an enormous volume of food carrying very little energy. The leatherback answers the problem by hardly ever stopping. It is also big enough to hold everything it swallows.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Its throat is lined with hundreds of stiff spines, every one pointing backwards, running from the mouth all the way down to the stomach. A jellyfish goes down and cannot come back up.","more":"The spines are called papillae. Seawater comes in along with the prey and gets pushed back out through the mouth. The spines pin the jellyfish in place while that happens. It is a built-in strainer, for food with no bones and nothing to grip.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"It is a reptile that does not go cold. Leatherbacks have been measured with body temperatures up to 18°C (32°F) warmer than the sea around them.","more":"Biologists call it gigantothermy. A very large body loses heat slowly. Working muscles make heat constantly, and a layer of oily fat holds it in. That is how a turtle feeds off Norway and Canada, in water cold enough to hurt your hands.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"Some cross entire oceans. One tracked leatherback swam over 12,000 miles (20,000 km) between the beach where it nested and the far side of the Pacific.","more":"That journey took 647 days — the turtle was not racing. It fed as it went, drifting through cold water where jellyfish swarm, then turned for the tropical sand it started from.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"speed","text":"In a short burst a leatherback has been clocked at about 22 mph (35 km/h) — faster than you can pedal a bike. Cruising, it moves at roughly walking pace.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"She comes ashore at night and digs with her back flippers. She lays around a hundred eggs the size of billiard balls. She covers them over and is back in the sea before dawn.","more":"On top of the real eggs she lays a batch of smaller ones with no yolk inside. They never hatch. Every leatherback nest has them sitting in the upper layer, and no other sea turtle lays them in anything like these numbers.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Each leatherback has a pale pink patch on the top of its head, and no two are shaped the same. Researchers photograph the patch to tell individual turtles apart.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"It nests on tropical beaches hot enough to burn your feet and feeds in water cold enough to ache. No other reptile lives across a range like that.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Skin where the others have armor — and it still dives deeper than the whales. God does not build every strength to look like one."},{"id":"saltwater-crocodile","name":"Saltwater crocodile","group":"reptiles","blurb":"The largest living reptile. It is an armored ambush hunter of river mouths and coastlines. Fresh water and the open sea suit it equally.","size":"A big male is about 14.8 ft (4.5 m) long and can weigh 1,102 lb (500 kg).","homes":["ocean","freshwater"],"stats":{"length":14.8,"weight":1102,"speed":15,"life":70},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"The largest one ever caught and measured was 20 ft 3 in (6.17 m) long. It weighed 2,370 lb (1,075 kg).","more":"Males average about 13.1 to 14.8 ft (4 to 4.5 m). Females are far smaller, usually around 9.8 ft (3 m), so a big male and a grown female barely look like the same animal. The largest males hold the best stretches of river against all comers.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Scientists clamped a force meter between the jaws of a 15.1 ft (4.6 m) saltwater crocodile. It bit down with 16,414 newtons, about 3,700 pounds of force. No living animal has been measured biting harder.","more":"The team tested every crocodilian species they could get hold of, one after another. The pattern was simple. The bigger the animal, the harder the bite. The saltwater crocodile is the biggest, so it tops the list.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Those black speckles across its jaws are pressure sensors. In total darkness, in water like brown soup, a crocodile can feel a fish move nearby from the ripple it makes.","more":"They are called integumentary sense organs, and on a saltwater crocodile they are not just on the head — they are scattered right across the body. An alligator has them on the face only. This animal feels the water with its back.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"On its tongue sit salt glands, small pores that pump salt back out of the body. That is why this crocodile can live in the sea and an alligator cannot.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"It doesn't swim across open ocean so much as surf it. Tracked crocodiles wait for the current to turn their way, ride it, then stop and rest when it turns against them.","more":"One tracked animal covered 370 miles (590 km) of coastline in 25 days this way. It barely swam at all. This is how crocodiles turn up on islands hundreds of kilometers from anywhere. They do not battle the sea. They time it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It waits. Sometimes for hours, with only eyes and nostrils breaking the surface. Then a lunge, a grip, and a spin — the death roll twists off a piece too big to swallow.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"She heaps up a mound of mud and rotting plants, buries 40 to 60 eggs inside it, and guards it for months. Rotting plants give off heat, so the heap keeps the eggs warm.","more":"The temperature inside that mound decides what hatches. Around 31 to 32°C produces mostly males; warmer or cooler produces females. A couple of degrees, in a pile of leaves, settles the whole clutch one way or the other.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"The babies call before they hatch. From inside their eggs, buried under the mound, they chirp — and the mother digs them out and carries them down to the water in her mouth.","more":"The calling does two jobs at once. It tells the mother it is time to open the nest, and it keeps the clutch together so they all emerge as one group. She may carry a dozen at a time, in jaws that bite harder than any other animal's, without leaving a scratch.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Those ridged lumps down its back are not just thick skin. Each one has a plate of bone inside it, called an osteoderm — armor with a skeleton of its own.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"It never runs out of teeth. A replacement is always growing in underneath each one, ready to push up the moment the old tooth goes, and it happens all its life.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"A resting crocodile can stay under about an hour, and voluntary dives of up to two hours have been reported. It drops its heartbeat to a couple of beats a minute to manage it.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Eyes, ears and nostrils set in one straight line along the top of the skull — the whole animal vanishes and still sees, hears and breathes. Someone thought that through."},{"id":"alligator","name":"American alligator","group":"reptiles","blurb":"A broad-snouted, freshwater cousin of the crocodile. It rules the swamps, rivers and marshes of the southeastern United States.","size":"About 11.2 ft (3.4 m) long and up to 507 lb (230 kg).","homes":["freshwater","forest"],"stats":{"length":11.2,"weight":507,"speed":11,"life":50},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"A grown male runs 11.2 to 15.1 ft (3.4 to 4.6 m) (11 to 15 ft), females rather smaller. The heaviest males reach roughly 1,000 lb (450 kg) — half a tonne lying in a meter of water.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Broad, rounded snout, and when the mouth shuts every tooth vanishes inside. On a crocodile the big fourth tooth juts up outside the closed lip. That is how you tell them apart in a photo.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"A large American alligator has been measured biting at about 13,000 newtons. That is nearly 3,000 pounds of force pressing down through its back teeth.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"The muscles that slam an alligator's jaws shut are enormous. The muscles that open them are feeble — researchers hold a mid-sized one's mouth closed with a few wraps of tape.","more":"It is all one-way power. The same animal that can crush a turtle shell cannot shove a hand off its own snout. Handlers rely on that gap every working day of their lives, and it never stops being strange.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"A bellowing male makes a sound so low you feel it in your chest before you hear it. Below the roar is infrasound, and it makes the water on his back leap into dancing droplets.","more":"The droplets jump in a ring above his ribs while he holds head and tail up clear of the surface. Other alligators answer from across the marsh, and in spring a whole swamp can go off at once, one after another.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"She heaps up a mound of mud and plants, buries twenty to fifty eggs in it, and guards it. Cool nests hatch females. Hot nests hatch females too — only the middle makes males.","more":"A narrow band around 32.5 to 33.5°C produces males. Cooler than that or hotter than that, and the whole clutch comes out female. When the babies grunt from inside the mound, she opens it up. She carries them to the water in her mouth. She stays with them for a year or more.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"In a hard freeze, alligators tip their snouts up through the surface. They let the pond ice over around them. Each one breathes through its own hole in the ice until the thaw comes.","more":"They can hold that position for days. The rest of the body sits still and cold below, running so slowly it barely needs anything at all — a state called brumation. When the ice breaks up they slide out and carry on.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"It digs. Snout, feet and tail scoop out a deep pool that still holds water when the marsh dries. In a drought, every fish, frog, turtle and wading bird for miles crowds into it.","more":"These are called gator holes, and the biggest are wider than a house. The alligator that dug it eats well, of course. But the best read is bigger than that. Without those holes, a dry summer would empty the marsh completely. A great deal of the swamp survives because one animal was hungry and had claws.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"food","text":"Fish, turtles, snakes, birds, whatever fits. And fruit — alligators have been found with wild grapes and elderberries in their stomachs, taken off low branches over the water.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Shine a flashlight across a swamp at night and pairs of red coals stare back. A mirror layer behind each eye, the tapetum lucidum, bounces light back through so it gets used twice.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"It carries 74 to 80 teeth at once and replaces each one as it wears out. Count the replacements up over a long life and the total works out at more than 2,000.","kind":"worked"}],"wonder":"The hole it digs to save itself in a drought ends up saving the whole swamp along with it."},{"id":"horned-lizard","name":"Texas horned lizard","group":"reptiles","blurb":"A flat, spiky little desert lizard that lives almost entirely on stinging ants. When a coyote gets too close, it shoots blood out of its own eyes.","size":"About as long as your hand, and flatter than a bar of soap","homes":["desert","grassland"],"stats":{"length":0.4,"life":5},"facts":[{"cat":"defence","text":"Push a Texas horned lizard too far and it fires a jet of its own blood out of the corner of its eye, up to 5 feet (1.5 m). It aims for the face.","more":"It clamps down on the veins draining its head. Blood pressure climbs until tiny vessels around the eyelid burst. Coyotes, foxes and dogs hate the taste and back off shaking their heads. Predatory birds are not bothered at all, so the lizard saves the trick for mammals.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"About 70 percent of everything it eats is harvester ants. That makes it one of the very few animals willing to swallow an insect armed with a serious sting.","more":"A harvester ant stings hard enough to make a grown person yell. The horned lizard licks them up whole, one at a time, and its stomach takes up more of its body than most lizards' does. An ant is a tiny meal, so it needs a great many of them, and it needs somewhere to put them.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"A big female is about 5 inches (13 cm) from her nose to the base of her tail. She would sit comfortably in your cupped palm, if she would sit still.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"It is built like a living pincushion. The two long spikes at the back of its head are true horns: solid bone, growing straight out of the skull.","more":"Those head spines are not scales and not claws. They are bone, grown out of the skull itself. When something leans in too close, the lizard tips its head forward and jabs, turning the widest, sharpest part of itself toward the trouble.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"Its first defense is doing absolutely nothing. Flattened against the ground and color-matched to the local dirt, it will let you walk right past and never twitch.","more":"Lizards from red-dirt country come out reddish; lizards from pale limestone country come out pale. It also flattens sideways to kill its own shadow. A rounded animal casts a dark giveaway blob on bright sand. A pancake does not.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"There is a third eye on top of its head. It cannot see pictures with it, but it reads light and dark, which helps the lizard track the sun through the day.","more":"The parietal eye is a pale spot in the middle of the skull with its own lens and light-sensing cells, wired straight to the brain. Plenty of lizards have one. It does not form an image. It registers brightness, which is enough to run a daily rhythm and time a basking session.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"In a country with almost no standing water, it drinks through its skin. Rain landing anywhere on its back runs into tiny channels between the scales and travels to its mouth.","more":"The channels work by capillary action, the same reason water climbs into a paper towel instead of just sitting on it. In a storm the lizard arches its back and the whole animal becomes a gutter system feeding the corners of its jaw. Then it opens and closes its mouth to pump the water in.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Cornered, it gulps air and swells up like a spiny balloon. A lizard that was flat and easy to swallow becomes a wide, sharp shape that will not go down a throat.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"The blood trick is expensive. Squirting can cost the lizard up to 6 percent of its entire body weight, which is why it holds that card back until nothing else has worked.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A female digs a burrow, lays two or three dozen eggs at the bottom, covers them over and walks away. The hatchlings emerge about the size of a quarter, spines already in place.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"When the nights turn cold it digs into loose soil and shuts down for the winter. This is called brumation: months underground, not eating, barely breathing, waiting out the season.","more":"It is not quite the same as a bear's hibernation. A brumating reptile simply runs cold and slow because the air around it is cold and slow. Come spring, it warms back up on the surface and starts hunting ants again as though nothing happened.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Texas made it the official state reptile, and then it started vanishing from Texas.","more":"Imported fire ants are a big part of it. They shove out the native harvester ants the lizard depends on, and a horned lizard cannot simply switch to another dinner. Pesticides sprayed to kill ants wipe out the food supply just as thoroughly, and plowed-up grassland leaves nowhere to dig in.","kind":"record"}],"wonder":"Bone horns, a skin that drinks rain, and a blood cannon behind each eye. Every part of it fitted to a hard, dry country."},{"id":"axolotl","name":"Axolotl","group":"amphibians","blurb":"A pink Mexican salamander with feathery gills sprouting from the outside of its head. It never leaves the water. It can regrow almost any part of itself.","size":"About 9.5 in (24 cm) long, though a big one reaches the length of your forearm.","homes":["freshwater"],"stats":{"length":0.8,"life":12},"facts":[{"cat":"build","text":"Those pink feathers on its head are gills, worn on the outside where you can watch them work. Blood runs through the frills and pulls oxygen straight out of the water.","more":"Three stalks on each side, each branching into fine filaments. Feathery means a huge surface area for oxygen to cross, packed into a small space. When the water runs low on oxygen the axolotl fans them; when it is startled it snaps them flat against its neck.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Most salamanders start life in the water, then grow lungs and strong legs and crawl out onto land. The axolotl skips the whole business and stays in the water for life.","more":"It keeps its baby gills and its baby tail fin and grows up anyway. Biologists call this neoteny: keeping juvenile features into adulthood. A full-grown axolotl can breed and hunt and live a decade while still wearing the body plan of a larva.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Cut off an axolotl's leg and it grows a new one. Bone, muscle, nerve and skin, in the right order, in the right places, with no scar. It can do this over and over.","more":"It regrows far more than limbs: tail, jaw, parts of the eye and heart, even sections of its own brain and spinal cord. The wound closes without scarring, a bud of cells gathers at the stump, and the missing piece is rebuilt from scratch.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"Most axolotls are about 9.6 in (24 cm) long. A big one reaches roughly 12 inches (30 cm). Lay a school ruler beside it and the animal covers the whole thing. Larger than that is rare.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"In the entire wild world there is one place axolotls live: a maze of canals and floating gardens at the edge of Mexico City. Nowhere else. Not anywhere.","more":"Lake Xochimilco is what remains of a much bigger lake system that was drained away as the city grew. Counts have collapsed. One survey found roughly 6,000 axolotls per square kilometer; a decade later the same water held about 100; a later four-month search turned up two.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It does not bite its food. It opens its mouth fast and the water rushes in, dragging the meal with it. Worms, insect larvae and small fish all get inhaled whole.","more":"This is called suction feeding, and the axolotl finds its target by smell before it snaps. Its teeth are tiny, barely breaking the surface of the gums. They grip, they do not chew or cut. Everything goes down in one piece.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"It breathes three different ways. Those external gills are one. Oxygen also passes straight through its skin. And it rises to the surface to gulp air into a small pair of lungs.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Young axolotls crowded together bite each other's legs off. It is less of a catastrophe than it sounds, because the leg simply grows back.","more":"Larvae snap at anything that moves, including a brother's limb or a sister's gill frill. Anyone raising them either separates the young or accepts a lot of missing toes for a while. In a healthy tank, every one of those limbs is rebuilt.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A female lays hundreds of eggs in one go, sticking them one by one onto water plants with her back feet, and then swims away. Nothing guards them.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Its instruction manual is enormous. The axolotl genome is roughly 32 billion letters long, about ten times the length of a human's.","more":"Longer does not mean more complicated. Axolotls carry around 23,000 genes, not far off our own count. The extra length is mostly vast stretches of repeated sequence sitting in between. Reading the whole thing took an international team years of work.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"copied","text":"Hospitals want what the axolotl has. Researchers study its healing because a human wound closes with scar tissue, while an axolotl's closes with the actual missing part.","more":"Human skin repairs a deep cut by laying down tough, blank collagen. That is a patch, not a rebuild, and it never becomes skin again. Axolotl tissue skips the patch entirely and reconstructs blood vessels, nerve and bone in the correct arrangement.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"home","text":"The axolotl is close to gone in the wild. At the same time there are more of them alive than ever, because they are bred by the thousand in laboratories, classrooms and home tanks.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"A body that answers a lost limb by quietly building another one. Nothing about that is ordinary."},{"id":"glass-frog","name":"Glass frog","group":"amphibians","blurb":"A small green rainforest frog with see-through skin on its underside. Turn one over and you are looking straight in at its beating heart.","size":"About as wide across as a bottle cap","homes":["rainforest","forest","freshwater"],"stats":{"length":0.1},"facts":[{"cat":"build","text":"Turn a glass frog belly-up and the skin is translucent. You can watch the heart beating, the liver sitting there, and food traveling through the gut.","more":"Not the whole frog. The back is opaque leaf-green, and it is the underside that is clear. In some species a sheet of shiny white tissue covers part of the works. So you get the heart hidden and the intestines on display, or the other way round.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"Asleep, it does something no other animal is known to do. It pulls almost all its red blood cells out of circulation and parks them inside its liver.","more":"Blood is what makes a small body visible, so with the red packed away the sleeping frog turns markedly harder to see. Packing blood cells that tightly ought to cause dangerous clotting. This frog does it every day, then releases them again on waking, when it needs to move.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"It sleeps pressed against a leaf and lets the daylight come through it. Green leaf, green frog, and edges that fade out instead of ending.","more":"A solid frog on a sunlit leaf makes a dark blob with a hard outline, and hunting birds read outlines. Because light passes through the glass frog's legs and flanks, its edges blur into the brightness of the leaf rather than cutting a shape across it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"Most glass frogs are about an inch (2.5 cm) long. Even the largest species in the whole family only reaches about 3 inches (7.5 cm).","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"The eggs are laid on a leaf hanging out over a stream. When the tadpoles hatch, they wriggle to the edge and drop into the running water below.","more":"It is a serious fall for something that small. They land in fast water and immediately burrow down into the leaf litter and silt on the stream bed. The leaf nursery keeps the eggs safely away from fish, and the current takes over from there.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"In many glass frogs it is the father who stays. He sits with the clutch for days, turning the eggs, and when they start drying out he urinates on them to keep them wet.","more":"A clutch of twenty or thirty eggs sitting on an exposed leaf can dry out in an afternoon or be found by wasps. The male crouches over them, keeps the jelly damp with his own water, and shoves off anything that comes for them.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"In many glass frogs even the bones are green. A pigment tints them. Most animals treat that pigment as waste and throw it away.","more":"The color comes from biliverdin, the same compound that turns a healing bruise green. Most bodies get rid of it as fast as they can. These frogs hold on to it and let it stain the skeleton, so the animal is green the whole way through.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Males call from leaves above rushing water, a single high peep repeated all night. Each one holds his own leaf, and they space themselves out along the bank.","more":"Streams are loud, so the calls are pitched high and thin to cut through the roar of the water. Stand beside the right forest stream after dark and you will hear dozens of them, none of them visible, all of them somewhere just above your head.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It hunts after dark for anything small enough to fit: flies, crickets, spiders, ants. Whatever it swallows, you can then watch making its way through it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Glass frogs live only where fast, clean water runs through forest. No stream, no glass frogs. The whole life cycle depends on a leaf with moving water underneath it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"It has no venom, no speed and no size to fall back on. Its entire defense is a body you cannot easily pick out from the leaf it is sitting on.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"A window into a working body, out walking about on a leaf, all the machinery on show and running perfectly."},{"id":"poison-dart-frog","name":"Golden poison frog","group":"amphibians","blurb":"A frog the length of your thumb, colored like a highlighter pen. Its skin carries enough poison to kill about ten grown adults.","size":"About 2.4 in (6 cm) long, as long as your thumb, and about as heavy as a AA battery","homes":["rainforest"],"stats":{"length":0.2,"weight":0},"facts":[{"cat":"defence","text":"One wild golden poison frog carries roughly a milligram of batrachotoxin on its skin. That is a smear you could barely see. Nobody has tested it on people. Scaled up from lab mice, it works out at about ten adults.","more":"Batrachotoxin jams open the tiny gates that let sodium into nerve and muscle cells. Those gates are meant to flick open and shut to fire a signal. Held open, muscles seize and stop working, including the heart. There is no antidote for it.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"weird","text":"The frog does not manufacture the poison. It collects it from what it eats, which means a frog raised in a tank on crickets and fruit flies is completely harmless.","more":"In the forest it eats tiny insects and mites carrying the raw chemicals, and it concentrates them in glands in its skin. Remove that diet and the supply stops. Zoo-bred golden poison frogs can be safely handled, which is the only reason anyone can study them closely.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Poisonous is not the same as venomous. Nothing about this frog injects you. No fangs, no sting. The poison sits on the outside of the skin.","more":"Venomous animals deliver a dose into you. Snakes, wasps and stonefish all do that. Poisonous animals are dangerous to eat or to handle. The golden poison frog is the second kind. It does its damage through a cut, a mouth or an eye, not through a wound of its own making.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"It makes no attempt whatsoever to hide. That screaming yellow, mint green or orange is called aposematic coloring. The best read is that it works as a signal rather than camouflage. The message is: leave me alone.","more":"Camouflage is for animals that can be eaten. This one advertises. It sits in plain sight on the forest floor in broad daylight with its color blazing. Plenty of brightly colored animals in the world are bluffing about how dangerous they are. This one is not bluffing.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"size","text":"It is the giant of the poison dart frogs at about 2 inches (5 cm) long, which still means the whole animal would sit on a matchbox.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A parent carries the tadpoles up into the trees on its own back, one trip at a time. Each one gets dropped into a puddle of rainwater held in a plant.","more":"Bromeliads are plants whose leaves form a cup, and the cup holds water: a private pond in the canopy with no fish in it. The tadpoles wriggle onto the parent's wet back and stick there for the climb. Water-filled tree holes work just as well.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It spends its days picking ants, termites, mites and tiny beetles off the forest floor, one flick of the tongue at a time. Its entire weapon comes out of that diet.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Every wild golden poison frog on Earth lives in one strip of rainforest. It sits on the Pacific coast of Colombia, in one of the wettest places anywhere.","more":"It needs heavy rain, warmth above 26°C (79°F) and thick humid air. Its whole range covers less than 5,000 square kilometers, a patch you could drive across in a morning, and it is shrinking as forest is cleared. The IUCN lists the species as Endangered.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"copied","text":"The Emberá people of Colombia arm blowgun darts with these frogs. They wipe the dart tip across the frog's back, and that tip stays deadly for two years or more.","more":"Only a handful of frog species are used this way. The golden poison frog needs no preparation at all, because the toxin is already sitting on the skin. Hunters handle the frogs with leaves, never bare hands. This is where the name poison dart frog comes from.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Males call with a long trill, and they do it in daylight, out in the open. An animal this dangerous has no particular reason to keep quiet.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"There are well over a hundred kinds of poison dart frog, in just about every color you can name. Most could not seriously hurt you. This one is in a class entirely of its own.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Bright as a warning sign and impossible to overlook. Some creatures were simply never meant to hide."},{"id":"goliath-frog","name":"Goliath frog","group":"amphibians","blurb":"The largest frog on Earth. This West African river frog is longer than a dinner plate is wide. It is heavy enough that you would want both hands.","size":"Body about 1 ft (30 cm) long — longer still with its legs stretched out — and about 7 lb (3.2 kg)","homes":["rainforest","freshwater"],"stats":{"weight":7,"length":1.0,"life":15},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"Body alone, a big goliath frog measures about 13 inches (32 cm), longer than a dinner plate is wide. Stretch the legs out behind it and the whole animal passes 30 inches (80 cm).","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"The heaviest one ever properly weighed came in at 7.3 lb (3.3 kg).","more":"No other frog is close. A big American bullfrog, the largest frog most people will ever meet in person, tops out at around a pound (0.5 kg). The goliath frog is in a size class entirely of its own.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"It has no vocal sac. Other frogs inflate a balloon of skin under the chin to boom across a pond. This one has nothing to inflate. So it whistles with its mouth open.","more":"A vocal sac works as a resonator, and it makes a frog far louder than its size suggests. The largest frog in the world does without one entirely. Its whistle is thin for such a huge animal and is easily lost under the noise of the rapids it lives beside.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"It builds nurseries. A goliath frog clears a patch of river gravel into a shallow pond about 3 feet (1 m) across, shoving stones out of the way as it works.","more":"Researchers watching them recorded three kinds of nest. One is a rock pool cleaned out of leaves and debris. One is an existing hollow, widened and dammed. One is a basin dug fresh into the gravel. Some of the stones they shift weigh a couple of kilograms. Very few frogs anywhere build anything at all.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"The nest gets guarded. Once the eggs are in the cleared pool, an adult stays with them through the night, which is unusual work for a frog.","more":"The pools sit at the edge of fast water, but out of the main current. Eggs and tadpoles are not swept downstream, and river fish cannot easily get in. The frog builds a safe pool and then stands over it all night. That is close to what a bird does with a nest.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"speed","text":"For all that bulk it is a serious jumper. One leap covers somewhere between 4 and 10 feet (1.2 to 3 m). It goes from a standing start, with no run-up at all.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Adults eat crabs, insects, worms, spiders, millipedes and the occasional small snake. They hunt by sitting completely still at the water's edge and waiting.","more":"It does not chase anything. It waits, and whatever moves within reach and will fit in its mouth gets swallowed whole, because frogs do not chew. Crabs are a favorite, hard shell and all.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"It lives in one narrow band of forest in Cameroon and Equatorial Guinea, always beside fast rivers and waterfalls. Take away the rapids and the frog is gone.","more":"It needs clean, fast, oxygen-rich water running over sand and gravel. That is a very specific address, and it is disappearing. The IUCN lists the goliath frog as Endangered, hunted for food and squeezed by logging and farming.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Its tadpoles are nothing special to look at. They are no bigger than an ordinary frog's. Everything remarkable about a goliath frog happens after it climbs out of the water.","more":"They are fussy eaters, though. Goliath tadpoles graze on a single kind of water plant that grows only on rocks in fast, splashing water. No plant, no tadpoles, which is one more reason the whole species is tied to the rapids.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"It is powerfully strong and, in the ways that count, defenseless. No poison, no bite worth worrying about. What it has is legs, and one jump puts it into white water.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"A frog given a size no other frog gets, and forelimbs strong enough to build with, set down beside a waterfall of all places."},{"id":"wood-frog","name":"Wood frog","group":"amphibians","blurb":"A small brown forest frog with a dark robber's mask across its eyes. It survives northern winters by letting its own body freeze solid.","size":"About 1.5 to 3 in (4 to 7 cm) long — small enough to sit in your cupped palm.","homes":["forest","freshwater"],"stats":{"length":0.2,"life":3},"facts":[{"cat":"weird","text":"In winter this frog freezes. Not nearly — actually. Ice fills the spaces around its organs, its heart stops, and its brain goes quiet.","more":"A frozen wood frog has no heartbeat, no breathing, no blood flow and no measurable brain activity. Well over half the water in its body has turned to ice. Warm it up and within hours the heart restarts, a leg twitches, and the frog hops away.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Before the freeze its liver floods the body with glucose — sugar. The sugar works as antifreeze inside every cell, so ice can only form around cells, never in them.","more":"Ice crystals growing inside a cell would slice it apart. Wood frogs pack their cells with glucose and urea, which lower the freezing point and hold water in place. Ice is allowed to form only in the gaps between muscles and just under the skin.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"It lives farther north than any other frog in North America. Its range crosses the Arctic Circle, into country where winter runs most of the year.","more":"Alaskan wood frogs have been recorded frozen solid for about 193 days straight. That is over six months, at average temperatures near minus 6°C (21°F). Then spring arrives and they thaw. Within days they are calling from the nearest pond.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"A pond full of wood frogs in early spring does not sound like frogs. It sounds like ducks — a low clucking quack rolling across the water.","more":"Males call for only a week or two, sometimes just a few days. Biologists call this explosive breeding. The whole population arrives, pairs up, lays eggs and leaves. The ice has not finished melting off the edges of the pond.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"It breeds in vernal pools — woodland puddles that fill with snowmelt and vanish by summer. No fish can survive there, and the best read is that this is exactly the point.","more":"Fish eat tadpoles. A pool that dries out every year never holds fish, so wood frog tadpoles grow up with far fewer things hunting them. The catch: they must finish growing legs before the water disappears.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"babies","text":"One female lays a jelly ball about the size of your fist. It holds a thousand eggs or more. Dozens of females pile theirs into the same sunny corner.","more":"Massed together in the top few inches of water, the egg rafts sit slightly warmer than eggs laid alone. Warmer eggs develop faster, which matters a great deal when the pool may be dry mud by July.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"Look at the eyes. That dark patch running back from each one is why people call it the masked frog — a bandit's mask on a body the color of wet leaf litter.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Grab one and it screams — a short, piercing cry, startlingly loud for something you could hold in a cupped hand.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Adults hunt the forest floor for beetles, spiders, moth larvae, slugs and snails. Tadpoles are grazers instead, scraping algae off sunken leaves and stones.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"Wood frogs spend most of the year in forest, not water. Some walk a few hundred meters (several hundred yards) back to their home pool, crossing roads and leaf litter in one wet night.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"Nose to rump, 1.5 to 3 inches (4 to 7 cm). Females run noticeably bigger than males.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Everything it needs to come back from frozen is already packed inside it before the first frost arrives."},{"id":"surinam-toad","name":"Surinam toad","group":"amphibians","blurb":"A flat brown tongueless frog that looks exactly like a dead leaf. It lies on the bottom of muddy South American ponds. It raises its babies inside the skin of its own back.","size":"About 7 in (18 cm) long, close to a dollar bill in length — and nearly as flat as one.","homes":["rainforest","freshwater"],"stats":{"length":0.6},"facts":[{"cat":"babies","text":"The babies grow inside the mother's back. After mating, her skin swells up over the fertilised eggs and seals each one into its own pocket with a lid on top.","more":"For three to five months she carries them, her back a honeycomb of bumps. Then the lids open and tiny toads push their way out, one at a time. Afterwards she sheds that used layer of skin and starts over.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"There is no tadpole stage at all. What crawls out of her back is a finished toad about the size of your thumbnail — flat and brown, a miniature of its mother.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"It has no tongue. None. It cannot flick prey into its mouth, so instead it inhales — snapping its jaws open and sucking prey in with a rush of water.","more":"Every frog in its family is tongueless. The Surinam toad feeds by suction. The mouth and throat expand so suddenly that water rushes in. Whatever was drifting in front of the toad comes with it and goes down whole.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"The tips of its front fingers end in tiny stars. They are feelers, sweeping the mud for food in water far too dark to see through.","more":"Its eyes are tiny lidless black beads, close to useless in a black-water swamp. The star-tipped fingers do the searching instead, then shovel whatever they find toward the mouth. Some books call it the star-fingered toad for exactly this reason.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Mating is a slow series of underwater somersaults. The pair loops upward together, flips upside down at the top of the arc, and releases eggs while both are on their backs.","more":"As they roll, the eggs drop onto the female's back and the male presses them down into her skin. They repeat the loop again and again, a few eggs at a time, until roughly a hundred are in place.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Males call underwater without pushing out any air at all. Hard parts inside the voice box snap together, and the sharp metallic click carries away through the water.","more":"Most frogs balloon out a throat sac and push air across vocal cords. A Surinam toad does neither. The clicking is made entirely inside the larynx, and sound carries much further through water than it ever would through air.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"Lying still on the bottom, it is not a toad. It is a dead leaf — brown, ragged-edged, flat, and completely uninteresting to anything hunting.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"A lateral line runs along its flanks. It is a row of pressure sensors. A fish uses the same equipment to feel movement in the water around it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Everything about it is built for water and nothing for land. Out of the pond it is close to helpless, barely able to drag itself along.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Worms, insects, small crustaceans and little fish — anything that drifts close enough to be sucked in whole. There is no chewing involved.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"Females reach about 6.5 inches (17 cm) nose to rump, bigger than males. The whole animal is squashed almost flat. It looks as though something heavy stood on it.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"A nursery, a hiding place, and a mother's own skin — all the same thing."},{"id":"red-eyed-tree-frog","name":"Red-eyed tree frog","group":"amphibians","blurb":"A small neon-green rainforest frog with enormous red eyes, orange feet and blue-and-yellow striped sides. It keeps all of that hidden until the second it needs it.","size":"About as long as your thumb.","homes":["rainforest","freshwater"],"stats":{"length":0.2,"life":5},"facts":[{"cat":"defence","text":"Asleep, it is a green leaf. Touch it and the eyes snap open — two red discs — while orange feet and blue-and-yellow flanks flash out. Then it is gone.","more":"Biologists call this a deimatic display: a sudden burst of color meant to startle rather than fight. The predator stalls for a fraction of a second working out what just happened, and that fraction is all the frog needs.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Its eggs decide when to hatch. If a snake starts eating the clutch, the embryos still inside burst out early and drop into the water below.","more":"Left alone they hatch at six or seven days. Under attack they can go at four or five, and the hatching itself takes only seconds. They read the specific shudder of a snake chewing through jelly — rain shaking the leaf does not fool them.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"When it sleeps it vanishes. Legs folded tight under the body, feet tucked away, eyelids down — every scrap of color covered, leaving green on green.","more":"Its lower eyelids are not solid curtains. Each is a fine mesh veined with gold, drawn up over the eye like a net. Light still gets through, so a sleeping frog can keep watching, dimly, without opening its eyes and giving itself away.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Look at those feet. Every toe ends in a sticky disc, and the frog can hold its whole weight on a wet, smooth leaf that anything else would slide straight off.","more":"The pads work by wet adhesion, not suction. Their surface is a mosaic of tiny six-sided cells holding a thin film of mucus. That film grips smooth surfaces the way a wet sheet of paper clings to glass.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"The clutch is glued to the underside of a leaf hanging over water, so hatchlings fall straight into the pond below. Nothing has to carry them anywhere.","more":"About forty eggs go into a clear pad of jelly on the shaded underside of the leaf, where they will not dry out. Wasps hunt them there as well — and a wasp attack triggers the same early escape a snake does.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Males call after dark with a short dry chack. They also shake the branch they stand on, so a rival a few feet (about a meter) away feels the tremor through the plant.","more":"This is called tremulation. Two males will trade quivers back and forth from separate leaves, and if neither backs down they close in and wrestle, gripping the stem with their toe pads.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"All that color stays hidden. A poisonous frog advertises constantly; this one keeps its reds and blues folded away and spends them only in the instant it needs to escape.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"It is strictly nocturnal. The whole day is spent flattened against the underside of a leaf, motionless, waiting for dark.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Crickets, moths, flies, grasshoppers — anything moving and small enough to swallow. Newly changed froglets start on fruit flies and other tiny insects.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"About 2 inches (5 cm) for a male, up to 3 inches (7.5 cm) for a female — half again his size. She carries him on her back to the egg leaf.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"It lives from southern Mexico down through the rainforests of Central America. Most of its time is spent up in the leaves. It comes down toward ponds and pools mainly to breed.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Color kept folded away until the exact half-second it is needed."},{"id":"hellbender","name":"Hellbender","group":"amphibians","blurb":"A giant, flat-headed, wrinkled salamander that spends its whole life under rocks in cold fast rivers. The largest salamander in North America.","size":"About as long as two school rulers laid end to end.","homes":["freshwater","mountain"],"stats":{"length":2.0,"weight":3,"life":25},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"Two school rulers end to end — up to 2 feet (60 cm). The largest ever measured reached 29 inches (74 cm). Nothing else in North America comes close.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"It breathes through its skin. Those loose ragged folds down each side are surface area, packed with blood vessels pulling oxygen straight out of the river.","more":"Hellbenders have lungs but barely use them for breathing. Nearly all their oxygen crosses the skin. That is why they need cold, fast, tumbling water. Cold moving water holds far more dissolved oxygen than warm still water does.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"When the water goes still, the hellbender rocks. A slow sway, side to side, pushing fresh water past those skin folds — breathing by moving instead of by inhaling.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"It senses light through its skin, and the cells on its tail are the most sensitive of the lot. It can tell light from dark without using its eyes at all.","more":"Its eyes are small and its water is often cloudy. So most of what it knows comes from elsewhere. A line down its body feels movement in the current, and a sharp sense of smell tracks crayfish in the dark.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Crayfish, mostly. Look inside a hellbender's stomach and more often than not it is crayfish and very little else.","more":"It hunts at night, walking along the riverbed rather than swimming. It takes prey with a fast sideways snap of the head. That pulls in water and crayfish together. It will also eat small fish, worms, insects, tadpoles and smaller salamanders.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"The father does the childcare. He digs the nest under a rock, guards the eggs for weeks, and fans oxygen-rich water over them with his own body.","more":"A nest usually holds 150 to 450 eggs, sometimes from more than one female, and nests of well over a thousand have been counted. He drives off anything that comes near, then stays on with the hatchlings.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"It will use the same rock for years. A hellbender picks one big flat stone with a gap beneath it and defends that address against every other hellbender.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"It is a living water-quality test. It breathes through its skin, so everything in the river passes across it. Hellbenders vanish from water that turns warm, silty or polluted.","more":"Silt is the quiet killer. When soil washes off bare ground into a river, it fills the gaps between stones where hellbenders shelter and where their eggs sit. Find a healthy hellbender population and you have found genuinely clean, cold, fast water.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"People call it a snot otter. Also mud devil, devil dog, Allegheny alligator, and lasagna lizard — that last one for the rippled, layered folds of skin.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Despite every one of those names, it is not venomous and not dangerous to people. It would far rather be left alone under its rock.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"It stays put. Most of a hellbender's life is spent lying motionless beneath one flat stone, waiting for something edible to walk past in the dark.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Nobody has followed one hellbender from egg to the end. Marked animals keep turning up under the same rocks year after year. The best read on a wild lifespan is 25 to 30 years. That is long enough to outlast your entire childhood.","kind":"worked"}],"wonder":"Two feet of animal that breathes without lungs, tucked under a single stone in a cold river, seen by almost nobody."},{"id":"olm","name":"Olm","group":"amphibians","blurb":"A pale, blind, eel-shaped salamander from the flooded limestone caves of southeastern Europe. People once took it for a baby dragon.","size":"About 12 in (30 cm) long, the length of a school ruler.","homes":["cave","freshwater"],"stats":{"length":1.0,"life":68},"facts":[{"cat":"record","text":"It can go ten years without eating. Not ten days — ten years, longer than you have been alive, with nothing at all going into its stomach.","more":"Food is scarce in a cave, so the olm is built to wait. It slows its whole body down. It lives off fat and glycogen banked in its liver. Under extreme starvation it will even reabsorb some of its own tissue. Then it rebuilds when a meal finally turns up.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Nobody has watched one olm's whole life. The numbers come from a marked cave population. The average adult lives around 68 years, and the maximum runs past 100. You could meet this salamander as a child and visit it again as a grandparent.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"senses","text":"It is blind. The eyes are there, but tiny, buried beneath the skin, and they never finish developing. In black water there is nothing for an eye to do.","more":"It still avoids light. The skin itself is light-sensitive, carrying a pigment called melanopsin, so an olm moves away from a flashlight beam it cannot actually see. Shine a light on a cave pool and the pale bodies slide back under the stones.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"It hunts using electricity. Ampullary organs in its head pick up the faint electric fields every living animal gives off. There is no light whatsoever in that water.","more":"It also smells, feels water movement through a lateral line, hears, and lines itself up with the Earth's magnetic field. Five different ways of knowing where things are — and not one of them is sight.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Those pink feathery fans behind its head are gills, and it keeps them for life. An olm holds its larval shape forever and never leaves the water.","more":"This is called neoteny. A typical salamander begins as a gilled swimmer, then absorbs the gills and walks onto land. The olm simply stops halfway and stays there, growing to full size and breeding while still shaped like a baby.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Its skin holds almost no pigment — pale enough to see blood moving underneath. Leave an olm in the light and it slowly darkens, then fades pale again in the dark.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Floodwater used to wash olms out of the caves into daylight. People who found these pale wriggling things decided they were baby dragons, flushed out from under the mountain.","more":"The story stuck, and so did the name. In Slovenian the olm is still called the human fish, for skin the color of pale human skin. It remains the emblem of the caves it came wriggling out of.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"In a cave there is almost nothing to eat, so an olm does almost nothing. It lies still, drops its heart rate and its whole metabolism, and waits.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A female lays around 35 eggs beneath a stone and guards them, and they take months to hatch. The young then need roughly fourteen years to grow up.","more":"Fourteen years just to reach breeding age, and a female may lay only about once a decade. In a place with hardly any food, everything runs slow — including growing up.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"It lives in flooded limestone caves under the Dinaric mountains. The water there is black and far below the surface. That country is now Slovenia, Croatia, Bosnia and a corner of Italy.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Cave shrimp, snails and insects, swallowed whole, because it cannot chew. When food is plentiful it eats enormously and banks the surplus as fat in its liver.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"About 8 to 12 inches (20 to 30 cm) long, the length of a school ruler. It is no thicker than your finger. The legs are tiny: three toes in front, two behind.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Fitted out for a place with no light at all — and thriving there."},{"id":"great-white-shark","name":"Great white shark","group":"fish","blurb":"A huge, warm-bodied hunting shark that patrols cool coastal seas and takes seals with a single strike from below.","size":"Longer than a car — up to 19.8 ft (6 m)","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"speed":15,"weight":2425,"length":16.1,"life":70},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"A big female is longer than a car. The longest one anyone has properly measured was 19.8 ft (6 m), and the heaviest weighed 5,580 lb (2,530 kg) — more than most cars.","more":"Much bigger numbers get thrown around, but they come from lengths guessed over the side of a boat, not from a tape measure. When scientists went back through the catch records and checked, those were the largest anyone could actually prove. Females run larger than males.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"speed","text":"It cruises at barely walking pace, then sprints at about 15 mph (24 km/h) for the last few seconds of an attack.","more":"Speed costs energy, so a white shark spends almost its whole life idling. The sprint is saved for the final rush, usually straight up from below, where a seal at the surface cannot pick out a dark shark against dark water.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"A great white runs warm. Its stomach can sit at 25°C (77°F) while the sea around it is only 14°C (57°F). Warm muscle hits harder and faster.","more":"Bundles of blood vessels run side by side, called the rete mirabile, the wonderful net. Blood heading out to the cold gills warms the blood coming back in. The heat is trapped inside the body instead of leaking away. Most fish are exactly as cold as the water they swim in.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Hundreds of jelly-filled pores sit under its snout. They are called ampullae of Lorenzini. They read the faint electricity leaking from every beating heart and twitching muscle nearby.","more":"Every living animal gives off a weak electric field simply by being alive. A shark that has lost sight of its prey can close the final stretch on that signal alone. It is also why a great white will sometimes bite a metal cage. Metal in seawater makes an electric buzz.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"One white shark tagged off South Africa swam to Australia and back — about 12,000 miles (20,000 km) — in under nine months.","more":"That is roughly halfway around the world and back, across open ocean with nothing to steer by. Researchers recognize the sharks on their return by the dorsal fin. The ragged notches along its edge are as individual as a signature.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Seals, sea lions, dolphins and other sharks. Young great whites eat fish and squid, and only switch to seals once they pass about 10 ft (3 m) long.","more":"Their teeth change shape as they grow: narrow and spiky for gripping a slippery fish, then broad and saw-edged for cutting through blubber. You can tell a young white shark from a full-grown one by its teeth alone.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Off South Africa, great whites hunting seals launch completely clear of the water. The whole shark goes up to 10 ft (3 m) into the air.","more":"It begins the run about 66 ft (20 m) down, points itself almost straight up, and arrives carrying so much speed that it cannot stop at the surface. Photographers call it a breach. The seal usually never sees it coming.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Its teeth sit in rows on a conveyor belt. When a front tooth snaps off, the one behind rolls forward to replace it. About 300 in the mouth at once.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Pups grow inside their mother for about a year. They are born already 3.3 to 5.2 ft (1 to 1.6 m) (3 to 5 ft) long, about as tall as you are. Then they swim off alone.","more":"There is no nursing and no parental care at all. A litter is small, usually two to ten. Before they are born the pups feed on unfertilised eggs inside the mother, which is why they arrive so large and so completely ready.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"Dark gray on top, white underneath. From above it vanishes into the deep; from below it disappears into the bright surface. This is called countershading.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Two separate nostrils let it work out which side a scent reached first, and steer toward it. It cannot smell blood from miles away, though — that claim is stretched.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Cool coastal water is where you usually find it. But it also drops past 3,300 ft (1,000 m) into open ocean. That is far below any sunlight.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"A hunter given warm blood for cold water, and a set of teeth that never runs out of edge."},{"id":"whale-shark","name":"Whale shark","group":"fish","blurb":"The biggest fish in the world. This spotted, slow-moving giant swims with its mouth open. It eats some of the smallest food in the sea.","size":"Up to 61.7 ft (18.8 m) long","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"speed":3,"weight":33069,"length":39.4,"life":80},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"The largest fish alive. The biggest one ever properly measured was 61.7 ft (18.8 m) from nose to tail.","more":"You will see larger numbers repeated online, but those come from lengths eyeballed off the side of a boat. When scientists reviewed all the reports, 62 ft (18.8 m) was the one they judged reliable. Even an ordinary adult whale shark is longer than most living rooms.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It has around 3,000 teeth, none bigger than a grain of rice, and never uses a single one for eating. It eats plankton — creatures mostly too small to see.","more":"Food is caught on 20 spongy filter pads behind the mouth while the water carries straight on out through the gills. A whale shark can push over 1,585 gallons (6,000 liters) of seawater through those pads every hour — about forty bathtubs' worth.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Its eyeballs are covered in teeth. About 2,900 tiny tooth-shaped scales, called denticles, armor the surface of each eye.","more":"Shark skin is covered in these hard little scales, but the whale shark is the only animal known to grow them on the eye itself. Each one is shaped like an oak leaf. If something still comes too close, the shark yanks its eye halfway back into its head in under a second.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Its mouth sits at the very front of its head, not tucked underneath like most sharks. It opens about 5 ft (1.5 m) wide. That is wide enough to swallow a doorway.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"copied","text":"Every whale shark's spot pattern is unique. Researchers tell individuals apart with software first written to match patterns of stars in Hubble Space Telescope photographs.","more":"An astronomer adapted the star-matching math for shark flanks. Now anyone who photographs a whale shark can upload it to a shared library. The software says whether that exact animal has been logged before. Sometimes it was logged years earlier, an ocean away.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"travel","text":"A pregnant female nicknamed Rio Lady was tracked 4,829 miles (7,772 km) from Mexico. Her route ran out toward the middle of the Atlantic. She dived to 5,249 ft (1,600 m) over and over the whole way.","more":"That is most of the way across an ocean, alone, while carrying pups. Nobody knows for certain where whale sharks go to give birth. Following pregnant females like her is one of the main ways researchers are trying to find out.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"It dives deep. The deepest recorded was 6,325 ft (1,928 m) — well over a mile down, far below where any sunlight reaches.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"One pregnant whale shark was found carrying around 300 pups, all at different stages of development. It is the largest litter known from any shark.","more":"The pups hatch from eggs inside the mother and are born 15.7 to 24 in (40 to 60 cm) (16 to 24 in) long — about as long as your arm. Newborn whale sharks are almost never seen in the wild. Where they spend their first years is one of the genuine mysteries left in ocean science.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Its skin is up to 6 in (15 cm) thick — about as deep as your hand is long — and hard and rough to touch. Very little in the ocean can bite through it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Warm water, above about 21°C (70°F). They gather at the same places at the same time each year, wherever the plankton blooms.","more":"Ningaloo Reef in Australia and the coast of Mexico's Yucatán are two of the reliable ones. In one year, more than 400 whale sharks turned up off the Yucatán at once. They fed on plankton swarms and clouds of fish eggs. Then they scattered back out into open ocean.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Sometimes it stops swimming altogether. It hangs upright in the water like a huge column. Then it gulps downward at a swarm of food above its mouth.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"The biggest mouth in the ocean, fitted out to catch the smallest food in it."},{"id":"hammerhead-shark","name":"Great hammerhead","group":"fish","blurb":"The largest of the hammerhead sharks, and a stingray specialist. Its head is wide, flat and shaped like a blade. Its dorsal fin is taller than your arm is long.","size":"Up to 20 ft (6.1 m) long","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"weight":507,"length":15.1,"life":30},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"The biggest hammerhead there is. A large one reaches 20 ft (6.1 m). The heaviest ever put on scales came in at 1,280 lb (580 kg).","more":"That record-holder was a 14.4 ft (4.4 m) female caught off Florida. She was carrying 55 pups at the time. That is a good part of why she weighed what she did. Length is the fairer way to measure a big hammerhead.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"That head has a proper name: the cephalofoil. Flattened like a wing, it sweeps side to side across the sea floor as the shark hunts.","more":"Watch one and it looks exactly like someone working a metal detector along a beach. The wide head does give some lift as the shark swims, but its bigger job is searching — spreading the shark's sensors over as much ground as possible.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Hundreds of pores are spread across the underside of that head. They read electricity. A stingray buried in sand holds perfectly still. It gives itself away anyway, with every heartbeat.","more":"The pores are called ampullae of Lorenzini. Every shark has them, but the hammerhead has spread its set across a much wider surface — the same trick as building a bigger antenna. It finds prey it cannot see, smell or hear.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Eyes at the far ends of the hammer give it a full 360° view in the vertical. It can see above itself and below itself at the same moment.","more":"It also gets better depth perception than a pointed-nose shark. Those wide-set eyes overlap more out in front than you would expect. Researchers shone light into hammerhead eyes from many different angles. They recorded which angles the eye answered.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Stingrays, above almost anything else. Also bony fish, squid, crabs and other sharks — including, when food runs short, smaller hammerheads.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"It hunts stingrays by slamming the ray down into the sea floor with the flat of its head, pinning it there, and eating the wings first.","more":"One great hammerhead caught off Florida had 96 stingray spines stuck in and around its mouth. The venom does not appear to trouble them in the slightest. They simply keep hunting rays.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"It swims tilted on its side, sometimes rolled almost halfway over. Not a wobble — the tilt is held on purpose, and worked out on paper it cuts drag by around 10%.","more":"Scientists strapped small motion sensors to wild great hammerheads and found they spend as much as 90% of their swimming time rolled onto one side. That huge dorsal fin then acts like a wing, giving lift, so the shark burns less energy holding itself up.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"build","text":"The first dorsal fin is enormous — tall, sharply pointed and curved back like a scythe. On a full-grown adult it stands taller than your whole arm is long.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Pups grow inside their mother for about eleven months and are born live, 20 to 40 at a time, each already 19.7-28 in (50-70 cm) (20-28 in) long.","more":"A female only does this once every two years. That slow pace is exactly why hammerhead numbers drop quickly when too many are caught, and climb back very, very slowly afterwards.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Warm coastal seas all around the world. They work the shallows over sand and reef, and move toward the poles in summer before heading back.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"Those famous photographs of hammerheads schooling by the hundred show a different species. It is the scalloped hammerhead. The great hammerhead is a loner.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"The great hammerhead is listed as Critically Endangered. That is the highest warning level there is before an animal is gone from the wild.","more":"The main cause is fishing for their fins. Hammerheads also fare badly on a line. They are unusually likely to die from the stress of being caught, even when they are let go. Catch-and-release is far less forgiving for them than for other sharks.","kind":"record"}],"wonder":"A head shaped to find what is hidden — every square inch of it listening."},{"id":"greenland-shark","name":"Greenland shark","group":"fish","blurb":"A huge, slow, nearly blind shark of the freezing Arctic deep. It is the longest-lived animal with a backbone that anyone has ever measured.","size":"Longer than a car — up to 21 ft (6.4 m) and over a tonne","homes":["ocean","polar"],"stats":{"speed":0.8,"length":13.1,"life":250},"facts":[{"cat":"record","text":"This is the longest-living animal with a backbone anyone has ever measured. The oldest one studied had been swimming for something close to four hundred years.","more":"Scientists worked it out by reading the very center of the eye lens. That tissue forms before the shark is born and then never changes again, so whatever is locked inside it is a record of the very beginning. The measurement is rough at the edges, but it put the whole species comfortably past 250 years.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"speed","text":"It is the slowest-swimming fish for its size ever recorded. Cruising speed is about 0.8 mph (1.2 km/h) — slower than you walk to the kitchen.","more":"One full sweep of its tail takes around seven seconds. At that pace it ought to have no hope of catching a seal, and yet seal remains keep turning up in Greenland shark stomachs. How they manage it is still argued over.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Length is the part anyone can measure: a female Greenland shark is about 14 ft (4.2 m) long before her first pups. Put beside the eye-lens ages, that works out at somewhere around 150 years old.","more":"That is older than almost any other animal ever gets, period. It also means a shark pulled up in a net today may never have had a single pup. That is why the species recovers so terribly slowly from any losses.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Most Greenland sharks swim around with a worm-like parasite dangling off each eyeball, chewing at the surface. It leaves the shark nearly blind.","more":"The parasite is a copepod called Ommatokoita elongata. It is a few centimeters long and hangs from the cornea like a luggage tag. It scars the eye badly. The shark hunts perfectly well without sight, using smell and the vibrations it feels in the water. So it seems to lose almost nothing.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Its flesh is poisonous to eat fresh. It is loaded with a chemical called trimethylamine oxide, and anyone who eats it is left staggering as though drunk.","more":"Sled dogs fed fresh Greenland shark have been seen unable to stand up. In Iceland the meat is buried, fermented for weeks and hung to dry for months until it is safe — a dish called hákarl. The chemical is not there to poison anyone: it helps the shark's body cope with the pressure and cold of the deep.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"It lives in water between about -1°C and 7°C (30-45°F). That is cold enough that the sea is close to freezing. It goes down to around 3,900 ft (1,200 m).","more":"Far north it will come almost to the surface, because up there the surface is already as cold as the deep. Further south it stays down where the cold is. Either way it is choosing the same thing — water only a degree or two above freezing.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Fish of every kind, seals, and a great deal of whatever it finds already dead on the sea floor. Reindeer, moose, horse and even polar bear remains have come out of their stomachs.","more":"Anything that drowns or dies at sea eventually sinks, and the Greenland shark is the animal waiting where it lands. One was found with an entire reindeer carcass inside it. Being unhurried is no handicap when most of your dinner is not going anywhere.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"Up to 21 ft (6.4 m) long and over 2,200 lb (1,000 kg). It is blunt-nosed and heavy-bodied. It cruises at about 0.8 mph (1.3 km/h).","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Its top and bottom teeth do completely different jobs. The upper teeth are thin spikes that grip; the lower ones interlock into a broad, square cutting edge. It anchors with the top and saws with the bottom by rolling its head.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"In the pitch dark of the deep it navigates by smell. A Greenland shark will find bait from a long way off, tracking the scent trail with eyes that barely work at all.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"Its scientific name, Somniosus, comes from the Latin word for sleep. Anyone who has watched one drift past a camera would tell you the name is fair.","kind":"record"}],"wonder":"Slow, half-blind, and centuries old — flourishing in water that almost nothing else can survive."},{"id":"manta-ray","name":"Giant oceanic manta ray","group":"fish","blurb":"The largest ray in the ocean. It is a huge, flat, completely harmless plankton-eater. It swims by flapping its fins like a pair of wings.","size":"Wider than a giraffe is tall — up to 23 ft (7 m) wingtip to wingtip","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"weight":4409,"life":45},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"The biggest ray in the ocean. Wingtip to wingtip a giant manta reaches about 23 ft (7 m) — wider than a giraffe is tall — and the heaviest weigh over 4,400 lb (2,000 kg).","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Those two paddles either side of its mouth are cephalic fins. It unrolls them to funnel water in while feeding, and curls them into tight spirals to swim clean and fast.","more":"They are forward extensions of the huge pectoral wings, reaching around the front of the head. Rolled up they look like a pair of horns, which is where the old sailors' name for mantas — devil rays — came from. Unrolled, they are simply a funnel.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It eats plankton, straining it out of seawater. To do that it swims along with its mouth held open like a wide doorway and lets the ocean pour through.","more":"It never swallows the water. A mouthful goes in the front and straight out through the gill slits underneath, and the food is left behind on filter plates on the way past. A big manta processes an astonishing volume of sea in a day.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"copied","text":"Its filter should clog within minutes and never does. Water hits the filter plates and ricochets off at an angle, carrying plankton along the surface instead of jamming into it.","more":"Researchers who studied manta gill plates found the particles bounce rather than sieve. That is why nothing ever blocks. They named it ricochet separation. Filters built the same way could strain water without ever needing to be scrubbed clean. Engineers have taken very close notice.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"When it finds a thick patch of plankton, it does backward somersaults through it. It rolls over and over. That keeps it inside the cloud instead of swimming out the far side.","more":"It is called barrel rolling and it looks exactly like play. It is not. A straight line through a plankton patch is over in seconds, but a loop keeps the manta inside the food indefinitely. Several will sometimes line up nose to tail and cycle through together.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"For a fish, its brain is enormous. It runs up to 7 oz (200 g), the largest brain of any fish. That is five to ten times the size of a whale shark's.","more":"Parts of that brain are wrapped in a fine mesh of blood vessels called the rete mirabile. It keeps the brain warm while the manta is hunting down in cold water. A brain that stays warm carries on working properly when the rest of the body is chilled through.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"It has no sting. Stingrays carry a barb on the tail; the manta has only a small knob where one would sit, and nothing to defend itself with but size.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A manta has one pup at a time, after carrying it for about a year, and only every two or three years. The pup is born rolled up and unfurls as it swims away.","more":"It arrives already 4.6 ft (1.4 m) (4 ft 7 in) across and weighing about 20 lb (9 kg). That is bigger than a large dog. It gets no help from its mother afterwards. One pup at a time is why manta populations climb back so slowly once they fall.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"It dives past 3,300 ft (1,000 m) into cold black water. Nobody has watched it feed down there. The chemistry of its tissue is the argument. It points to roughly three-quarters of its food coming from the deep rather than the sunlit surface.","more":"For a long time people assumed mantas fed only at the top, because that is where we see them. Then chemical tests on manta tissue showed most of their food was coming from the deep. The surface feeding everyone photographs is the smaller part of the story.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"It returns to the same spots on the reef again and again to be cleaned. Small fish swim right into its gills and mouth, picking off parasites.","more":"Mantas remember these cleaning stations and come back to the exact same ones over many years. One will hover almost motionless above the reef, wings barely moving. It holds position while the little fish work. Then it moves off perfectly calmly.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"Every manta has a different pattern of dark blotches on its white belly. It works like a fingerprint. Researchers photograph bellies to keep track of who is who.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Giant mantas throw themselves clear out of the water and land flat with a crack you can hear a long way off. Nobody is certain why they do it.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"The largest brain in all the fish, and a net of blood vessels to keep it warm in the cold and the dark."},{"id":"electric-eel","name":"Electric eel","group":"fish","blurb":"A long, air-breathing river fish from South America. It can flood the water around it with the strongest electric shock any animal is known to make.","size":"About 6.6 ft (2 m) long, as long as a doorway is tall, and about 44 lb (20 kg).","homes":["freshwater","rainforest"],"stats":{"weight":44,"length":6.6},"facts":[{"cat":"record","text":"A wall socket in an American house carries 120 volts. One kind of electric eel has been measured at 860 — the strongest electric shock any animal on Earth is known to produce.","more":"Most electric eels top out nearer 600 volts, which is still five wall sockets. What keeps it from being deadly is how brief each pulse is: thousandths of a second. It stuns rather than kills. Human deaths are very rare, and usually from drowning after a shock rather than from the shock itself.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Everything an electric eel needs to stay alive is crammed into the front fifth of its body. Heart, gills, guts — all up front. The whole long tail behind it is electric organ.","more":"The organ is built from flat cells called electrocytes, stacked like coins in a roll. There are about 6,000 in a row. Roughly 35 of those rows sit side by side along each flank. Each cell adds its own tiny voltage to the total. Batteries stack in a flashlight exactly the same way.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"It has a much weaker setting too. A soft pulse of around ten volts goes out constantly, and the eel reads it back. That maps muddy water it cannot see through. This is electrolocation.","more":"Amazon backwater is the color of strong tea, and eyes are nearly useless in it. The weak field wraps around rocks, roots and fish, and sensors in the eel's skin feel exactly where that field bends. It is hunting by electricity, not by sight.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"When a fish hides in the leaves, the eel fires a pair of high-voltage pulses. The shock seizes the hidden fish's own muscles and makes it twitch — and the twitch gives it away.","more":"The eel is not zapping at random. Those paired pulses reach the nerves that control a fish's muscles and fire them all at once, the way a doctor's little hammer makes your knee jerk. The hidden fish cannot help moving. Then the eel unloads a full volley.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Struggling prey gets the curl. The eel bends into a horseshoe so head and tail press the same fish from both sides, and the shock running between them roughly doubles in strength.","more":"Water carries a shock away in every direction. So most of the eel's output is normally wasted on empty river. Folding the body puts both ends of the battery against one animal. That is the difference between a warning and a takedown.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"An electric eel will leap clear of the water and press its chin against whatever is threatening it. That runs the shock straight into the animal instead of losing it in the river.","more":"The higher it climbs, the more of the jolt goes into the target instead of leaking away into the river. One researcher measured this by letting a small eel leap at his own arm: 40 to 50 milliamps went through him, and his hand came back fast.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"An electric eel can drown. It breathes air through the roof of its mouth, surfacing about every two minutes, and the folded, blood-rich lining in there works like a lung.","more":"Its gills are small and barely used. Warm, still Amazon water holds very little oxygen. Gulping air at the surface solves that neatly. But an eel pinned underwater by a net or a fallen log runs out of breath the way you would.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"It is not an eel at all. Scientists file it with the knifefish, and it swims by rippling one long fin that runs the entire underside of its body — forwards or backwards, whichever it prefers.","more":"That single ribbon fin has hundreds of rays. It can send its wave in either direction, so the eel reverses out of a tangle of roots without turning around. Its back carries no fin at all. The whole body is smooth as a rope.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"home","text":"Look for it in the slowest, muddiest water in the Amazon and Orinoco. That means flooded forest, swamps and creeks. They are so warm and still that most fish would struggle to get a breath there.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"The male whips up a nest of foam out of his own saliva. The female lays around 1,200 eggs into it, and he stands guard over the froth until they hatch.","more":"He builds it in the dry season, in a shallow pool cut off from the main river. It looks like nothing much — a patch of bubbles under a bank — which is rather the point.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"A big one is as long as a doorway is tall — 6 ft 7 in (2 m). It weighs about 44 lb (20 kg), all of it stretched into the shape of a rope.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"copied","text":"The very first battery was built to copy a fish. Alessandro Volta stacked metal discs in a pile. He was imitating the stacked cells of an electric fish's organ. The volt is named after him.","more":"Engineers are still borrowing. Soft, bendable power sources have been built from stacked gel cells modeled on electrocytes. They are aimed at devices that could one day run inside a human body. No hard metal battery needed.","kind":"record"}],"wonder":"That long tail is a living battery — wired, stacked and finished before the eel ever hunts once."},{"id":"anglerfish","name":"Anglerfish","group":"fish","blurb":"A deep-sea fish that hunts in total darkness. It dangles a glowing lure in front of its own mouth and waits for something to come and look.","size":"Most are hand-sized; the largest females grow longer than your arm","homes":["ocean"],"facts":[{"cat":"build","text":"The glowing bulb over an anglerfish's mouth hangs from a spine on its back that works as a fishing rod. Scientists call the rod the illicium and the lit bait the esca.","more":"In one species the rod stretches nearly five times the length of the fish's own body. In others it is barely there at all. Every species carries a differently shaped esca — some fringed like seaweed, some branched like a tiny antler, each one its own advertisement.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"The anglerfish does not make its own light. Billions of bioluminescent bacteria live inside the lure and glow on its behalf, and the fish gives them a safe home and food in return.","more":"The fish is not the source of the light, but it still works the lamp. It flashes it, dims it, and tucks the whole rod away. Sunlight has never once reached down there. A small blue-green glow is the most interesting thing for miles. Hungry animals swim straight at it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"An anglerfish can swallow prey up to twice its own size. The jaw opens far past what looks possible, the stomach stretches to take it, and the whole meal goes down at once.","more":"Food is desperately scarce down there — you might not meet another meal for weeks — so the rule is simply never refuse. Long, thin teeth angle inwards, letting prey slide in easily and giving it nothing to push back against.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"In some species the male is a fraction of the female's size. He bites onto her and never lets go — their tissues join together, and he spends the rest of his life attached to her body.","more":"He is built for one job. His nostrils are huge, for tracking her scent through the dark. He has no lure of his own, and a gut too small to feed himself with. Once fused, he draws food from her blood. In return he supplies what she needs to fertilize her eggs.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"In one species the female reaches 30 in (77 cm) (2 ft 6 in) and the male stops at 6 in (16 cm). In another the male is 0.28 in (7 mm) — a grain of rice beside a female seven times longer.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Deep-sea anglerfish live far below the last of the sunlight, often more than half a mile down. The water there is black, close to freezing, and pressing in from every side.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"Some anglerfish have ultra-black skin that absorbs more than 99.5% of the light hitting it. In the beam of a submarine's lamp they show up as a fish-shaped hole in the water.","more":"Their skin is packed with pigment granules, arranged so tightly that light cannot get back out. It bounces around inside until it is used up. That matters because the lure is a beacon. The animal holding it needs to stay completely invisible right beside it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Its eyes are small and weak, and there is almost nothing down there to see with them. The best read is that it hunts by its lateral line — a row of sensors along its flank that feels water shifting when something swims near.","more":"In deep-sea anglerfish those sensors sit out on little stalks. Held clear of the body, they are not drowned out by the fish's own movements. A shrimp kicking half a meter away makes a pressure ripple. The anglerfish reads it like a footprint.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"size","text":"Forget the monster from the films. A humpback anglerfish female is about as long as your hand, 7 in (18 cm), and plenty of deep-sea anglerfish are smaller than that.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"An anglerfish is a poor swimmer. Its muscle is soft and watery, and its flesh is loose. That is the argument for why weak swimming suits it. Cheap flesh costs almost nothing to carry. So it can hang motionless in the dark for days.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Not all anglerfish live deep. The same group includes frogfish that walk across coral on their fins, and the goosefish, a flat wide-mouthed thing that lies half-buried on the seabed.","more":"Frogfish have arm-like fins with a bend partway along and a proper grip at the end. They stroll rather than swim. They carry the rod and lure too. A frogfish can suck in prey in about six thousandths of a second. That is one of the fastest gulps ever measured.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"A fish that cannot make light, carrying a lamp made of living things that can — two lives fitted together, and neither one works alone."},{"id":"seahorse","name":"Seahorse","group":"fish","blurb":"A small upright fish with a horse-shaped head, a curling tail and bony armor instead of scales. And it is the male who gets pregnant.","size":"From shorter than your thumb to about the length of your forearm, depending on the species","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"length":0.5,"life":4},"facts":[{"cat":"babies","text":"The male gets pregnant. The female places her eggs into a pouch on his belly, he fertilises them in there, and he carries them for anywhere from 9 to 45 days before giving birth.","more":"The pouch is not just a bag. It bathes the eggs in fluid, feeds them, and slowly shifts the saltiness inside until it matches the open sea, so the babies are ready for it. When labor comes he grips a stem with his tail and pumps until the young shoot out.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"One father can release between 100 and 1,000 babies in a single birth — and the best estimate is that fewer than five in a thousand survive to grow up. That is still far better odds than most fish ever get.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"food","text":"A seahorse has no stomach at all. Food rushes straight through, so it has to eat almost without stopping — a dwarf seahorse can put away up to 3,000 tiny shrimp in a day.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Its snout is a spring-loaded trap. Muscles wind up like a bow being drawn, then let go, snapping the head upwards and sucking prey in faster than you can blink.","more":"Biologists call it pivot feeding. It runs on elastic recoil: energy stored in stretched tissue and released all at once. A catapult uses the same trick. A seahorse has no teeth and no way to chew. Whatever it catches goes down whole.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Each eye swivels on its own. One can track a passing shrimp while the other scans behind for a crab, so a seahorse never has to choose between eating and not being eaten.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"The dwarf seahorse is the slowest fish in the world: about 5 ft (1.5 m) per hour. Crossing your bedroom would take it over three hours. It would take you four seconds.","more":"Being slow is not really a problem for it, because it was never built to chase or to flee. It hides, it holds on, and it lets the food come within snout range. Speed would cost energy it would rather spend on eating.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"copied","text":"Cut across a seahorse's tail and you get a square, not a circle. Square segments grip better and survive being crushed — which is why engineers have copied the shape for robot arms.","more":"Researchers 3D-printed one tail with round segments and one with square, then squashed both. The square tail sprang back and kept its grip; the round one buckled and stayed bent. Grippers built on the design are aimed at medical tools and at robots that reach into rubble.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Seahorses have no scales. Bony plates ring the body instead, like a suit of armor, which is why a dried seahorse holds its shape and feels like a small carving.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"A seahorse winds its tail around seagrass or coral and stays put. It swims so weakly that a strong current can carry it away and wear it out — so the tail reads as a holdfast, not a paddle.","more":"In a storm, seagrass beds thrash and the anchors tear loose. Seahorses are sometimes found exhausted after rough weather. They have spent everything they had trying to hold position in water that would not stay still.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Seahorses click. They grind two bones in the skull against each other to make a sharp snap. It happens while feeding and while courting. Some growl when handled, a low buzz you feel more than hear.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"Many seahorse pairs meet every single morning. They change color, spiral around the same stem of grass and hold tails for a few minutes, then part for the rest of the day.","more":"Biologists call it the greeting dance. It keeps the two of them in step, so the female's eggs come ready on the very day the male's pouch is empty again. Miss that window and a whole batch of eggs is wasted.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"Pygmy seahorses grow bumps on their skin matching the coral they live on, color and all. Some are under 0.59 in (15 mm) — about as wide as your thumb — and one kind was only found when someone collected the coral and looked closer.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"The father carries them, feeds them, and slowly tunes the water in his pouch to match the sea outside — a nursery that gets them ready for the world before they ever see it."},{"id":"clownfish","name":"Clownfish","group":"fish","blurb":"A small orange-and-white reef fish that lives its whole life inside a stinging sea anemone. It is the one address on the reef that nothing else dares approach.","size":"About as long as your finger","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"length":0.4,"life":20},"facts":[{"cat":"home","text":"A clownfish lives inside an animal that stings. Anemone tentacles fire venom-loaded harpoons at anything that brushes them. The clownfish sleeps in there.","more":"The tentacles are covered in stinging cells called nematocysts. Each one is a coiled dart set to fire on contact. The clownfish wears a thick coat of mucus that does not trigger them. So the anemone treats it roughly the way it treats its own tentacles.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"The anemone gives the clownfish a fortress nothing will enter. The clownfish drives off anemone-eating fish, clears away scraps, and fans fresh water through the tentacles all night. Add it up and both sides gain.","more":"The fanning matters more than it sounds. A clownfish wriggling among the tentacles pushes oxygen-rich water past them. Its droppings feed the anemone as well. Anemones with a clownfish in residence grow faster than anemones without one.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A clownfish does not hatch male or female. It grows up male first — then the largest fish in the anemone turns female, the second largest becomes her mate, and the rest queue up behind without breeding.","more":"The word for this is protandry, meaning male first. If the female dies, her mate physically changes sex and takes her place — a permanent change, not a costume — and every fish below shuffles up one rung. The whole household reorganises itself in a matter of weeks.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"The father does the childcare. He scrubs a bare patch of rock beside the anemone for the eggs. Then he fans them with his fins and picks out any that go moldy. He keeps that up for six or seven days, until they hatch.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"Newly hatched clownfish drift in the open ocean for up to twelve days. Then they find their way back to a reef. They steer by smell, and by the sound of the reef itself.","more":"A healthy reef is loud: snapping shrimp, breaking waves, fish. Tiny larvae swim toward that noise, and toward the chemical signature of anemones and even of leaves from trees on reef islands. Most never make it. The ones that do choose an anemone and rarely leave it again.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Rank is measured in millimeters. Each fish in the queue keeps itself to about 80% of the size of the one above it, staying small enough not to look like a challenge.","more":"Being thrown out of an anemone on a coral reef is usually a death sentence. So the smaller fish deliberately eat less and stay stunted, holding their place in line rather than risking a fight they would lose. When a rank above them opens up, they grow into it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Clownfish talk with their teeth. Snapping the jaw shut makes pops and chirps, boomed out through the swim bladder, and the female uses them to tell the others to stay in their place.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"A fish this small ought to live a couple of years. Wild orange clownfish have been estimated at twenty to thirty — extraordinary for an animal the length of your finger.","more":"The anemone is why. In a safe home, an old clownfish is not a lucky clownfish. It is simply an established one. Holding the top spot in a good anemone is worth more than gambling on a new one.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"home","text":"Out of roughly a thousand anemone species in the sea, only ten will host clownfish. Each kind of clownfish settles in certain ones. It tests the tentacles carefully before moving in.","more":"A young clownfish acclimates: a brief touch, then a longer one, building its mucus coat a little at a time until it can bury itself in the tentacles. Wash that mucus off and the very same anemone will sting it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"Most clownfish are finger-length, about 3 in (8 cm). The biggest, the maroon clownfish, reaches 6 in (16 cm) — and in every species the female is the largest fish in the anemone.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"A clownfish is a poor swimmer and behaves like one. Most spend their entire lives within a meter or two of their anemone and never cross open sand, where a bright orange fish is simply a snack.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Count the white bars and you can name the species. Orange clownfish have three; an adult tomato clownfish has just one, behind the eye — young ones start with more and lose them as they grow.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Of all the thousands of fish on the reef, one can live inside the stinging tentacles — and the anemone ends up better off for it."},{"id":"archerfish","name":"Archerfish","group":"fish","blurb":"A mangrove fish that hunts insects in the air above it by shooting them down with a jet of water fired from its mouth.","size":"Most are about the length of your hand; the biggest reach 16 in (40 cm)","homes":["freshwater","forest"],"stats":{"length":0.7},"facts":[{"cat":"food","text":"It shoots its dinner down. An archerfish spots an insect on a leaf above the water and lines up underneath. It fires a jet that knocks the insect clean off. Then it eats the insect as it falls.","more":"It presses its tongue up against a groove in the roof of its mouth to make a barrel. Then it slams its gill covers shut to force water down the barrel. A large archerfish can bring down prey 6.6 to 9.8 ft (2 to 3 m) (6 to 10 ft) above the surface.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The jet is not a steady stream. The archerfish makes the back of it travel faster than the front, so the water bunches into one heavy blob at exactly the moment it arrives.","more":"That timing is the whole trick. A spread-out stream would only splash. All the water has to land at once. That delivers a punch far harder than the mouth muscles could manage on their own. The fish retunes the timing for how far away the target is.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Look at a straw in a glass of water: it appears bent where it enters. Light bends the same way coming the other direction, so an insect is never quite where the archerfish sees it — and it corrects for that anyway.","more":"The bending is called refraction. Archerfish shoot accurately at angles anywhere from 45 to 110 degrees off the horizontal. One fixed correction would never cover that. Somewhere in that small head, the aim is being recalculated for every single shot.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"speed","text":"The fish starts swimming before the insect lands. Within about a tenth of a second of the hit, it has worked out where the prey will come down and is already turning that way.","more":"A tenth of a second is roughly one blink. In that time the archerfish reads which way the insect is tumbling, how fast, and from what height, picks a landing spot and goes. Everything else in the pool wants that insect too.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Shooting is the easy part. Archerfish feed in crowds. A fish that never fired a drop can still reach the falling insect first. The shooter often watches its dinner vanish into someone else.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Young archerfish are terrible shots. They start spitting when they are about 1 in (2.5 cm) long, fire short sloppy jets and miss constantly — the accuracy has to be practiced into them.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"copied","text":"An archerfish can learn to shoot by watching another fish do it. Some only ever spectated and never fired at that kind of target themselves. They still hit moving prey accurately on their first attempt.","more":"In the experiment, one fish was trained while others watched from the side. The watchers had never aimed at a moving target, yet their first tries matched the trained fish's. Learning from someone else's practice rather than your own is rare enough in animals that the result made news.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Archerfish can learn to tell human faces apart. Shown two photographs and rewarded for spitting at one of them, they picked the right face about four times in five — even in black and white.","more":"Mammals recognize faces using the neocortex, a layer of brain that fish do not have at all. The archerfish manages the same job without one. Spitting is what made the test possible: the fish points straight at its answer, so there is no guessing what it chose.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"It does not always shoot. If an insect is sitting low enough, an archerfish launches its whole body clear of the water. It takes the insect off the leaf. That is a jump of roughly twice its own length.","more":"Jumping is costlier than spitting and it puts the fish in the air, where a bird could take it, so it is a short-range option only. High-speed film shows the archerfish lines the jump up from below the surface, the same way it lines up a shot.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Archerfish hold the edges — mangrove creeks and estuaries where river water meets the sea, and rivers well inland. Overhanging leaves are the whole point, because that is where the insects sit.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"Most archerfish are about the length of your hand, 4.7 to 7.1 in (12 to 18 cm) (5 to 7 in). The largest kind stretches to 16 in (40 cm) — longer than a school ruler.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"It must aim through the bending surface of its own world, and it hits anyway."},{"id":"pufferfish","name":"Pufferfish","group":"fish","blurb":"A slow, round, big-eyed fish that swells into a spiky ball when something tries to eat it. It also carries one of the deadliest poisons in the ocean.","size":"From smaller than your thumb to longer than your forearm, depending on the species","homes":["ocean","freshwater"],"stats":{"length":1.6},"facts":[{"cat":"defence","text":"Underwater, a puffer doesn't blow up with air. It gulps seawater into a stomach that stretches like a balloon, until it's a spiky ball too wide for a predator's mouth.","more":"It has no ribs and no pelvic bones in the way, so nothing stops the stomach expanding. Those spines are its scales, stiffened into spikes. They lie flat against the body and stand up only when the fish inflates.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Many puffers carry tetrodotoxin. It is a poison with no antidote. It plugs the tiny gates that let nerves fire, so the muscles simply stop answering.","more":"Nerves send signals by letting sodium rush through openings called sodium channels. Tetrodotoxin blocks them shut. The chilling part is what it does not do. A poisoned person can be completely paralyzed and still wide awake, thinking clearly the whole time.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Puffers don't make the poison themselves. It comes from bacteria in what they eat, which is why a puffer raised on clean food ends up completely harmless.","more":"Feed a toxin-free puffer a diet containing tetrodotoxin, and the poison builds back up in its liver. The fish is borrowing a weapon from the bacteria in its food. It stores that weapon in its own body. Its own nerves are unbothered by it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Its family name means four teeth, and four teeth is all it has. Two are fused above and two below. Together they form a beak that cracks crab shell and snail.","more":"Those teeth keep growing all through its life, so a puffer has to eat hard-shelled food to wear them down. In aquariums, a puffer fed only soft food grows a beak so overgrown that it can no longer close its mouth.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A male puffer small enough to sit in your palm plows a ridged circle into the seabed. The circle measures 6.6 ft (2 m) across. The whole job is to attract a female.","more":"He digs it with his fins and body, then keeps the ridges and valleys sharp while females come and inspect. She lays in the fine sand at the center. He never reuses a circle: every batch of eggs gets one dug from scratch.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"speed","text":"Watch one swim and you'll wonder how it survives anything. It sculls with small fins, slow as a drifting leaf, but it can hover, reverse, and turn on the spot.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Each eye swivels independently. One can track a crab off to the left while the other keeps watch on open water to the right.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"In Japan puffer is eaten as fugu, and only licensed chefs may prepare it. The poison sits in specific organs that have to be cut away perfectly.","more":"The training takes years and ends in an exam. Poisonings still happen, most often when someone prepares a puffer at home instead of buying it from a licensed kitchen. There is no antidote, so treatment is life support until the body clears it.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"weird","text":"The very same poison turns up in the blue-ringed octopus and in the rough-skinned newt. Three animals that look nothing alike carry one identical chemical.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Not every puffer lives in the sea. About 35 species spend their whole lives in African, Asian and South American rivers. They crack snails off the riverbed.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"Puffers run from smaller than your thumb up to the mbu of Africa's Congo rivers. The mbu is the biggest freshwater puffer. It passes 20 in (50 cm), longer than your forearm.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"No ribs so the stomach can stretch, and spines that lie flat until the moment they're needed. A defense thought through from the bones outward."},{"id":"flying-fish","name":"Flying fish","group":"fish","blurb":"A slim silver ocean fish with pectoral fins as long as wings. It launches clear of the sea and glides above the waves. That is how it escapes whatever is chasing it.","size":"Between a pencil and a school ruler long, about 5.9-11.8 in (15-30 cm) (6-12 in)","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"speed":43,"length":1.0},"facts":[{"cat":"travel","text":"It doesn't flap. Once it's airborne the fins lock out stiff and it glides, like a paper plane with a fish inside.","more":"Real flight means beating wings to make lift, and a flying fish never does that. All the energy comes from the launch underwater, plus rising air off the front of a wave, which is why the longest flights happen along the wave crests.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"speed","text":"It builds its speed underwater first, then breaks the surface at over 43 mph (70 km/h) with its tail still beating behind it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"The longest flight ever filmed lasted 45 seconds. A television crew off southern Japan recorded it. That fish stayed up longer than most people can hold their breath.","more":"To keep going that long, a flying fish drops the tip of its tail back into the water and beats it hard. It never fully lands. It launches straight into a fresh glide before the first one has run out.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"A normal flight covers about 160 ft (50 m), the length of an Olympic pool. Riding the rising air off wave crests, one can string glides together for 1,300 ft (400 m).","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The bottom half of its tail is far longer than the top. That long lower blade stays in the water and beats while the rest of the fish is already flying.","more":"It works like a tiny outboard motor. The fish is in the air but still being pushed, so it can build speed for another glide without properly landing. Everything that powers the flight happens in the water. The air is only where it ends up.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Some species have two pairs of wings. The long pectoral fins go out front, with enlarged pelvic fins behind. The result is a fish-shaped biplane.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Almost everything eats flying fish. Tuna, marlin, dolphins, squid, mackerel. Flight is the only answer they have, and it works right up until a frigatebird is waiting overhead.","more":"Frigatebirds learn to patrol above hunting tuna. Tuna are what push flying fish into the air in the first place. The fish escapes one predator by launching itself straight into the reach of another.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"They live in the open ocean, in warm water near the surface. It's the emptiest place on Earth, with nothing to hide behind — and that emptiness is the best explanation anyone has for why they fly.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Their eggs come with sticky threads. Adults wind them around drifting seaweed, feathers, even floating trash, so the eggs have something to hold onto instead of sinking.","more":"Out in open water there is nothing to lay eggs on. No rock, no reef, no plant rooted to anything. A raft of drifting weed is the only nursery available, and a good one can end up carrying clumps of eggs from many different fish at once.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"They feed mainly on plankton, the tiny drifting animals near the surface. The fish that flies to avoid being eaten spends its day hunting things you'd barely be able to see.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Barbados is nicknamed the land of the flying fish, and flying fish served with cou-cou is the national dish. A whole country named itself after a glide.","kind":"record"}],"wonder":"Wings for the air, fins for the sea, and a tail that works in both at once. One small fish given two worlds to move through."},{"id":"mudskipper","name":"Mudskipper","group":"fish","blurb":"A fish that walks. It spends most of its life out of the water on tropical mudflats. It props itself up on its front fins, with its eyes bulging off the top of its head.","size":"Most are shorter than a pencil; the largest reach about 12 in (30 cm), roughly your forearm","homes":["ocean","freshwater"],"stats":{"length":1.0},"facts":[{"cat":"build","text":"Its front fins are jointed and set far forward, underneath the body. It plants them and hauls itself along, a movement called crutching, because that's exactly what it looks like.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"A mudskipper breathes through its skin. As long as it stays damp, oxygen soaks straight in through the skin and through the lining of its mouth and throat.","more":"This is cutaneous respiration: breathing through skin. It only works wet, which is why a mudskipper that has been out a while flops back into a puddle and rolls in it. Let one dry out completely and it suffocates in open air.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Both eyes sit on top of its head and swivel like periscopes. It lowers them into their sockets while a fold of skin called the dermal cup rises over them. That's a fish blinking.","more":"The blink isn't about dust. It's a wipe. The eye dips into a pocket of water inside the head and comes back up wet. Most fish never blink because they are already underwater and never need to. A mudskipper is not underwater.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"It carries its own air supply. Before heading out it packs the enlarged chambers around its gills with air and seals them shut, built-in cheeks that keep the gills damp ashore.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A giant mudskipper takes mouthfuls of air down into its flooded burrow. It releases them in the dark, trip after trip. That gives its eggs oxygen to develop in.","more":"Deep mangrove mud holds almost no oxygen at all, and the water in the burrow would smother the eggs. So the father surfaces, fills his mouth, dives back down and lets the bubble go, building an air pocket up in the burrow's ceiling.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"speed","text":"Startled, it flexes its whole body sideways and flings itself up to 24 in (61 cm) in one hop. Two school rulers, cleared by a fish shorter than one of them.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"It lives where the river meets the sea, in mangrove mud that's underwater half the day and bare the other half. Mudskippers spend up to three quarters of their lives on land.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Males argue in sign language. The body arches, the posture changes, the dorsal fin flicks up like a flag. On an open mudflat everyone can see everyone. So disputes get settled by display.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"It climbs. Using those same front fins, a mudskipper hauls itself up mangrove roots and out onto low branches, well clear of the water it came from.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It hunts on land: crabs, insects, worms. Some species carry a mouthful of water, push it out over their food, then suck it back in, using water as a tongue.","more":"A fish has no tongue that works in air, and dry food won't slide into a fish's mouth the way it does underwater. So the mudskipper brings the water with it. The bulge of water flows out over the prey and draws it back in.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"The burrow is home, nursery and panic room all at once. A frightened mudskipper skips across the mud and drops straight down a hole it dug itself and knows by heart.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Most fish were given one world to live in. This one was given the shoreline, and everything it needs to work on both sides of the tideline."},{"id":"sailfish","name":"Sailfish","group":"fish","blurb":"A long blue open-ocean hunter with a spear for a nose. The fin on its back opens like a sail. Up it goes when the sailfish closes in on a school of small fish.","size":"About 10 ft (3 m) from bill tip to tail: lay two nine-year-olds head to toe and add a bit","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"speed":22,"weight":198,"length":9.8},"facts":[{"cat":"record","text":"You'll read everywhere that a sailfish hits 68 mph (110 km/h). It doesn't. Careful measurements put its real ceiling nearer 20-35 mph (35-55 km/h).","more":"The famous number came from an old fishing test, not a laboratory. Researchers later measured how fast sailfish muscle can actually contract, and filmed real sailfish attacking real sardines: bursts around 15 mph (25 km/h), never past 20 mph (35 km/h). Still one of the fastest fish alive.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The sail is a dorsal fin that can stand taller than the fish is deep. It folds down flat into a groove along the back when the sailfish is just swimming.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"Raise the sail and the head stops swinging side to side. Nobody can ask a sardine what it saw. But a steadier bill slides through the water almost still. That is the argument for why the sail goes up.","more":"Researchers filming attacks found the sail does more than look impressive. With it up, the hunting sailfish's head oscillates less as it closes in, so the bill slides through the water almost still and the school reacts late.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"Sailfish gather in loose groups around a school of sardines, but they don't all rush in together. They attack one or two at a time, taking turns at the ball of fish.","more":"Filming shows the turn-taking clearly. Only one or two sailfish press the attack at any moment while the rest hold off. Fewer bills swinging at once means fewer collisions, and the sardine ball stays packed instead of scattering in every direction.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"The bill isn't a spear. The sailfish swipes it sideways through the packed sardines, tap, slash, tap, then comes back for the ones too stunned or too cut to swim straight.","more":"The bill is covered in tiny rough teeth, so a slash doesn't just knock a sardine sideways, it scrapes it. Watch one attack from beginning to end and the count of injured sardines in the ball climbs steadily. That climb is the strategy working.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"A hunting sailfish lights up. Bright blue bars flicker along its flanks and fade within seconds, while the other sailfish in the hunt do the same thing.","more":"The color comes from cells in the skin that the fish controls directly, the way an octopus does. In a crowded attack, with several bills swinging in a small space, the flashing appears to keep the hunters from hitting each other. It stops when the hunt does.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A sailfish hatches as a speck of a larva and reaches 3.9-4.9 ft (1.2-1.5 m) (4-5 ft) inside its first year. Taller than you are, in twelve months.","more":"The bill grows in along the way. A young sailfish's snout starts short. It stretches out as the rest of the body lengthens underneath it. So a half-grown sailfish looks oddly stubby-nosed next to a full-sized adult.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"speed","text":"Everything on a sailfish folds flat. The pelvic fins tuck into grooves, the gill covers seal down, the sail drops away. Nothing sticks out to drag through the water.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"A big one runs 10 ft (3 m) bill to tail. They rarely top 200 lb (90 kg), about what a full-size fridge weighs.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"It lives in the warm surface layer of the open ocean. Mostly it stays above the thermocline. That is the sharp boundary where sun-warmed water sits on top of cold water below.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"Sailfish do not hold territory. They are travelers, following warm water and bait fish across whole ocean basins. They turn up enormous distances from where they were tagged.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"A fin that hides the bill, steadies the head, and folds into a groove until it's wanted. Nothing on this fish does only one job."},{"id":"ocean-sunfish","name":"Ocean sunfish","group":"fish","blurb":"A huge flat gray fish shaped like a head with two fins and no proper tail. It drifts through the open ocean. It eats almost anything small enough to swallow.","size":"Up to 10.8 ft (3.3 m) (nearly 11 ft) long and even taller fin tip to fin tip: bigger than a doorway in both directions","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"speed":2,"weight":2205,"length":10.8},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"A grown sunfish weighs 551-2,205 lb (250-1,000 kg). The heaviest one ever measured came in at 5,100 lb (2,300 kg).","more":"Measured fin tip to fin tip it can be taller than it is long, so a big one is roughly a square of fish standing on edge. Another sunfish, the bump-head, holds the record for the heaviest bony fish ever weighed: 6,049 lb (2,744 kg).","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"It hatches 0.1 in (2.5 mm) long, covered in spines. Three of them would sit side by side across your little fingernail. A survivor ends up 60 million times heavier.","more":"Nothing else with a backbone changes size like that. A human grows about twenty times heavier from birth to adult. And the larva looks nothing like the parent: a spiky little star, far closer to a pufferfish than to the giant it becomes.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"One female was estimated to be carrying 300 million eggs at once. That is more than any other known backboned animal on Earth.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"build","text":"It has no tail. Where a tail should be there's a stiff lumpy rudder called a clavus, and the fish flaps its top and bottom fins like wings instead.","more":"Most fish sweep a tail from side to side to move. The sunfish hasn't got one to sweep. Its dorsal fin and anal fin beat up and down together, in time, the way a penguin flies underwater, and the clavus does the steering at the back.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"speed","text":"It looks lazy and isn't. Tagged sunfish cruise at about 2 mph (3.2 km/h) and cover 16 miles (26 km) in a day, steering the whole way.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It was long described as a jellyfish eater. When researchers checked what was actually inside them, jellyfish and salps came to only about 15%. The rest was small fish, squid and prey off the seabed.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"You'll find one lying flat on its side at the surface, looking like something has gone badly wrong. Nothing has. It's rewarming after a dive into cold deep water.","more":"Adults hunt below 660 ft (200 m), where the water is dark and cold enough to slow a big body right down. Back at the top it turns broadside to the sun and lets its enormous flat side soak up heat. Researchers call it thermally recharging.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"More than 40 kinds of parasite live on and in a sunfish. Getting rid of them takes up a serious part of its day.","more":"It queues at cleaning stations where small reef fish pick parasites off its skin. At the surface it lies sideways and lets seabirds do the same job from above. And it breaches, throwing its whole body about 10 ft (3 m) clear of the water and landing flat.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Under that gray surface its skin runs up to 2.9 in (7.3 cm) (nearly 3 in) thick. It is tough rubbery collagen you could not push a thumb into. The whole thing is coated in slime.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Its teeth are fused into a single beak and its mouth doesn't fully close. It sits permanently ajar, giving every sunfish a look of mild, lasting surprise.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Mola is Latin for millstone, the big round gray stone used for grinding grain. In German the fish is called Schwimmender Kopf: the swimming head.","kind":"record"}],"wonder":"From a spiky speck you could balance on a fingernail to a ton of fish, and it is the same creature the whole way through."},{"id":"salmon","name":"Sockeye salmon","group":"fish","blurb":"A Pacific salmon that lives silver in the open sea. It comes home fire-red to the exact river it hatched in.","size":"About 2.5 ft (76 cm) long and around 8 lb (3.6 kg)","homes":["ocean","freshwater"],"stats":{"length":2.5,"weight":8,"life":4},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"At sea a sockeye is plain silver-blue, about 2.5 ft (76 cm) long. Nothing about it hints at the color that is coming.","more":"Most weigh 5 to 15 lb (2.3 to 7 kg) (5 to 15 lb). They run from 24 to 33 in (60 to 84 cm) long. Youngsters spend a year or two in a lake. Then they spend two or three more out in the open Pacific, feeding and growing. After that the journey home begins.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Then it changes. The body turns fire-engine red, the head turns bottle-green, and the male grows a humped back and a hooked jaw called a kype.","more":"The red comes from a pigment called astaxanthin, which the salmon collects by eating krill and other tiny shrimp-like animals at sea. Out there the pigment sits in the muscle, which is why salmon flesh is pink. For spawning, it moves into the skin.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"A sockeye finds the one stream it hatched in — after years in the open ocean, past hundreds of wrong turns — by smell.","more":"As a young fish it memorises the exact chemical scent of its home water. The returning adult follows that scent upriver to the same gravel bed. Fish that end up in the wrong stream are few enough that biologists can count them.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Some sockeye swim over 870 miles (1,400 km) (900 mi) up the Columbia and Snake Rivers. They finish at Redfish Lake in Idaho. They climb 6,500 ft (2,000 m) uphill doing it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"The moment it enters fresh water to spawn, a sockeye stops eating. Everything after that — the swimming, the climbing, the fighting — runs on its own body.","more":"Its digestive system shuts down and its stomach shrinks. The whole journey is powered by fat and muscle stored at sea. By the time it reaches the spawning gravel, a sockeye has burned through most of itself.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"Rapids and small falls do not stop them. A salmon uses the churn at the bottom of a drop to launch itself and leaps clear of the water.","more":"Bears know exactly where those spots are and stand in them, waiting. The fish that gets past the bear still has the current to fight, and it is doing it on an empty stomach.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"The female digs the nest herself, turning on her side and beating her tail against the riverbed until a pit opens in the gravel. It is called a redd.","more":"She lays her eggs in the pit and covers them with stones. Eggs hatch in six to nine weeks, and the hatchling still has its yolk attached in a sac under its belly. It hides down in the gravel and lives off it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Before a young salmon can leave for the sea, it rebuilds its own body chemistry. The change is called smoltification.","more":"Fresh water and salt water are opposite problems: one floods a fish with water, the other drains it. Special cells in the gills switch modes, the fish turns silvery, and it stops holding against the current and starts riding it downstream.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Sockeye spawn once and then die. Every adult in that river is on a one-way trip.","more":"The bodies do not go to waste. Bears, eagles, gulls and beetles feed on them, and the rest rots into the streambed. Scientists can measure sea-borne nutrients from those salmon inside the trees growing along the bank — the ocean, feeding a forest.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Some sockeye never see the sea at all. Landlocked in lakes, they live their whole lives in fresh water and stay small, rarely over 14 in (35 cm). They are called kokanee.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"It leaves as a fingerling and comes back years later to the same handful of gravel, carrying a map it was given rather than taught."},{"id":"piranha","name":"Red-bellied piranha","group":"fish","blurb":"A flat, deep-bodied South American river fish with a red belly. Its teeth are a single row of interlocking triangles.","size":"About 10.8 in (27 cm) long, with a deep body flattened from side to side","homes":["freshwater"],"stats":{"length":0.9},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"Usually 7.9 to 11.8 in (20 to 30 cm) (8 to 12 in) long, and deep from back to belly, so it looks like a plate swimming edge-on. Big ones reach 14 in (35 cm).","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"One row of teeth top and bottom, each a flat triangle with a razor edge, meeting like the blades of a pair of scissors.","more":"The upper and lower teeth mesh into each other instead of overlapping. So a piranha bite shears rather than grips. It takes a clean disc out of whatever it lands on, then lets go. That is why a bitten fish usually swims away.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"A piranha does not lose teeth one at a time. It sheds a whole half-jaw of them at once and replaces the lot in one go.","more":"That is why a wild piranha's mouth is never gappy. One side is always fully armed while the other side regrows. Researchers found this by scanning the jaws. A complete spare set was already waiting in the bone underneath.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Mostly it eats insects, worms, snails, small fish, and fruit and seeds that drop into the river. A great deal of its food is already dead when it finds it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Piranhas shoal, and the reason worked out from the experiments is fear, not hunting. Watch a shoal and you are watching frightened fish, not a pack.","more":"In experiments, piranhas kept in bigger shoals calmed down faster than those kept in pairs — their gill-beat rate, which rises with stress, dropped sooner. No study has ever shown piranhas hunting cooperatively. They are prey too: caimans, river dolphins and big birds all eat them.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Piranhas bark. They make three different sounds, and you can hear them clearly when one is lifted out of the water.","more":"A low harmonic bark goes with a face-to-face stare-down. A shorter, deeper thud goes with squabbling over food. A sharp single pulse goes with chasing and biting. The barks come from muscles drumming against the swim bladder, which amplifies them the way a guitar body does.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Both parents build a nest in flooded plants and both guard the eggs. A piranha on nest duty will charge things many times its own size.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Young red-bellies eat the fins of other fish — a quick bite, then away. The victim usually survives and grows the fin back.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"In the dry season the rivers shrink into pools. Hundreds of piranhas end up trapped together with nothing to eat. That is the situation behind almost every dramatic story.","more":"In the wet season the Amazon spreads out into flooded forest. Food is everywhere, so piranhas scatter and ignore anything large. People swim, wash and fish in piranha rivers every day. Injuries do happen. They are almost always a single nip on a toe or a finger, in shallow, shrinking water.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Its bigger cousin, the black piranha, has been measured biting at about 320 newtons. Picture a 70 lb (32 kg) weight pressing down through those teeth.","more":"Researchers caught wild black piranhas in the Amazon and let them bite a padded force meter. For its body size, that is one of the hardest bites ever measured in a fish. You would never guess it from looking.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Those blades are a river-cleaner's tools, not a monster's — exactly the equipment its actual job requires."},{"id":"lungfish","name":"Lungfish","group":"fish","blurb":"A freshwater fish that breathes air with real lungs and can survive months sealed inside a cocoon of dried mud.","size":"Around a meter — an arm-span — and the largest African kind is longer than a bed","homes":["freshwater"],"stats":{"length":3.3},"facts":[{"cat":"build","text":"A lungfish has lungs. Real ones, joined to the throat, and it swims to the surface and gulps air with them, the same way you breathe.","more":"Most lungfish have a pair of lungs; the Australian lungfish has just one. Roughly nine-tenths of an African lungfish's oxygen comes from the air it swallows, and only a tenth from its gills and skin.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Hold an adult African lungfish underwater and it will drown. Its gills have shrunk so far that they can no longer keep it alive on their own.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"When the river dries up, it burrows into the mud, wraps itself in a cocoon of its own hardened slime, and waits. Months later, rain wakes it.","more":"The cocoon leaves one narrow tube open to the air at the mouth. Inside, the fish's whole body slows to about one-sixtieth of its normal rate — a heartbeat you would have to be patient to count. The waiting is called aestivation.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Sealed in mud, it cannot flush anything out. So it converts its own waste into urea and stores the lot inside its body until the water comes back.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"It walks. Its fins are thin threads rather than paddles, and it pushes off the bottom with them and steps along the mud instead of swimming.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"A lungfish packs more DNA into one cell than any other animal with a backbone. The marbled lungfish carries roughly 130 billion letters of code. You carry about 3 billion.","more":"That is more than forty times the amount inside one of your cells, in a fish. Extra length does not mean extra instructions, though — huge stretches of it are the same short sequences repeated over and over. A much bigger book telling a story of ordinary size.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Instead of rows of small teeth it has two hard fan-shaped plates in its mouth, and it crushes snails, crayfish and clams between them.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"The male digs the nest burrow, then stays with it. He guards the eggs and the hatchlings and beats his tail to fan fresh water over them.","more":"Newly hatched lungfish look startlingly like tadpoles. They even have feathery external gills sticking out behind the head. Those shrink away as the lungs take over. The young fish starts coming up for air like its father.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"The largest African lungfish reach about 6.6 ft (2 m) (6 ft 6 in) — longer than most beds — and look more like a thick eel than a fish.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"It lives in swamps and floodplains that disappear every dry season. Water that vanishes is not a disaster for a lungfish; it is simply the schedule it lives by.","more":"People have cut a hardened block of mud out of a dried-up riverbed and carried it a long way. Then they put it in water and watched a live fish work its way out. The fish had been in there the whole trip, sealed and waiting.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Lungs and a mud cocoon: everything a fish needs to outlast a river that comes and goes."},{"id":"honeybee","name":"Honeybee","group":"insects","blurb":"A small furry insect that lives in a colony of tens of thousands. It turns flower nectar into honey.","size":"Small enough to crawl inside a flower","homes":["backyard","forest","grassland"],"stats":{"speed":15,"length":0.0},"facts":[{"cat":"talk","text":"A bee that finds flowers comes home and dances. The angle of her waggle tells the others which way to fly. The length of it tells them how far.","more":"She runs a figure of eight on the vertical comb, shaking her body through the straight middle part. Straight up means fly toward the sun. Forty degrees left of straight up means forty degrees left of the sun. Other bees follow her, then leave and find the flowers.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Bees cannot see red — it looks like black to them. But they can see ultraviolet, and plenty of flowers wear ultraviolet stripes and bullseyes pointing straight at the nectar.","more":"To a bee, a plain yellow flower can carry a dark landing target. Your own eyes will never see it. Photographers can capture it with ultraviolet filters. The flower suddenly has a runway painted down the middle of it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"On a cloudy day she can still find the sun. A bee's eyes read the pattern of polarised light across the sky and work out where the sun must be behind the cloud.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The comb is made of wax the bees produce in their own bodies. Glands on the underside of the abdomen squeeze out flakes. The bees chew those soft and press them into shape.","more":"The hexagons are not decoration. Of all the shapes that tile a surface with no gaps, the hexagon holds the most space for the least wall — the most honey for the least wax. Mathematicians puzzled over that for a very long time before one of them finally proved it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"The hive is heated. Bees hold the nursery at about 35°C (95°F) all year, close to your own body temperature. They shiver their flight muscles to warm it. They fan their wings to cool it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"One worker makes about a twelfth of a teaspoon of honey in her whole life. The jar in your cupboard is the finished work of thousands of lives.","more":"To fill a 1 lb (450 g) jar, the colony's foragers visit roughly two million flowers. Nectar arrives thin and watery. The bees fan it inside the comb until enough water has gone. Then it will keep without spoiling.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"A worker's sting is barbed like a fish hook. In skin it holds fast, and pulling away tears it out of her. She can only ever use it once.","more":"The queen's sting is smooth and she can use hers again and again. Worker stings work fine against other insects — the barbs only catch in the stretchy skin of animals like us. A sting left behind keeps pumping for about a minute, so flick it out fast.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"Japanese honeybees kill giant hornets by piling onto one and shivering. The ball of bees hits about 46°C (115°F) — hot enough to kill the hornet, just survivable for the bees.","more":"A hornet can bite a bee in half, so fighting it head-on fails. Instead several hundred workers smother it and vibrate. The heat and the carbon dioxide they pump out work together: the bees can take about 50°C, and the hornet cannot. The whole trick lives inside that narrow gap.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"At the height of summer a queen lays over 2,000 eggs a day. That is more than her own body weight in eggs, every day. Each one goes into a cleaned cell, one at a time.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Male bees, called drones, have no sting and do no work at all. They exist to mate — and a drone that succeeds dies in the act.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Some workers are undertakers. They find dead bees inside the hive, pick the bodies up, and fly them well clear of the entrance before letting go.","more":"A hive is warm, damp and crowded. That is exactly the sort of place disease loves. Removing the dead is one of several cleaning jobs. Beekeepers actively breed for colonies that are good at it, because those colonies stay healthier.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Honey does not go moldy. Fanned down until it is thick and slightly acidic, it is a place where almost nothing can grow — nectar, preserved by wings.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Tens of thousands of bees, not one of them in charge, and what comes out of it is a warm, ordered, hexagon-perfect city."},{"id":"leafcutter-ant","name":"Leafcutter ant","group":"insects","blurb":"A tropical American ant that cuts leaves and carries them underground. It does not eat them. It feeds them to a fungus it farms.","size":"From smaller than a grain of rice to about the width of your fingernail","homes":["rainforest","forest","grassland"],"stats":{"length":0.0},"facts":[{"cat":"food","text":"Leafcutter ants cut leaves they do not eat. The leaves go underground to feed a fungus, and it is the fungus the ants live on.","more":"Down in the nest the leaves are chewed into mash and planted with the fungus, which grows through it in spongy gray gardens. The fungus grows swollen, food-packed tips, and the ants harvest those and feed them to the young. Adults also drink sap from the leaves they cut.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Workers come in sizes for different jobs. Tiny ones tend the young. Medium ones cut and carry. The largest soldiers have jaws that will cut through leather.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"A carrier cuts a piece of leaf several times heavier than she is. She hoists it over her head like a green sail. Then she jogs home holding it up the whole way.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"Look closely at a carried leaf and there is often a much smaller ant riding on it. Nobody has asked her what she is doing up there, but she snaps at the flies that come near — a bodyguard, watching the sky.","more":"Phorid flies hover above the trails and try to lay an egg on a carrier's head while her jaws are full and she cannot fight back. The rider snaps at them. The colony has essentially put a gunner on every truck.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"weird","text":"The ants grow bacteria on their own bodies. Those bacteria produce antibiotics. The ants use them to keep a weed-mold out of the fungus gardens.","more":"The mold is called Escovopsis, and it attacks nothing in the world except the fungus gardens of farming ants. The helper bacteria grow in whitish patches on the ants' outer shell, fed by the ants' own glands. They are farmers carrying their own pesticide.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"When a young queen flies off to start a colony, she carries a pellet of the old fungus garden in a pocket inside her mouth. The entire farm travels in her cheek.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"One excavated nest ran deeper than a house is tall. It held more than a thousand chambers. Digging it had taken tonnes of soil out of the ground.","more":"Every grain of that soil came up in an ant's jaws. Big colonies hold millions of ants, with separate chambers for fungus gardens, for young, and for trash, all connected by tunnels wide enough for two-way traffic.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"The nest breathes. Warm stale air drifts up and out of the central turrets while fresh air is drawn down the outer tunnels, so the gardens far below never suffocate.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"The colony has a trash dump and a crew that works it. Those ants do not go back into the main nest, and the other ants will not let them.","more":"Waste from the fungus gardens is dumped in its own chamber, where it rots and grows mold. The ants handling it are the oldest workers, given the most dangerous job last. It keeps the rot away from the gardens and the young.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"While she cuts, a worker vibrates her jaws about a thousand times a second. The buzz passes into the leaf and helps the blade slice cleanly.","more":"You can feel a version of it in a kitchen: a floppy sheet is hard to cut, a taut one is easy. The vibration also travels through the leaf and into the ground, so nearby ants know that cutting is happening here.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"Their trails are real roads, swept clear of pebbles and litter, running tens of meters from the nest and busy in both directions.","more":"A large colony can strip a small tree bare in a single night. The column heads out empty and comes back green, hour after hour, and the same road gets used again the following night.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"If something digs into the nest, the big soldiers come up out of the ground. They clamp down with those leather-cutting jaws. Then they simply do not let go.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"An insect that plants, weeds, fertilises and composts — farming perfectly well without ever having been taught how."},{"id":"bullet-ant","name":"Bullet ant","group":"insects","blurb":"A giant rainforest ant, up to 1.2 in (3 cm) long. It carries the most painful sting ever measured in an insect.","size":"Workers run 0.71–1.18 in (18–30 mm) long — among the biggest ants alive.","homes":["rainforest","forest"],"stats":{"length":0.1},"facts":[{"cat":"record","text":"An entomologist named Justin Schmidt let insects sting him on purpose and ranked the pain from 1 to 4. The bullet ant broke his own scale: 4.0+.","more":"Schmidt described it as walking over flaming charcoal with a three-inch nail embedded in your heel. In parts of South America the ant is called the veinticuatro, the twenty-four. The burning can roll on in waves for a full day.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"defence","text":"The venom carries a molecule called poneratoxin. It jams the sodium gates your nerve cells use to fire, so the pain signal never switches itself off.","more":"Nerve cells fire by flicking open tiny channels that let sodium rush in, then snapping them shut. Poneratoxin stops the shutting, so the nerve fires over and over. Muscles near the sting can shake on their own, and the pain arrives in waves for up to a day.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Disturb one and it squeaks at you. It scrapes a ridged file on its abdomen against a ridge on its waist, and lets off a sharp alarm scent at the same time.","more":"The squeaking is called stridulation — the same trick a cricket uses. Nestmates pick it up as a vibration through the nest wall. It is a warning first. The sting only comes if you keep going.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"A worker can reach 1.2 in (3 cm) — bigger than a honeybee. Set one beside an ant from your backyard and they barely look like the same kind of creature.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"For all that weaponry, the commonest thing a bullet ant carries home is sugar. It gathers nectar high in the canopy. Small insects and spiders come home too.","more":"A worker drinks nectar until her abdomen is stretched tight and hauls it home inside her own body. The sting is mostly for defending the nest, not for hunting. Very little bothers a bullet ant nest twice.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"The nest is a burrow dug in at the foot of a rainforest tree. Workers climb the trunk to the canopy to forage, then walk the whole trunk back down.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Among the Sateré-Mawé people of Brazil, a boy proves he is ready to be a man. He wears woven gloves packed with bullet ants, stingers turned inward.","more":"Around eighty ants go into a pair of gloves. He keeps them on for five to ten minutes, and must complete the ordeal twenty times over months or years. Afterwards his arms shake and go numb. It is one of the toughest initiation rites anyone has recorded.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"A bullet ant colony is small as ant cities go — several hundred workers, not millions. The best read is that the sting does the job other ants need numbers for.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"senses","text":"A forager who finds a good nectar tree walks home dragging the tip of her abdomen along the ground. That lays a scent trail. Her sisters follow it straight back to the tree.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"copied","text":"Chemists are testing the sting's poison as a crop insecticide. It is the exact molecule that makes the sting unbearable, pointed at pests instead.","more":"In one experiment a virus was engineered to deliver poneratoxin to fall armyworm caterpillars. As little as 10 nanograms was enough to paralyze them. That is far less than a grain of salt weighs. Taking a weapon apart to see how it works is a large part of venom research.","kind":"record"}],"wonder":"God gave the fiercest sting in the forest a squeak and a smell to warn you first — a fair chance to back away."},{"id":"army-ant","name":"Army ant","group":"insects","blurb":"A wandering ant that builds no permanent nest. The whole colony marches and hunts as one enormous swarm. It makes its shelter out of its own bodies.","size":"Workers run from 0.12 to 0.47 in (3 to 12 mm) (0.1–0.5 in) — the smallest like a comma on this page, the biggest about the length of your smallest fingernail.","homes":["rainforest","forest","grassland"],"stats":{"length":0.0},"facts":[{"cat":"build","text":"At the end of a day's march the colony builds its shelter out of itself. The ants link legs into a hanging ball, bigger than a basketball, with the queen sealed inside.","more":"It is called a bivouac. The oldest, toughest workers form the outer shell; the young ones and the growing brood stay in the warm middle. The living wall even holds the inside at a steadier temperature than the air outside.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"When a gap opens in the trail, ants throw themselves across it and lock together into a living bridge. The column runs over their backs. Then the bridge gets up and follows.","more":"Others do road repair. A worker whose body is the right size flattens herself into a pothole. The traffic then runs smooth over the top of her. Researchers measured the bridges too. The ants take one apart as soon as holding it costs more bodies than the shortcut saves.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"A single Eciton burchellii colony holds 100,000 to 600,000 ants. One raid can send up to 200,000 of them out of the bivouac at once.","more":"The workers come in a range of sizes, from 0.12 in (3 mm) minims up to 0.47 in (12 mm) submajors, and the biggest of all are the soldiers. Every one of them is a sister, and every one of them came from the same single queen.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"A raiding colony hauls home around 3,000 prey items an hour — and almost every one is an insect, a spider or a scorpion. Their favorite target is other ants' nests.","more":"They do not eat cattle, and they do not eat people. That is film-making. What a swarm really does is flush the forest floor: everything that can run, runs — which is exactly why other animals have learned to follow along behind.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Most workers are effectively blind. Each eye is reduced to a single lens, enough to tell light from dark and nothing more. They read the whole world by smell and touch.","more":"The trail is a chemical line laid down by the ants in front. Antennae sweep it constantly, left and right, like a blind person's cane. Rub out a stretch of the scent and the column stalls and casts about until it finds the line again.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"The queen has no wings and an abdomen swollen like a broad bean. In one burst each cycle she lays tens of thousands of eggs, and they all hatch together.","more":"She never leaves the middle of the colony. When a colony grows too large it splits in two. The old queen takes one half and a daughter queen takes the other. The two armies march away from each other and never meet again.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"They live on a 35-day clock. For about 15 nights the colony marches to a brand-new bivouac every single night. Then for about 20 days it stops and stays put.","more":"The brood runs the schedule. While the larvae are growing they are ravenous, so the colony hunts hard and moves nightly. One move takes six to eight hours. The moment the larvae spin up into pupae and stop eating, the colony settles and the queen begins laying.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"Researchers have recorded 557 kinds of animal traveling with this one species of army ant. Twenty-one species of antbird check the bivouac each morning to see whether the ants are raiding.","more":"The antbirds do not eat the ants. They eat the insects the ants panic out of the leaf litter. And certain butterflies follow the antbirds to drink from their droppings. One column of ants carries a whole traveling neighbourhood along with it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"A raid front can spread 66 ft (20 m) across. That is about five cars parked bumper to bumper. A column trails 650 ft (200 m) behind it, the length of two football fields.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Soldiers grow sickle-shaped jaws so long and so curved that they cannot feed themselves. Other workers feed them. Their whole job is to stand at the edge of the column facing outward.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"They have no nest. No tunnels, no chambers, no address — an army ant colony is a city that never stands still for more than about three weeks at a stretch.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Above 43°C (109°F) the ants abandon the trail entirely. On a hot day one sunlit patch of bare ground can shut a raid down until the shade comes back.","more":"Ants caught out on ground that hot need upwards of half an hour in the shade before they can work again. To a column that reads the world by touch and smell at ground level, bare sunlit soil is a wall as real as a river.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Not one of them can see the gap, and not one of them is giving orders — yet the bridge appears exactly where a bridge is needed."},{"id":"monarch-butterfly","name":"Monarch butterfly","group":"insects","blurb":"An orange-and-black butterfly that eats poison as a caterpillar. One single generation then flies most of the way across a continent.","size":"Wings spread 3.5–3.9 in (9–10 cm) — about as wide as a grown-up's open palm.","homes":["grassland","forest","backyard"],"stats":{"speed":6},"facts":[{"cat":"travel","text":"Some monarchs fly from Canada to central Mexico — up to 2,800 miles (4,500 km). Not one of them has ever been there before, and no butterfly alive remembers the way.","more":"They gather on the same mountainsides, in the same groves of oyamel fir, generation after generation. The butterflies that arrive are the great-grandchildren of the ones that left Mexico. That was the previous spring. Whatever they navigate by, it is not memory.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"A monarch steers by the sun. But the sun slides across the sky all day, so it needs a clock to correct for that — and the clock sits in its antennae.","more":"Researchers painted monarch antennae with black enamel and the butterflies lost their heading; clear paint changed nothing. The antennae hold a working daily clock that tells the brain what time it is, so the brain knows where the sun ought to be.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Summer monarchs live two to five weeks. The generation that hatches in late summer lives seven or eight months — it flies south, waits out the winter, and starts back north in spring.","more":"Its body simply postpones growing up: no eggs, no mating, all fall. It coasts through the cold on a Mexican mountainside, and only the lengthening days of spring switch its reproduction back on.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Monarch caterpillars eat milkweed and nothing else. Milkweed is loaded with cardenolides, which are heart poisons. The caterpillar stores them in its own body instead of breaking them down.","more":"A bird that eats a monarch usually throws up. Then it learns the orange-and-black pattern and leaves the next one alone. The wings carry roughly three times the dose the rest of the body does. So a bird that grabs a wingtip gets the warning and lets go.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Milkweed fights back. Cut a leaf and it bleeds sticky latex that can glue a caterpillar's mouth shut. So the caterpillar bites a trench across the leaf first, drains the pressure, then eats past it.","more":"Look at a monarch caterpillar on a big leaf and you can usually spot the notch it cut. The latex pours out of the trench instead of into its jaws. It is a caterpillar solving a plumbing problem with its mouth.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"A monarch egg is barely bigger than a pinhead, about 0.05 in (1.2 mm) tall. In roughly two weeks the caterpillar that hatches out of it grows around two thousand times heavier.","more":"It eats almost without stopping and moults five times, shedding a skin it has already outgrown at every stage. The last skin it wriggles out of reveals the chrysalis waiting underneath.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The chrysalis is jade green with a rim of gold dots, like something out of a jeweler's case. Inside it the caterpillar rebuilds itself completely. That takes about eleven or twelve days.","more":"Near the end the green wall turns clear, and you can see the folded orange and black wings pressed against the inside, finished and waiting. Warm weather speeds it up; cool weather can stretch it to a fortnight.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"A female monarch tastes with her feet. She drums her front legs on a leaf, and chemical sensors in her tarsi tell her whether it is milkweed before she will lay an egg.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"They winter in a handful of mountain fir forests in Michoacán, Mexico. They pack onto the branches so thickly that the trees look wrong. It is bark made of butterflies.","more":"They need it cold but not freezing, and the fir canopy works as blanket and umbrella at once. When midday sun reaches the clusters, whole sections of the forest lift into the air together.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"The viceroy butterfly looks almost exactly like a monarch. People long assumed it was a harmless copycat. Then taste tests showed viceroys are foul too — both of them are telling the truth.","more":"When two nasty-tasting species share one warning pattern, it is called Müllerian mimicry. A bird that learns the lesson from either one then leaves both alone. The two species share the work of teaching the birds.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"speed","text":"A monarch cruises at about 6 mph (9 km/h) — slower than you can run. It crosses a continent by finding rising columns of warm air and gliding, saving its wingbeats.","more":"Glider pilots have spotted monarchs hundreds of meters up, riding thermals the way a hawk does. Flapping the whole way south would burn more fuel than a butterfly that light could possibly carry.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Nobody knew where they went. Volunteers stuck numbered tags on butterfly wings and mailed in sightings. It took decades of that before the Mexican wintering forests were finally tracked down.","kind":"record"}],"wonder":"Half a gram of butterfly crossing a continent to a forest it has never seen — and arriving. Nobody taught it the way. It was given the map."},{"id":"atlas-moth","name":"Atlas moth","group":"insects","blurb":"One of the largest moths on earth. This hand-sized insect has snake heads drawn on its wingtips. As an adult it never eats a single meal.","size":"Wings spread about 10 in (25 cm) — wider than a sheet of paper — and the largest females reach 12 in (30 cm).","homes":["rainforest","forest"],"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"Hold your hand out flat and spread your fingers as wide as they go. An atlas moth's wings can be wider than that, tip to tip.","more":"Very few insects carry that much wing. Females are the big ones — noticeably larger and heavier than males — while the males have far broader, feathery antennae. The wings are huge in area as well as span, which is what makes one so startling up close.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"An adult atlas moth has no working mouth. It cannot eat or drink, not once, not ever. Everything it will spend was eaten by the caterpillar it used to be.","more":"The adult lives about a week or two on fat stored by the larva, and every hour of that goes to one job: finding a mate. It is essentially a flying reproductive stage with a fuel tank that never refills.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"Look at the tip of each forewing. There is a snake's head drawn there — dark eye, curved snout, mouth line — one on the left and one on the right.","more":"In Hong Kong it is called the snake's head moth. Markings that startle a predator rather than hide from it are called deimatic — a bluff rather than camouflage. Whether birds truly see a snake here is not settled, but the resemblance is hard to miss.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"The male's antennae are wide feathery combs. They pick up a female's scent from kilometers downwind — a few molecules of pheromone drifting in a whole night's worth of air.","more":"Each branch of the comb is furred with receptor hairs tuned to that one chemical and nothing else. He flies zig-zags across the wind, turns back whenever the scent drops out, and rides that invisible thread all the way in.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"The caterpillar reaches 4.5 in (11.5 cm) long and 1 in (2.5 cm) thick. That is longer than your hand is wide and thicker than your thumb. It is pale green under a dusting of white wax.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"It spins a papery brown cocoon 2.8–3.1 in (7–8 cm) long. The cocoon is slung inside a dead leaf. The whole thing hangs off a twig looking like ordinary forest litter.","more":"The silk is called fagara. It is coarser, browner and tougher than the smooth white silk of the domestic silkworm. In parts of India people gather the cocoons and weave with it. Often they collect them after the moth has already flown.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Each wing carries a triangular window with no scales on it at all, clear as cellophane. Hold a spread atlas moth up to the light and you can see straight through in four places.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"It belongs to the warm forests of South, East and Southeast Asia, from India across to Borneo, and it flies at night.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Which moth is 'biggest' depends entirely on how you measure. The atlas moth is near the top for wing area and near the top for wingspan — but it is not top of either.","more":"The white witch of Central and South America stretches further from wingtip to wingtip. The Hercules moth of Australia and New Guinea carries more wing area. All three are enormous in a way that photographs never quite convey.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"For all that wing, it flies slowly and heavily. Most of the day it simply rests with its wings held flat and open, and the best read is that the pattern does the work of a bodyguard.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"travel","text":"The female barely travels at all. She waits near the cocoon she came out of, releases her scent into the dark, and lets the males do the flying.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"A creature that will never once eat, given a week to live and wings worth stopping everything to look at."},{"id":"dung-beetle","name":"Dung beetle","group":"insects","blurb":"A beetle that finds fresh animal droppings and carves out a piece. It shapes the piece into a ball and rolls it away. Then it raises its family inside it.","size":"Most are between a grain of rice and your thumb in length — 0.2 in (5 mm) to 1.2 in (3 cm) (0.2–1.2 in) — though a few tropical giants get much bigger.","homes":["grassland","forest","desert"],"facts":[{"cat":"record","text":"A male Onthophagus taurus was measured pulling 1,141 times its own body weight. Scaled up to you, that is like towing 1,141 kids your size at once.","more":"The pull happens underground. Two males meet head to head in a narrow tunnel and shove, horns locked, until one of them gives up and backs out. Almost everything about that beetle's body is built around winning that one contest.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"A dung beetle rolls its ball in a dead straight line. On moonless nights the African species Scarabaeus satyrus holds that line by reading the pale band of the Milky Way.","more":"Researchers fitted beetles with tiny caps and tested them inside a planetarium. Under a full starfield they rolled straight. Under a blank ceiling, or wearing a cap, they wandered in circles. They are not reading individual stars — they steer by the whole bright smear of the galaxy.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"Before it sets off, the beetle climbs on top of its ball and turns a full circle. That little dance is how it takes a snapshot of the sky and locks in a heading.","more":"It dances again every time something knocks it off course. Straight-line rolling matters because the dung pile behind it is crawling with thieves. Any direction will do, as long as it is away, and as long as it never curves back.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Adults of many species do not chew dung at all. They squeeze it and drink the liquid, straining out microscopic particles, because the fluid is where the nutrition actually is.","more":"Their mouthparts are soft and brushy rather than hard and biting. The larva is the one that handles solid food, eating its way through a sealed ball its mother built and packed for it in advance.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"The mother buries a ball, lays one single egg inside it, and seals it shut. The larva hatches into the middle of its own food supply and eats its way outward.","more":"In some species she stays underground with it for weeks, patching cracks in the ball and scraping off mold. That is unusually careful parenting for an insect, and the grubs she tends survive far better than the ones left alone.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"There are three ways to make a living here. Rollers cut a ball and drive it away. Tunnellers dig straight down beneath the pile and drag dung under. Dwellers just move in and live in it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"One beetle can bury 250 times its own mass of dung in a single night. Drop a 2 lb (1 kg) pile of elephant dung on African ground after dark and 13,700 beetles turn up to work it.","more":"Beetles land within seconds of dung hitting the ground. With that many at work a pile can be shredded and buried within hours, which is why the savanna is not knee-deep in dung. One elephant's daily output can feed two million beetles.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Midday desert sand gets hot enough to cook a beetle's feet. So it climbs on top of its own dung ball to cool down, then hops off and keeps rolling.","more":"The moist ball is genuinely cooler than the sand. Researchers filmed it and measured it: on hot ground the beetles climbed far more often, and beetles fitted with tiny silicone boots climbed much less. The ball is a portable patch of shade.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Australia's imported cattle dropped wet pats that the native beetles would not touch, so twenty-three kinds of dung beetle were brought in from overseas. Bush fly numbers fell by around 90%.","more":"Native Australian dung beetles are built for small dry marsupial pellets, not for big soggy cow pats. The pats simply sat there, killing the grass underneath and breeding flies by the million. Import the right beetle and the problem buries itself.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"gross","text":"One relative gave up dung altogether. It is called Deltochilum valgum. In the Peruvian rainforest it hunts live millipedes. It wrestles them down and decapitates them.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Male horns come in two settings. Large males grow long horns and fight at the tunnel mouth. Small males grow no horns at all and dig a side tunnel to sneak past the fight.","more":"Which one a male becomes is decided by how much dung he managed to eat as a larva. Same species, same parents. Two completely different body plans, and two completely different strategies for the same goal.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"copied","text":"The Egyptians carved this beetle in stone by the thousand. They had watched it push a ball across the ground, and made it their emblem for the sun being pushed across the sky.","kind":"record"}],"wonder":"The dirtiest job in the world, handed to a beetle that steers by the galaxy."},{"id":"bombardier-beetle","name":"Bombardier beetle","group":"insects","blurb":"A small ground beetle that defends itself by firing a boiling-hot chemical spray out of its back end.","size":"Small enough to balance on your thumbnail.","homes":["forest","grassland","backyard"],"stats":{"length":0.0},"facts":[{"cat":"defence","text":"Poke a bombardier beetle and it fires back. The spray leaves its body at about 100°C (212°F) — the temperature of boiling water.","more":"Two liquids, hydrogen peroxide and hydroquinones, sit quietly in a storage sac. When the beetle is threatened it squeezes them into a tiny reaction chamber lined with enzymes. The reaction is instant and violent, and the boiling liquid blasts out of a nozzle at the tail.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"The spray does not come out in one smooth squirt. It fires in pulses — around 500 every second — which is why you hear a sharp pop.","more":"A single blast runs to about 70 pulses and is over in a fraction of a second. High-speed X-ray films show the chamber filling, firing and refilling faster than an eye can follow. The pulsing is what keeps the beetle from cooking itself: cool liquid refills the chamber between shots.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The tip of its abdomen swivels. The beetle can aim over its own back, out to either side, and even forward past its own head.","more":"Some species skip aiming altogether. They fire against a small shield-shaped plate near the leg. That splashes the spray sideways, in whatever direction the attack came from. Between swiveling and deflecting, there is almost no angle from which you could safely grab one.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Toads that swallow a bombardier beetle sometimes vomit it back up — alive, walking, unbothered. In one case the beetle came back out more than an hour later.","more":"In experiments, swallowed beetles fired their spray inside the toad's stomach. A good number of toads gave up and spat them out. Some beetles had been made to empty their spray beforehand. Those were far less likely to come back out at all.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Adults are night hunters. They run down caterpillars, maggots and other soft-bodied insects on the ground — a bombardier beetle is a sprinter, not a stalker.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"In many bombardier beetles the larva is not a hunter at all. A newly hatched grub goes looking for the buried pupa of a water beetle, latches on, and eats it slowly.","more":"It only ever needs that one meal. Once settled, the larva changes into a fat, legless grub that never leaves the spot. Different bombardier species specialize in different victims. Some take whirligig beetle pupae, others take water scavenger beetles.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"About a centimeter of beetle — less than half an inch. The reservoir, the mixing chamber, the valve and the swivelling nozzle are all packed into the back end of that.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"No other animal is known to fire boiling liquid at an attacker. More than 500 bombardier beetle species do it. They live on every continent except Antarctica.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"copied","text":"Engineers have studied that pulsing blast while designing fuel injectors and fire extinguishers. A nozzle that fires in bursts throws liquid further than one that simply streams.","more":"The beetle solves a problem engineers care about. It pushes a fine spray a long way out of a very small opening, without a pump. Working prototypes based on the beetle's fill-fire-refill cycle have been built and tested.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"home","text":"Look under stones, logs and damp leaf litter, often close to water. Turn a rock over gently — most bombardier beetles would far rather run than fire.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"The spray is not just hot. It carries benzoquinones, chemicals that stain skin brown and burn the eyes and mouth of anything trying to eat the beetle.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"God fitted a mixing chamber, a valve and a swiveling nozzle inside a tiny beetle."},{"id":"hercules-beetle","name":"Hercules beetle","group":"insects","blurb":"The longest beetle in the world — a rainforest giant whose males carry a horn longer than the rest of their body.","size":"As long as your whole hand, wrist to fingertip — up to about 6.7 in (17 cm), horn included.","homes":["rainforest"],"stats":{"length":0.6,"life":2},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"A big male stretches about 6.7 in (17 cm) — long enough to hang over both edges of your palm. More than half of that length is horn.","more":"The horn is really two pieces: a long one curving down from the thorax and a shorter one curving up from the head. Together they work like a pair of pincers. Females have no horn at all and are noticeably shorter.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The two horns close like a nutcracker. A male grips a rival between them, prises him off the branch, lifts him into the air, and drops him.","more":"Fights break out over sap runs on tree trunks and the females feeding there. The beetles rarely injure each other — most contests end the moment the loser loses his grip. Falling out of a tree is no great problem when you are this armored.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Its wing cases change color. In dry air they are olive-yellow. Let the air turn humid and they darken to black — then pale again as it dries out.","more":"The color is not pigment. The outer layer of the wing case is a sponge-like lattice of microscopic channels that scatters yellow light back at you. When moisture soaks in and fills those channels the scattering stops, and you see the dark layer underneath.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"The Hercules belongs to a group called the rhinoceros beetles. They were tested carrying loads in the lab. They walked around under thirty times their own body weight. They barely worked harder for it.","more":"You will often hear that these beetles lift 850 times their weight. That number gets repeated everywhere, but it does not come from a careful measurement. The thirty-times figure does. Researchers strapped weights to rhinoceros beetles and measured the oxygen they burned while walking.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"The grub is as long as your hand and as thick as your thumb. It can top 3.5 oz (100 g) (a quarter of a pound). That is heavier than the adult beetle it will become.","more":"Hercules grubs are white, C-shaped and enormous. They spend up to two years chewing through rotten logs on the forest floor, and everything they break down goes back into the soil. When the grub finally pupates, the whole horn is folded up inside the case, waiting.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"For all that armor, the adult is a fruit-eater. It drinks tree sap and mashes soft, rotting fruit. It has no interest whatsoever in biting you.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Pick one up and it hisses at you. The beetle rasps its abdomen against the underside of its wing cases to make the sound — a warning, not a voice.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"It flies. A beetle this heavy gets airborne with a low droning buzz you can hear right across a forest clearing, horn and all.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Its antennae end in a stack of flat plates that open out like a tiny paper fan. That fan is its nose — it can smell fermenting fruit from a long way off.","more":"Spreading the plates apart hugely increases the surface carrying smell receptors. Then the beetle folds them shut again for protection. All scarab beetles share this design. It is one of the quickest ways to recognize the family.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"No sting, no venom, no bite worth worrying about. Nothing much eats one, and the best read is that it is simply too large, too hard and too awkward to swallow.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"home","text":"They live in the rainforests of Central and South America and the Caribbean islands. The range runs from southern Mexico down to Bolivia. They stay mostly high up in the trees.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Only well-fed males grow the giant horns. A grub that finds a rich, rotting log becomes a monster; a grub on poor wood becomes a small male with a stubby one.","more":"The horn is built during the pupal stage. It comes out of whatever the grub managed to store while eating. Two brothers from the same batch of eggs can end up looking like different animals entirely. The only difference is the logs they landed in.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Everything about it is oversized — and God still folded that whole horn away inside a grub in a rotten log."},{"id":"firefly","name":"Firefly","group":"insects","blurb":"A beetle that makes its own light in its abdomen. It uses that light to talk, flashing a coded signal on summer nights.","size":"Even the biggest fireflies are under an inch (25 mm) long. Most are smaller.","homes":["forest","grassland","backyard"],"stats":{"length":0.0,"life":1},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"A firefly is not a fly. It is a beetle, small enough to sit on your fingernail — watch one land and you will see the hard wing cases fold down over its back.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The lamp is on the underside of the last segments of its abdomen. A chemical called luciferin meets oxygen and an enzyme called luciferase. The reaction gives off light.","more":"This is bioluminescence — living light. The firefly controls the flash by controlling oxygen: it opens microscopic air tubes to let oxygen reach the reacting cells, then shuts them off to stop the glow. A gas called nitric oxide works the switch.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"It is cold light. A bulb turns most of its energy into heat, but a firefly's lamp gives off so little you cannot feel it at all through your closed hand.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Every species has its own flash pattern — a length, a rhythm, a number of pulses. A male signals as he flies; a female answers from the grass if he got the code right.","more":"Some males flash one long dash. Some draw a quick J-shaped swoop of light. Some fire a burst of five or six. She replies after a precise pause, measured in fractions of a second. Get the pause wrong and nobody answers.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Firefly larvae are ferocious. They hunt snails and slugs in the leaf litter, inject them to turn their insides to soup, and drink the result.","more":"Most of a firefly's life is spent down there as a glowing larva, sometimes for a year or two. The adult flashing over your lawn in July may have only a few weeks left, and in some kinds the adult barely eats at all.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Females of one group, the Photuris fireflies, copy the flash codes of other species. She answers a male who is not her own kind, he flies down to her, and she eats him.","more":"She is not only hungry. The males she eats carry bitter defensive chemicals called lucibufagins, which she cannot make herself. After the meal those chemicals are in her body and in her eggs — and a jumping spider that grabs her is likely to let her go again.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Firefly blood tastes appalling. A bird that grabs one usually drops it fast, and fireflies are genuinely poisonous to some lizards, so never feed one to a pet.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"In a few valleys of the Great Smoky Mountains, thousands of fireflies flash at the same instant. Dark, dark, dark — then the whole hillside lights up together.","more":"The synchronous firefly fires four to eight flashes in a row, then the entire group falls dark for eight to twelve seconds before the next wave rolls through. So many people want to sit in those woods and watch that the park hands out places by lottery.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"copied","text":"Firefly luciferase is used in laboratories all over the world. Swab a kitchen surface, add the chemical, and anything living on it glows. That is how food factories check a bench is truly clean.","more":"The reaction needs ATP, the fuel molecule every living cell runs on. No ATP, no glow. Researchers also put the firefly's gene into other cells to make them light up, so they can follow exactly where those cells travel inside a living body.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Not every firefly flashes. Plenty of day-flying kinds have no working lamp at all as adults and find each other by smell instead.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"A male firefly has far bigger eyes than the female. The best read is the job those eyes do: picking a half-second flash out of a dark field full of waving grass, with one chance at it.","kind":"worked"}],"wonder":"God put a lantern in a beetle under an inch (25 mm) long — and then gave it a language to speak with it."},{"id":"dragonfly","name":"Dragonfly","group":"insects","blurb":"A long-bodied hunting insect with four glassy wings. It can flap them one at a time. That makes it one of the finest fliers alive.","size":"Body about as long as your finger; the biggest spread about 6 in (16 cm) of wing — wider than your hand.","homes":["freshwater","grassland","forest"],"stats":{"speed":22,"length":0.3,"life":1},"facts":[{"cat":"build","text":"Four wings, four separate controls. A dragonfly can beat each wing on its own, which is how it hovers, flies backwards, and stops dead in mid-air.","more":"Most insects flap both wings on a side together. A dragonfly's muscles attach straight onto each wing, so it can twist one, hold another still, and pivot around a single point in the air. It can even fly upside down for short stretches.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"In laboratory tests, hunting dragonflies caught up to 95 out of every 100 flies they went after. No lion or shark comes anywhere near that strike rate.","more":"It does not chase. It calculates. The dragonfly reads its target's speed and direction and works out where that target is going to be. Then it flies to the empty patch of air to meet it. It arrives from below and behind, where a fly cannot see.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Almost the whole head is eye. A big dragonfly's compound eyes hold more than 20,000 separate lenses and see nearly all the way around it at once.","more":"Each tiny lens, an ommatidium, sends back a single dot of the picture — one hawker was counted at 22,650 of them. The lenses pointing forwards and upwards are the largest and sharpest, because that is the patch of sky it hunts in. Dragonflies also see ultraviolet, which we cannot.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A young dragonfly lives underwater — for months, sometimes for years — before it ever sees the sky. Then it climbs a reed stem, splits its skin, and walks out with wings.","more":"The nymph breathes through gills inside its rectum, at the far end of its body. Squeeze that chamber hard and water shoots out the back, so a nymph can jet forward like a tiny squid when it needs to move in a hurry.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"The nymph's lower lip is a folded harpoon. It shoots out from under the face, grabs a tadpole or a young fish, and folds back — faster than your eye can follow.","more":"Biologists call it the labial mask. At rest it covers the nymph's face like a visor. It fires on hydraulic pressure. The nymph squeezes its abdomen, and the fluid inside shoots the lip out. That is how something that small moves that fast from a standing start.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"Nobody has followed one the whole way. The best read is that the globe skimmer crosses open ocean between India and Africa. That is about 1,900 miles (3,000 km) of water in one flight, with nowhere to land.","more":"The full round trip runs to roughly 11,000 miles (18,000 km), and no single dragonfly completes it. A chain of generations does. Each one lays eggs in temporary pools that appear after a storm and dry up weeks later. Globe skimmers ride high winds. One was recorded at 20,000 ft (6,200 m) over the Himalayas.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"speed","text":"A big hawker dragonfly can hit 22 mph (36 km/h) in a burst — quick enough to run down almost anything else flying at that size.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Its legs are a basket, not a walking kit. A dragonfly can barely walk at all; it holds those spiny legs forward and scoops prey out of the air with them.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Look near the tip of each wing for a small dark rectangle. It is called a pterostigma, and it is a weight — it steadies the wing and stops it fluttering at speed.","more":"Take the weight away and the wing tip starts to shudder as the dragonfly speeds up. That wastes energy and can tear a wing. The pterostigma pushes the shuddering back to higher speeds. Measurements put the gain at 10 to 25 percent.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Find them at ponds, slow rivers and marshes. A male will patrol one stretch of bank all day, flying the same beat over and over and chasing off every rival that enters it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"copied","text":"Engineers building tiny flying robots study dragonfly wings closely. Nothing people have made yet matches that mix of hover, speed and instant turning in something so light.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"size","text":"The biggest dragonflies spread about 6 in (16 cm) of wing — wider than your hand — on a body you could balance on one finger. Damselflies are the slim cousins.","more":"Most damselflies fold their wings up along the body when they land; a dragonfly holds its wings out flat, always. That is the quickest way to tell the two apart, and it works even on a species you have never seen before.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Four wings, twenty thousand lenses, and the sense to aim at where you will be — all in something light enough to land on a reed."},{"id":"praying-mantis","name":"Praying mantis","group":"insects","blurb":"A long-necked ambush hunter. It sits perfectly still with its spined front legs folded. Then it grabs whatever walks past.","size":"Most are about as long as your finger; the biggest are longer than your whole hand — over 6 in (15 cm).","homes":["forest","grassland","backyard"],"stats":{"length":0.3,"life":1},"facts":[{"cat":"speed","text":"The strike is over in less than a tenth of a second. You cannot blink that fast — and the mantis can still change its aim halfway through.","more":"It holds its forelegs folded and cocked like a spring. Biologists call them raptorial legs, the same word used for a hawk's talons. When a fly comes within reach they snap out and shut again with the prey pinned between two rows of spines.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Scientists glued tiny 3-D glasses onto mantises and showed them films of bugs. The mantis struck at the spot where the two images overlapped — it really does judge depth with two eyes.","more":"It is the only insect known to have stereo vision, but it works nothing like ours. A mantis does not compare the two pictures for detail, only for movement. A perfectly still object stays invisible to its depth sense, which is exactly why it waits for you to twitch.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"A mantis hears with a single ear, sunk in a groove down the middle of its chest between the back legs. It is tuned to a sound you cannot hear: the hunting calls of bats.","more":"There is one narrow chamber with a hearing membrane on each wall, which is why it works as one ear rather than two. When a flying mantis picks up bat ultrasound, it stops flapping, folds up and spirals toward the ground. In field tests, mantises that dived were caught far less often.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"It can turn its head almost halfway round — close to 180 degrees — and look straight back over its own shoulder. Almost no other insect manages that; a mantis has a real neck.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"A mantis eats whatever it can hold: crickets, moths, wasps, other mantises. Some of the large ones have been photographed catching hummingbirds at garden feeders.","more":"Researchers gathered records from a dozen countries of mantises taking small birds, nearly always hummingbirds caught while hovering. The mantis simply waits on the feeder itself. It is rare, but it is well documented, and it is why feeders should be checked.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"The orchid mantis looks like a flower — and not in order to hide. Bees fly straight to it. In tests it pulled in more insects than the real blossoms growing around it.","more":"Its legs are widened into pale pink petal shapes. Researchers set real flowers beside orchid mantises and counted the visitors, and the mantis won. That makes it a lure, not just a hiding place — it does not even need to be sitting on a plant for it to work.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"Other mantises copy dead leaves, green stems, bark and lichen. They do it so precisely that you can stare straight at one for a minute. You still see nothing but a twig.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"The female whips up a froth with her own body and lays her eggs inside it. It sets into a tough tan case called an ootheca, and in spring the hatchlings squeeze out all at once.","more":"One case holds anywhere from ten to four hundred eggs, depending on the species. The foam sets about as hard as styrofoam and shrugs off rain and frost all winter. The hatchlings come out on fine threads within minutes of each other and scatter fast — because the first thing a young mantis will eat is another young mantis.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Females do sometimes eat the male after mating. But not always, and not in every species. Plenty of males simply walk away afterwards.","more":"Careful field studies found cannibalism in roughly a quarter of the matings they watched. When it does happen, researchers have traced the male's body directly into the eggs. A female who has eaten one lays eggs that are better supplied.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Cornered, a mantis rears up, throws its wings wide and shows patches of color it normally keeps hidden. It suddenly looks twice its real size, and the best read is a bluff — one that usually works.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"size","text":"A newly hatched mantis is tiny. A typical adult runs about 3.6 in (9 cm), and the biggest are longer than your whole hand. Hatchling and adult hunt exactly the same way — by standing still until something walks past.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Look on shrubs, long grass and flower heads through the warm months. A mantis hardly moves at all, and when it does walk it rocks back and forth, the way a leaf moves in a breeze.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"God gave the mantis two eyes that judge distance, one ear that hears bats, and a neck to turn and watch you back."},{"id":"orchid-mantis","name":"Orchid mantis","group":"insects","blurb":"A hunting insect from the rainforests of Southeast Asia. Its whole body is shaped and colored like a flower. The flower is the trap.","size":"A grown female is about as long as your thumb. The male is less than half her size.","homes":["rainforest","forest"],"stats":{"length":0.2,"life":1},"facts":[{"cat":"disguise","text":"Look at those legs. Each of the four walking legs is flattened into a wide curved lobe, the exact shape of a petal. The mantis is not sitting on a flower. It is the flower.","more":"Biologists call this aggressive mimicry: a predator dressed as something harmless or inviting. The mantis holds still, forelegs folded, and waits. A bee or a butterfly comes shopping for nectar that was never there.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Researchers set orchid mantises out in the rainforest, beside real flowers. Then they counted the visitors. More pollinating insects flew to the mantis than to the flowers.","more":"That is the startling part. The lure works better than the real thing. Bees, flies and butterflies chose the fake over blossoms growing an arm's length away — exactly what a hunter wants, and exactly what a flower does not.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Despite the name, it is not copying one particular orchid. Measured the way a bee's eye would see it, its color sits inside the range of the flowers blooming around it.","more":"Bees see color differently from you — they pick up ultraviolet light your eyes cannot. When scientists compared the mantis with nearby blossoms using bee vision, they could not tell it apart from a flower. A general disguise, not a portrait of one species.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"A grown female is about as long as your thumb. The male is less than half her length and far lighter — the two barely look like the same insect.","more":"He is slim and quick, and he flies well. She is broad, petalled and heavy, and mostly stays put. The famous flower-shaped photographs are nearly always females.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A newly hatched orchid mantis is not pink at all. It is red and black, and it looks like an assassin bug — an insect that tastes foul and bites hard.","more":"So the smallest, softest stage of its life is spent pretending to be something dangerous. After its first moult the red and black go, the white and pink come in, and the flower disguise begins.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Mantises see in 3D. Scientists fitted them with tiny glasses and showed them an insect cinema, and the mantises struck at prey that existed only in the depth of the image.","more":"That makes them the only insects known to have stereopsis — judging distance by comparing what the left eye sees with what the right eye sees. It is the same trick your own two eyes are running right now.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Bees, flies, butterflies, small wasps. The strike comes out of those folded front legs and it is finished before you could blink.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Some mantises can swivel the head nearly halfway round without shifting the body. Almost no other insect can turn and look behind itself.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"It can shift between pink and brown to match whatever it is sitting on. The change is slow — days, not seconds. Nothing about this insect is in a hurry.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Cornered, a mantis rears up, throws its front legs wide and shows every marking it has. It suddenly looks twice its size and nothing at all like a flower.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Warm, wet rainforest across Southeast Asia: Malaysia, Indonesia, Thailand, Vietnam. It waits on leaves and stems in bright places. Flowers are everywhere there, and so are the insects that visit them.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"The disguise reaches all the way down to the shape of one leg joint — that much care, on an insect the size of your thumb."},{"id":"stick-insect","name":"Stick insect","group":"insects","blurb":"A long, slow, leaf-eating insect. It is shaped exactly like the twig it is standing on. Your eye slides straight past it.","size":"Most are about 3.6 in (9 cm) long, about the length of a finger. The record-holder stretched longer than two 12-in school rulers laid end to end.","homes":["rainforest","forest","grassland"],"stats":{"length":0.3,"life":1},"facts":[{"cat":"record","text":"The longest insect ever measured is a stick insect. Front foot to back foot with its legs stretched out, it reached 24.6 in (62.4 cm) — two school rulers, end to end.","more":"Its body alone, legs not counted, was 14.2 in (36.1 cm). The record-holder was found in southern China and is kept in a museum in Chengdu. One of its offspring, raised in captivity, grew longer still — 25 in (64 cm).","kind":"record"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"The disguise is not only the shape. It is the stillness. A stick insect can hold one position for hours, and a hunting bird's eye is built to catch movement.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"When the wind blows, it sways. Rocking gently from side to side is exactly what a real twig does in a breeze — so moving makes it harder to spot, not easier.","more":"Stillness only works while everything around it is still. The moment the branch starts moving, a perfectly motionless twig is the odd one out. So it joins in, swaying along with the branch.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Many stick insects need no male at all. A female on her own lays eggs that hatch into daughters, every one of them a copy of her. It is called parthenogenesis.","more":"In some species almost the entire population is female. Where males do exist they can be rare, and much smaller than the females. A female that has never met one will still fill a branch with young.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"The eggs look like seeds — hard-shelled, and capped with a fatty knob called a capitulum. Ants find them, carry them underground, eat the knob and leave the egg unharmed.","more":"The egg then sits inside an ant nest: dark, damp, and guarded by ants against the wasps and beetles that would eat it. When it finally hatches, the young stick insect walks out through the ants' own tunnels.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Grabbed by a leg, a stick insect can simply let the leg go. The joint has a built-in break point. A young one grows the missing leg back over its next few moults.","more":"This is autotomy — shedding a body part to escape. The bird is left holding a twitching leg while the rest of the insect walks away. The replacement usually comes back a little smaller than the original.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Some species fight back with chemistry. A pair of glands just behind the head fire a fine spray, and they aim it. If it reaches your eyes it burns badly.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"Not all of them are thin. The Lord Howe Island stick insect grows up to 8 in (20 cm) and weighs about 0.8 oz (23 g) — the weight of a AA battery. Islanders called it the tree lobster.","more":"It has no wings at all — heavy, glossy black, and it runs rather than flies. It was thought gone from the world entirely, until scientists climbing a bare sea stack called Ball's Pyramid found twenty-four of them living under a single shrub.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"Tree lobsters pair up. Keepers watching them at night see the male resting with three of his legs draped over his female — behavior almost unheard of anywhere else among insects.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Leaves, and nothing else. Stick insects chew through foliage at night when birds are not hunting, and spend the daylight hours pretending to be part of the plant.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Over 3,500 species are known. Some are shaped like leaves rather than twigs — flat bodies with fake veins, dried-looking brown edges, and holes that look nibbled.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Nothing about a stick insect asks to be noticed. Every part of it was made to be looked straight past — and it works, all day, every day."},{"id":"cicada","name":"Periodical cicada","group":"insects","blurb":"A big red-eyed insect that spends more than a decade underground, drinking from tree roots. Then it climbs out in millions all at once to sing.","size":"About 1.2 in (3 cm) long, with clear wings that reach past the end of its body.","homes":["forest","backyard"],"stats":{"length":0.1,"life":17},"facts":[{"cat":"home","text":"A periodical cicada spends 13 or 17 years underground. Not asleep — feeding, the whole time, on xylem fluid pulled from tree roots in the dark.","more":"Xylem is the plumbing that carries water up a tree, roots to leaves. It is thin stuff, mostly water, so a nymph has to pump a great deal of it to grow. It usually stays within about 2 ft (60 cm) of the surface the whole time.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Thirteen and seventeen. Both are prime numbers. Nothing divides into them evenly except one and themselves. Every periodical cicada runs on one of those two clocks. Never twelve, never sixteen.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"When they come up, they come up together. More than 1.5 million cicadas per acre have been counted — around 370 in every single square meter of forest floor.","more":"That is five or six cicadas on every patch of ground the size of your hand. It runs for miles in every direction. Every bird, squirrel, fish and dog eats until it cannot manage another. Millions are still left over to sing.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"The chorus can reach 100 decibels — as loud as a chainsaw or a motorbike going past. It is not a noise you hear so much as a wall you stand inside.","more":"Only the males sing. Each has a pair of ribbed plates called tymbals on the sides of his body. Muscles buckle those plates in and out hundreds of times a second, and the mostly hollow abdomen behind them works like the body of a guitar.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"They do not use a calendar. The nymphs come up when the soil about 8 in (20 cm) down passes 18°C (64°F). They are reading the temperature, underground, in the dark.","more":"Then the whole neighborhood goes at once. They dig upward and climb the nearest vertical thing. That means a trunk, a fence post, or your leg if you stand still long enough. Then they grip on tight for the last molt of their lives.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The nymph digs with front legs thickened into shovels. At the end its back splits open, a soft pale crumpled adult hauls itself out, and the empty shell stays clinging to the bark.","more":"For an hour or two the new adult is pale and helpless while its wings unfold and its shell hardens. Those brown hollow hook-footed cases get left by the thousand on tree trunks — the easiest insect skin in the world to find.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A female cuts slits into pencil-thin twigs with a blade on her abdomen and packs about 20 eggs into each one. She will lay 600 or more before she dies.","more":"The cutting damages the twig ends and the leaves beyond them turn brown — foresters call it flagging. A grown tree shrugs it off. When the young hatch they drop to the ground, dig in, and the long wait starts over.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"Adults are 0.9 to 1.3 in (2.4 to 3.3 cm) (about an inch) long. The body is black and the wing veins are orange. Two enormous red eyes stand out from across a room.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"No sting. No bite. No poison, no speed, no hiding. The best read is that the whole defense is arithmetic — arrive in numbers too vast to be finished off.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"food","text":"Adults sip a little sap through a straw-shaped mouth, but they are not really here to eat. Four to six weeks above ground, and one job: find a mate.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"There are seven kinds of periodical cicada — three on the 17-year clock, four on the 13-year clock. Each sings a different song, and females answer their own with a flick of the wings.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Seventeen years in the dark for six weeks in the light — and a whole forest keeps the appointment on the same day."},{"id":"termite","name":"Termite","group":"insects","blurb":"A small, pale, blind insect that eats wood and lives in colonies of millions. Some colonies sit inside earth towers that rise higher than a house.","size":"A worker is tiny. The queen of the same colony can reach 4 in (11 cm), longer than your index finger.","homes":["grassland","rainforest","backyard"],"stats":{"length":0.0},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"A worker termite is tiny. The queen of the same colony can reach 4 in (11 cm), longer than your index finger — and she cannot walk.","more":"Her abdomen swells into a soft white sausage packed with eggs, far too heavy for her legs to lift. Workers bring food to her, groom her, and haul the eggs away as fast as she makes them. She never leaves the royal chamber again.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A big queen can lay more than 20,000 eggs in a single day. That works out at roughly one egg every four seconds, all day and all night, for years on end.","more":"She can head the same colony for decades. Termite queens are among the longest-lived insects known. Every worker in the mound came out of her. So did every soldier, and every winged one that flies off to start a colony somewhere else.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Termites eat wood. The tough stuff inside it is called cellulose, and very few animals can break it down. Termites cannot manage it on their own either.","more":"A termite's gut is a packed zoo of microbes that break the cellulose down and hand the sugars over. Without them a termite would starve on a full stomach. Partners like this are called symbionts: two living things doing one job between them.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Every time a termite moults it sheds its gut lining and loses its microbes along with it. It has to be topped up by a nestmate — and often that food comes out the back end.","more":"Termites feed each other constantly, mouth to mouth and end to end. The word for it is trophallaxis. A newly hatched termite starts with no microbes at all and gets its starter culture the same way. The colony passes its digestion around like a recipe.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Some African mounds stand more than 16 ft (5 m) high. Stack three of yourself on top of each other and you still would not reach the top — and the builders are blind.","more":"In most species workers and soldiers have no eyes at all. They build by touch, by smell, and by feeling vibration through the walls. Nobody is holding the plan. Each termite simply responds to what the termite before it did, and a tower comes out of it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"The mound is not the nest. The colony lives below it and inside it, and air really does move through the tower standing above — the best read is that it works as a chimney.","more":"As the sun heats one side of the mound and the night cools it down again, air rises and sinks through the tunnels in a slow daily cycle. Stale air and carbon dioxide go out, fresh air comes in. The mound breathes on a 24-hour rhythm.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"copied","text":"Architects in Harare, Zimbabwe built a large office block to work the way a termite mound works. It stays cool through the African day without normal air conditioning.","more":"The Eastgate Center pulls cool night air in at the bottom. The concrete soaks up that cool. Chimneys at the top push the day's hot air out. Cooled that way, the building runs on about a tenth of the energy. A conventional air-conditioned building of the same size needs the other nine tenths.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"Some termites are farmers. They chew wood, pass it through their bodies, and build the result into combs where they grow a fungus called Termitomyces — then eat the fungus.","more":"The fungus finishes the job the termites started, breaking the last of the wood down into food they can actually use. The combs sit deep inside the mound at a steady temperature. Very few animals on earth farm anything at all — leafcutter ants do, some beetles do, and these termites do.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Soldier termites in some species have heads drawn out into a nozzle. They cannot bite at all. Instead they spray a sticky, resinous liquid that gums an attacking ant's legs together.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Termites drum. A disturbed soldier bangs its head against the tunnel wall, and the vibration runs out through the wood and the soil to the rest of the colony.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"In north-eastern Brazil there are around 200 million termite mounds, spread over an area larger than Great Britain. Each is about 8 ft (2.5 m) tall and 30 ft (9 m) across.","more":"They stand roughly 66 ft (20 m) apart, built by one species, Syntermes dirus, an insect a centimeter and a half long. The mounds are not nests — they are spoil heaps from an enormous network of tunnels running underneath. The spacing is so regular it shows up from the air.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Termites are not ants. People call them white ants, but scientists classify them with the cockroaches — a completely separate group from ants, bees and wasps.","kind":"record"}],"wonder":"Blind builders, working by touch, raising a tower that breathes on its own — and not one of them can see what they made."},{"id":"flea","name":"Flea","group":"insects","blurb":"A tiny wingless insect that drinks blood and jumps further for its size than almost anything else alive.","size":"Tiny, and squashed flat from side to side.","homes":["backyard","grassland","forest"],"stats":{"length":0.0},"facts":[{"cat":"record","text":"A flea can jump 7 in (18 cm) straight up — about 60 times its own body length. At your size that would be clearing a 25-story building from a standing start.","more":"Do the sum yourself. Sixty times the height of a nine-year-old comes out at roughly 275 ft (84 m). Sideways it manages 13 in (33 cm), further still for its size. There is no run-up — just a push off something as unhelpful as a carpet fiber.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The jump is not muscle. Muscle is far too slow. A flea squeezes energy into a pad of rubbery protein called resilin, latches it shut, then lets it go all at once.","more":"Resilin is one of the springiest materials known — press it and it gives back almost every scrap of the energy you put in. The flea loads it slowly over a long moment, then releases the catch. What went in over a second comes out in an instant.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"People argued for years about which part of the leg did the pushing. High-speed film settled it: a flea shoves off from the tibia and tarsus — its shin and foot — not from the joint above them.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"A flea is 0.06 to 0.13 in (1.5 to 3.3 mm) long. Three of them nose to tail would sit across your thumbnail. Everything else on this list is done by an animal that size.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"A flea is flattened side to side like a page in a book, so it slips between hairs. It is covered in backward-pointing bristles. It also carries hard combs called ctenidia.","more":"Those combs catch in hair like the teeth of a ratchet, so a scratching paw drags over the flea rather than out with it. Its shell is tough enough that squeezing one between two fingers often does not kill it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Blood, and nothing but blood. A flea's saliva carries an anticoagulant — a chemical that stops blood clotting — so the wound keeps flowing while it drinks.","more":"The itch is not the bite. It is your own body reacting to the flea's spit, which holds more than a dozen substances that can set off an allergy. Some animals barely notice fleas at all; others are driven frantic by a handful of them.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A female lays 20 to 30 eggs a day, about one an hour, and the eggs are smooth and non-sticky. They roll straight off the animal and land wherever it sleeps.","more":"So the bed, the carpet and the back of the sofa become the nursery, not the pet. Over a lifetime one female can produce hundreds of eggs, sometimes thousands. This is why treating the animal on its own almost never clears an infestation.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Flea larvae are blind, legless and pale, and they do not drink blood. They eat the dried blood the adults drop — the black specks in a pet's fur that people call flea dirt.","more":"In feeding trials, larvae given adult flea droppings did better than larvae given dried blood alone. The adults are, in effect, feeding their own young by leaving crumbs behind them everywhere they go.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"A flea can wait inside its cocoon, fully formed and doing nothing at all. It comes out when it feels warmth, vibration through the floor, or the carbon dioxide of something breathing nearby.","more":"That is why an empty house can seem flea-free for weeks and then, an hour after someone moves back in, be full of them. The pupae had not gone anywhere. They were lying there listening for footsteps.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"There are about 2,500 kinds of flea, and most stick to a favorite sort of host. The one you are most likely to meet is the cat flea — which is also the flea usually found on dogs.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"The oriental rat flea carries a bacterium back and forth between rats and people. It is called Yersinia pestis. That bacterium causes plague, one of the most serious diseases in human history.","more":"A blocked flea is a dangerous flea. The bacteria multiply in its gut until food can no longer get through. The starving insect bites again and again. Each bite pushes some of the blockage into the fresh wound.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Flea circuses were real. Showmen fitted fine wire harnesses to live fleas and had them pull miniature carts and chariots using nothing but the flea's own strength.","kind":"record"}],"wonder":"No wings, no size, nothing to look at — and folded into one leg, a spring that works perfectly every single time."},{"id":"water-strider","name":"Water strider","group":"insects","blurb":"A long-legged insect that spends its whole life standing on top of the water without ever sinking.","size":"A tiny body, with legs that spread far wider than the body itself.","homes":["freshwater","ocean","backyard"],"stats":{"speed":2,"length":0.0,"life":1},"facts":[{"cat":"build","text":"It is not swimming. It is standing on the skin of the water, held up by surface tension, the invisible stretch across the top of a pond that behaves like a trampoline.","more":"Water molecules cling to each other harder at the surface than anywhere else, and that pull makes a thin elastic film. A strider is light enough and spread wide enough that its feet dent the film without ever breaking through. Look closely at a sunny pond and you can see the dimples its feet press into the water.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Its legs are furred with hydrofuge hairs. More than a thousand are crammed into a single millimeter. They trap a layer of air, so water never actually touches the leg.","more":"Hydrofuge means water-repelling. Each hair is grooved, and the grooves hold air the way a wool sweater holds warmth. That is why a strider stays bone dry in the middle of a rainstorm, and why a drop of water rolls off its leg instead of soaking in.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"speed","text":"The middle legs are the oars. It rows across the surface at around 1 meter per second (2 mph), which is about a hundred of its own body lengths every single second.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Its legs are ears. When a fly falls in and struggles, the ripples spread outward, reach those legs, and the strider reads direction and distance from the wave alone.","more":"It does not need to see the fly at all. The front legs are the shortest pair and carry small claws set back from the tip, so they can grip prey without ever piercing the water film. Sensor and hook in one limb.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Water striders talk in waves. A male drums the surface about 3 beats a second to court a female, 10 beats to threaten a rival, and 25 beats to tell one to clear off.","more":"Researchers measured these frequencies by filming striders and recording the ripples they made. The pond is basically a shared telephone line. Every message a strider sends spreads out in rings. Every other strider within range feels it arrive through its legs.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It cannot chew. A curved mouthpart called a proboscis punches into a drowning insect, pumps in enzymes that turn the insides to soup, and then drinks it back out.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"One group of water striders, called Halobates, lives out on the open ocean far from any shore. They are the only insects on Earth known to do this.","more":"Insects are everywhere on land and in fresh water. The open sea is almost entirely closed to them. Halobates is the exception. Five of its species live their whole lives skating on the swells, hundreds of miles from the nearest coastline. They stay in the warm band of ocean roughly between 40 degrees north and 40 degrees south.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Ocean striders have no land to lay eggs on, so they glue them to whatever floats: feathers, drifting seeds, cuttlebones, and now plastic trash. A single scrap can come up carpeted in eggs.","more":"Cuttlebones are the chalky internal shells of cuttlefish, and they wash about the open sea in great numbers. Anything solid and drifting will do. Scientists sampling ocean plastic keep hauling up pieces with the surface hidden under layers of strider eggs.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Push one under and it pops straight back up. The air trapped in its leg hairs makes the whole insect buoyant, so the pond simply spits it out again.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"In one pond you can find striders with full wings, stubby wings, and none at all. When the water starts drying up, the winged ones fly off to find somewhere new.","more":"Which forms turn up depends on the water. Ponds that hold steady all year are usually full of short-winged and wingless striders. Puddles and ditches that vanish every summer are dominated by the long-winged kind, which simply leave when the water goes.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"copied","text":"Engineers have built small robots that copy those legs and that rowing stroke. The robots skim across water without ever needing a hull or a float.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"size","text":"Most are grain-of-rice small. One species in Southeast Asia is not. Gigantometra gigas has a body as long as your thumb. Single legs run over 4 in (10 cm), spanning wider than your open palm.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Six hairy feet and a film of water, matched so exactly that the pond holds the strider up without ever letting it in."},{"id":"goliath-birdeater","name":"Goliath birdeater","group":"spiders","blurb":"The heaviest spider on Earth. It is a rainforest tarantula big enough to cover a dinner plate.","size":"Legs spread right across a dinner plate; the furry body alone is longer than your palm.","homes":["rainforest"],"stats":{"weight":0,"length":1.0,"life":20},"facts":[{"cat":"record","text":"Set one on a dinner plate and its feet hang over the rim. Leg span reaches 12 in (30 cm), and weight reaches 6 oz (175 g), about the same as a large apple.","more":"The giant huntsman spider has longer legs, but it is a spindly thing that weighs a fraction of this. By sheer mass, nothing beats a Goliath. Picking one up feels less like holding a spider and more like holding a hamster with too many legs.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It hardly ever eats birds. The real menu is earthworms, beetles, frogs, toads, lizards, and now and then a small snake, all ambushed at night on the forest floor.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"So why 'birdeater'? An old engraving from South America showed a tarantula eating a hummingbird. That picture traveled the world, the name stuck, and the spider has been misnamed ever since.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Corner one and it turns its back on you. The hind legs scrape the abdomen and fling up a cloud of urticating hairs, which are barbed, nearly invisible, and agony in the eyes and nose.","more":"Urticating means stinging or itching. Each hair carries backward-pointing barbs, so once it works into skin it will not slide out. Curious animals that stick a nose down the burrow get a faceful and learn fast. The spider grows a bald patch where it has thrown too many.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"It hisses at you. Rubbing the stiff bristles on its legs and pedipalps against each other makes a dry rasping sound, a trick called stridulation, and it means back away.","more":"Crickets stridulate too, and so do grasshoppers, but they rub wings or legs to call for mates. The Goliath uses it purely as a warning. The hiss usually comes first, the hair-flinging second, and the fangs only if you have ignored both.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The fangs swing straight down like two pickaxes, 0.8 in (2 cm) or more of curved black chitin. The venom will not seriously hurt you; the bite is more like a very deep splinter.","more":"Tarantulas and their relatives strike downward. So they hunt from above, or from a burrow mouth with the prey pinned beneath them. Garden spiders are built the other way. Their fangs pinch together sideways, like a pair of tongs.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Eight eyes, and it still barely sees. It hunts by touch, reading the leaf litter through fine hairs called trichobothria that pick up the faintest stir in the air or ground.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"The female spins a thick silk sac and packs 100 to 200 eggs inside it. She guards it in her burrow, turning it over and over, and will not leave it until they hatch.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Lose a leg and it grows back. Every time the spider moults, climbing out of its old skin like a hand out of a glove, a missing leg returns a little bigger than before.","more":"Molting is the most dangerous thing a tarantula does. It flips onto its back and splits the old shell along the edge. Then it drags itself out, soft and pale. It cannot run or bite for days, until the new skin hardens.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"It builds no web to catch food. It digs a deep burrow in swampy rainforest ground, lines the walls with silk, and lays threads at the doorway that tremble when something walks past.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"It cannot chew either. It crushes prey between its fangs and floods it with digestive juice. Then it drinks the liquid. A dry knot of skin and bone gets tipped out at the burrow door.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Turn one over and you would see four pale patches on its belly. Those are book lungs, stacks of paper-thin tissue like the pages of a book, with air moving between the pages.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"It cannot see the forest floor, and it never once gets lost on it. The whole spider is built to read the dark by touch."},{"id":"jumping-spider","name":"Jumping spider","group":"spiders","blurb":"A small, bright-eyed spider that stalks prey on foot and pounces on it, instead of sitting in a web and waiting.","size":"Most are smaller than your little fingernail; the largest has a body about as long as your thumb.","homes":["backyard","rainforest","mountain"],"stats":{"length":0.0,"life":1},"facts":[{"cat":"record","text":"There are more kinds of jumping spider than any other spider on Earth. Roughly 7,000 species have been named, which is about one in every eight spiders alive.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Look at that face. Two huge headlight eyes at the front and six smaller ones ranged around the sides give it the sharpest eyesight of any animal anywhere near its size.","more":"The two big front eyes are long tubes, with a lens at one end and a retina at the other. They are more like a pair of telephoto cameras than eyes. The side eyes are almost useless for detail but brilliant at catching motion. Anything that moves nearby gets the spider's head swung round instantly.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Those big front eyes cannot move. So the retinas inside them do. Watch closely and you can see the dark spot behind each lens swivel and sweep, scanning the world like a searchlight.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"It judges distance in a way nothing else does. Each big eye stacks several retinas at different depths, and the spider compares the sharp image with the blurred one to measure range.","more":"You use two eyes and compare the slightly different pictures to judge depth. A jumping spider does it with one eye, using image defocus. Green light focuses perfectly on the deepest layer of the retina. On the layer in front it is fuzzy. How fuzzy tells the spider how far off a fly is.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"It has no muscle for straightening its legs. Spiders push two of their leg joints open hydraulically, forcing haemolymph, which is spider blood, into the limb until it snaps out like a ram.","more":"You straighten your arm by pulling on a muscle at the back of it. A spider has no equivalent at those joints and uses fluid pressure instead. It is also why a dead spider curls up: the pressure is gone, and nothing is left to push the legs straight again.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Before every leap it glues a silk thread to the spot it is standing on. Miss the target and it swings on that line, then climbs calmly back up.","more":"The dragline is more than a safety rope. Filming shows the spider paying out or braking the silk in mid-air to keep itself level and to control where it lands. It is a rope, a rudder and a parachute at once, and the spider never forgets to clip in.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"speed","text":"Most can clear several times their own body length from a standing start, with no run-up. They aim the whole jump before taking it. Those front eyes range the target first.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Portia hunts other spiders. It will walk away from its prey and take a long detour up a completely different plant. Then it drops in from above. It follows a route it cannot see the end of.","more":"In lab tests, Portia was shown a target. The only path through meant moving away from the prey first and losing sight of it entirely. It still got there. So it worked out the whole route before setting off. It held that route in mind the whole way.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Almost every spider on Earth eats meat. One jumping spider, Bagheera kiplingi, mostly eats plants. It lives off nubs called Beltian bodies, which are rich in protein and fat. They grow on the leaf tips of acacia trees.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"One jumping spider feeds her young milk. A female Toxeus magnus makes a nutritious fluid in her body, and her spiderlings drink it for the first forty days of their lives.","more":"She drips it onto the nest floor at first, then the spiderlings climb on and feed straight from her underside. If she is removed, they stop growing and die. Nothing else in the spider world does anything quite like this.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Male peacock spiders dance. He raises a fan of brilliantly colored skin over his back. He waves his legs in a set pattern. Then he drums the leaf so hard she feels the beat through her feet.","more":"Some jumping spiders also carry markings that only show under ultraviolet light, which their eyes pick up and ours cannot. So a male showing off to a female may be wearing patterns you would need a UV lamp to see at all.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"They run from specks to giants. The smallest have bodies about a millimeter long, the size of this period, and Hyllus giganteus is twenty-five times that, roughly as long as your thumb.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"A brain smaller than a grain of rice, running eyes that measure, aim and pounce. Small was never the same thing as simple."},{"id":"trapdoor-spider","name":"Trapdoor spider","group":"spiders","blurb":"A stout, burrowing spider that spends its whole life in a silk-lined tunnel. The tunnel sits behind a hinged door it built itself.","size":"About 1.2 in (3 cm) long, with a thick, solid body.","homes":["forest","grassland","desert"],"stats":{"length":0.1,"life":20},"facts":[{"cat":"build","text":"The door is the whole trick. Soil, plant fiber and silk are packed into a thick bevelled plug that fits the burrow mouth exactly and drops shut behind her without a sound.","more":"Beveled means cut at a slant. So the door wedges into the rim like a cork in a bottle, instead of resting on top of it. The hinge is pure silk, laid down in layer after layer along one edge. It folds and folds again without tearing. She widens it as she grows.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"Walk right over one and you would never know. She finishes the outside of the door with the same moss, grit and dead leaf as the ground around it, so the burrow vanishes into the floor.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Many species lay a fan of silk lines or carefully placed twigs radiating out from the door. A beetle steps on one, the line tugs, and she knows exactly which way to strike.","more":"It turns a doorstep into a tripwire alarm covering a whole circle of ground. She sits underneath with her legs against the lid. She can tell a leaf landing from an insect walking. She can tell roughly how big it is, before she has seen anything at all.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"speed","text":"She never chases anything. The door flicks open and legs and fangs shoot out. The prey is hauled underground and the lid drops shut. All of it is over in a fraction of a second.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Beetles, crickets, moths, ants, millipedes: whatever walks over the door goes down through it. Her fangs swing straight down like two pickaxes, so she always strikes upward from beneath.","more":"Spiders built this way are called mygalomorphs. Their fangs stab downward. A garden spider's fangs pinch together sideways instead. Stabbing downward is exactly right for an animal that attacks from below, out of a hole in the ground.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"She is about 1.2 in (3 cm) long: squat body, short thick legs, nothing at all spent on running. The tunnel below her can run deeper than your forearm is long.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Try to lift the door and it fights you. She hooks her fangs and claws into the underside, braces all her legs against the burrow wall, and pulls down astonishingly hard for her size.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"The oldest spider ever recorded lived in a hole in Western Australia. Known only as Number 16, she was checked in her burrow year after year for over forty years, and reached the age of 43.","more":"Number 16 was simply the label on that burrow, at a reserve near Tammin. The arachnologist who began the study went back to it season after season, and an assistant took the work over at the end. Number 16 did not die of old age, either. A wasp got her.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"She hatches, she digs, and she stays. A female trapdoor spider may live her entire life inside a tunnel just a few steps from the burrow she was born in.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"The males are the only ones who ever leave. After his final moult he abandons his burrow for good and walks the ground on damp nights, looking for a door to knock on.","more":"This is when people meet them, plodding across a patio after rain. He has no home to go back to and only a season or so to find a female. She may live another thirty years underground after he is gone.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Her worst enemy walks on six legs. A spider wasp finds the burrow, chews through the silk plug, stings her into paralysis, and lays a single egg on her. The larva eats her alive.","more":"The venom does not kill, it only freezes. That keeps the spider fresh while the grub feeds, and the grub eats in an order that keeps her alive as long as possible. This is exactly how Number 16's forty-three years ended.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"The spiderlings stay inside their mother's burrow for months after hatching. Then one damp night they slip out and start digging doors of their own, sometimes only a hand's width away.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"A door, a hinge, a fan of trip lines, and the patience to sit behind them for forty years."},{"id":"diving-bell-spider","name":"Diving bell spider","group":"spiders","blurb":"The only spider in the world that lives its entire life underwater. It stays inside a bubble of air it builds for itself.","size":"About as long as your thumbnail.","homes":["freshwater"],"stats":{"length":0.0,"life":2},"facts":[{"cat":"build","text":"It builds a house out of air. First it spins a silk dome between underwater plants, then it ferries bubbles down from the surface one at a time until the dome inflates.","more":"The result looks like a small silver balloon anchored in the weed. The spider sits inside it perfectly dry. Everything happens in there: resting, eating, molting, mating, laying eggs, and waiting out the winter.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"That bubble is not just stored air. It works as a physical gill. Oxygen seeps in from the pond water through the surface of the bubble, and waste carbon dioxide seeps out.","more":"The silk is waterproof but gas passes straight through it, so the bell is not a tank slowly running down. It keeps drawing fresh oxygen from the pond. Nitrogen leaks the other way, though, which is why the bell gradually shrinks and has to be topped up.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"To fetch fresh air it swims up and pushes the tip of its abdomen through the surface. A silver bubble catches in the hairs on its belly. Then it rows back down carrying it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"It never actually gets wet. Dense water-repelling hairs cover its abdomen and hold a permanent skin of air, so even outside the bell the spider is wrapped in its own bubble.","more":"That trapped film is where the name comes from. Its scientific name, Argyroneta, means something close to 'silver spinner'. Underwater the air layer catches the light. The whole spider gleams like a drop of mercury.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"It hunts by feel. Silk threads run out from the bell through the weed, and when something blunders into one the spider feels the tug and shoots out to grab it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Dinner is whatever swims past: mosquito larvae, water fleas, small crustaceans. It seizes them, hauls them back inside the bell, and eats sitting in the dry.","more":"Water fleas are the little jerky specks you see hanging in pond water, properly called Daphnia. A spider that worked its way through a pond's mosquito larvae would be doing you a considerable favor.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"She lays her eggs in air, underwater. The top of the bell is walled off into a nursery, and the spiderlings hatch out into a dry room sitting at the bottom of a pond.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"In nearly every spider on Earth the female is the larger one. Here it is flipped. Males reach about 0.59 in (15 mm) and females about 0.47 in (12 mm), so the male is close to a third bigger.","more":"Bigger usually means more eggs, which is why the female is normally the larger spider. Underwater the pattern runs the other way round. Males swim far more than females do, crossing open water to find them, while a female may stay close to her bell for days at a time.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"It spends the winter down there too. It builds a tougher, sealed bell, stocks it with air, and waits out the cold months below the ice.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"More than 50,000 kinds of spider have been named. Exactly one of them spends nearly its whole life under water, and this is it.","kind":"record"}],"wonder":"A pond is no place for a lung. This one was given silk that holds a bubble and hair that will not wet, and made the water home anyway."},{"id":"golden-orb-weaver","name":"Golden orb-weaver","group":"spiders","blurb":"A big tropical spider that spins a wheel-shaped web out of silk the color of real gold.","size":"A big female's body is as long as your thumb; with her legs out she covers your palm.","homes":["rainforest","forest","backyard"],"stats":{"length":0.1},"facts":[{"cat":"build","text":"The gold is real color, not a trick of the light. Her silk carries a yellow pigment called xanthurenic acid, laid down as she spins.","more":"It works two ways, depending on where the web hangs. In a sunlit gap the gold glows and insects fly at it the way they fly at a flower. Against dark forest shade the same threads read as nothing at all.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"Find the tiny spider loitering at the edge of the web. That's the male. Her body can be about 1.2 in (3 cm) long; his is many times smaller.","more":"Hers is the web, the food and the decisions. He spends his life on the rim, waiting for a safe moment to approach, and he often times it for when she is busy eating something else.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Her web can be wider than you are tall — up to about 5 ft (1.5 m) across — slung from support lines strung meters between two trees.","more":"The guy-lines are the giveaway. Walk into one across a forest path and it doesn't snap and drape over you like an ordinary cobweb. It pushes back, and you feel it stop you.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Weight for weight, her dragline silk is stronger than steel. It also soaks up more energy before snapping than Kevlar, the fiber used in bulletproof vests.","more":"Strength alone isn't the trick. The silk stretches roughly a third of its length again before it breaks, and strong-plus-stretchy is what engineers call toughness. A fly hits the web at speed and the silk absorbs the whole punch instead of pinging apart.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Small birds and bats have been found tangled in golden orb webs. The silk really is that strong — though a bird is a disaster, not a dinner.","more":"Researchers who gathered reports of birds caught in spider webs from around the world found this group of spiders turning up again and again. She isn't hunting them. A thrashing bird wrecks a web that took her hours.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"copied","text":"People have woven cloth from this silk. One golden cape took silk from over a million wild orb-weavers, each gathered, milked and then released.","more":"In Madagascar, teams collected spiders each morning, held each one gently while a few meters of silk were drawn from her, then let her go. The work took years. The finished textile is gold with no dye in it anywhere, and it now sits in a museum case.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"copied","text":"Before machines could match it, spider silk was strung inside telescopes and gun sights as crosshairs. It was fine and dead straight. It was also tough enough to survive the recoil.","more":"Nothing manufactured came close. A drawn wire that thin would sag or snap; silk holds its line, holds tension, and is nearly invisible against whatever you are aiming at. Instrument makers kept spiders on hand and drew fresh line whenever a new reticle was needed.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"senses","text":"She has eight eyes and barely uses them. The web is her real sense organ: a fly, a falling leaf and a courting male each arrive as a different vibration.","more":"Spiders read those vibrations through slit sensilla — narrow stress-detecting cracks in their leg joints. She keeps the web under tension so signals travel cleanly. A male approaching has to tap out a rhythm that is unmistakably not prey.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"She walks across her own sticky web without getting caught. Almost nothing of her actually touches the glue.","more":"Filmed close up, spider feet do three things at once. Dense hairs cut the contact area down to almost nothing. A non-stick coating sits on those hairs. And she lifts each leg in a peeling motion rather than a straight pull. Glue cannot get a grip on any of it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Look at the edge of her web for tiny silver spiders that are not her babies. They're dewdrop spiders — freeloaders that steal food off her table.","more":"They tiptoe along her lines. Some snip out a wrapped parcel of prey and carry it off. Others feed at the same carcass while she is busy at the other end. A large web can carry dozens of them.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"She can bite, but for a human the venom is weak — closer to a bee sting. Her first move is almost always to drop out of the web and hide.","more":"An animal that has invested hours in a web does not want a fight it could lose. Dropping on a dragline costs nothing, and she can climb straight back up the same thread once whatever alarmed her has gone.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"She wraps her eggs in a fluffy silk purse and carries it off the web. She hides it in leaf litter or against bark. A layer of camouflage silk goes over the top.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"A fiber tougher than anything in our factories, spun in the dark by an animal the size of your thumb."},{"id":"emperor-scorpion","name":"Emperor scorpion","group":"spiders","blurb":"One of the largest scorpions in the world. It is a glossy black, heavy-clawed burrower from West Africa.","size":"Up to about 8 in (20 cm) long, and wide enough to cover an adult's palm.","homes":["rainforest","forest","grassland"],"stats":{"weight":0,"length":0.7,"life":7},"facts":[{"cat":"weird","text":"Shine ultraviolet light on one at night and it glows an eerie blue-green. The glow comes from substances built into the clear outer layer of its shell.","more":"A scorpion that has just moulted barely glows at all — the glow strengthens as the new shell hardens. What the glow is actually for is still an open question; the scorpion itself gives no sign of using it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"One of the biggest scorpions there is. It runs about 8 in (20 cm) long. Around 1 oz (30 g), it is heavy enough to feel sitting in your palm.","more":"Big does not mean deadly here. Some of the largest scorpions are among the mildest, and this one is calm enough that it is a common first scorpion for people who keep invertebrates.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"The rough rule with scorpions is: fat claws, mild venom — skinny claws, nasty venom. The emperor has enormous claws, and its sting is roughly a bee sting.","more":"It crushes most of what it catches outright and hardly bothers to sting at all. The scorpions that are genuinely dangerous to people are slim-clawed ones, which have no way to stop prey except chemistry.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Turn one over and you'll find two combs on its belly. It drags them along the ground as it walks, tasting and feeling the dirt.","more":"They're called pectines and they're studded with thousands of tiny sensors. A male reads a female's chemical trail with them; both sexes use them to judge whether the ground is right for digging. Nothing else in the animal kingdom has this organ.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"She doesn't lay eggs. She gives birth to live young — pale, soft and helpless — and they climb straight onto her back and ride there.","more":"A litter can be a dozen or more. For the first week or two they are white and soft-shelled. They ride stacked on the one place nothing can reach them. She carries the whole heap with her wherever she goes.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"Emperor scorpions put up with each other. Family groups share a burrow, and a mother will let her grown young stay on — unusual for a scorpion.","more":"Most scorpions treat every other scorpion as either a rival or a meal. These live in loose colonies, and young ones have been seen feeding on prey their mother caught rather than being driven off.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"It digs. A burrow can run as deep as an adult is tall. It will also happily move into a termite mound. The air there stays damp and the food walks in.","more":"Underground is where the water is. Above ground in West Africa a scorpion dries out. Down in the burrow the humidity stays high and the temperature stays steady. Termites keep blundering past the front door.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Termites make up much of what it eats. An adult usually just grabs and crushes — stinging is a last resort, because venom costs the body real effort to make.","more":"Venom is expensive chemistry, rebuilt slowly once it is spent, so a scorpion that stings everything runs empty. Watch a big adult with a small insect and the claws do all the work while the tail stays folded.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"talk","text":"It can make a rasping sound, scraping a rough patch on its pincer arm against its jaws. Up close it's like a fingernail dragged over sandpaper.","more":"The signal is aimed at animals too big to fight. A scorpion that would rather not be trodden on can rattle out a warning before it ever has to raise its tail.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"There's a pair of eyes on top of its head and more along the front edge, and it still can't see much detail. What those top eyes do is catch light — almost any light.","more":"They are among the most light-sensitive eyes measured in any animal. They are tuned for a starlit night rather than for shapes. The scorpion reads brightness and direction, not pictures. It does the rest of the work by touch.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"Its body runs slow. A well-fed adult can go months between meals without coming to harm — sit still, spend nothing, and let dinner walk past the burrow.","more":"Some scorpions can last close to a year without eating. That low idle is why they survive where food arrives unpredictably. It is not laziness. It is an engine deliberately turned right down.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"copied","text":"Chemists found germ-killing molecules in this scorpion's venom and named them pandinins after it. Venom turns out to be a whole chemistry set, not one single poison.","more":"A single scorpion's venom can hold dozens of different peptides, each doing a different job. Some jam nerves and some kill bacteria. Researchers now comb through them looking for medicines.","kind":"record"}],"wonder":"An animal armed with venom that mostly chooses not to use it, walking about with a heap of white babies on its back."},{"id":"horseshoe-crab","name":"Horseshoe crab","group":"spiders","blurb":"A helmet-shaped sea animal with a spike for a tail and bright blue blood — and despite the name, it is not a crab.","size":"About 2 ft (60 cm) from front to tail tip; the tail spike is nearly half of that.","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"length":2.0,"life":20},"facts":[{"cat":"weird","text":"It isn't a crab. Look at the mouthparts and it lines up with spiders and scorpions instead — a chelicerate, named for the small pincers beside its mouth.","more":"A true crab has antennae and jaws. A horseshoe crab has neither. What it has is a pair of chelicerae, the same feeding pincers a spider uses. That is why zoologists file it with the arachnids, not with anything else in a rock pool.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"size","text":"A big female is a broad shell with a spike behind it. She runs about 2 ft (60 cm) front to tail-tip. Males run smaller.","more":"Almost none of that is her body. Most of what you are lifting is armor — one curved plate over the front, a hinged plate behind it, and the tail. Underneath, the animal itself is surprisingly small and soft.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Its blood is blue. Ours carries oxygen using iron; a horseshoe crab uses copper, in a protein called haemocyanin, and copper turns blue when it grabs oxygen.","more":"The color only shows once the blood meets air. Inside the animal, before it picks up its oxygen, the blood runs almost colorless — so the famous blue is really the moment of loading, caught on camera.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"copied","text":"That blue blood matters to hospitals. It clots instantly around bacterial poison. That reaction is used to check that vaccines and injectable medicines are clean.","more":"Cells in the blood called amebocytes turn liquid to gel at the faintest trace of bacterial endotoxin. Hundreds of thousands of crabs are collected and bled each year and most are returned to the sea, though not all survive it. Synthetic replacements are now taking over some of the work.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Its two big side eyes are built from about a thousand separate units. Studying them won a scientist a Nobel Prize, because they revealed how any eye sharpens an edge.","more":"Each unit quietens its neighbors when it fires. That is called lateral inhibition, and it is why edges look crisper to you than they physically are. This eye was simply big and orderly enough to wire up and measure — and what it taught applies to yours.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Two big eyes on top are only the start. There are simpler eyes dotted over the shell, and light sensors on the tail spike itself.","more":"A buried animal with a tail sticking out can still tell day from night. Much of that scattered light-sensing seems to be about tides and moonlight rather than about seeing objects at all.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"The tail spike doesn't sting and cannot hurt you. It's a lever. When a wave flips the animal upside down, it uses the tail to arch, push and roll back over.","more":"A stranded horseshoe crab that cannot right itself dies in the sun. If you find one flipped on a beach, lift it by both sides of the shell. Set it down the right way up. Never carry it by the tail, which can snap off.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"On the biggest spring tides, whole beaches fill with them. Females dig into wet sand and lay thousands of pale green eggs the size of pinheads, batch after batch.","more":"Delaware Bay is the great gathering place. The tides that count are the highest ones, around the new and full moon. Those bury the eggs high up the beach. Ordinary waves will not dig them back out.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"Those eggs feed a bird. Red knots arrive exhausted from South America, land on the same beaches in the same few weeks, and eat almost nothing else.","more":"A red knot can put on close to its own body weight again on horseshoe crab eggs before flying north to the Arctic. The timing has to line up exactly, which is why limits on collecting the crabs are partly about protecting the birds.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Under the shell are five pairs of gills stacked like the pages of a book. The animal flaps them to breathe, and young ones flap them to swim — upside down.","more":"Book gills is the actual name. The pages are thin flaps stacked in the hundreds. That packs a huge surface into a small space. A juvenile paddles along upside down just under the surface, which is a genuinely odd thing to watch.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"It grows by climbing out of itself. The old shell splits open at the front rim, the animal walks out, and what's left behind is a perfect hollow copy — legs and all.","more":"People find these on the tideline and assume they have found a dead crab. Pick one up: it weighs almost nothing, and there is a clean split seam around the front edge. A horseshoe crab does this about seventeen times before it is full-grown.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It has no jaws. It grinds its food between the bristly bases of its own legs as it walks, then pushes the mash into a mouth sitting in the middle of them.","more":"So a horseshoe crab has to keep moving in order to chew. Stop walking and the mill stops. Those leg bases are called gnathobases, and they are lined with stiff spines that do the work teeth would.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Blue blood that keeps hospital medicine safe, and eyes that taught us how seeing works — from an animal most people step over on a beach."},{"id":"giant-pacific-octopus","name":"Giant Pacific octopus","group":"sea","blurb":"The largest octopus in the world. It is a soft, boneless, color-shifting hunter of the cold North Pacific.","size":"Arms spread, about as wide across as a car is long.","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"weight":33,"length":14.1,"life":4},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"A big adult spans about 14 ft (4.3 m) from arm tip to arm tip. It weighs around 33 lb (15 kg) — with not a single bone in its body.","more":"There is no skeleton anywhere in it. That whole mass is muscle and skin, which is why an octopus this size can flow into a shape you would swear it could not fit. Exceptional ones grow several times heavier.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Its only truly hard part is a beak, like a parrot's. Anything the beak fits through, the rest of it can follow — a whole heavy adult through a gap not much wider than two fingers.","more":"Aquarium keepers bolt lids down and screen off pipes, because an octopus will patiently find the one gap in an enclosure and pour itself through. The beak sits right where the eight arms meet, and it sets the only limit.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"About 280 suckers run down each arm — roughly 2,000 in all. Every one of them grips and tastes at the same time, so this animal can taste whatever it touches.","more":"The suckers are packed with chemical sensors. Reaching blind into a dark crevice tells it not just the shape of what is in there but the flavor. One big sucker alone can hold up 35 lb (16 kg).","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"It has three hearts. Two push blood through the gills and one drives it round the body — and that third heart stops beating every time the octopus swims.","more":"Which is exactly why it would rather walk. Jetting through open water tires an octopus out because the heart supplying its body quits while it does. Given any choice, a giant Pacific octopus crawls along the bottom on its arms.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"About two-thirds of its nerve cells are not in its head. They are in its arms, and each arm can feel, taste and work out a small problem largely by itself.","more":"Around half a billion nerve cells in total, most of them strung down the eight arms. The central brain seems to hand down a general instruction — go into that hole — and the arm sorts out the details on its own.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"It repaints itself in under a second, squeezing thousands of tiny pigment sacs open and shut. And as far as anyone can measure, it is colorblind.","more":"Octopus eyes carry just one light-sensitive pigment. That normally means seeing the world in a single color. Yet the skin matches red rock and brown kelp closely. It changes texture too, raising bumps called papillae to fake weed, gravel or bare stone.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It hunts crabs, clams and fish. When a clam refuses to open, the octopus drills a neat round hole through the shell and squirts in saliva that loosens the muscle holding it shut.","more":"The drill is a rasping tongue called a radula. Those little bored holes are a giveaway: divers looking for an octopus den hunt for the trash heap of empty shells piled outside the front door.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"She lays tens of thousands of tiny eggs. She hangs them in long plaited strands from the roof of her den.","more":"Then she stops eating. For around six months she blows fresh water over them and cleans them one by one, and never leaves. They hatch, and she dies. It is the only brood she will ever raise.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"A hatchling weighs about 0 oz (0.03 g) — you could lose it in a teaspoon. To reach adult size it has to grow roughly half a million times heavier.","more":"It drifts off as plankton first, tiny and see-through, riding currents with no control at all. Only a handful out of tens of thousands ever settle to the bottom and start becoming the animal you would recognize.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"In one study, giant Pacific octopuses learned to tell two people apart. One fed them. The other prodded them with a bristly stick. The octopuses treated each of them differently.","more":"Same uniform, same time of day, so the only difference was the person. The octopuses aimed water jets at the one who had bothered them. They moved toward the one who brought food. They had it worked out within a couple of weeks.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Its ink is more than a smokescreen. It carries chemicals that blunt a hunter's sense of smell, so the octopus doesn't just vanish from view — it becomes hard to track at all.","more":"And if something gets a grip anyway, the octopus can shed the arm. It grows back, suckers and all. Losing a limb is a survivable inconvenience rather than a permanent injury.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"It lives in cold water, from the shallows down past 6,600 ft (2,000 m). It does best between about 8 and 12°C (46-54°F). That is the chilly rim of the North Pacific.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Three hearts, blue blood, and a mind spread out along eight arms — built on a plan nothing like ours, and still curious about us."},{"id":"mimic-octopus","name":"Mimic octopus","group":"sea","blurb":"A small octopus that lives out on bare open sand. It survives there by pretending to be other animals.","size":"Stretched out, about as long as your arm — but the arms themselves are no thicker than a pencil.","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"length":2.0},"facts":[{"cat":"home","text":"It lives where nothing can hide: flat open sand and silt at shallow river mouths, mostly shallower than 50 ft (15 m). No reef, no crevice, no weed.","more":"Most octopuses answer danger by backing into a hole. Out here there are no holes. This one meets danger in the open, in full view, and deals with it by being mistaken for something else.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"Watch it cross open sand and it doesn't look like an octopus. It flattens, sweeps all its arms back into a leaf shape, and glides along the bottom like a flatfish.","more":"It copies the movement as well as the outline — that low, wobbling skim a few centimeters above the sand, held all the way across. Reef hunters leave soles alone: bony, awkward to swallow, not worth the chase.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"Threatened, it hangs in the water and fans all eight arms out around itself. The stripes come up bold brown and white. The shape is a lionfish, an animal bristling with venomous spines.","more":"The pose does the work. Arms held out stiff and evenly spaced turn a soft round body into a spiky wheel, and fish in these waters already know to leave that silhouette alone.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"Attacked by a small damselfish, it buries six arms and waves the other two in opposite directions, banded black and white. From a fish's eye level, that is a sea snake.","more":"Sea snakes eat damselfish. The octopus appears to bring this one out mainly when damselfish are the problem. It is the right lie told to the right audience. And it comes from an animal with no bones to build a snake out of.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"Laid out flat it is about as long as your arm, but there is almost nothing to it. The arms are pencil-thin and the body is smaller than your fist.","more":"That thinness is what makes the impersonations work. Thick, muscular arms could never pass for a sea snake or fold flat into a fish shape. Everything this animal does depends on being long, thin and boneless.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It hunts by touch, pushing a long arm down burrows in the sand to flush out small fish, crabs and worms that thought they were safe.","more":"The arm goes in on its own and works blind, feeling and tasting the inside of a hole the octopus cannot see into. Whatever bolts out the far end meets the rest of the octopus waiting.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Most octopuses hunt at night. This one is out in daylight, on open ground, in plain sight. The best read is that it can only afford that because of everything else it can do.","more":"Daylight is when the impersonations pay. Color and pattern are useless in the dark. An octopus that can look like something dangerous can afford those hours. Everything else with eight arms stays hidden then.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"build","text":"Its skin does the whole trick. Thousands of tiny pigment sacs open and shut under the surface, so a pattern can switch from sandy beige to hard black-and-white bands in a second.","more":"Nothing is painted on and nothing is fixed. The same square of skin that was blank sand a moment ago becomes a warning stripe. Then it goes blank again. Muscle controls it directly, which is why it happens so fast.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"When a display does not work, it drops into a burrow in the sand and is simply gone. One moment there is an animal the length of your arm. The next there is a small hole.","more":"The whole body funnels into an opening you would not think it could use. There is no skull, no shell and no bones anywhere. Nothing can catch on the way in.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Under the act it is an ordinary octopus. Eight arms, a parrot-like beak, blue blood. It also has ink it can fire if all the impersonations fail.","more":"Every octopus can ink. What is unusual here is how much this one gets done with shape and pattern before it ever needs to. Ink buys a second or two of confusion; a costume can work for as long as the other animal keeps believing it.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"No bones, no shell, no armor — staying alive on an empty plain by being the shape of something else."},{"id":"colossal-squid","name":"Colossal squid","group":"sea","blurb":"The heaviest animal on Earth without a backbone. It is a deep-water squid from the seas around Antarctica. It is built like a barrel and armed with hooks.","size":"About 13.8 ft (4.2 m) long and up to about 1,091 lb (495 kg).","homes":["ocean","polar"],"stats":{"weight":1091,"length":13.8},"wonder":"The finest eye in all the world, made for a place where almost nothing is there to see it.","facts":[{"cat":"record","text":"No animal without a backbone weighs more. One hauled up off Antarctica tipped the scales at 1,091 lb (495 kg).","more":"A New Zealand fishing crew brought it up from the Ross Sea, froze it whole, and shipped it to a museum. Scientists later thawed it in a bath of salt water so they could measure it properly. Its body alone was 8 ft (2.5 m) long.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Its eye is the largest known on any animal — about 10.5 in (27 cm) across when measured, and probably wider still in life. A dinner plate, with a lens like a grapefruit.","more":"Below 2,000 ft (600 m) — deeper than two Eiffel Towers stacked — the only light is light that living things make. A huge eye gathers whatever faint glow reaches it. Sperm whales hunt in that same black water, and they are hunting for this.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Look at the arm tips. Every one carries hooks that swivel round in their sockets — no other squid in its whole family has them.","more":"The two long tentacles end in clubs fitted with three-pointed hooks as well. Between the hooks and the suckers, the squid gets a grip that a thrashing fish cannot twist its way out of.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"The only hard part of a colossal squid is its beak. Everything else is muscle and water — and that beak is the biggest of any squid known.","more":"It works like a parrot's: two curved plates that shear instead of crush. Beaks do not break down quickly, so they collect in the stomachs of sperm whales. Counting them is one of the main ways we know these squid are down there at all.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"That record-breaking squid was caught mid-meal. It was still gripping a hooked Patagonian toothfish when the fishing line reached the surface.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"Its flesh is soaked in ammonium, the sharp-smelling stuff in cleaning spray. That fluid is lighter than seawater, so the squid simply hangs in the dark without swimming.","more":"Floating for free is a clever way to live where food is scarce — no fins beating, no energy burned. The cost is flavor. Colossal squid meat is bitter and reeks of ammonia, which is why it never turns up on anybody's plate.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"It circles Antarctica in water barely above freezing. The young drift in the top 1,600 ft (500 m); the adults sink far below that, into permanent dark.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A deep-sea camera 1,969 ft (600 m) down has filmed a living colossal squid. It was the first time ever. The animal was a young one, see-through, about the length of your forearm.","more":"The juvenile was roughly 12 in (30 cm) long and almost completely transparent. Its arms trailed like glass threads. Adults are not transparent at all. Until that footage, every colossal squid anyone had ever examined was already dead.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"It is not the longest squid — the giant squid wins on tentacles. But the colossal squid is far heavier: thick and barrel-bodied where the giant squid is stretched thin.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Sperm whales dive after them. We know because whale stomachs are full of squid beaks, and in one Antarctic study one beak in every seven belonged to a colossal squid.","more":"A sperm whale can dive past 3,300 ft (1,000 m) and stay down for the better part of an hour. There is no light in that water at all. It hunts by echolocation. It finds its meal by listening to the echo of its own clicks.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Nobody has ever seen a fully grown colossal squid alive. Everything we know about the adults comes from bodies in fishing nets, or beaks inside whales.","kind":"record"}]},{"id":"vampire-squid","name":"Vampire squid","group":"sea","blurb":"A small, soft, red deep-sea animal that is neither a squid nor an octopus, and wears its eight arms like a hooded cloak.","size":"About as long as your forearm, hand included.","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"length":1.0},"wonder":"Built for a layer of ocean almost nothing else can live in — and given a light of its own to carry there.","facts":[{"cat":"weird","text":"Its scientific name means \"vampire squid from hell.\" It drinks no blood, chases nothing, and eats mostly falling scraps. The name is about the cloak.","more":"The webbing between its eight arms pulls up over its whole body like a cape, and the skin is deep red to black. Under a ship's floodlight it looks sinister. Down where it actually lives, red light never reaches, so a red animal simply looks black — which is to say, invisible.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"food","text":"It fishes with a thread. One long sticky filament drifts out into the dark, collects whatever is sinking past, and gets reeled slowly back in.","more":"What it collects is marine snow. That is crumbs of dead plankton, shed skin and waste, drifting down from the sunlit water far above. Tiny finger-like cirri wipe the filament clean. They mix the catch with mucus and roll it into a ball small enough to swallow.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Eight arms, but it is no octopus. Two thin filaments, but they are not squid tentacles. It belongs to a group all of its own, and it is the only kind in it.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"home","text":"It lives in a layer of ocean with almost no oxygen in it — as little as three percent of what surface water holds. Almost nothing else can stay there.","more":"That band sits roughly 1,969 to 2,953 ft (600 to 900 m) (2,000 to 3,000 ft) down — call it a mile of black water overhead. Most hunters cannot follow it in. The vampire squid drifts through on a body that burns less fuel than any other deep-sea cephalopod ever measured.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"Threatened, it turns itself inside out. The cloak flips up over its head, and what was soft webbing becomes a ball of spines pointing outward in every direction.","more":"The spines are not really sharp — they are the soft cirri it feeds with, and they bend. But the shape is convincing. Remote submarine cameras have caught the animal holding this \"pineapple\" pose under a bright light, then flipping back the moment the light swings away.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"It has no ink at all. Instead it fires a cloud of sticky mucus packed with glowing blue orbs, and by the time that cloud fades — nearly ten minutes — it is long gone.","more":"Ink works in sunlit water, where a black cloud hides you against the blue. At 2,297 ft (700 m) there is nothing for a black cloud to hide against, because everything is already black. A cloud of light is the version that works down there.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"For its size, its eyes are the largest of any animal. Its soft body is only 6 in (15 cm) long, and its eyes are 1 in (2.5 cm) across — marbles, set in your palm.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Nearly every squid and octopus breeds once and then dies. Not this one. Nobody has followed a female through her whole life, but the bodies say she can spawn, rest, and spawn again — twenty times or more.","more":"Researchers found this by studying preserved females. Some carried the spent remains of an old batch of eggs. A fresh batch was getting ready alongside it. That shows a resting phase in between. In the slow, cold, low-oxygen deep, there is time to do it more than once.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"travel","text":"As a youngster it jets about. Grown up, it flaps two ear-like fins instead and cruises — far cheaper travel when meals are only crumbs.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Its skin is dotted with light organs it can switch on and off, and the tips of all eight arms can glow. A flash may last a blink or several minutes.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Its body is a jelly loaded with ammonium, weighing almost exactly what the seawater around it weighs. So it does not sink and does not float. It just hangs.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"Full grown, it is about 12 in (30 cm) from arm tips to tail — and the soft body is only half of that. Smaller than the name makes you expect.","kind":"found"}]},{"id":"cuttlefish","name":"Common cuttlefish","group":"sea","blurb":"A shallow-water relative of the squid and octopus. It can redraw the pattern on its own skin in less than a second.","size":"About as long as your forearm — and the biggest have a body half a meter (19 in) long all by itself.","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"length":1.6,"weight":9,"life":2},"wonder":"Skin that thinks — two hundred color sacs in a pinhead of it, every one wired straight to the brain and ready in a heartbeat.","facts":[{"cat":"build","text":"In one square millimeter of skin — a pinhead — there can be 200 color sacs. Each is a bag of pigment ringed with tiny muscles, wired straight to the brain.","more":"Pull the muscles and the sac stretches wide, showing its color. Let go and it shrinks to an invisible speck. Because nerves run to each one directly, with nothing in between, a whole pattern can sweep across the body in well under a second.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Here is the strange part. A cuttlefish is colorblind. It has one single kind of light detector and sees the world in shades of gray — and still matches the colors of the seafloor.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Its pupil is a wavy W. And it focuses like a camera, sliding the whole lens forwards and backwards, instead of squashing the lens the way your eye does.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Eight arms are for holding. The other two are hidden weapons: they fire out of pockets under the eyes in about 30 thousandths of a second, several times faster than you can blink.","more":"There is no bone anywhere in a tentacle. Muscle alone stiffens it. Squeezing one set of muscles makes the tentacle longer instead of fatter. It shoots out most of its own length again. The shrimp has not registered anything at all.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"It can see the twist in light — polarisation — that your eyes cannot. Silvery fish scales and see-through shrimp show up in patterns completely invisible to you.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"Watch one stalk a crab. Dark bands roll down its body, over and over, like cloud shadow crossing a field. Scientists call the display the passing cloud.","more":"It shows up mostly while the cuttlefish is closing in on crabs and shrimp. There is still real argument about what it does. One thing is certain. The crab is watching a moving pattern on the front of the animal when the tentacles arrive.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Inside its back sits the cuttlebone: a stack of thin chambers holding gas and liquid, layered like the pages of a book. Shift the balance and the cuttlefish rises or sinks without moving a fin.","more":"A submarine does the same trick with ballast tanks. Here it is packed into a piece of shell lighter than a slice of bread. The chambers cannot take deep pressure. A cuttlebone crushes somewhere between 656 and 1,969 ft (200 and 600 m) (650 to 2,000 ft) down. That keeps cuttlefish in shallow water.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Three hearts. Two push blood through the gills, one pushes it round the body. And the blood is not red but green-blue, because it carries oxygen on copper instead of iron.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Two males meeting go striped. Bold black-and-white zebra bars flare across both bodies. Usually one of them backs off before anybody actually touches anybody.","more":"Smaller males have been seen doing something sneakier. They drop into the soft mottled pattern of a female and tuck their arms in close. Then they drift straight past a much larger guarding male without ever being challenged.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A cuttlefish starts learning before it hatches. Embryos that watched crabs through the clear wall of the egg chose to hunt crabs once they came out.","more":"The eggs are laid in dark bunches that look like grapes. They turn clear as the embryo grows inside. The eyes work well before hatching. In experiments, hatchlings shown crabs later picked crabs over shrimp. Shrimp is the usual favorite. That preference was set before the animal had eaten anything.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"Resting cuttlefish twitch. Their skin flickers through colors, their eyes roll, their arms jerk — a state that looks a great deal like the sleep in which people dream.","more":"It arrives in short bouts of a minute or two in an otherwise still animal, over and over. Whether a cuttlefish dreams, nobody can say. What the cameras record is a sleeping creature whose skin will not hold still.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"copied","text":"Its ink has a name you already use. Sepia — that warm red-brown in old photographs — was made from cuttlefish ink for hundreds of years.","more":"People borrowed the cuttlebone too. Caged birds gnaw it for calcium. Jewelers press a shape into a split cuttlebone and pour molten silver straight in. Those fine chambers hold the detail and survive the heat.","kind":"record"}]},{"id":"nautilus","name":"Chambered nautilus","group":"sea","blurb":"A cephalopod that lives inside a coiled shell. About ninety small tentacles and two pinhole eyes peer out of it.","size":"Shell about as wide across as a side plate; the biggest reach dinner-plate size.","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"length":0.7,"life":20},"wonder":"Thirty sealed rooms, grown one at a time, each exactly the right size — built by an animal that cannot see clearly enough to admire what it made.","facts":[{"cat":"weird","text":"Every other cephalopod alive — octopus, squid, cuttlefish — keeps its shell on the inside, or has none at all. The nautilus is the only one that lives inside one.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The shell is a spiral of sealed chambers, around thirty in a grown animal. The nautilus lives only in the outermost room. The rest are empty and full of gas.","more":"A living tube called the siphuncle threads through every wall. It pumps salt out of a chamber and the water follows the salt out, leaving gas behind. Pump more out and the nautilus rises; let water back in and it sinks. A ballast tank, made of shell.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"copied","text":"The world's first nuclear-powered submarine was named Nautilus, and the name fits. Machine and animal both change depth by moving water in and out of sealed chambers.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Its eye has no lens whatsoever. It is a pinhole, open to the sea, filling and draining with seawater — a working eye with a hole where the lens should be.","more":"Light comes through the tiny opening and lands on the retina, exactly the way a pinhole camera works. The picture is dim and blurry. A nautilus can make out shapes and shadows, but it does not read a reef the way an octopus reads one.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Smell does most of the work instead. A nautilus tracks a dead crab through pitch-dark water by scent alone, and baited traps pull them in from a long way off.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Count the tentacles: about ninety of them. Not one has a sucker. They are grooved and coated in sticky mucus, and they hold prey firmly without a single sucker between them.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Pull back, and a thick leathery hood folds down over the shell opening like a door filling a doorway. Then there is simply nothing left to bite.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It has been described as eating anything that smells. Dead fish, crab scraps, shed shells — the nautilus is mostly a tidier-up of the reef slope.","more":"It even eats the old moulted shells that crabs and shrimp leave behind. It moves slowly, eats rarely, and can go a long stretch between meals — a completely different life from a squid, which hunts almost constantly.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"Every night it climbs. By day it hangs deep. The deepest one ever tracked was at 2,306 ft (703 m), two Eiffel Towers down. After dark it rises up the reef slope to feed.","more":"There is a floor it cannot cross. Go deep enough and the water pressure would crush the chambers and the shell would implode. The animal that carries its own ballast tank also carries its own depth limit.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A single egg takes nine to fifteen months to hatch — a very long wait for a sea creature.","more":"There is no swimming baby stage at all. Most sea animals hatch as a speck of drifting larva. A nautilus stays shut inside the egg until it is a tiny finished nautilus — shell, chambers, tentacles and all — and then crawls out ready to go.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Most cephalopods live a year or two and die. Nobody has followed a nautilus from hatching to death. The shell grows very slowly, and that is the clue. The best read is that a nautilus can pass twenty years. It does not breed until it is well into that long life.","more":"That slow schedule is why collecting nautiluses for their shells is risky. A population that takes so long to replace itself cannot bounce back quickly. International trade in nautilus shells is now restricted by treaty.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Four gills, arranged in two pairs. Every other living cephalopod makes do with two. The nautilus is the only one that breathes through four.","kind":"found"}]},{"id":"box-jellyfish","name":"Box jellyfish","group":"sea","blurb":"A cube-shaped jellyfish with tentacles trailing from each corner. It swims after its prey instead of drifting. Its sting is one of the fiercest in the ocean.","size":"The largest bells reach basketball size; most are smaller. Tentacles trail up to 9.8 ft (3 m). A big one weighs about 4 lb (1.8 kg).","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"speed":3,"weight":4,"length":9.8},"wonder":"Twenty-four eyes and not one brain behind them — and still it steers, hunts, and finds its way home.","facts":[{"cat":"senses","text":"Twenty-four eyes. They sit in four clusters of six, one cluster on each flat side of the box, and two eyes in every cluster have a proper lens.","more":"That is more kinds of eye than you own. Most are simple pits that only register light and dark. The two lensed ones build a real image — a blurry one, but an image — in an animal with nothing you could call a face.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Twenty-four eyes, and no brain at all. What it has instead is a ring of nerve running right round the rim of the bell, coordinating every pulse and every turn.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"One box jellyfish in the Caribbean keeps an eye aimed permanently straight up. It watches the mangrove trees through the surface and steers by them, back to the lagoon edge where the food is.","more":"Researchers tested this by floating the jellyfish in a clear tank among the mangroves and then moving the tank around. The animals swam toward the tree canopy every time. Take the canopy out of view and they swam in no particular direction at all.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"speed","text":"This one hunts. Most jellyfish go wherever the water goes. A box jellyfish beats its bell instead. It reaches about 1.5 meters a second (5.4 km/h, 3.4 mph), a brisk walking pace. It turns a half-circle in a few pulses.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Every tentacle is carpeted in stinging cells, so tiny the tentacle still looks smooth. Each is a coiled harpoon held under pressure, and it fires in less than a thousandth of a second.","more":"They do not go off just because something brushed past. It takes touch plus a chemical signal — the kind of proteins found on fish skin, and on yours. Which is exactly why a smooth plastic bag, or a thin nylon stinger suit, slides by untouched.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Vinegar is the standard first aid on Australian beaches. Poured over a fresh sting, it shuts down for good every stinging cell that has not fired yet.","more":"It does nothing about venom already in the skin. That needs a hospital, and there is an antivenom. Stopping the unfired cells still matters enormously. A tentacle stuck to the skin keeps firing for as long as it stays there.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"It is among the most venomous animals known. At least 64 deaths have been recorded in Australia in all the time anyone has kept count — serious, but genuinely rare.","more":"Roughly three meters (10 ft) of tentacle stuck to skin can be life-threatening. One study followed 225 stings. Only about one in twelve needed a hospital bed. Most stings hurt badly and then heal.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"Almost perfectly clear. In shallow tropical water a box jellyfish is very nearly invisible. Lifeguards net off the swimming areas instead of trying to spot them.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Prawns and small fish, mostly. The tentacles stay short and coiled while it cruises, then stretch out to three meters (10 ft) — basketball-hoop height — once it starts hunting.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"It begins life in a river mouth. Tiny polyps sit in mangrove creeks and brackish water, and the swimming jellyfish only appear in the warm wet season.","more":"Which is why northern Australia has a stinger season rather than a stinger problem all year round. The swimming adults live a matter of months and then are gone; the polyps back in the creeks simply carry on.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Sea turtles eat them. A green turtle's skin and throat are thick enough that the stingers cannot get through, so it swallows box jellyfish whole, tentacles and all.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"Not every box jellyfish is big. The Irukandji is the size of your thumbnail, small enough to lose in a glass of water, and it still puts people in hospital.","more":"Its sting is barely felt at first. The trouble comes about half an hour later — a wave of pain, sweating and sickness that doctors call Irukandji syndrome. Two deaths in Australia have been linked to it.","kind":"found"}]},{"id":"lions-mane-jellyfish","name":"Lion's mane jellyfish","group":"sea","blurb":"The largest jellyfish in the ocean. Its bell can reach 6.6 ft (2 m) across. A shaggy curtain of stinging tentacles hangs beneath it. It drifts in cold water.","size":"The biggest bells reach about 6.6 ft (2 m) across; most are far smaller.","homes":["ocean","polar"],"stats":{"length":6.6,"life":1},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"The biggest ones measured had a bell over 7 ft (2 m) across. That is wider than a door is tall. The tentacles trailed more than 100 ft (30 m) behind.","more":"Most lion's manes you would actually meet are far smaller: a bell about 20 in (50 cm) across, roughly a bicycle wheel. The giants grow in the coldest water. Even in the record-holders nearly all of that length is tentacle — the body itself is a shallow bowl, and everything else just trails.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Try to count the tentacles and you will give up. Around 1,200 of them hang in eight thick bunches, like eight separate mops under one umbrella.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Every tentacle is loaded with stinging cells called nematocysts. Brush one and it fires a coiled, barbed thread into your skin before you can pull away.","more":"A nematocyst is a capsule holding a barbed thread coiled under enormous pressure. Touch the trigger and the thread turns inside out and shoots. It is one of the fastest movements known in any animal, and a single tentacle carries them in their thousands.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"A tentacle torn loose still stings. A scrap of one washed up in the sand can fire into your foot long after the jellyfish itself is gone.","more":"This is why beach warnings mention broken-up jellyfish, not just whole ones. The stinging cells work on contact, not on command — no brain is involved anywhere in the process. A stranded fragment is still fully loaded.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"No brain. No heart. No bones. No blood. A lion's mane is a nerve net, a stomach, and an enormous amount of seawater.","more":"About 95% of a jellyfish is water. That is why a stranded one shrinks to a thin film on the sand as it dries — there was never much else there. Between its two thin layers of skin sits a jelly called mesoglea, doing the job your bones do.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"This is a cold-water animal. The giants live in the Arctic and the chilly northern Atlantic and Pacific. Farther south, lion's manes stay small.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"Look inside the tentacle curtain and you may find small fish living there. They keep to the gaps, and nothing that hunts them will follow them in — so the best read is that the sting is the reason they stay.","more":"Young whiting and other small fish shelter among lion's mane tentacles. They thread carefully between the stingers. Predators that would happily eat them will not follow them in. It is a dangerous address with an excellent security system.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"food","text":"It fishes by drifting. Anything that blunders into the curtain — small fish, shrimp, even other jellyfish — is stung, hauled up and swallowed.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"It has nothing like your eyes. Eight little sensory clubs sit spaced around the rim of the bell. Each one holds light detectors and a balance stone. They are called rhopalia.","more":"Inside each rhopalium a tiny grain rolls with gravity. It presses against hairs and tells the jellyfish which way is up. Eight of them sit evenly around the bell. The animal senses in every direction at once, with no head to point anywhere.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"It pulses its bell to swim, but mostly it goes where the water goes. A big lion's mane is less a swimmer than a drifting net.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Something does eat it. Leatherback turtles swallow jellyfish whole, stingers and all, while seabirds and ocean sunfish mostly pick off the smaller ones.","more":"A leatherback turtle's throat is lined with backward-pointing spines. Slippery, half-liquid prey can go one direction only. Down. Around eastern Canada in summer, leatherbacks eat lion's manes and almost nothing else.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"It does not start as a jellyfish. It starts as a tiny polyp stuck to the seafloor, which stacks up like a pile of saucers and flicks them off one at a time.","more":"The polyp divides crossways into a stack of discs. The top disc pulses free as a swimming baby jellyfish. The process is called strobilation. One anchored polyp releases many babies, one after another, from the same spot.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"No brain to plan with, no bones to stand on — and the largest jellyfish in the ocean still finds everything it needs in the drift."},{"id":"immortal-jellyfish","name":"Immortal jellyfish","group":"sea","blurb":"A jellyfish smaller than your fingernail. When it is injured or starving, it rewinds itself back into a baby. Then it starts its life over.","size":"Small enough to lose on your fingertip.","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"length":0.0},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"A full-grown adult is about 0.18 in (4.5 mm) across. That is smaller than the nail on your little finger. It is see-through enough that you would miss it in a glass of seawater.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"It is one of the very few animals known to go all the way back. Injure it, starve it, or chill the water, and instead of dying it sinks and turns into its own young stage.","more":"A student collecting jellyfish off the Italian coast noticed it first. The specimens simply refused to die. Kept in a dish, they shrank instead. Plenty of animals regrow lost parts. Almost nothing else rewinds the whole body after already growing up.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"The trick has a name: transdifferentiation. Cells that had already committed to being one thing — muscle, say — switch jobs and become something else entirely.","more":"In almost every animal, a cell that has become muscle stays muscle for life. In this one, cells reassign themselves, and the body takes itself apart and reassembles as an earlier version. Nothing new is added — it is rebuilt entirely out of itself.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"The shrunken blob settles on the seafloor and grows into a polyp, a tiny stalk. That polyp then buds off brand-new jellyfish, identical to the one that started over.","more":"One medusa that resets can become a polyp colony. That colony can bud off many new medusae. All of them carry the same instructions as the original. A single starving jellyfish can end up as a crowd of copies.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"It is see-through except for one thing: a bright red stomach glowing in the middle of the bell like a warning light.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"A newly released one carries eight tentacles, evenly spaced. A grown one can carry eighty or ninety, fringing the whole rim of the bell like eyelashes.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It eats plankton, fish eggs and tiny molluscs, and it chases none of it. The tentacles hang in the water and wait for food to arrive.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"It was first described in the Mediterranean and now turns up in harbors around the world. Big ships carry seawater for balance, and tiny drifters ride along inside.","more":"Large ships take on seawater to keep steady and pump it out again at the next port. Whatever was floating in that water goes with it. Turritopsis has since been found on both sides of the Atlantic and in the Pacific, far from where it was first named.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Immortal is a nickname, not a promise. Out in the sea these jellyfish are eaten and struck down by disease constantly. The rewind only helps if nothing eats you first.","more":"Being able to reset is not the same as being safe. The reset needs time — the animal has to sink, settle and shrink, which takes days. Most never get that chance. What can go on and on in a laboratory dish usually does not, out in the open water.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"defence","text":"It has no brain and no speed. It cannot outswim anything that wants it. Its entire answer to serious trouble is to start again.","kind":"worked"}],"wonder":"The smallest animal in this whole section carries the largest surprise: a body that knows how to begin again."},{"id":"sea-star","name":"Sea star","group":"sea","blurb":"A five-armed animal that walks on hundreds of tiny water-powered feet. It eats by pushing its stomach out through its own mouth.","size":"Most would cover your open hand; the biggest spreads wider than a bicycle wheel.","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"speed":0.04,"length":1.0,"life":10},"facts":[{"cat":"weird","text":"It is not a fish. No fins, no scales, no backbone — no bones at all. Sea stars are echinoderms, a word that means 'spiny skin'.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Turn one over. Those hundreds of wriggling stalks are tube feet, and they run on plumbing — a water vascular system that pumps seawater right through the animal.","more":"Seawater enters through a small pale sieve on the top surface called the madreporite and runs down canals into every arm. Squeeze water one way and a tube foot stretches out; squeeze it back and the foot pulls. Hundreds working together haul the whole animal along.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It eats by turning its stomach inside out. The stomach slides out of the mouth, wraps around the meal, digests it outside the body, then folds back in.","more":"The stomach is thin and stretchy and pushes out through the mouth on the underside. Digestive juices go to work right where the food is, and the softened meal is drawn back in. This is how a sea star eats things far bigger than its own mouth.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"To open a mussel it grips with several arms and pulls — steadily, for hours, longer than the mussel can hold. It does not need much of a gap.","more":"The shell only has to part by a fraction of a millimeter. A sea star can slide its stomach through a crack thinner than a sheet of paper. Once it is inside, the mussel's shell is a lunchbox instead of a fortress.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"There is an eye at the tip of every arm. Each one is a cluster of up to 200 tiny light detectors. They cannot read print. They can make out the dark shape of a reef and steer for it.","more":"Each eyespot sits right at the arm's end, tucked under the tube feet. It is a patch of 80 to 200 pigmented light-detecting units. The picture it gives is blurry and coarse. It tells dark reef from open water, which is exactly enough to crawl home.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"No head anywhere on it, and no brain. A ring of nerve around the mouth and a cord down each arm run the whole animal, and no one arm is in charge.","more":"It does have a small heart, beating around six times a minute. A fluid circulates too. There is nothing like the red pigment in your blood. Most of the pushing and hauling is done by plain seawater in the tube-foot plumbing.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Lose an arm and it grows back. A few kinds do the reverse as well: one dropped arm, drifting alone, can slowly rebuild an entire sea star around itself.","more":"For most sea stars a piece of the central disc has to come along with the arm. Linckia manages from the arm alone. The lone limb crawls off and sprouts four small new arms. For a while it looks like a comet with a tail. Divers call these comet forms.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"The sunflower star is the giant of the group. It grows up to a meter (3 ft) across. It can have as many as 24 arms and about 15,000 tube feet underneath.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"speed","text":"Sea stars are slow, but they are not still. The quickest, the sunflower star, covers about a meter (3 ft) of seafloor in a minute — plenty, when your food cannot run.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"The crown-of-thorns sea star eats living coral. A single one can strip up to 6 square meters (65 sq ft) of reef in a year, and its spines are loaded with toxin.","more":"The spines do not inject anything. They are wrapped in tissue full of a bitter chemical called saponin, and when a spine punctures skin that tissue tears off inside the wound. It makes for a nasty, long-lasting sting for anyone who kneels on one.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"Flip one upside down and it does not panic. It reaches out with the tube feet on one arm, takes a grip, and slowly rolls itself back the right way up.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Baby sea stars look nothing like sea stars. They are transparent drifting specks with waving bands of hair, floating in the plankton until they settle.","more":"The larva feeds and drifts for weeks. It is bilateral, with a left and a right, like you. When it settles on the seafloor it reorganizes completely into the five-part body. The swimming shape is gone for good.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"No brain anywhere in it — and yet fifteen thousand tiny feet all decide to pull the same way."},{"id":"sea-urchin","name":"Sea urchin","group":"sea","blurb":"A living pincushion that walks on sticky tube feet. Underneath is a five-toothed jaw that scrapes at solid rock.","size":"Body about 3.6 in (9 cm) across, ringed with spines as long as your fingers.","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"length":0.3,"life":30},"facts":[{"cat":"build","text":"Turn one over and there is a mouth in the middle with five teeth meeting in a point. The whole jaw has a name of its own: Aristotle's lantern.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"copied","text":"The teeth never go blunt. As the tip wears away it breaks off along a set line, leaving a fresh edge behind — so the jaw sharpens itself by being used.","more":"Each tooth is built from layers of calcite. They are arranged so worn material snaps away cleanly instead of rounding off. Materials scientists have studied that arrangement closely. They want blades and drill bits that stay sharp as they wear down.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"In some places urchins chew hollows into soft rock and sit down in them. They grip with their tube feet while the surf tries to tear them out.","more":"They grind at the rock with the same jaw they eat with, and the pit deepens until the animal is sunk into the shoreline. On some coasts the rock is pocked with thousands of these hollows, each one occupied.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Every spine sits in a ball-and-socket joint and swivels. Touch one side of an urchin and the whole pincushion turns its points toward your finger.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Between the spines sit pedicellariae — tiny jaws on stalks that snap shut on anything landing on the urchin. In the flower urchin, they carry venom.","more":"A pedicellaria is a stalk topped with three jaws, and thousands are scattered among the spines. Most simply grip and pick off settling larvae and debris. The flower urchin's are venomous, which makes a pretty pastel animal one of the worst things on a reef to pick up.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"A sea urchin has no eyes anywhere, yet it reacts to a shadow passing over. Light-sensing cells are spread across its body, so the whole animal works something like one big eye.","more":"Light-sensitive proteins have been found in the tube feet and skin. The spines shade some of that skin and leave the rest bare. A passing shadow falls unevenly across the animal. The unevenness itself tells the urchin which direction the shadow came from.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"travel","text":"The spines are not the feet. Long, sticky tube feet stretch out past the spines, take hold of the rock, and haul the urchin slowly along.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Urchins graze kelp. Where sea otters hunt urchins, the kelp forest stands tall; where the otters are gone, the urchins mow it down to bare rock.","more":"Divers call the bare version an urchin barren, and it is a startling sight — pink rock and spines where a forest full of fish used to be. Sea otters crack urchins open and eat them, and their hunting is what keeps the kelp standing.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Red sea urchins are among the longest-lived animals in the sea. Nobody has watched one for a century. Tagging and the chemical traces locked in the shell do that work instead. They put the oldest at well past a hundred, still grazing the same rock.","more":"Their ages came from tagging and re-measuring wild urchins. Chemical traces locked into the shells filled in the rest. The oldest were well past a century. They were still feeding, still growing, still breeding.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"Hand an urchin a scrap of seaweed or a bit of broken shell. It holds the scrap overhead with its tube feet. It wears the thing like a hat while it grazes.","more":"It is called covering behavior. Urchins in bright shallow water pile on far more cover than urchins in the shade. Some kinds drop the whole load at night. They pick it up again in the morning.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Under the spines is a shell called a test: chalky plates locked together in a five-part pattern. Find an empty one on a beach and you can trace all five with a finger.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A baby urchin is a see-through speck with long glassy arms, drifting in the plankton. Nothing about it hints at a pincushion until it settles and rebuilds itself.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"A mouth that never goes blunt, in an animal with no hands to sharpen it."},{"id":"sea-cucumber","name":"Sea cucumber","group":"sea","blurb":"A soft, leathery animal shaped like a fat sausage. It vacuums the seafloor clean. It can fire its own insides at anything that bothers it.","size":"Most are forearm-length; the longest stretches taller than a grown-up.","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"length":0.8,"life":10},"facts":[{"cat":"build","text":"It looks nothing like a sea star, but it is built to the same plan: five rows of tube feet running nose to tail. It is a five-armed animal, lying on its side.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Squeeze one hard and it may fire its own guts out through its back end. The attacker gets a mouthful of stomach; the sea cucumber crawls off and grows a new one.","more":"The process is called evisceration. Depending on the species the organs go out the front or the back, and the animal is left nearly hollow. Over the following weeks it regrows what it lost. It is an expensive trick — but cheaper than being eaten.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"It breathes through its bottom. Water is drawn in through the anus and into branching tubes inside the body called respiratory trees, which pull the oxygen out of it.","more":"The respiratory trees are two feathery tubes that branch just inside the anus. They fill much of the body cavity. Water is pumped in and oxygen crosses into the body fluid. Then the water is pushed back out. It happens over and over, through the same opening everything else leaves by.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Some species shoot sticky white threads, called Cuvierian tubules, out of that same opening. The threads swell in seawater and glue a crab's legs shut.","more":"The tubules are branches off the base of the respiratory tree. They are fired out through the animal's back end. On the way they stretch to many times their resting length. Seawater turns them intensely sticky. A crab that blunders into the mess can end up wrapped and unable to walk.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"It can go from firm to nearly liquid on purpose. Its skin is mutable collagenous tissue, which it can loosen at will and pour through a crack you would swear was too small.","more":"Mutable collagenous tissue is skin that changes stiffness under nerve control. A sea cucumber can go rigid and wedge itself immovably into a crevice. It can also slacken until it flows like thick soup. Then it pours into a gap far narrower than its own body.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Some sea cucumbers have a lodger. The pearlfish reverses in through the anus, tail-first, and lives inside the body cavity, coming out at night to feed.","more":"The pearlfish is long and thin and tracks its host down by smell before backing in. Inside, it is safe from nearly everything in the sea. Some kinds slip out to hunt and return by morning; some simply eat their host's insides while they are in there.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It eats sand. Feeding tentacles sweep grains into the mouth, the animal digests the film of dead matter coating them, and clean sand comes out the other end.","more":"A sea cucumber cleans sand by eating it. What it leaves behind is grain stripped of the bacteria and dead scraps that covered it. On a reef, thousands of them turn the sediment over the way earthworms turn a garden bed.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Go past 29,000 ft (8,900 m) down, deeper than Everest is tall. Sea cucumbers make up around 90% of the animal weight living on that mud.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Not all of them crawl. Enypniastes swims above the seafloor, flapping a skirt of skin, and it is bioluminescent — its body glows blue in water where nothing else does.","more":"It has been filmed swimming in the deep sea, lit up and rippling. Its glowing tissue can rub off onto whatever touches it. An attacker swims away marked and shining in the dark. That is an awkward place to be, when everything down there hunts by light.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"Most are the length of your forearm. Synapta maculata is the stretch: over 10 ft (3 m) long, but no thicker than a garden hose.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Around the mouth is a ring of tentacles, ten to thirty of them. They are modified tube feet. They mop food off the seafloor. Each one gets wiped clean in the mouth, one at a time.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"There is no brain in there at all. A ring of nerve around the mouth, with five nerve cords running the length of the body, handles everything it does.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"The animal that cleans the seafloor is the one nobody notices — and the sand it leaves behind is cleaner than the sand it ate."},{"id":"giant-clam","name":"Giant clam","group":"sea","blurb":"A shellfish up to 3.3 ft (1 m) wide that sits on a coral reef and farms its own food inside its own body.","size":"About 3.3 ft (1 m) across and up to 441 lb (200 kg)","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"length":3.3,"weight":441,"life":100},"wonder":"It plants a garden in its own skin, bends the light to reach it, and never has to leave home to eat.","facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"The longest giant clam ever measured was 54 in (137 cm) across. It weighed 510 lb (230 kg).","more":"That is the record for any shelled mollusc alive. Most stop growing somewhere near a meter, which is still wide enough that you could not get your arms around one. They keep growing their whole lives, and can pass a hundred years.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"A giant clam grows its own lunch. Millions of tiny algae live inside its flesh, catching sunlight and handing over sugar — about two-thirds of everything the clam eats.","more":"The algae are called zooxanthellae. The clam farms them the way you would tend a garden. Every morning it spreads its brilliant mantle out into the light. It pulls the mantle back in when a shadow crosses. It still filters plankton from the water for the rest of its meals.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"That electric blue-and-green skin is not paint. Cells called iridocytes bend sunlight and scatter it deep down into the clam's flesh. The best read is that this feeds the algae growing in the shade.","more":"Without those cells only the top layer of algae would ever get light. The iridocytes work like a hall of mirrors, spreading the sun evenly through the tissue so the whole crop can work at once. Different clams scatter the light in different colors, which is why no two look the same.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"babies","text":"One adult can release more than 500 million eggs in a single spawning. That is more eggs, from one animal, than there are people in the United States.","more":"Giant clams are hermaphrodites. Every adult makes both eggs and sperm. None of them fertilize themselves. A whole reef spawns at once, filling the water like smoke. Only a handful of those eggs will ever become a clam.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"A grown giant clam of the biggest kind cannot shut its shell all the way. It is the only giant clam that can't — there is simply too much clam for the box.","more":"Look along the gap and you will see the mantle bulging out, too swollen with light-catching tissue to be tucked away. Smaller kinds close up fine. This one outgrows its own door.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"There is no head anywhere on a giant clam. Instead the mantle is dotted with hundreds of pinhole eyespots, each about half a millimeter wide, watching for shadows.","more":"They cannot see shapes. What they read is a change in light — a shadow sliding across, the way a hand passes over a flashlight. That is enough. The clam starts closing before whatever cast the shadow arrives.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"No shellfish on Earth wears a bigger shell. Giant clams are the largest shelled molluscs alive, and they build every gram of that shell out of plain seawater.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"It only settles once. After about a week drifting as a speck of plankton, a young clam picks a patch of reef, glues itself down, and never moves again.","more":"That one choice is the last big move it ever makes. Land somewhere too dark and the algae starve. Land somewhere too deep and the same. Everything after that is just growing — slowly, in the light, in one spot, for a very long time.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"The old story about giant clams trapping divers by the ankle is not true. A giant clam closes far too slowly, and has to shove a tub of water out of the way as it goes.","more":"Sailors' charts once carried warnings about man-killing clams. Watch one shut and the story falls apart: the closing muscle works at a lazy pace, and the shell has to push all that water aside first. You could pull your foot out and go and get lunch.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"home","text":"Giant clams need clear, shallow, sunlit water, usually no deeper than 66 ft (20 m). Any deeper and the algae inside them cannot catch enough light to pay the rent.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"A young giant clam holds on with byssal threads — tough strands it spins to anchor itself to rock. The biggest kind lets go as it grows and relies on being too heavy to shift.","more":"By then, a storm big enough to move a giant clam is a storm big enough to break the reef underneath it. Weight is the entire defense. There is no running, no hiding and no fighting back — just a couple of hundred kilograms of clam, staying put.","kind":"worked"}]},{"id":"cone-snail","name":"Cone snail","group":"sea","blurb":"A beautiful sea snail that hunts fish with a venom-loaded harpoon it fires out of its own mouth.","size":"Most fit in your palm; the biggest runs about 8.5 in (21.6 cm)","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"length":0.3},"wonder":"One drop of something built to stop a fish turned out to be the thing that lets a person in pain finally sleep.","facts":[{"cat":"food","text":"A cone snail hunts with a harpoon. One tooth in its mouth is hollow, barbed and loaded with venom, and it fires that tooth into a fish on the end of a stretchy tube.","more":"The tube is called a proboscis. The snail creeps up, extends it like a slow gray finger, and then a muscle fires. The tooth stays attached, so the snail can reel its catch straight back in. A fishing rod, a spear and a syringe, all in one.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The harpoon is disposable. After a meal the snail spits out the used tooth along with the fish's scales, and a fresh one is already waiting in the tube behind it.","more":"The teeth are grown in a row inside a pouch called the radular sac, each one hollowed and barbed before it is ever needed. Fire, discard, reload. There is always another loaded tooth ready to go.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"speed","text":"The strike is one of the fastest movements ever measured in an animal. In the cat cone the harpoon leaves the mouth at 56 mph (90 km/h) — from a snail.","more":"It goes from still to full speed at about 40,000 times the tug you feel standing still. A rollercoaster manages four. The whole strike is over before a fish can flinch, and the animal doing it moves at a snail's pace the rest of the day.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"A cone snail hunts by smell. It waves a snorkel-like tube called a siphon in the current, reads the water, then buries itself in the sand with only that tube showing.","more":"It never has to chase anything, which is just as well. Some cone snails eat worms, some eat other snails, and a few hunt fish — a strange job for an animal that cannot run anything down. The fish comes to the snail.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"The geography cone stuns fish with insulin. It floods the water with the same chemical your body uses to handle sugar, and the fish's blood sugar crashes so hard they go limp.","more":"The fish drift into the snail's waiting mouth in a daze, already too weak to swim off. Cone snails are the only animals known to use insulin as a weapon, and only a few kinds of cone snail do it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Do not pick up a cone shell. A few species carry venom strong enough to kill a person, and the snail can drive its tooth straight through a glove.","more":"About 27 human deaths have been confidently traced to cone snails, and nearly all of them were people who picked up a pretty shell. Most of the 800-odd species are no threat to us at all. The rule on the beach is simply look, don't touch.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"copied","text":"One cone snail's venom became a medicine. A single ingredient from the magician's cone is now a painkiller for people whose pain morphine cannot reach.","more":"It is called ziconotide. It blocks the channels that carry a pain signal up into the spinal cord. Morphine uses a completely different trick. That is exactly why ziconotide works for people morphine has stopped helping. The body does not become addicted to it.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Every species mixes its own venom. A single cone snail's venom can hold as many as 200 different active ingredients, and no two species make the same recipe.","more":"Across the whole family that adds up to tens of thousands of separate compounds called conotoxins. Each one is a tiny key shaped to fit one particular lock in a nervous system. It is why chemists keep coming back to these snails.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"The smallest cone snails are about 0.5 in (1.3 cm) long; the biggest reach 8.5 in (21.6 cm).","kind":"found"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"The shell is the disguise. Those brown-and-white patterns look like nothing much on a sandy reef, and the snail spends most of the day buried anyway, with only its siphon showing.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A cone snail glues its eggs to rock in tough capsules. What hatches out is not a snail at all but a swimming speck called a veliger, which drifts before settling down.","more":"The veliger has a tiny shell and a pair of beating flaps to swim with. It lives up in the plankton with everything else too small to see. Only when it finds the right patch of seabed does it drop down. Then it changes shape and starts being a snail.","kind":"found"}]},{"id":"coral","name":"Coral","group":"sea","blurb":"Not a rock and not a plant. Coral is a colony of tiny soft animals. They build a stone city and then live inside it.","size":"One coral animal is smaller than a pea; together they build reefs you can see from space","homes":["ocean"],"wonder":"Animals smaller than a pea, working without ever meeting, raised the largest structure any living thing has built.","facts":[{"cat":"build","text":"Coral is an animal. Each polyp is a soft sac, usually a few millimeters across. A ring of stinging tentacles sits on top. It builds itself a limestone cup to live in.","more":"The polyp pulls calcium and carbonate straight out of the seawater. It lays them down underneath itself, cup by cup. A reef is mostly stonework left behind by polyps that lived there before. Only a thin skin of living coral covers the outside.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"The Great Barrier Reef runs more than 1,243 miles (2,000 km) (1,250 mi) along the coast of Australia. It is the largest structure on Earth built by living creatures, and you can see it from space.","more":"It is not one reef but around 2,900 of them, plus some 900 islands, all of it made by animals small enough that several would sit on your thumbnail. Nothing else alive has ever built anything on that scale.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"A coral polyp is a farmer and a hunter at once. Algae living inside its body hand over sugar made from sunlight — as much as nine-tenths of the coral's food.","more":"The algae are zooxanthellae, the same partners giant clams keep, and they are also where the color comes from. At night the polyp changes job: out come the tentacles, and it catches plankton drifting past in the dark.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"The color you see in coral mostly isn't the coral. It's the algae in its skin. When water gets too warm the coral throws them out and turns bone white — that's bleaching.","more":"A bleached coral is not dead yet. It is starving. If the water cools soon enough the algae move back in and the color returns. If it stays hot, the polyps die and the stone city is left empty. A rise of only 1–2 °C can do it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Whole reefs spawn on the same night. Millions of corals release their eggs together a few nights after a full moon, and the sea turns into a slow pink snowstorm.","more":"Nobody signals them. Corals track water temperature, day length and moonlight, and they all arrive at the same answer. Releasing everything at once means the water is so thick with eggs that no predator could possibly eat them all.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Corals fight each other. Neighbors growing too close send out long sweeper tentacles at night to sting. Some push their stomach lining out onto a rival and digest it where it stands.","more":"The prize is space — a patch of sunlit rock. These battles run for weeks and the winner is usually whichever colony can reach furthest. Look closely at a crowded reef and you can spot the no-man's-land gaps between colonies that have fought to a standstill.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Coral reefs cover less than one percent of the ocean floor. Nobody has counted every creature in the sea, but the best estimate puts about a quarter of all the kinds on reefs.","more":"It is the most crowded address in the ocean. Fish, crabs, octopuses, eels, snails, sponges and worms all pack into the cracks and branches of the stonework, the way a hedge fills up with birds.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"speed","text":"A boulder coral grows about a centimeter taller in a year — the width of your little fingernail. Fast branching kinds can manage ten times that, but the city is still stacked in slow motion.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Coral polyps are cousins of jellyfish and sea anemones. Same group, same stinging cells — nematocysts — but a coral is the one that built a stone cup and stayed in it.","more":"Brush a coral and you are being stung. You simply cannot feel most of them through your skin. Each nematocyst is a coiled harpoon held under pressure. Touch the trigger hair and it fires in less than a thousandth of a second.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Not all coral needs sunlight. There are coral gardens far below where any light reaches, in cold black water. Those polyps have no algae at all. They catch every scrap of their food from the dark.","more":"Reef-building corals hug warm, clear, shallow water because their algae need the sun. The deep ones have no such partner and no color to lose. They spread their tentacles into the current and wait, in the cold, for whatever drifts by.","kind":"found"}]},{"id":"sea-slug","name":"Sea slug","group":"sea","blurb":"A sea snail with no shell, soft and ludicrously colorful. It is also a thief. It takes weapons, poison and even sunlight from the things it eats.","size":"Most are shorter than your thumb; the biggest is as long as your whole arm","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"length":0.2,"life":1},"wonder":"Armor, poison, even a way to eat sunlight — it borrows every one of them, and wears the whole borrowed kit in color.","facts":[{"cat":"build","text":"Nudibranch means \"naked gills\". Look at the feathery plume standing up on a sea slug's back — that is the gill, worn on the outside, with nothing covering it at all.","more":"Most snails do their breathing tucked inside a shell. A nudibranch starts life as a swimming speck with a tiny coiled shell and sheds it when it changes into its adult shape, so everything ends up exposed. Which raises the obvious question — how does anything that soft survive on a reef?","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"The bright colors look like a warning label. Nobody can ask a fish what it makes of them. The best read is that a slug blazing orange and purple is advertising that it tastes foul, or worse. Predators learn that lesson fast.","more":"Some brew their own bad chemicals. Others take them straight from dinner. A slug that eats a poisonous sponge stores the sponge's poison in its own skin. It wears the sponge's colors while it does it.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Some sea slugs steal weapons. They eat stinging hydroids and swallow the sting cells without setting them off. The cells get moved up into the frilly tips on their back. Then the slug fires them at anything that bothers it.","more":"The stolen cells are called kleptocnidae — literally \"stolen stingers\". They travel through the slug's gut untriggered and end up stored point-outwards in little sacs at the ends of the frills, which are called cerata. The slug never has to build a weapon of its own.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"The blue dragon eats Portuguese man o' war and keeps the stings. It packs them in tighter than they started, so a sting from this thumb-sized slug can be worse than one from the animal it ate.","more":"It floats upside down at the surface, holding a bubble of gas in its stomach to stay up, riding wind and current. Blue side up toward the sky, silver-gray side down into the water — hard to spot from above and hard to spot from below.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Some sea slugs steal solar panels. They eat algae, keep the chloroplasts alive inside their own gut lining, and turn leaf-green while those stolen parts go on catching sunlight for months.","more":"The trick is called kleptoplasty. Elysia chlorotica is the famous one — leaf-green, leaf-shaped, and so packed with working chloroplasts that at a glance you would file it under plant rather than animal. No other group of animals does this the way sea slugs do.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"One sea slug can throw away its entire body. The head crawls off on its own, wound and all, and grows a new heart, gut and everything else back.","more":"Researchers watched Elysia marginata do it. The head takes hours to work free at a groove just behind it. It is crawling and feeding again the same day. It has a complete new body in about three weeks. Only young ones manage it. The cast-off body moves for days but never grows a head.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Those two horns on its head are noses. Rhinophores pull scent out of the water and steer the slug toward food, and many species can pull them down into a pocket if danger gets close.","more":"Its eyes are almost useless — two dots about a quarter of a millimeter across that tell light from dark and little else. A sea slug lives in a world of smell and touch, feeling its way across a reef it has never really seen.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Every sea slug is male and female at the same time — and still needs a partner. Two line up side by side, and both crawl away able to lay eggs.","more":"The eggs go down in a coiled ribbon, often bright white or pink, stuck to a rock or sometimes to the very animal the parent was eating. Some ribbons hold tens of thousands of eggs. The parents do not stay around for any of it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"Nudibranchs run from 0.16 in (4 mm) — smaller than a grain of rice — up to 2 ft (60 cm). The big one is the Spanish dancer, which swims by flapping its whole body like a red skirt.","more":"For all that color and machinery, the show is short. Most sea slugs are gone inside a year, and some last only a few weeks.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"Others go the opposite way and disappear. A slug that lives on one particular sponge grows the exact color and bumpiness of that sponge — you can stare straight at it and miss it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Sea slugs live in every ocean on Earth. Tide pools you could stand in. Tropical reefs. Water more than 2,300 ft (700 m) down. The cold dark underneath polar ice.","kind":"found"}]},{"id":"mantis-shrimp","name":"Mantis shrimp","group":"crustaceans","blurb":"Not actually a shrimp. It is a reef crustacean called a stomatopod. It has the fastest punch in the ocean and the strangest eyes on Earth.","size":"About as long as your hand; the biggest is the length of your forearm","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"length":0.3},"wonder":"A spring, a latch, three-way eyes and armor that engineers are still trying to copy, all packed into something the length of your hand.","facts":[{"cat":"speed","text":"The punch travels at 23 m/s (51 mph), about the speed of a car on a main road. It starts from a dead stop, inside a body the length of your hand.","more":"The arm goes from still to full speed in around two ten-thousandths of a second, pulling more than 10,000 times the tug of gravity you feel standing still. You never see it happen. You only see the result.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Muscle alone is far too slow for that. The mantis shrimp cocks its arm against a saddle-shaped spring in its shell, latches it, and lets go. That is a crossbow, not an arm.","more":"The spring is a curved plate of stiff and stretchy layers that squashes flat as the muscle slowly pulls it down. A latch holds everything in place. When the latch slips, all that stored energy dumps out at once. Slow to load, impossible to dodge.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"It lands about 1,500 newtons of force. That is more than a thousand times its own body weight. It cracks open the shell of a crab, a snail or a clam like a nut.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"The punch is so fast the water cannot keep up. The arm tears open a bubble of vapor behind it. A heartbeat later the bubble collapses with a bang and a burst of heat. That is a second hit on the same spot.","more":"The effect is called cavitation. It means a mantis shrimp can damage a snail it technically missed. Aquarium keepers know the sound. It is a dry crack from somewhere inside the tank, like someone tapping the glass with a coin.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"People keep mantis shrimp in aquariums, and people keep replacing the glass. If one decides it does not like the wall of its tank, it will punch the wall, and glass has lost before.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Your eye has three kinds of color cell. A mantis shrimp's has twelve to sixteen, reaching from deep ultraviolet all the way past red into light you will never see.","more":"You would assume that means richer color than we get. It doesn't — tested carefully, a mantis shrimp is actually worse than you at telling similar shades apart. It seems to sweep its eyes across a scene and read color in one pass instead of comparing. Cruder, but far faster.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Each eye works on its own. It sits on a stalk, swivels wherever it likes, and is split into three strips — so one eye by itself can judge how far away something is.","more":"You need both of your eyes to judge distance. A mantis shrimp needs one, and has a spare aimed somewhere else entirely. For an animal whose whole plan is a single perfectly aimed strike, knowing the exact range matters more than almost anything.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Mantis shrimp see a kind of light no other animal is known to see. Light can spiral as it travels — circular polarisation — and they read it, and reflect it off their own bodies.","more":"It amounts to a private channel. Almost nothing else in the sea can pick up that spiralling light, so a signal sent in it is invisible to a predator swimming right past. Only a handful of mantis shrimp species are known to do it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"There are two kinds of mantis shrimp: smashers and spearers. Smashers carry a club and break shells open. Spearers carry a spiny arm and stab soft, fast things like fish.","more":"A spearer sits in a burrow with just its eyes out. It waits for a fish to swim within reach. Then it snaps the barbed arm out and drags the fish in. A smasher goes shopping instead. It crawls the reef, turning over crabs and snails and hammering them apart.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"copied","text":"Engineers are copying the club. The smasher's hammer takes thousands of hits without shattering, because inside it the fibers are stacked in a slow twisting spiral that stops cracks running.","more":"A crack tries to travel straight. In a spiral stack there is no straight line to follow, so the crack bends, splits and runs out of energy. Materials scientists have built the same twisting pattern into test panels and body armor, chasing something light that simply refuses to break.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"Some mantis shrimp pair up for life. A male and female zebra mantis shrimp share one burrow, and the same pair turns up in the same hole year after year. Nobody has followed a couple the whole way; twenty years together is the best read.","more":"They take turns. One guards the mouth of the burrow while the other hunts, and the pair keep the tunnel dug out and clear between them. For an animal famous for hitting things, it is a surprisingly settled home life.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"size","text":"Most mantis shrimp are about 4 in (10 cm) long — the width of your hand. The zebra mantis shrimp reaches 15 in (38 cm), roughly the length of your forearm.","kind":"found"}]},{"id":"pistol-shrimp","name":"Pistol shrimp","group":"crustaceans","blurb":"A thumb-sized shrimp with one oversized claw that kills by snapping a bubble at things.","size":"About as long as your thumb — and one claw is nearly half of that","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"length":0.2},"wonder":"God put one of the ocean's loudest sounds inside a claw smaller than your thumb.","facts":[{"cat":"weird","text":"Its weapon isn't the claw — it's the bubble. The claw shuts so fast the water tears open into a vapor pocket called cavitation, and the pocket's collapse is the punch.","more":"Snap the claw and a jet of water shoots out at about 56 mph (90 km/h) — highway speed. Water cannot follow that fast, so it splits, leaving an empty bubble behind. Less than a thousandth of a second later the bubble caves in, and that collapse is the shockwave.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It never has to touch its dinner. A small fish or worm passing the burrow gets hit by the shockwave and stops moving. The shrimp strolls out and collects it.","more":"That is why the claw is shaped the way it is. There is no cutting edge and no crushing surface — just a plunger and a socket, built to slam and make a bubble. A shrimp that misses simply resets and waits.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"Point a microphone at a coral reef and the crackle you hear — like bacon frying — is thousands of these shrimp snapping. It is one of the loudest sounds in the sea.","more":"Snapping shrimp compete with sperm whales and belugas for loudest animal in the ocean. In the Second World War, submarine crews learned to hide inside shrimp beds. The racket swamped enemy sonar completely. A steel submarine full of people simply vanished into the noise.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"When the bubble collapses, it flashes. Real light, made by a shrimp — far too faint for your eye, but cameras in dark tanks have caught it.","more":"The name for it is shrimpoluminescence. The collapse squeezes that speck of gas violently. It passes 5,000 degrees, about the temperature of the sun's surface. That lasts a few billionths of a second, then it goes dark. The shrimp itself is completely unbothered.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"Some share a home with a fish. The shrimp digs and repairs the burrow; the goby stands guard at the doorway and signals when danger comes.","more":"The shrimp sees very poorly, so it keeps one antenna resting on the goby's body the whole time it works. A flick of the fish's tail sends the shrimp straight back down the hole. Neither animal could hold that patch of seabed alone.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"One kind lives inside a sponge in a colony of over three hundred, with a single egg-laying queen and big-clawed defenders at the door. Eusocial — the same word used for ants.","more":"Everyone in the sponge is family. The defenders do not breed; they patrol the tunnels and snap at any shrimp that is not kin. This was the first animal ever found living an ant's kind of life underwater.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The claws don't match — one huge, one ordinary. Lose the big one and the small claw grows into a snapper, while a new small claw sprouts where the big one was.","more":"Lose the snapping claw and the tool on the other side gets promoted. A replacement tool then grows in the empty socket. No other animal is known to do this.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"Most snapping shrimp are 1.2 to 2 in (3 to 5 cm) long (1 to 2 inches). That is small enough to sit in a tablespoon. It is also loud enough to rival a whale.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"The snap is a warning as much as a weapon. Two shrimp arguing over a burrow will snap at each other until one backs down — no contact, just pressure and noise.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Most live in burrows they dig themselves under rubble and coral. In many species a male and female share one tunnel. They keep it in repair together.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"It sees very poorly. Two pairs of antennae do nearly all the work, tasting the water and reading the current for anything that moves close by.","kind":"found"}]},{"id":"coconut-crab","name":"Coconut crab","group":"crustaceans","blurb":"The largest crab that walks on land — a hermit crab that outgrew every shell in the sea and climbs trees instead.","size":"Leg tip to leg tip, about 1 meter (3 feet) — the width of a doorway","homes":["forest","ocean"],"stats":{"weight":9,"length":3.3,"life":60},"wonder":"God gave one crab lungs, climbing claws, and armor tough enough to survive the fall.","facts":[{"cat":"record","text":"It is the largest arthropod that walks on land. Leg tip to leg tip a big one spans a meter (3 feet) and weighs about 9 lb (4 kg) — a large housecat.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Scientists measured the pinch. The strongest crab tested squeezed with 1,765 newtons — like balancing two adults on a single point, and it does not let go quickly.","more":"Researchers coaxed twenty-nine wild crabs into gripping a force sensor, and the biggest produced that figure. A strong adult's hand manages a few hundred newtons at most. The claw is not a threat display — it genuinely crushes.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"It is a hermit crab. As a youngster it lugs a borrowed snail shell around like any other, then outgrows every shell in the sea and hardens its own back instead.","more":"Young coconut crabs will even use a broken coconut shell when snail shells run short. Once the abdomen toughens up the crab gives up shells for good, and a full-grown one could never squeeze into one again.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"It cannot swim, and it cannot breathe underwater. Leave an adult in the sea for about an hour and it drowns, because it breathes air through a branchiostegal lung.","more":"The lung sits where a sea crab's gills would be, in a chamber under the shell. It only works while it is damp, so the crab dips its legs in a puddle and lets the water wick up into the chamber.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"It hunts by smell, and it is extremely good at it. The antennae pick up rotting meat, split fruit and coconut from a long way across an island.","more":"Its smelling organs work more like an insect's than a sea crab's. Scent drifting through air behaves nothing like scent dissolved in water. The equipment has to match. A dead animal on a beach pulls crabs in from well inland.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Yes, it really does open coconuts. It hammers at the three soft eye-spots with its claw, sometimes over several days, then digs the flesh out with its smaller legs.","more":"It is slow, unglamorous work — hours of stripping husk fiber by fiber before the shell underneath is even exposed. Coconuts are not the main course either. These crabs eat fruit, seeds, dead animals, and sometimes each other.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"It is not only a scavenger. A researcher on a Pacific island watched one climb a tree at night, grip a sleeping seabird by the wing, and break it.","more":"The bird was a red-footed booby, which roosts up in the branches. Once it was down, more crabs arrived out of the dark to share it. That was the first clear record of a coconut crab hunting an animal with a backbone.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"It climbs. Adults go straight up coconut palms, hooking in with the sharp tips of their walking legs — and the quickest way down is to let go.","more":"A coconut crab can drop 4.5 meters (15 feet) onto hard ground — higher than a basketball hoop — and simply walk away, protected by its own armor. Up a trunk there is nothing on the island that can eat it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"The mother carries her eggs down to the sea and shakes them loose at high tide. The larvae drift as plankton for three or four weeks before crawling ashore — where the adults can never follow.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"It digs a burrow in sand or leaf litter and lines it with shredded coconut fiber, which holds moisture. A coconut crab in a dry burrow is a coconut crab in trouble.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"It grows only by shedding. The whole armored shell splits and slides off, and the crab hides underground for weeks, soft as rubber, eating its old shell for the calcium.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"It takes its time. A coconut crab can breed at about five years old, but reaching full size takes forty years or more. Nobody has followed one from hatching to that size; the growth rate is the argument, and it puts the biggest past sixty.","more":"That is the strange thing about a really large coconut crab. The animal in front of you has been walking that same patch of island since before you were born, growing a fraction wider at every shed.","kind":"worked"}]},{"id":"japanese-spider-crab","name":"Japanese spider crab","group":"crustaceans","blurb":"A deep-water crab whose legs stretch further than any other animal with a shell on the outside.","size":"Claw tip to claw tip, up to 12 ft (3.7 m) — two grown adults lying head to toe","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"weight":42,"length":12.1},"wonder":"Twelve feet across, in water where almost no light reaches — made beautiful with nobody down there to see it.","facts":[{"cat":"record","text":"No animal with an exoskeleton reaches further. Claw tip to claw tip: 12 ft (3.7 m). Lie down beside one and its legs would stretch past your head and your feet.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"The body is the surprise. Nearly all that span is leg — the shell itself, the carapace, is only about 15.7 in (40 cm) across.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"For all that reach, it is not heavy. The biggest weigh about 42 lb (19 kg) — a five-year-old child, spread thin across twelve feet of leg.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"It lives deep. Most are found 656 to 984 ft (200 to 300 m) down (650 to 1,000 ft) on sand and rock, in cold water where almost no daylight arrives.","more":"The full range runs from about 164 to 1,969 ft (50 to 600 m). That depth is why almost nobody meets one. They come up in traps and nets off the Pacific coast of Japan. A handful live in aquariums. In the wild they are a submarine's find, not a diver's.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"It decorates itself. The crab picks up sponges, anemones and scraps of seaweed and presses them onto its shell until it looks like a lump of seabed with legs.","more":"The passengers are called epibionts, and they keep growing where they are put. Some sponges taste foul, which makes an already well-hidden crab an unappetising one too. Decorator crabs will move their trimmings across to the fresh shell after a moult.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Those legs come off on purpose. If something grabs one, the crab casts it away at a built-in break point — autotomy — and walks off on the other nine.","more":"Crabs pulled up in nets are often short two or three legs, and they get along perfectly well on what is left. A really big spider crab is almost never quite symmetrical.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It is a scavenger with enormous patience. Dead fish, shellfish, algae, anything soft on the bottom — it prises shells apart and picks the seabed over slowly.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A female can carry up to 1.5 million eggs at once, glued in a mass under her body. They hatch in about ten days as specks of plankton, and hardly any survive.","more":"Almost all of that million and a half drift off and are eaten within days. A handful settle to the bottom and begin the long, slow climb to twelve feet across.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"It grows only by moulting. The old shell splits along the back and the crab drags all ten enormous legs out through the gap, like peeling off soaking wet gloves.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"The males are the giants. A male's front claw-bearing legs, the chelipeds, grow far longer than the rest of him, while a female's stay shorter than her walking legs.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"Once a year they come up. In winter and early spring they gather in shallower water, sometimes only 164 ft (50 m) down, to breed — the one season anyone finds them easily.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"For all its size it is a mild animal. Keepers describe them as slow and gentle — though a claw on the end of a six-foot leg still has surprising reach.","kind":"found"}]},{"id":"hermit-crab","name":"Hermit crab","group":"crustaceans","blurb":"A crab with a soft, curled tail that borrows an empty snail shell to live in, and trades up as it grows.","size":"Most are between a thumb and a fist in size, shell included","homes":["ocean","forest"],"stats":{"length":0.3,"life":30},"wonder":"A creature born without armor — set down on a shore already scattered with exactly the right empty shells.","facts":[{"cat":"build","text":"Its back half is bare. Under the borrowed shell the abdomen is soft, pale and twisted into a spiral, curled to fit the inside of a snail shell exactly.","more":"That spiral is the whole reason for the shell. The front of a hermit crab is armored like any crab — claws, legs, hard head end. Everything behind it is naked, and would be a free meal in seconds.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"It doesn't just sit in the shell — it grips. Hooked rear legs and a muscular tail clamp onto the columella, the shell's inner pillar, and it will not let go while it lives.","more":"Pull at a hermit crab and it will let its own legs tear off before it comes out. If you want to see the crab, put a better empty shell nearby and wait. Never tug.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"They queue for houses. A crab that finds a shell too big waits beside it. As others arrive they line up largest to smallest. Then all of them swap at once, down the line.","more":"Biologists call it a vacancy chain, and a line can reach twenty crabs holding onto one another. The largest takes the new shell, the next takes the one it just left, and the smallest walks off in a shell nobody else wanted.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"When shells are scarce, they fight. One crab grabs another's shell and raps it hard against its own — knock, knock, knock — until the owner gives up and is evicted.","more":"The rapping is a test as much as an attack. A strong, well-fed occupant holds on far longer than a tired one. How hard and how fast the attacker knocks tends to decide who ends up in which shell.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Not everything they live in is a shell. Hermit crabs have been found inside bottle caps, broken glass and burned-out light bulbs — anything hollow with a rim to grip.","more":"On some beaches this turns deadly. A crab can climb into slippery plastic and never climb out. One that dies inside gives off a scent that says empty shell going spare. That draws in more crabs. Hundreds have been found in a single container.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"Some carry a bodyguard. A sea anemone rides on the shell, stinging anything that comes too close, and gets the crab's leftovers and a free ride in return.","more":"When the crab moves house it strokes the anemone until it releases its grip, then lifts it across to the new shell by hand. Neither animal is trapped. This is mutualism — two creatures, each getting something out of it.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"senses","text":"It shops carefully. Finding an empty shell, a crab will roll it over, run its claws around the opening, weigh it, and climb in and out before committing.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"It starts life at sea. The eggs hatch into drifting plankton. A young crab eventually crawls ashore and goes looking for its first tiny shell.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"To grow, it buries itself. Land hermit crabs dig down, shed the whole exoskeleton, and stay hidden for up to two months while the new one hardens — then eat the old one.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"The biggest hermit crab gives up on shells altogether. The coconut crab starts out shell-carrying like the rest, then outgrows every shell in the ocean and hardens its own back.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"A land hermit crab carries its own water supply. A little puddle of seawater sloshes in the back of the shell, keeping the gills damp — no water, no breathing.","more":"This is why land hermit crabs stay near the coast, and why a tank needs both fresh and salt water. Gills only work wet. Let that puddle dry out and the crab suffocates in open air.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"They last. The big land species live for decades, not seasons — and the oldest hermit crab ever kept as a pet reached forty-five, older than most people's parents.","kind":"found"}]},{"id":"fiddler-crab","name":"Fiddler crab","group":"crustaceans","blurb":"A small mudflat crab. The male has one ordinary claw and one absurdly enormous one. He waves the big one like a flag.","size":"About 1.2 in (3 cm) across, with a claw nearly as big as the rest of him","homes":["ocean","forest"],"stats":{"length":0.1,"life":2},"wonder":"God set the rhythm of the tides ticking inside a crab about 1.2 in (3 cm) across.","facts":[{"cat":"build","text":"One claw is a spoon and the other is a wrecking ball. In a male fiddler crab that big claw — the major cheliped — can reach half his total weight.","more":"He carries it everywhere: in and out of a burrow, across open mud with birds overhead. It is heavy, it slows him down, and it is almost useless for eating. He waves it for hours anyway.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"He waves it. Every species has its own wave — a slow arc, a sharp snap, a circle — and females read the timing as carefully as the size.","more":"Put a hundred males on one mudflat and the whole surface flickers. A female walks through the display, stopping at burrow after burrow, and inspects the hole he dug as well as the crab waving beside it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Watch the mud and you can see the leftovers. A fiddler crab scoops up sediment, sifts out the algae and bacteria, and spits the cleaned grains back out as neat little balls.","more":"The mouthparts work like a stack of sorting sieves, floating the light organic bits upward and letting the heavy sand drop. One crab can leave hundreds of pellets around its burrow in a single low tide — a map of exactly where it walked.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Females have two small claws and both work as spoons. A male can only feed with one — so the female beside him gets through her dinner at roughly twice his rate.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Lose the big claw and a new one grows at the next moult, about the same size but much weaker. From any distance it looks identical, so it still works as a bluff.","more":"A fight between two males is mostly a shoving match of interlocked claws. A regrown claw is lighter to carry and hopeless in a real contest. Most disputes are settled by waving, long before anyone tests it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Its eyes are on sticks. Two long stalks lift them clear of the mud for almost all-round vision — a fiddler crab can watch a bird behind it while walking forwards.","more":"The stalks fold flat into grooves on the shell when the crab reverses into its burrow. Above the eye's midline is sky; below it is ground. Anything appearing above that line gets treated as a bird, and the crab runs first and checks later.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"It digs a hole and shuts the door. At high tide the crab seals the top with a plug of mud, trapping a pocket of air to breathe until the water drains away again.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"It knows the tide without seeing it. Keep a fiddler crab in a lab with no sea and no daylight, and it still turns active exactly when low tide would be.","more":"The rhythm runs on a roughly 12.4-hour cycle, matching the tides, and it keeps time for weeks with no cue at all. The color change runs on schedule too — dark by day, pale by night, inside a sealed box far from any shore.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Some males build. Beside the burrow they raise a small pillar or hood of mud. Females walk toward the structures. So it looks like a landmark, pulling them in from further away than a waving claw can.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"defence","text":"When a shadow crosses the flat, the whole colony vanishes. Hundreds of crabs drop into their burrows at the same instant, and the mud goes still and empty in under a second.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"talk","text":"It drums as well as waves. At night, when no one can see a claw, the male raps it against the ground and the female feels the beat through the mud.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"The female carries her eggs in a spongy orange mass under her body, then wades out at high tide and shakes them loose. The larvae go to sea and return weeks later as pinhead crabs.","kind":"found"}]},{"id":"american-lobster","name":"American lobster","group":"crustaceans","blurb":"A heavily armored sea-floor crawler with two mismatched claws. It walks the cold Atlantic seabed hunting anything it can crack open.","size":"A common one is about as long as your forearm. The big old ones reach the length of your whole arm.","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"length":0.8,"weight":2},"facts":[{"cat":"build","text":"Look at the claws — they don't match. One is a fat crusher with blunt knobs for smashing shells. The other is a thin ripper with a jagged edge, like a steak knife.","more":"Which side gets which claw isn't fixed. A lobster can be right-crushered or left-crushered, the way you are right- or left-handed. Lose a claw to a fight or a predator and a young lobster can grow a new one back over several moults.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"A lobster chews with its stomach. Just behind the mouth sits a gastric mill — three hard teeth that grind up food after it has already been swallowed.","more":"The mill is a set of calcium-hardened plates worked by muscle. Food goes down barely broken, gets ground there, and only then passes on. When the lobster moults it sheds the lining of that stomach too, and grows a fresh set of teeth with the new shell.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Lobsters argue by weeing at each other. Two nozzles beneath the antennae fire a plume of urine one to two meters (3-6 ft) straight at a rival's face.","more":"The chemicals in that stream carry a message: how big I am, how ready I am to fight, whether we have met before. Two lobsters square up, exchange squirts, and often one simply backs down without a claw ever landing. The loser remembers the winner's smell for days.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"A lobster tastes with its feet. Chemical sensors cover its legs and the little antennules on its head, so crossing the seabed is like licking every step.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"The heaviest lobster ever recorded weighed 44.4 lb (20.1 kg). It was hauled up off Nova Scotia.","more":"Lobsters never stop growing; they simply have to keep swapping shells to do it. A lobster that heavy has climbed out of its own shell and rebuilt it dozens of times, and its claws alone would fill both of your hands.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"defence","text":"It escapes backwards. One hard flick of the tail fan shoots the lobster away from a predator, tail-first, faster than you could clap your hands.","more":"The flick runs on a giant nerve fiber — a single nerve cell so thick it carries the signal almost instantly. The lobster is already moving before there is time to decide anything. It is the same trick a crayfish uses when you reach into the bucket.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Lobster blood runs clear, then turns blue in air. It carries oxygen with copper instead of iron, using a molecule called haemocyanin.","more":"Your blood is red because haemoglobin holds oxygen with iron. Haemocyanin holds it with copper, and copper goes blue when it grabs on. Copper works well in cold water with little oxygen in it — which is exactly where a lobster lives.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Lobsters walk the cold rocky floor of the north Atlantic. They usually stay between 13.1 and 164 ft (4 and 50 m) (13-164 ft) down. By day they wedge into crevices. After dark they come out hunting.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A mother lobster glues her eggs under her own tail. She carries them there for ten or eleven months. She fans them with her swimmerets until they hatch.","more":"She may carry tens of thousands at once. The hatchlings look nothing like lobsters — they are transparent specks that drift near the surface, moulting as they go. Only about one in a thousand survives long enough to sink down and start life on the seabed.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Lobsters eat crabs, clams, sea urchins, worms and fish, and they are not above scavenging. They also eat their own shed shell, to win back the calcium for the next one.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"To grow, a lobster climbs out of itself. The shell splits at the back and the whole animal drags free — legs, antennae, claws, eye coverings and all.","more":"The escape takes about fifteen minutes. It is the riskiest thing a lobster does. The new shell underneath is soft as wet paper. The lobster swallows water to swell into a bigger size. Then it hides for days or weeks until the shell hardens.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Claws, legs, eyes, even the teeth inside its stomach — all shed and rebuilt again and again, and the lobster steps out whole every time."},{"id":"barnacle","name":"Barnacle","group":"crustaceans","blurb":"A crustacean, and a cousin of crabs and shrimp. When young it glues its own head to a rock. It spends the rest of its life inside a little limestone cone.","size":"Most are smaller than your thumbnail.","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"length":0.0},"facts":[{"cat":"build","text":"A barnacle is lying on its back in there, head cemented down, legs waving out of the top. Every time it feeds, it kicks food into its own mouth.","more":"Those feathery legs are called cirri. They sweep out through the opening, curl shut around whatever plankton they catch, and haul it back inside. Watch a barnacle in a tide pool and you will see the little fan flick out and in, out and in.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A barnacle gets one decision in its whole life: where to land. The swimming larva, called a cyprid, crawls over a rock testing it, then cements its forehead down for good.","more":"The cyprid checks the surface texture, the chemistry, how well it wets, and whether other barnacles already live there — company proves the spot works. Once it commits it glues itself head-first and rebuilds its entire body inside a shell. It will never move again.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Cemented in place, a barnacle cannot go looking for a mate. So it reaches — stretching a long flexible tube across to a neighbor. The best read is that this is why barnacles nearly always settle in crowds.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"copied","text":"Barnacle glue sets underwater, on a wet, dirty, moving surface — exactly what human glues fail at. Engineers copied it to make a paste that seals a bleeding wound in about fifteen seconds.","more":"Nothing manufactured sticks to a wet surface the way barnacle cement does. A team at MIT built a barnacle-inspired paste for surgeons. Shipbuilders chase the same glue from the opposite direction. They want a hull coating that barnacles cannot grip at all.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"When the tide goes out, a barnacle shuts its front door. Plates on top slide together and seal it in, with a drop of seawater trapped inside.","more":"An acorn barnacle's cone is six limestone plates it builds around itself, with a four-plate lid on top — ten in all. Sealed up, it survives hours of baking sun and drying wind. That is why a barnacle rock is silent at low tide and quietly clicking when the sea comes back.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It eats by filtering. Plankton, larvae, and scraps far too small to see get combed out of the water by six pairs of feathery legs, over and over and over.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"Some barnacles ride whales. They settle on a humpback's skin and cross entire oceans with it, filter-feeding wherever the whale happens to go.","more":"Whale barnacles grow rooted prongs of shell down into the skin and lock on there. A single humpback can carry a heavy crust of them. The pattern of markings and scars on its tail is unique. Researchers use it to tell individual whales apart.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Barnacles take the worst spot on the coast on purpose: the splash zone, where the waves hit hardest. Also harbor walls, pier legs, ship hulls and turtle shells.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"One barnacle weighs almost nothing. A ship's hull crusted with them drags so badly it burns far more fuel, which is why hulls get scraped clean in dry dock.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"An adult barnacle has no proper eyes — just three simple light detectors. That is enough to notice a shadow sweep overhead and snap its legs back inside.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"It picks one spot, once, and never moves again — and is still fed twice a day by a tide it does not control."},{"id":"krill","name":"Antarctic krill","group":"crustaceans","blurb":"A small, almost see-through, shrimp-like animal of the freezing Southern Ocean. It swarms in numbers vast enough to feed nearly everything else down there.","size":"About as long as your little finger.","homes":["ocean","polar"],"stats":{"length":0.2,"weight":0,"life":6},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"A full-grown krill is about 2.4 in (6 cm) long and weighs 0.1 oz (2 g) — roughly two paperclips. It is so nearly transparent you can see its green gut straight through the shell.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Nobody can count them. The best estimate puts all the Antarctic krill in the ocean together at hundreds of millions of tons. That is one of the greatest weights of any wild animal on earth.","more":"Nobody counts them one by one. Ships tow nets and bounce sound off the swarms to work out how much is down there. The swarms stretch for kilometers and pack so tightly that from above they stain the sea a rusty pink-red.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"Krill swarm. At their thickest, 10,000 to 30,000 of them pack into one cubic meter of water — a box you could just about curl up inside.","more":"Predators do not hunt individual krill; they swim straight into the swarm with their mouths wide open. Whales, crabeater seals, penguins, squid and fish all take their share. The swarm survives because there is so much of it that no single mouth makes a dent.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"A starving krill shrinks. Most animals just get thin — a krill moults into a smaller body and waits for the food to come back.","more":"It sheds its shell every 13 to 20 days, all its life. Normally the new one is bigger. Through the dark Antarctic winter there is little to eat. A krill can come out of a molt smaller than it went in. It goes backwards on purpose, then grows again when the light returns.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Krill are bioluminescent — they make their own light. Organs beneath the eyestalks and along the underside glow yellow-green for two or three seconds at a time.","more":"The light organs are called photophores, and a krill can rotate them to aim the glow. A swarm flickering in black water is one of the strangest sights in the Southern Ocean. What the flashing is for is still unsettled — keeping the swarm together in the dark is the leading idea.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Krill farm the underside of the sea ice. They hang upside down beneath it and scrape algae off the frozen ceiling with brush-tipped legs.","more":"Through the long, dark winter the ice is the pantry. A krill works along the rough underside of it like a shopper going down a shelf. Its front legs make a basket of bristles. It combs loose algae into the basket and swallows the lot.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"Every single day krill ride an elevator. They drop to about 330 ft (100 m) in daylight, where it is dark and safer, then climb back up at night to feed.","more":"This is called diel vertical migration. Across all the world's oceans it is the largest daily movement of animals on the planet. It happens everywhere at once, every night. Antarctic krill make the trip one to three times a day.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Grabbed by a predator, a krill can throw off its own shell in a heartbeat and shoot backwards. Nobody has watched what the predator does next; the empty moult left hanging in the water reads as a decoy.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A female releases thousands of eggs at a time and they sink. They fall for about ten days into the cold and the dark. Then they hatch.","more":"The larvae hatch far below and then have to swim all the way back up. The climb takes days and they eat nothing on the way; there is no food until they reach the sunlit surface layer where the algae grow. Getting there in time is the hardest thing a krill ever has to do.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Krill live in the Southern Ocean, the ring of freezing water that circles Antarctica. It is some of the coldest, roughest, darkest sea anywhere on the planet.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Something the length of your finger holds up a whole ocean — the smallest link carrying the heaviest load."},{"id":"earthworm","name":"Earthworm","group":"worms","blurb":"A soft, ringed, boneless animal that eats its way through soil. It swallows dirt at one end and leaves it richer at the other.","size":"A big garden nightcrawler is about 9.6 in (24 cm) long.","homes":["backyard","forest","grassland"],"stats":{"length":0.8,"weight":0,"life":2},"facts":[{"cat":"build","text":"An earthworm has no lungs at all. It breathes straight through its skin, which is why it has to stay damp — a dried-out worm suffocates.","more":"Oxygen dissolves into the film of moisture on the skin. Hemoglobin floating loose in the worm's blood picks it up. That is the same oxygen-carrying molecule that makes your blood red. It is exactly why a worm's blood is red too.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Five pairs of thick muscular tubes wrap around a worm's gut and squeeze blood along them. They are called aortic arches, and most people just say a worm has hearts.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"A bird can rip off a worm's tail and the worm may still survive. But cut one in half and you do not get two worms — the back end dies.","more":"The brain, the mouth and nearly all the vital organs sit in the front section, so the back half has nothing to rebuild with. Some composting worms regrow a lost tail well. The common nightcrawler in a garden often cannot regrow one at all.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"A worm eats soil. It swallows dirt, digests the dead leaves and microbes mixed into it, and pushes the rest out the back end as a casting.","more":"Castings come out richer than the soil went in. They are packed with nitrogen and phosphorus in forms a plant can actually use. In leaf-rich ground one worm can make over 10 lb (4.5 kg) of castings a year.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"No bones, no legs. A worm moves by squeezing fluid around inside a sealed body and gripping the tunnel wall with tiny stiff bristles called setae.","more":"Run a worm gently backwards between your fingers and it feels like sandpaper — those are the setae. It anchors its back half, stretches the front forward, anchors the front, then hauls the back up. Squeeze, grip, stretch, repeat. That grip is why a robin has to pull so hard.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"A worm has no eyes, but its skin can tell light from dark. Cells scattered over the body, thickest at the head end, make it flinch away from a flashlight beam.","more":"Light means the surface, and the surface means birds and drying out — so bright light sends a worm diving back down its burrow. Red light barely registers, which is how people watch nightcrawlers at night without sending them all underground.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"copied","text":"Worm charmers drive a stake into the ground and rattle it, and worms come up out of the soil. The vibrations feel like a digging mole — the one thing a worm flees upward from.","more":"Moles hunt worms by tunneling. A worm's escape is to bolt for the surface, where moles rarely follow. People have used the trick for generations to collect fishing bait. They are not the only ones. Wood turtles stamp their feet to do it, and gulls patter theirs on wet grass.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Worms do not come out in the rain because they are drowning. They can survive underwater for a long time. The best read is simpler: wet ground lets them travel over the surface without drying out.","more":"Damp air is a worm's chance to move house, find a mate, or leave a flooded burrow — journeys that would kill it on a dry day. Rain drumming on the soil may also sound enough like a digging mole to send some of them straight up.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Every earthworm is male and female at once. Two of them line up head-to-tail, swap sperm, and both go away able to make eggs.","more":"The pale saddle around a worm's body is the clitellum. After mating it oozes a slimy ring. The ring slides forward off the worm, collecting eggs and sperm on the way. Then it seals shut into a lemon-shaped cocoon no bigger than a grain of rice. A few tiny worms hatch out fully formed.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"Most garden worms fit across your palm. But one kind along the Mekong River in Southeast Asia stretches to 10 ft (3 m) — longer than you are tall, lying down.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Some worms live in the top few centimeters of leaf litter. Others dig permanent vertical shafts more than a meter straight down and drag dead leaves in after them.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"Worms rebuild the ground beneath your feet. Given time, they pass the whole top layer of soil through their bodies — mixing it, draining it, burying dead leaves inside it.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"The soil your food grows in was partly made by animals that never see it, working in the dark, one mouthful at a time."},{"id":"leech","name":"Medicinal leech","group":"worms","blurb":"A soft, muscular freshwater worm with a sucker at each end. It feeds on blood. Hospitals still keep them in tanks today.","size":"About as long as your hand, though it stretches and shortens constantly.","homes":["freshwater"],"stats":{"length":0.7},"facts":[{"cat":"build","text":"A leech has a sucker at each end. The back one is a clamp for holding on. The front one has the mouth right in the middle of it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Inside that front sucker are three jaws arranged in a Y, each lined with rows of tiny teeth. The bite leaves a mark shaped like a three-pointed star.","more":"The jaws saw rather than stab, rocking back and forth to open a small clean wound. There are around a hundred cutting edges in there, each finer than a pin point. The Y-shaped mark is distinctive enough that a doctor can spot a leech bite on sight.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"A leech drinks until it looks like a fat sausage. A big one takes up to ten times its own body weight in one meal, then may not eat again for a whole year.","more":"The blood goes into pouches called crops that branch off the gut. Bacteria living inside the leech break it down slowly over months. A freshly fed leech is several times its normal size and can barely move. So it lets go, drops off, and hides.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Ten little eyes in five pairs run along the top of its head, and not one of them makes a picture. They read light and shadow — enough to notice something entering the water.","more":"The leech also feels vibration through the water and tastes chemicals dissolved in it. Warmth, sweat, and the ripples of a wading animal all pull it in. Step into the right pond and leeches will start swimming toward you within a minute.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Where you have one brain, a leech has a chain of nerve clusters. They are called ganglia. There is one to a segment, running the whole length of the body, all wired together.","more":"There are 21 of them down the middle, plus fused clusters at the head and the tail. Each ganglion drives the swimming of its own segment while staying in step with the rest. The cells are big enough to study one at a time, which has taught people a great deal about how any nervous system times a movement.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"copied","text":"Leech saliva contains hirudin — an anticoagulant, a chemical that stops blood clotting. It is why a leech bite keeps bleeding for hours after the leech has let go.","more":"A leech makes about sixty different proteins in its spit at once. Hirudin stops the blood clotting. Others widen the blood vessels and keep the wound open. Drug companies have copied hirudin to make medicines. Those keep human blood flowing during surgery.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"The leech is officially medical equipment. In 2004 the American medical regulator classified the medicinal leech as a medical device. That is the same legal category as a scalpel.","more":"A surgeon reattaching a finger or an ear can usually reconnect the arteries bringing blood in, but the tiny veins carrying it out are far harder. Blood pools, the tissue turns purple and starts to die. A leech set on that spot drains the pooled blood and keeps it moving until the body grows new veins of its own.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"travel","text":"On land a leech walks like an inchworm — front sucker down, body arched, back sucker up. In water it swims by rippling its flattened body like a ribbon.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A leech is both male and female at once. After mating, the band around its body makes a foamy cocoon that it leaves tucked in damp soil at the water's edge.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Medicinal leeches live in shallow weedy ponds and marshes. Their range runs across Europe and into western Asia. They have become genuinely rare, partly from being collected for medicine.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"A leech has no fixed length. Three layers of muscle — running long, running around, running diagonally — let it be a squat blob one second and a thin thread the next.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"You usually feel nothing. A leech bite is painless, so the first sign is the blood rather than the pain — and by then it has already been drinking for a while.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"The creature most people flinch from carries, in its spit, exactly what a surgeon needs — and nobody had to invent it."},{"id":"planarian","name":"Planarian","group":"worms","blurb":"A soft, flat little worm that glides along the underside of stones in a cold stream. It can rebuild its whole body from a scrap.","size":"Small, and as flat as a scrap of wet ribbon.","homes":["freshwater","ocean","forest"],"stats":{"length":0.0},"facts":[{"cat":"record","text":"Cut a planarian in half and you get two planarians. Cut it into pieces and every single piece can rebuild what it is missing — head, brain, gut, eyes, all of it.","more":"A fragment as small as one two-hundred-and-seventy-ninth of the worm has regrown into a complete animal. That is a speck you could lose on your fingertip. It rebuilds an entire body, front, back and middle, in about a week.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Roughly one in five cells in its body is a stem cell called a neoblast. A neoblast is a blank cell. It can turn into whatever part the worm needs next.","more":"Most animals keep a few stem cells tucked away in special places. A planarian carries them scattered right through its body. That is why a random scrap of it already contains everything needed to start over.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Split the head down the middle, but only partway, and each half finishes itself. The worm grows two heads and gets on with life using both.","more":"Scientists do this deliberately to study how a body knows which end is which. Each cut half rebuilds a full head, eyespots included, and the two-headed worm crawls around perfectly well.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Those two dark dots are eyespots called ocelli. They cannot make a picture — they only report how bright it is and roughly which direction the light is coming from.","more":"Each eyespot sits inside a cup of dark pigment that shades it from one side. By comparing the two, the worm works out where the light is and crawls the other way. The pigment cups are also what give planarians that permanently cross-eyed expression.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Its mouth is halfway down its belly, not on its head. It is also the only opening for food and waste — everything that goes in must come back out the same way.","more":"The gut branches through the body like a tree with no back door. Whatever cannot be digested gets pushed out through the mouth again.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"To eat, it shoves a muscular tube called a pharynx out through its belly, presses it into its food, and sucks. Dinner is drunk, never chewed.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"Mostly it glides. Millions of microscopic hairs called cilia row against a strip of slime the worm lays down, and it slides along like a tiny hovercraft.","more":"Watch one on glass and it looks like it is being towed by an invisible string. When it needs to hurry it adds waves of muscle to the rowing, and it can swim with the same rippling motion.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Some planarians make more planarians by gripping the ground with the tail, crawling forward with the front, and tearing themselves in two. Each half then regrows what it lost.","more":"The split is called fission. The back end holds fast while the front end pulls, and the worm rips apart at the waist. Within a few days there are two whole worms where there was one.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"Starve a planarian and it does not just get thin. It shrinks — the entire animal gets smaller while staying in proportion, then grows back when food returns.","more":"It is closer to resizing a photograph than to losing weight. The worm reduces the actual number of cells in its body, and builds them back once there is something to eat.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Turn over a stone in a cool, clean stream and check the wet underside. Planarians hate light, hide beneath rocks all day, and slide out to feed after dark.","more":"Two little flaps on the sides of the head, called auricles, feel the water and pick up chemicals drifting in it. That is how a planarian finds a dead insect from a distance in total darkness.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"It has a real brain — a small two-lobed knot of nerve cells behind the eyespots, with nerve cords running the length of the body. Remove the head, and the tail grows a new brain.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Every scrap of it carries the plan for all of it — nothing in that body is spare, and nothing is ever quite lost."},{"id":"velvet-worm","name":"Velvet worm","group":"worms","blurb":"A soft, stubby-legged night hunter of damp forest floors that catches its dinner by shooting glue at it.","size":"About as long as your finger — the biggest are nearly as long as your forearm.","homes":["rainforest","forest","cave"],"stats":{"length":0.2},"facts":[{"cat":"food","text":"It hunts with glue. Two nozzles either side of the mouth fire jets of sticky slime that stiffen in seconds, leaving the prey wrapped in a net it cannot break out of.","more":"The jets leave the nozzles at 3 to 5 meters per second (10 to 20 ft/s) — faster than you could snatch your hand back. Most shots are fired from about a centimeter away, though the longest squirts on record reach ten times that.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The nozzles whip side to side 30 to 60 times a second — about as fast as a hummingbird beats its wings — crossing the two streams into a tangled mesh. No muscle does the whipping.","more":"The nozzles are floppy tubes, and fluid shoved through a floppy tube fast enough starts to oscillate all by itself. It is the same physics as a garden hose dropped on the lawn while the water is still running.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Slime is expensive, so nothing is wasted. Once the meal is over, the velvet worm eats its own dried glue and recycles the protein.","more":"Refilling an emptied slime store takes about 24 days. A velvet worm that fires and misses has just lost most of a month's hunting equipment — which is exactly why it creeps so close before it shoots.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Its legs have no joints at all. Each one is a baggy cone held stiff by the fluid pumped inside it — a walking water balloon, tipped with a pair of small hard claws.","more":"Onychophora, the group's name, means claw bearer. Depending on the species there are anywhere from 13 to 43 pairs of these legs. They move in smooth traveling waves. The animal seems to flow rather than step.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"The whole family runs from 0.04 to 8.7 in (1 millimeter to 22 centimeters). The largest is longer than your outstretched hand.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Its breathing holes cannot close. Air comes in through pores that are permanently open. So a velvet worm loses water forty times faster than a caterpillar. It dies quickly in dry air.","more":"That one fact explains everywhere it lives: rotting logs, leaf litter, termite tunnels, damp cave walls. It comes out only at night, when the air is wet, and is back underground by morning.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"Some velvet worms live in groups of up to fifteen, usually relatives, and hunt together. At the kill the order is strict: top female first, then other females, then males, then the young.","more":"The group is led by a dominant female who wins her place by fighting. Outsiders who try to join get chased off. That is a surprisingly organized social life for an animal with no jointed legs and two tiny eyes.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Run a finger along one and it feels like dry velvet, not slime. The skin is covered in thousands of minute bumps called papillae, and water beads up and rolls off instead of soaking in.","more":"Each of those bumps is tipped with a sensory bristle. The surface that keeps the animal dry is also the surface it feels the world with. It reads the ground through the same skin that repels the rain.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Many velvet worms give birth to live young instead of laying eggs. In some species the pregnancy runs up to 15 months. That is longer than a human one, in an animal that fits in your palm.","more":"There is no larva and no dramatic change of shape along the way. What comes out is simply a miniature velvet worm, legs and claws already in place.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"It is nearly blind. Two small simple eyes on top of the head tell light from dark and little else. So it hunts with its antennae, sweeping the ground ahead until something moves.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"A velvet worm eats about once every one to four weeks. In between it does almost nothing at all — folded into a damp crack, waiting for the next warm, wet night.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Two soft nozzles, not a muscle between them, and the shape alone weaves the net."},{"id":"banana-slug","name":"Banana slug","group":"worms","blurb":"A big, bright yellow slug that mops up the floor of the Pacific coast forests. It eats what has fallen and turns it back into soil.","size":"As long as the banana it is named after — up to 9.8 in (25 cm).","homes":["forest","rainforest"],"stats":{"length":0.6,"life":4},"facts":[{"cat":"record","text":"It is the second-largest land slug in the world. It grows up to 9.8 in (25 cm) long and about 4.1 oz (115 grams).","more":"Only one slug grows bigger, a European species called the ash-black slug, which reaches about 12 in (30 cm). Almost everything you can see of a banana slug is muscle, water and mucus.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"speed","text":"Top speed is about 17 centimeters (6.5 in) a minute. At that pace, crossing a football field would take it roughly nine hours of continuous, unbroken effort.","more":"It is not being lazy. A slug moves by rippling one huge muscular foot along a laid-down strip of slime, and that costs a great deal of energy for very little distance.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Slime is the whole trick. Banana slug mucus can soak up a hundred times its own volume in water, so the slug travels inside a wet coat it manufactures and carries with it.","more":"The mucus is not quite a liquid and not quite a solid. It is stiff enough to hold its shape, but goes runny the moment the foot presses on it — which is what lets the slug both stick and slide.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"The slime is also a weapon. It carries a chemical that numbs the mouth of an animal that bites down, and it is sticky enough that the attacker often gives up mid-meal.","more":"Pick one up and you will meet the same problem. The slime is nearly impossible to rinse off with water. Rubbing your hands with dry soil works far better than the faucet.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Four tentacles, all retractable. The upper two carry the eyes; the lower two smell and taste the ground. Lose one and the slug simply grows a new one.","more":"The eyes pull back inside the way you push a finger down into a glove. They cannot resolve shapes, only light, dark and movement. Almost everything a banana slug knows about the world arrives through the lower pair.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It is the forest's cleanup crew. Dead leaves, moss, animal droppings and mushrooms all go in, and come out again as fertiliser spread across the forest floor.","more":"Banana slugs are especially keen on mushrooms, and they carry spores and seeds around the forest as they travel. A slug feeding in one damp hollow is quietly seeding the next one.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"It breathes through a hole in its right side. The opening is called a pneumostome, and if you watch closely you can see it slowly widen and close like a small round mouth.","more":"Behind that hole is a single simple lung. Everything else about a slug is soft and shifting and hard to find twice, but that one neat opening stays exactly where it should be.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"In a dry spell it burrows into the leaf litter. It seals itself inside a layer of mucus and shuts down completely. It waits there, sometimes for weeks, until the rain comes back.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Every banana slug is male and female at the same time. When two meet, both can go on to lay eggs — up to 75 of them, tucked into a damp log or a hollow under bark.","more":"Before mating they circle each other for hours, nipping as they go. The eggs are clear and pearly and are left entirely alone; no parent ever comes back to check on them.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"disguise","text":"The yellow is not fixed. The same slug can turn green, brown, tan, white, or blotched with black. It depends on what it has eaten, how damp it is and how much light it has had.","more":"Among wet fallen leaves, a yellow slug speckled with black is much harder to pick out than you would expect. Walk a redwood trail after rain and you will step over several before you spot one.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"It lives where the fog lives: the damp coastal forests running from southeastern Alaska down the coast to central California. No fog, no banana slugs.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"It has fans. The University of California, Santa Cruz sits in the middle of banana slug country. Students there made it the official school mascot, over far fiercer suggestions.","kind":"record"}],"wonder":"An animal made almost entirely of water and slime, and set down in a forest wet enough that it never has to dry out."},{"id":"garden-snail","name":"Garden snail","group":"worms","blurb":"The common brown snail you find under a flowerpot after rain. It is a soft-bodied animal that carries its own house on its back.","size":"Shell about 1 to 1.6 in (25 to 40 mm) across.","homes":["backyard","grassland","forest"],"stats":{"speed":0.005,"length":0.1,"life":3},"facts":[{"cat":"food","text":"Its tongue is a ribbon carrying well over ten thousand tiny teeth, called a radula. A snail does not bite a leaf. It rasps it, dragging the toothed belt across like sandpaper.","more":"Teeth at the front wear down and are replaced by fresh rows sliding forward from behind, over and over for the snail's whole life. In a very quiet room you can sometimes hear a big snail rasping away at a leaf.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"speed","text":"Snails have been timed while actually racing. Top speed is about 2 millimeters a second. Crossing a one-meter garden path takes roughly eight minutes of steady, unhurried work.","more":"You may see a much faster figure quoted — around 1.3 centimeters a second. Timed races do not back it up. A snail is even slower than its reputation, which is quite an achievement.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The shell is not a house it found lying around. It grew there, it is attached to the body, and it gets bigger as the snail does — one new curl at a time.","more":"An adult shell has four or five whorls. It is built from calcium carbonate, the same mineral as chalk. If a snail's food is short on calcium, it will eat soil to get what the shell needs.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"When it gets too dry or too cold, the snail withdraws and seals the doorway with a lid of dried mucus called an epiphragm. Then it waits — sometimes for months.","more":"Sealed in like that, and with its blood chemistry changed for winter, a garden snail can survive down to about minus 5 degrees C (23 F). Rain drumming on the shell is the signal to break the seal and come back out.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"A garden snail has an address. It feeds at night and returns to the very same crack or stone to rest, finding its way back partly by following slime trails.","more":"Mark a few with a dot of nail polish and you will keep finding the same individuals in the same spot night after night. Good resting crevices get shared, so one crack may hold a dozen snails.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"The two long tentacles carry the eyes on their tips. Touch one and it collapses inwards like a sock pulled inside out, then slowly pushes back out again.","more":"Those eyes cannot form a sharp picture — only light and shadow. The short lower pair does the real work, smelling and tasting the ground just ahead of the snail's head.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"It moves on one enormous muscular foot. Put a snail on a sheet of glass and look up from underneath. Waves of muscle ripple forward along the sole. Each wave shoves the snail a little further.","more":"The mucus underneath is thixotropic — solid until the foot presses on it, briefly runny, then solid again. So the snail is gluing and un-gluing itself to the ground, wave after wave, the entire way.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Startled, it snaps back into the shell. Then it blows out a froth of bubbly mucus around the opening. The froth is sticky, hard to grip, and thoroughly unpleasant to bite into.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Every garden snail is male and female at once. Two snails court for hours, then each fires a tiny chalky spike into the other — a love dart — before they mate.","more":"The dart is about 0.28 in (7 mm) long, and the snail cannot aim well: about a third of shots miss altogether or fail to go in. It is not a weapon. Its coating carries a substance that helps the sender's sperm survive inside the other snail.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Its waste opening is not at the back. A snail's body is twisted around inside its shell, so the opening ends up near the front, just inside the shell's mouth — right above its own head.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"One snail can lay around six batches of eggs in a single year, about fifty pearly white eggs a batch. That is why a single snail in a lettuce bed stops being a single snail rather quickly.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"It comes from around the Mediterranean and now lives almost everywhere with a mild climate. People carried it around the world in soil and in plant pots, and on purpose, as food.","kind":"record"}],"wonder":"A house that grows with its owner, built out of chalk the snail gathers from the very ground it crawls across."},{"id":"tardigrade","name":"Tardigrade","group":"tiny","blurb":"A microscopic eight-legged animal. It is small enough to live in a film of water on a scrap of moss. It can survive being dried to dust and brought back.","size":"About 0.02 in (0.5 mm) long — roughly the size of a period at the end of a sentence.","homes":["backyard","polar","ocean"],"stats":{"length":0.0},"facts":[{"cat":"size","text":"Half a millimeter long, and yet a complete working animal: eight legs, claws, muscles, a gut, a brain. The whole thing is built from only about a thousand cells.","more":"Your body holds trillions of cells. A tardigrade runs a mouth, a gut, a brain and eight legs on roughly a thousand — a number you could sit down and count in an afternoon.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"sleep","text":"Dried out, a tardigrade pulls in its legs, folds into a barrel shape called a tun, and stops. No feeding, no moving, almost no chemistry happening at all.","more":"The state is called cryptobiosis, meaning hidden life. A lump of Antarctic moss sat in a freezer at minus 20 C (minus 4 F) for more than thirty years. Then it was thawed and watered. Two tardigrades and one egg came back to life.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"As it dries, it fills its own cells with special proteins. Those proteins set into a glass-like solid. Every fragile part is held exactly in place until water comes back.","more":"Cells losing their water normally tear themselves apart, because everything inside collapses together and sticks. Nothing inside a tun can move at all, so nothing can break. The animal becomes, for a while, a piece of living glass.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"In the tun state, tardigrades have survived being chilled to minus 272 C (minus 458 F) — one degree above the coldest temperature there can possibly be. Short spells only.","more":"Heat is the opposite story. Warmth is the one thing a tardigrade is genuinely bad at. A hot afternoon on a dry wall is far more dangerous to it than a freezer ever was.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"A dose of radiation that would kill a person many times over barely troubles a tardigrade. They have come through roughly a thousand times what a human body can take.","more":"Part of the answer is a protein found in tardigrades and nowhere else. It is nicknamed Dsup, short for damage suppressor. It wraps around the animal's DNA like packing material. Far fewer strands snap when radiation goes through.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"A batch of dried tardigrades was carried into orbit and the container opened for ten days. Exposed to the raw vacuum of space, most of them came home alive.","more":"Adding the Sun's full ultraviolet, though, killed nearly all of them. That detail matters. Tardigrades are not indestructible. They are extraordinarily good at one specific thing, surviving without water. Ultraviolet light is a different problem entirely.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Tardigrades have been fired out of a gas gun. They survived hitting a target at about 900 meters per second (2,000 mph). That is roughly the speed of a rifle bullet.","more":"That impact squeezes them at something like ten times the crushing pressure at the bottom of the deepest ocean trench. Fired any faster and they do not survive. So this is a limit, not a superpower.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Here is the surprise. They do not live in extreme places on purpose. Most tardigrades live in a film of water on moss, lichen or leaf litter — including your own garden wall.","more":"Their real enemy is a sunny afternoon. When the moss dries out the water film vanishes and the tardigrade must shut down or die. The same shutdown that carries it through a dry Tuesday is what carries it through the cold and the vacuum.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It eats by stabbing. Two needle-sharp stylets punch through the wall of a moss cell, an algal cell, or another tiny animal, and the tardigrade sucks the contents out.","more":"The stylets are built from aragonite, the same mineral that makes seashells and pearls. They are not permanent either. Every time a tardigrade sheds its skin it loses the stylets too. It must grow a fresh pair before it can eat again.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"speed","text":"Its name means slow stepper, and it earns it. Filmed under a microscope, a tardigrade plods along using the same ordered leg pattern insects use, rather than moving its legs at random.","more":"It even adjusts. On a soft surface it slows down and takes short steps. On a hard surface it speeds up and lengthens its stride. A far larger animal makes the same choices crossing sand or stone.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Scoop up a handful of damp soil and you may be holding hundreds. Soil can hold 300,000 tardigrades in a square meter — and a square meter of moss over two million.","more":"That is more animals in one patch of moss than there are people in most cities. They turn up from Himalayan mountaintops to deep-sea mud to Antarctic ice, and on the wall outside your window.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Some tardigrade species lay their eggs inside the skin they have just shed, leaving them in a tidy little bag. Others lay eggs covered in spikes, cones and crowns, like microscopic sea mines.","kind":"found"}],"wonder":"Something so small it lives in a drop of water on a wall, and built to wait out anything that drop can do."},{"id":"rotifer","name":"Bdelloid rotifer","group":"tiny","blurb":"A complete animal with a brain, gut, jaws and muscles. All of it is built into a body far too small to see without a microscope.","size":"About the size of the dot on the letter i","homes":["freshwater","forest","backyard"],"stats":{"length":0.0},"wonder":"God built a whole animal — brain, jaws, muscles and all — into a body you would need a microscope to find.","facts":[{"cat":"build","text":"You need a microscope to find one. It is still a real animal. It has a mouth, a gut, a bladder, a brain and muscles. All of that is packed into less than a millimeter.","more":"Most things this small are single cells. A bdelloid is not. It is built from about a thousand cells, and here is the strange part — that number is fixed. It never grows new ones. The cells it hatches with are the cells it dies with; they only get bigger.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Its head ends in two rings of beating hairs called the corona. Under a lens they look exactly like two spinning wheels — but nothing is actually turning.","more":"The hairs, called cilia, beat one after another in a traveling ripple, the way a stadium crowd does a wave. Your eye reads the ripple as rotation. The wheels drag a current of water — and any bacteria floating in it — straight down into the mouth.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Dry one out completely and it does not die. It shrivels into a speck of dust, waits, and swells back to life when water returns. This is called anhydrobiosis.","more":"Rotifers have been brought back after nine years dried out. As they dry, their DNA snaps into pieces — and on rewetting they stitch it back together again. Add a drop of water to a pinch of dry moss and you can watch them uncurl and swim away.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"A bdelloid shrugs off radiation doses more than a hundred times what would kill a person. The best read is that this is the same trick that lets it survive drying out.","more":"Drying shatters DNA. So does radiation. An animal that can already glue its own broken genome back together barely notices the difference. It is one of the toughest creatures ever tested in a laboratory, and it is smaller than a grain of salt.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Nobody has ever found a male bdelloid rotifer. Every single one ever caught, anywhere in the world, is female, and each lays her eggs on her own.","more":"That is not for lack of looking. Bdelloids have been collected from ponds, gutters, roof moss and desert crusts on every continent, and the count of males still stands at zero. Each mother's eggs hatch into daughters that are copies of her.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Behind its mouth sits a mastax. That is a muscular grinder holding hard jaws called trophi. The jaws chew bacteria and algae into paste before swallowing.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Up to a tenth of a bdelloid's genes match bacteria, fungi and plants rather than anything animal. Nobody has watched one take a gene in; the match in the DNA is the argument.","more":"This is called horizontal gene transfer. Bdelloids carry more borrowed genes than almost any other animal. Some of them are working genes, too. They are recipes for enzymes no other animal has, switched on and running inside a rotifer.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"travel","text":"A dried rotifer is basically dust, and the best read of how they turn up everywhere is that the wind carries them. A speck that lands in a rain puddle on your driveway can uncurl and be feeding again within hours.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"home","text":"Look in the green moss on a wall, a roof gutter, or the damp grit in a plant pot. Squeeze the moss into a drop of water and they are there.","more":"Moss is a rotifer city. It holds water after rain, bakes dry in the sun, then wets again — a rhythm that would kill most animals and suits a bdelloid perfectly. A single pinch of roof moss can hold dozens of them.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"speed","text":"Bdelloid means leech-like, and that is how they usually travel: gripping with the head, arching the body, reaching forward again, exactly like an inchworm. They can swim too, using the corona.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Many bdelloids carry a pair of red eyespots on the snout. They cannot form a picture — they only tell light from dark, which is enough to know whether you are out in the open.","kind":"found"}]},{"id":"amoeba","name":"Amoeba","group":"tiny","blurb":"A single living cell with no fixed shape at all — it simply flows into whatever shape it needs next.","size":"A big one is about half a millimeter — just barely a visible speck","homes":["freshwater","backyard"],"stats":{"length":0.0},"wonder":"One cell, and God left nothing out of it: it hunts, builds, bails out water and makes more of itself, without a single organ.","facts":[{"cat":"build","text":"An amoeba has no front, no back and no fixed shape. It pushes out a bulge of itself called a pseudopod, pours its insides into it, and that is how it travels.","more":"The name comes from a Greek word meaning change. Its insides are part stiff jelly and part runny liquid, and it switches between the two on demand — stiffening to make a wall, softening to flow. Watch one for a minute and it is never the same shape twice.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"It has no mouth. It simply flows around its dinner until the dinner is inside it, two arms of cell meeting on the far side and sealing shut. This is phagocytosis.","more":"The trapped meal ends up in a bubble called a food vacuole. Enzymes take it apart in there. Amoebae eat algae, bacteria and other single cells. A captured paramecium can go on thrashing inside that bubble for a minute or two before it stops.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Fresh water leaks into an amoeba non-stop, by osmosis. If it did nothing it would swell and burst — so it bails, filling a bubble with the extra water and squirting it out.","more":"That bubble is the contractile vacuole. Under a microscope you can time it: swelling slowly, then collapsing in an instant, over and over, for the whole life of the cell. It is a pump that never once gets to stop.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"A relative called Chaos can reach 0.2 in (5 mm). That is a single cell you can spot on the pond floor with no microscope at all.","more":"Most are smaller, between one and three millimeters. And Chaos does not have one nucleus the way most cells do. It has hundreds, sometimes as many as a thousand, scattered all through it — one single cell running on a whole crowd of control centers.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"The water dries up, or the food runs out. Many kinds of amoeba then roll into a ball and build a tough wall around the outside. They wait inside this cyst until things improve.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"An amoeba does not have babies. It becomes two. The cell pinches in at the middle, pulls apart, and where there was one amoeba there are now two, each a complete one.","more":"This is called binary fission. The nucleus copies itself first, so both halves get a full set of instructions. The two halves stay joined by a thinning thread of cytoplasm that stretches and stretches until it finally snaps.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Some amoebae build houses. They glue sand grains, or tiny plates they make themselves, into a shell with a single doorway, then stick their arms out through it.","more":"These are called testate amoebae. Difflugia picks sand grains out of the mud and cements them into a neat flask — and it is fussy about grain size. A single cell, with no eyes and no hands, sorting building material.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"It has no eyes, no nose and no nerves, but it still finds food. It senses the chemicals leaking out of its prey and grows its pseudopods toward the strongest trace.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"An amoeba drinks as well as eating solid food. It puckers tiny channels inward and swallows droplets of water whole. The habit is called pinocytosis, which means cell-drinking.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Scoop the brown gunk off the bottom of a pond, not the clear water above it. Amoebae live down in the ooze among rotting leaves, where the bacteria they hunt are thickest.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"speed","text":"Amoebae are in no hurry. A big one crosses a few millimeters in an hour, which makes the width of your fingernail most of a morning's travel.","kind":"found"}]},{"id":"paramecium","name":"Paramecium","group":"tiny","blurb":"A single cell shaped like a slipper that swims by rowing with thousands of tiny beating hairs.","size":"About the size of the dot on a letter i","homes":["freshwater","backyard"],"stats":{"length":0.0},"wonder":"No brain, no nerves, no eyes — and it still backs up, turns, and tries again. God fitted the whole instruction set inside one cell.","facts":[{"cat":"speed","text":"It is covered in thousands of hairs called cilia, and they all row together. A paramecium crosses about a millimeter every second. That is four of its own body lengths.","more":"Four body lengths a second is a serious sprint. For you, that would be four of your own body lengths every second. The cilia never all beat at once; they beat in traveling waves, so the cell is always being pushed and never coasting.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"For a single cell it is a giant. Line up a hundred of the bacteria it eats and you have the length of one paramecium. Hold a jar to the light and it shows as a white speck.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"It does not swim in a straight line. It spirals, turning slowly around its own long axis the entire way, like a rifle bullet or a well-thrown football.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Bump a paramecium into an obstacle and it does something clever: every hair reverses, it backs up, turns slightly, and tries forward again. It repeats this until it gets past.","more":"This is called the avoidance reaction, and it is the whole of a paramecium's problem-solving. It has no brain and no nerves. The bump changes the electricity across its skin, the cilia flip direction, and back it goes — a reflex built into one single cell.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Under its skin sit hundreds of loaded darts called trichocysts. Threaten it and they fire at once, shooting out long threads that swell into a spiky halo around the cell.","more":"The threads are not poisonous. They work by shoving — a predator gets a face full of them and the paramecium jets away through the gap. Fire them and they are gone; the cell has to build a fresh set from scratch.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"A groove runs down one side, lined with more cilia that sweep bacteria into it like a conveyor belt. At the bottom the food is wrapped in a bubble and digested.","more":"Those bubbles are food vacuoles, and each one takes a tour. It is carried along a set path around the inside of the cell. Enzymes work on it as it goes. The leftovers are dumped out through one fixed spot in the skin, a permanent back door.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"A paramecium keeps two kinds of nucleus. The big one, the macronucleus, runs the daily work. The small one is held in reserve and used only when two cells meet.","more":"The macronucleus holds many copies of the instructions — it is the working, dog-eared reference book. The micronucleus is the pristine master copy, locked in a drawer. Damage the working copy and the cell can print a fresh one from the master.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Two paramecia can stick together side by side. They open a channel between them. They swap the contents of their small nucleus. Then they part and carry on separately.","more":"This is called conjugation, and it is not how they make new cells — they do that by simply splitting in two. Conjugation makes no babies at all. Two cells go in and two cells come out, each carrying a mixed set of instructions it did not have before.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Fresh water floods in constantly. A paramecium runs two pumps, each with canals spreading out like the spokes of a star, collecting the extra water and firing it back outside.","more":"You can watch a star fill and vanish, fill and vanish, several times a minute. Move the same cell into saltier water and the pumping slows right down — less water is pushing in, so there is less to throw back out.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Look in still water that has turned cloudy and smells faintly bad. A jar of pond water with a few dead leaves in it will be swarming within about a week.","more":"The leaves rot, the bacteria bloom, and the paramecia arrive to eat the bacteria. This is the classic way to grow them on purpose. Hay in water works just as well, which is why the mixture has its own name: a hay infusion.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"One kind, Paramecium bursaria, is bright green. Hundreds of tiny algae live inside it, making sugar from sunlight and handing it over. The best read is that the algae get a safe ride in return.","more":"It is a straight trade. The algae get shelter and a supply of carbon dioxide; the paramecium gets fed even when there are no bacteria about. Keep one in the dark long enough, though, and it loses its green.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"food","text":"Paramecia get hunted too. A barrel-shaped cell called Didinium rams into one and harpoons it. Then it swallows a paramecium bigger than itself, stretching open like a snake.","more":"Didinium fires out barbed threads to grip its prey, then opens a mouth that expands far beyond its normal size. Swallowing can take several minutes. It is one of the classic sights in a drop of pond water: one single cell eating another whole.","kind":"found"}]},{"id":"diatom","name":"Diatom","group":"tiny","blurb":"A single-celled drifter that makes food from sunlight and lives inside a shell made of real glass.","size":"Far too small to see without a microscope","homes":["ocean","freshwater","polar"],"stats":{"length":0.0},"wonder":"Twelve thousand kinds named so far, and God gave every one of them a different pattern in the glass.","facts":[{"cat":"build","text":"A diatom lives inside a shell it makes out of silica — the same mineral as sand and window glass. It is called a frustule, and it is a glass house of the cell's own making.","more":"The cell pulls dissolved silicon out of the water and lays it down in a shell so finely patterned that no two kinds look alike. Ribs, pores, spikes, stars, triangles, spirals. Microscopists arrange them on glass slides like jewels, and people still do it today.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The shell comes in two halves that fit together like a box with its lid. The top piece overlaps the bottom piece exactly the way a shoebox does.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Here is the odd part. When a diatom divides, each new cell keeps one old half and builds the new half inside it. So the next one is slightly smaller. And the next.","more":"A line of diatoms shrinks a little with every single split, until they reach a lower limit. Then one cell escapes its shell completely, swells back to full size as a bare cell called an auxospore, and builds a brand-new large shell. Then the shrinking starts over.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Diatoms make oxygen from sunlight, the way a leaf does. There are so many of them in the sea that, on the best estimate, roughly one breath in every five you take was made by a diatom.","more":"They are not plants and they are not seaweed — they are single cells drifting near the surface. When conditions are right they multiply into blooms so vast that the ocean changes color, and the swirling pattern can be photographed from space.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"travel","text":"Long boat-shaped diatoms can move. A slit called a raphe runs down the shell, and the cell squeezes a sticky ribbon out through it, gripping the ground and hauling itself along.","more":"You can find their tracks. On wet mud at low tide, diatoms glide up toward the light during the day. They slide back down into the mud before the water returns. Faint trails are left behind them in the surface film.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Some diatoms live inside sea ice, in the salty channels running through it. So many pack in that the underside of the ice turns brown.","more":"That brown layer feeds the whole polar food chain. Krill scrape it off the ice from below to graze on it, and whales eat the krill. When the ice melts in spring, the released diatoms bloom in open water and the whole system switches on at once.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"copied","text":"Chalky white diatomaceous earth is nothing but heaped-up diatom shells. It goes into swimming pool filters, cat litter, and is used as a gentle scrubbing powder.","more":"Alfred Nobel used it too. Nitroglycerine is dangerously twitchy on its own. Soak it into diatomaceous earth and it becomes a stable paste you can carry around safely. That paste is dynamite, held together by microscopic glass shells.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"defence","text":"A glass box sounds fragile. It is not. Diatom shells resist crushing so well that engineers study them, hunting for ways to build strong things that are almost entirely empty space.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"Most diatoms are about the width of a hair. The largest reach a millimeter or two. That is a single cell you could actually pick out with your own eye.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"More than twelve thousand kinds have been named, and each is told apart by its shell pattern alone. Circles, needles, boats, chains, stars, fans, crescents — all of them in glass.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"A diatom needs three things: sunlight, carbon dioxide, and dissolved silicon for its shell. Run the silicon out and a bloom stops dead, however much light and food is left.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"Many diatoms link into chains, ribbons and zigzags, cell joined to cell at the corners. The best read is that a long chain is far harder for a small grazer to fit into its mouth.","kind":"worked"}]},{"id":"dust-mite","name":"Dust mite","group":"tiny","blurb":"A tiny eight-legged cousin of spiders that lives in your mattress and eats the flakes of dead skin you shed.","size":"Smaller than the period at the end of this sentence","homes":["backyard"],"stats":{"length":0.0,"life":0.2},"wonder":"Even the skin you shed and never think about is somebody's daily bread. In God's world nothing is left over.","facts":[{"cat":"home","text":"Right now there are dust mites in your mattress, your pillow, your carpet and your sofa. Not because your house is dirty — they are in almost every house that has people in it.","more":"They want three things: warmth, damp air, and dead skin. A bed supplies all three, every night. This is not a sign that anything is wrong. Homes scrubbed absolutely spotless still have them, because the thing they are eating is you.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"You shed dead skin constantly without ever noticing, and it settles into your bedding. That is the dust mite's entire diet. Your bed is its dinner table.","more":"It struggles with a fresh dry flake. Dust mites do better on skin that mold has already softened up, so they favor the damper corners of a mattress. In effect, the mold does the cooking and the mite eats the meal.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Here is the good news. A dust mite cannot bite you and has no interest in your blood. It has no idea you exist. It only wants the flakes you leave behind.","more":"This matters, because dust mites get blamed for bites they are physically unable to make. Their mouthparts are built for scraping and chewing dry crumbs, not for piercing skin. If something bit you in bed, it was not a dust mite.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"gross","text":"Dust mites do not make people ill by touching them. It is their droppings. One mite makes about two thousand of them in a ten-week life, each smaller than the width of a hair.","more":"Every pellet is coated in digestive enzymes. Those proteins are called allergens, and some people's bodies react to them. Thump a pillow and the pellets lift into the air. Breathe them in and they set off sneezing, itching or asthma.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"A dust mite never drinks. It cannot. It pulls water vapor straight out of the air using a salty fluid it secretes near its front legs, which soaks up damp like a sponge.","more":"That is why they need humid air. Dry a room out and the mites dry out with it and die — no amount of skin flakes can save them. It is also why they are scarce in deserts and rare in houses high up in the mountains.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"It is about a quarter of a millimeter long, smaller than a printed period. You could line up eighty of them across the width of your thumbnail.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Count the legs and it gives itself away: eight. A dust mite is not an insect at all. It is an arachnid, a cousin of spiders, scorpions and ticks.","more":"It also has no eyes and no antennae. The body is a single see-through bag with legs on it — you can look straight through the shell at the gut inside. It navigates entirely by touch and by chemicals, feeling its way through the dust.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A female lives about ten weeks and lays sixty to a hundred eggs. The young hatch with only six legs, and grow the last pair when they moult.","more":"Sixty eggs is a modest number by insect standards, but it is plenty. Ten weeks is long enough for generations to overlap. Inside a mattress the temperature barely shifts and the air stays damp. Fresh food arrives every single night.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"A single gram of mattress dust — about a pinch — commonly holds a hundred mites or more. Allergy doctors use roughly that level as the mark where a house starts causing trouble.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Sprays do very little. Heat and dryness are what work: a wash hotter than about 55°C (130°F) kills them, and so does a spell in a hot tumble dryer.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"travel","text":"Dust mites cannot fly or jump, and they mostly stay put. Nobody has tracked one moving house — the best read is that they ride along on clothes, bedding and second-hand furniture.","kind":"worked"}]},{"id":"tyrannosaurus","name":"Tyrannosaurus rex","group":"dinosaurs","blurb":"The biggest meat-eating dinosaur known from North America. It was built around a five-foot skull and a bone-crushing bite.","size":"About 40 ft (12 m) nose to tail, and 12 ft (3.7 m) tall at the hips.","homes":["forest","freshwater"],"stats":{"length":40,"weight":17000},"wonder":"Nothing about that skull is accidental — the wide-set eyes, the arched jaw and the thick teeth all solve the same problem together.","facts":[{"cat":"bones","text":"Sue, at the Field Museum in Chicago, is the most complete T. rex found: about 250 of the roughly 380 bones a T. rex has. Susan Hendrickson spotted the first bones sticking out of a South Dakota cliff in August 1990.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"bones","text":"Scotty may be the biggest one. The bones came out of the ground near Eastend, Saskatchewan. They are kept at the Royal Saskatchewan Museum. Worked out from the leg and hip bones, Scotty comes to about 19,500 lb (8,870 kg) and 43 ft (13 m) long. That is a shade more than Sue. The two are close enough that figures worked out this way cannot separate them.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"bones","text":"A Montana rancher named Kathy Wankel found T. rex arm bones in 1988 — the first complete T. rex arm anybody had ever seen. Diggers went back and recovered most of the animal, and it now stands in the Smithsonian in Washington, D.C.","more":"Roughly 80 to 85 percent of that skeleton came out of the ground, skull included, which makes it one of the most complete T. rex specimens known. It went to the Smithsonian on a long loan and is nicknamed the Nation's T. rex.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"build","text":"The arms were only about 3 ft (1 m) long, which looks ridiculous on a 40-ft animal. They were not floppy flaps. The muscle scars on the arm bones are broad and deep, and one study figures each arm could curl about 440 lb (200 kg) — using just one arm muscle.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"food","text":"T. rex teeth were not blades. They were thick pegs. The largest one ever found is 12 in (30.5 cm) long, counting the root buried in the jaw. Only about 4 in of that showed above the gum. A tooth shaped like that is built for crunching, not slicing.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"A fossil dropping from Saskatchewan, studied by Karen Chin, is 17 in (44 cm) long and weighs 15 lb (7.1 kg). Between a third and half of it is crushed bone. Whatever left it did not spit the bones out.","more":"It was the first dropping ever confirmed from a meat-eating dinosaur, and more than twice the size of any other. Living meat-eaters mostly avoid swallowing bone. This animal bit through it and ran the pieces straight through its gut.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"The skull is pinched narrow at the snout and very wide at the back, so both eyes pointed forward at the same thing. That overlap is what gives you depth — the thing you lose when you cover one eye and try to catch a ball.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"record","text":"Karl Bates and Peter Falkingham built a three-dimensional model of the skull. They hung the jaw muscles on it and ran the numbers. The bite at the rear teeth came to nearly 12,800 lb of force. No living land animal comes anywhere close.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"speed","text":"Only two footprints anywhere are confidently T. rex. One of them is 33 in (83 cm) long. Geologist Charles Pillmore found it in 1983 at Philmont Scout Ranch in New Mexico. There was about 6 ft between one step and the next.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Counting growth rings inside the leg bones, Gregory Erickson's team worked out that a teenage T. rex packed on roughly 1,300 lb (600 kg) a year through its fastest stretch. That is a bit over 3 lb a day, every day, for four years.","more":"Bone lays down rings a bit like a tree. They are thick in a good year and thin in a lean one. Slice a leg bone across and you can count how old the animal was. The size of the bone then shows how fast it was piling on weight at each age.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"created","text":"Day six of the creation week covered the beasts of the earth — the land animals. T. rex is one, a walker. A track confidently matched to it, 33 in (83 cm) long, was pressed into New Mexico ground, about 6 ft from the next.","more":"Beasts of the earth are the land-walkers, and nothing about this one belongs in water or air. Hips 12 ft (3.7 m) off the ground, 40 ft (12 m) of body balanced over two feet, and a five-foot skull carried out in front.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"created","text":"T. rex and its close look-alikes — the other heavy-skulled, short-armed tyrannosaurs — sort into one created kind. Inside a kind, sizes shift: Scotty's leg and hip bones work out to 43 ft (13 m) and 19,500 lb (8,870 kg), a shade more than Sue. The equipment does not shift — thick peg teeth, 3-ft arms, forward-facing eyes.","more":"Counting growth rings in the leg bones works out to a young T. rex adding roughly 1,300 lb (600 kg) a year. A heavier adult is more of the same animal, not a different one. The parts list stays the same length; only the measurements move.","kind":"worked"}],"art":1},{"id":"triceratops","name":"Triceratops","group":"dinosaurs","blurb":"A three-horned plant-eater with a solid bone frill. Its skull is one of the largest of any land animal ever found.","size":"About 28 ft (8.5 m) long, and the skull alone can reach 8 ft — nearly a third of the animal is head.","homes":["forest","freshwater"],"stats":{"length":28,"weight":18000},"wonder":"A head that heavy only works if the neck, shoulders and legs were built to carry it in advance — and they were.","facts":[{"cat":"bones","text":"Big John, dug out of Hell Creek rock in South Dakota in 2014, has the biggest Triceratops skull known. About a quarter of it was missing, so the 8 ft 7 in (2.62 m) length is measured on a restored skull. Guinness lists Big John as the largest Triceratops skeleton documented.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"bones","text":"John Bell Hatcher collected thirty-one Triceratops skulls in Wyoming between 1889 and 1891. The Smithsonian's mounted Triceratops is nicknamed Hatcher after him. It is built from more than one animal. No single skeleton was complete enough on its own.","more":"Composite mounts are normal and museums say so on the label. Two of the specimens used are catalogued USNM 2100 and USNM 4842. The alternative would have been no Triceratops on display at all for a century.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"bones","text":"The first Triceratops fossil to get a name was a pair of brow horns found near Denver in 1887. O. C. Marsh took them for a huge bison and called it Bison alticornis. John Bell Hatcher dug up a proper horned skull in 1888, and in 1889 Marsh corrected himself.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"defence","text":"One Triceratops skull dug up in Montana in 1997 has tooth marks in a brow horn and the frill bone behind it, matching a big tyrannosaur's teeth. Bone had grown over the wounds, so this one survived and lived on for years. The outer third of the horn is gone — bitten off, is the reading — and a snapped horn never grows back.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"build","text":"The frill is solid bone with no big openings in it. Most other horned dinosaurs have two large windows in the frill. Holes that size take a lot of weight out of the bone. Torosaurus is the obvious example. Triceratops carried the extra bone instead.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Its teeth sat in stacked columns — 36 to 40 columns on each side of each jaw, 3 to 5 teeth deep. That works out to somewhere between 432 and 800 teeth in one head, with only the top tooth in each column doing any work.","more":"As the working tooth wore down, the one underneath moved up into its place. The animal never ran out. The wear on the teeth shows the jaws sheared straight up and down. They did not grind sideways. Tough plants got sliced like paper in scissors.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The front of the mouth is a toothless beak, built on a bone called the rostral that only horned dinosaurs have. A beak like that can nip but not chew, so the beak clipped the plant off and the tooth columns further back did the cutting up.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"Most horned dinosaurs turn up in bone beds, dozens or hundreds together. Triceratops almost always turns up alone. One Triceratops bone bed is documented, in southeastern Montana, holding three young ones. A group of three was found near Newcastle, Wyoming in 2012.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Triceratops bones come out of the Hell Creek Formation and rocks like it, across Montana, the Dakotas and Wyoming. Read from the way those rocks were laid down, that country was river floodplains and low inland forest — the same rock, and the same country, that produces T. rex.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Torosaurus has a two-windowed frill. Some researchers argue it is really just a fully grown Triceratops whose frill kept changing as it aged. Other researchers disagree. The argument is genuinely unsettled, and both camps are looking at the same bones.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"created","text":"The Roman writer Pliny described the catoblepas, a heavy beast that walked with its head down because it could not lift it. Medieval bestiaries copied him and drew it horned and thick-necked. Triceratops carried an 8 ft 7 in skull with three horns on the front.","more":"O. C. Marsh did the same thing with the first pair of brow horns from near Denver — he filed them under bison. Faced with a horned skull, people reach for the horned animal they already know and describe the rest as extra.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"created","text":"Triceratops ran on bulk — 18,000 lb of animal, worked out from the thickness of its limb bones — fed by a beak and hundreds of stacked teeth. The world after the Flood was colder and drier, and the reading is that its river forest thinned with it. An animal with a 6 ft 6 in head is not hard to track.","kind":"worked"}],"art":1},{"id":"stegosaurus","name":"Stegosaurus","group":"dinosaurs","blurb":"A plated plant-eater with a spiked tail and a dog-sized brain. Nobody could mistake that back for anything else.","size":"About 21 ft (6.5 m) nose to tail, and roughly 7,700 lb (3.5 tonnes).","homes":["forest","freshwater"],"stats":{"length":21,"weight":7700},"wonder":"Two rows of plates that deliberately do not line up, on an animal that was never asked to justify them.","facts":[{"cat":"bones","text":"Sophie, at the Natural History Museum in London, is the most complete Stegosaurus known: 360 bones, about 85% of the skeleton. Bob Simon found it at Red Canyon Ranch in Wyoming in 2003. It is 19 ft (5.8 m) long and 9 ft 6 in tall.","more":"Most fossil skeletons get squashed flat in the rock. Sophie's bones kept their real three-dimensional shape, so researchers could scan every one and build an accurate model. That model gave a weight of about 3,500 lb (1.6 tonnes) — worked out from body volume, not from a scale.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"bones","text":"Apex, dug up in Colorado in 2022, has about 80% of its 320 bones and is 27 ft (8.2 m) nose to tail. It sold at auction for $44.6 million, and it is now on loan to the American Museum of Natural History in New York.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Stegosaurus did not have a second brain in its hips. There is a wide space in the spine there, big enough for something about twenty times the size of the brain — but living birds have one too. In birds it holds a glycogen body, whose job nobody has pinned down. Not a spare brain.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"defence","text":"The four tail spikes run 2 to 3 ft (60–90 cm) long and stuck out sideways, not straight up. Around one in ten spike tips that get found are broken and then re-healed, which is what you expect from equipment that actually got used.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"bones","text":"A tail bone from an Allosaurus, catalogued UMNH 10781, has a puncture hole in it. The hole matches a Stegosaurus tail spike in size and shape. The edges of the hole had begun to heal. So the Allosaurus survived being stabbed.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"For years the plates were explained as radiators for shedding heat. Nobody dug up a job description. The display idea comes from the plates themselves. They are big, thin and set side-on. That makes the animal look taller and broader to a rival or an enemy. That now looks like the main job. Cooling may have been a side benefit.","more":"For years the plates were explained as radiators for shedding heat. Most researchers now think the main job was display — making the animal look taller and broader from the side, to rivals and to enemies. Cooling may have been a side benefit.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Marsh named it in 1877 believing the plates lay flat across the back and overlapped like roof shingles. That is exactly what the name means: roofed lizard. The plates were later stood upright, and the name stayed put.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"weird","text":"The cluster of tail spikes has a real name that scientists actually use: the thagomizer. It started as a joke in a Far Side cartoon in 1982. Ken Carpenter picked it up at a paleontology conference in 1993, and nobody has produced a better word since.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"speed","text":"The front legs are much shorter than the back legs, which tips the whole animal nose-down toward the ground. Worked out from the leg bones, its fastest walk came to somewhere around 10 mph (16 km/h). A brisk jog, not a sprint.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"food","text":"The teeth are small and leaf-shaped, set well back in the jaws behind a horny beak. Its bite was long thought feeble. A 3D study of Sophie's own skull put it in the range of a sheep or a cow. That is strong enough for tough, woody plants like cycads, not just soft ferns.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"created","text":"The first man was set to naming the animals, and people never stopped. Marsh called this one roofed lizard in 1877, for plates he thought lay flat like shingles. They stood upright instead. The name stayed. Diggers went on naming the skeletons too: Sophie, Apex.","more":"The tail spikes had no name at all until a cartoonist supplied thagomizer in 1982. Ken Carpenter put it in print in 1993 and it held. A name sticks when people find it useful, whether or not it is accurate. Roofed lizard has no roof.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"created","text":"Made on day six, with the land animals. Its front legs are much shorter than the back, so the head rides low. Down there are ferns and horsetails. That is the food a low head and a horny beak point to. The green plants had been standing since day three. The food was ready before the mouth was.","more":"Day six covers the cattle, the creeping things and the beasts of the earth. Stegosaurus fits. Four feet on the ground and a beak at the front. Small leaf-shaped teeth set well back. The shape of the skull works out to a jaw too weak for anything but soft low growth.","kind":"record"}],"art":1},{"id":"brachiosaurus","name":"Brachiosaurus","group":"dinosaurs","blurb":"A long-necked plant-eater built like a giraffe, with front legs longer than its back legs.","size":"Around 70 ft (21 m) long and tall enough to look in a third-floor window.","homes":["forest"],"stats":{"length":70,"weight":70000},"wonder":"Front legs longer than back legs is backwards for a dinosaur — unless reaching high was the point from the start.","facts":[{"cat":"bones","text":"Harold Menke found the skeleton that started it all near Fruita, Colorado, on 4 July 1900. It is specimen FMNH P 25107 at the Field Museum in Chicago. There is one arm bone, a thigh bone, a hip, a shoulder, part of the backbone and some ribs.","more":"Elmer Riggs named it Brachiosaurus altithorax in 1903. Brachiosaurus means 'arm lizard', because the upper arm bone was longer than the thigh bone — backwards from every other long-neck known at the time. The Colorado hill it came out of is now called Riggs Hill.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"bones","text":"That upper arm bone measures 6 ft 8 in (204 cm) today, and part of its lower end eroded away — whole, it was probably nearer 7 ft 1 in (216 cm). Either way, that is one bone, not the whole front leg. On you, the matching bone runs from shoulder to elbow.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"bones","text":"No complete Brachiosaurus skull has ever been found. The best there is, a partial skull from Colorado, works out to roughly 2 ft 4 in (70 cm) long. So the head on any Brachiosaurus model is partly a careful reconstruction.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Brachiosaurus did not stand on a lake bottom breathing through the nostrils on top of its head. In 1951 K. A. Kermack pointed out that with the lungs 20 ft under, water pressure would squeeze the chest shut. It could not have drawn a single breath.","more":"The old swamp pictures came from a second wrong idea. An animal this heavy supposedly could not carry itself on land and needed water to hold it up. The limb bones say otherwise. They are pillar-straight, stacked vertically, and built for walking.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"build","text":"The shoulders sat much higher than the hips, so the back sloped downhill from front to rear like a giraffe's. Nearly every other big long-neck did the opposite, with the hips high and the shoulders low.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"For most of a century that skeleton was labeled Brachiosaurus brancai. Gregory Paul had already floated the name Giraffatitan in 1988. Then in 2009 Michael Taylor listed twenty-six differences between its bones and the Colorado animal's. That settled it. The Berlin giant now keeps a name of its own.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"size","text":"Nobody ever weighed one. Published estimates run from about 62,000 lb to over 100,000 lb (28–47 tonnes), worked out from the thickness of the limb bones and from three-dimensional models of the body. The range is that wide because every figure starts from bones.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"food","text":"The teeth are broad spoon-shaped croppers. They run well back along both jaws, not just at the front. Teeth built like that point to snipping and slicing leaves off with no chewing at all. Food went down close to whole. An enormous gut did the rest of the work.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"build","text":"The neck bones are hollowed out with air spaces, the same trick a bird's skeleton uses. Solid bone at that size works out too heavy for the animal to raise. The air spaces look like the only way a neck that long gets lifted at all.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"home","text":"The bones come out of the Morrison Formation, which stretches under Colorado, Utah and Wyoming. Those same rocks hold fossil conifers and ferns. Working back from the rock layers and the plants in them, the place comes out as river channels and wide floodplains with a hard dry season every year.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"created","text":"Job 40 describes behemoth: a plant-eater with a tail like a cedar and bones like tubes of bronze. Brachiosaurus's neck bones really are tubes, hollowed out with air spaces, and its chisel teeth point to stripping leaves off whole. Nothing living answers that description. A 70 ft long-neck does.","more":"Leviathan, in the chapter after, is the sea creature; behemoth is the land one. Brachiosaurus fits the land side. Its limb bones are pillar-straight and stacked vertically, built for walking, not for standing on a lake bottom the way the old museum paintings showed it.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"created","text":"The brass on Bishop Richard Bell's tomb has a border of animals. The tomb is in Carlisle Cathedral, England. Among them are four-legged creatures with long necks, long tails and very small heads. Brachiosaurus carried a skull of roughly 2 ft 4 in on a 70 ft body.","more":"Babylon's Ishtar Gate does much the same in glazed brick. There are rows of lions and aurochs. Walking between them is the sirrush: four legs, a long neck, a long tail. It is set among animals anyone in the city could go out and look at.","kind":"record"}],"art":1},{"id":"apatosaurus","name":"Apatosaurus","group":"dinosaurs","blurb":"A heavy long-necked plant-eater from the Morrison rocks. Most people still call it Brontosaurus.","size":"About 72 ft (22 m) long, most of it neck and tail, and as heavy as three or four elephants.","homes":["freshwater","forest"],"stats":{"length":72,"weight":40000},"wonder":"Fifteen neck bones, each hollowed out to the last safe ounce, holding up something no engineer would have dared to draw.","facts":[{"cat":"bones","text":"Earl Douglass found the best Apatosaurus skeleton in Utah in 1909. It is catalogued CM 3018. It is mounted at the Carnegie Museum of Natural History in Pittsburgh. The species name louisae honors Louise Carnegie.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"bones","text":"The quarry Douglass opened is now Dinosaur National Monument. About 1,500 bones were deliberately left in the rock face. A building went up over the top. You can walk right up to a wall of dinosaur bones, still half-buried where they lay.","more":"Douglass worked that quarry for fifteen years. He pulled out close to twenty good skeletons. Diplodocus, Stegosaurus, Camarasaurus, Barosaurus and more. Leaving the last wall in place was the better idea. It is in Utah, near Jensen, and it is open to visitors.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Marsh named Apatosaurus in 1877 and Brontosaurus in 1879. In 1903 Elmer Riggs decided they were the same animal, and by the rules the older name wins — so Brontosaurus was struck off. In 2015 three researchers argued the differences are real after all. Still argued about.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"bones","text":"For decades museums put the wrong head on it. No skull was mounted with the body, so a boxy Camarasaurus-style skull got used instead. The Carnegie Museum finally fitted the correct long, low skull on 20 October 1979.","more":"The right skull had been lying a short distance from CM 3018 since 1909, and museum director W. J. Holland said at the time that it belonged. Henry Fairfield Osborn disagreed, Osborn's view won, and it took McIntosh and Berman in the 1970s to prove Holland had been right all along.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"build","text":"It has fifteen neck bones, and none of them are solid. Air spaces run right through them, the same way a bird's bones are built. Hollow is how you hold a neck like that up without breaking your own spine.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The neck bones carry long rib-like struts underneath and a deep split trough along the top, which probably held a thick cable of muscle and tendon. So the neck was wide and deep in life, not slim like a swan's.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"defence","text":"The tail tapers into a string of tiny bones, like the lash of a bullwhip. In 1997 Nathan Myhrvold and Philip Currie modelled a crack over 200 decibels — cannon-loud. A 2022 team redid the sums: the tip tops out near 70 mph (110 km/h), and the skin would tear long before it broke the sound barrier.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"food","text":"The teeth are pegs, and only at the front of the mouth. That shape points to raking leaves off a branch. In Diplodocus, a long-neck from the same rocks, a worn tooth got replaced roughly every 35 days.","more":"Michael D'Emic's team sliced fossil teeth open. They counted the daily growth lines inside the dentine, the way you count tree rings. Camarasaurus lived in the same landscape and took about 62 days per tooth. Different food, different wear, different replacement speed.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"size","text":"Nobody owns a tape measure that long. The 69–75 ft (21–23 m) figure comes from laying out the bones of the best skeletons end to end. The weight, somewhere near 36,000–49,000 lb, is worked out from the thickness of the limb bones.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Apatosaurus means 'deceptive lizard'. Marsh chose it because the bones under the tail looked to him like a sea-lizard's and fooled him. The animal is named after somebody's mistake.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"created","text":"Genesis has God bringing the kinds to Noah. So nobody had to go out and fetch 72 ft of animal weighing as much as three or four elephants. The pair He sent could be young ones. Fifteen neck bones already, only short ones.","more":"Length is the obvious problem and the wrong one. Food is what a year on a boat is actually about. A young long-neck rakes leaves with its front peg teeth. It needs a fraction of what a full-grown one strips off a tree.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"created","text":"In Genesis the first man named the animals in front of him. Marsh had only bones, and the ones under the tail fooled him, so he named this Apatosaurus — 'deceptive lizard'. The naming never stopped: its species name, louisae, honours Louise Carnegie.","more":"Louise Carnegie never dug anything up. Her husband paid for the expeditions, so the mounted skeleton in Pittsburgh, CM 3018, carries her name. Apatosaurus louisae: one half of the name is a mistake, the other half is a thank-you.","kind":"record"}],"art":1},{"id":"diplodocus","name":"Diplodocus","group":"dinosaurs","blurb":"A long-necked, whip-tailed plant eater. It is one of the longest animals ever to walk on land.","size":"About 85 ft (26 m) nose to tail. Most of that length is neck at one end and tail at the other.","homes":["forest","freshwater"],"stats":{"length":85,"weight":28000},"wonder":"The counterweight tail, the level neck and the hollowed-out back bones only work as one design; take any of them away and the animal cannot stand up.","facts":[{"cat":"bones","text":"The famous skeleton, CM 84, was quarried out of Sheep Creek, Wyoming in 1899 and still stands in the Carnegie Museum of Natural History in Pittsburgh. The mount is a composite: mostly CM 84, with the tail from two other animals and a sculpted skull. Museum copies worldwide were cast from it.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"bones","text":"Skin impressions from Howe Quarry in Wyoming, found in 1990, show narrow pointed spines along the whip end of the tail — the biggest maybe 7 in (18 cm) whole. The skin came off a close cousin, not Diplodocus itself, so the spines are the family's best guess at what it wore. Every earlier model was far too smooth.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"bones","text":"The Smithsonian's mount, USNM 10865, went up in 1931. It is among the most complete Diplodocus known. Its own head and most of its neck had already weathered away. Skulls are the rare part. They are small, light and easily washed off, so mounts often wear a cast of someone else's.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"build","text":"The tail runs to about 80 separate bones. It tapers to a tip no thicker than a broom handle. The name Diplodocus means 'double beam'. That is for the small paired bones tucked underneath those tail vertebrae. It is named for a detail, not for its size.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"A 1997 study suggested the tail could crack like a bullwhip and break the sound barrier. A 2022 study rebuilt it as a digital model 39 ft (12 m) long and got a tip speed near 60 mph (30 m/s) — a serious wallop, but no sonic boom.","more":"The second team also checked whether skin, tendon and ligament could survive the stresses of a supersonic crack. They could not. On those numbers the tail still works fine as a defensive club; it just does not break the sound barrier.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"food","text":"The teeth were slim pegs bunched at the front of the mouth. That shape points to raking leaves off a branch rather than chewing them — so everything went down whole.","more":"Worn pegs were replaced fast: a Diplodocus swapped out a tooth in under 35 days. Stripping tough conifer and fern growth all day long wears a tooth down quickly, so the factory never stopped.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"weird","text":"The nose openings sit high on the skull. That once had people picturing Diplodocus standing on a lake bed. It would breathe through the top of its head like a snorkel. Work published in 1951 showed the water pressure at that depth would have made a breath impossible.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"size","text":"A huge skeleton found in New Mexico in 1979 was named Seismosaurus, 'earthquake lizard'. Careful re-measuring showed it was simply a very large Diplodocus. The separate name was dropped.","more":"A 2024 estimate puts that individual near 108 ft (33 m) long and around 46,000 lb (21 tonnes). Both figures are worked out from the bones that were actually recovered. Nobody put it on a scale.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"build","text":"The neck held at least 15 bones. Slot the vertebrae together neatly on a computer and you get a roughly level pose, so most reconstructions have dropped the old swan curve. But living animals hold their necks higher than that neat fit. The argument is still open.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"home","text":"The bones come out of the Morrison rocks, which stretch from New Mexico up into Canada. The same layers hold ferns, horsetails and tall conifers, but no grass. Read those layers and you get river floodplains with a wet season and a hard dry one.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"created","text":"Diplodocus means 'double beam' — a pair of bones under each tail vertebra, arching around the blood vessel that runs to the tip. One beam covers nothing. Two that do not meet cover nothing. So the pair had to arrive on day six already a pair.","more":"The same all-or-nothing shows up in the back bones, hollowed out to cut weight without losing strength. Bone with holes in it is either a working truss or simply a weak bone, and there is no useful stage in between.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"created","text":"Behemoth lies among the reeds by the river. He is untroubled when the Jordan surges at his mouth. Diplodocus bones come out of the Morrison river floodplains. Ferns and horsetails sit in the same layers. Those layers read as a wet season and a hard dry season. Leviathan is the water half of that pair.","more":"Behemoth lies among the reeds by the river and is untroubled when the Jordan surges at his mouth. Diplodocus bones come out of the Morrison river floodplains: the layers read as a wet season, a hard dry season, ferns and horsetails at the water's edge. Leviathan is the water half of that pair.","kind":"record"}],"art":1},{"id":"velociraptor","name":"Velociraptor","group":"dinosaurs","blurb":"A turkey-sized hunter from the sand dunes of Mongolia. It was built around one oversized sickle claw on each foot.","size":"About 6 ft 8 in (2 m) long, but most of that is neck and stiff tail. It stood roughly knee-high on a grown-up and weighed about 40 lb (18 kg).","homes":["desert"],"stats":{"length":6.8,"weight":40},"wonder":"A small animal was given the equipment of a much larger one, along with the frame and the balance to carry it.","facts":[{"cat":"bones","text":"The first Velociraptor was a crushed skull and a single hand claw. Peter Kaisen found it on 11 August 1923 at the Flaming Cliffs in Mongolia. Henry Fairfield Osborn named it the following year. That specimen, AMNH 6515, is still at the American Museum of Natural History in New York.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"bones","text":"The Fighting Dinosaurs was dug up in 1971. It is a Velociraptor and a Protoceratops buried mid-fight. The raptor's sickle claw is at the plant eater's throat. One of its own arms is clamped in the Protoceratops' beak. Mongolia treats the fossil as a national treasure.","more":"Sudden burial is the only way a scene like that survives. Sand poured over both animals fast. Neither came apart, and nothing scavenged them. Fossils catching two animals in the act of anything at all are extraordinarily rare.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"bones","text":"The skull is long, low and narrow — up to 9.1 in (23 cm) — and uniquely up-curved at the snout, which takes up about 60 percent of its length. Count the sockets and you get close to 60 teeth, each serrated more coarsely on the back edge than the front.","more":"Denticles are the tiny points along a tooth's edge. They are what makes a meat-eater's tooth work like a steak knife instead of a nail. Velociraptor's are not matched front and back. The ones on the rear edge are clearly the larger of the two.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"The raptors in the movies stand about 6 ft tall. A real Velociraptor stood roughly 1 ft 7 in (0.5 m) at the hip. From the size of its skeleton its weight works out at 31 to 43 lb (14 to 20 kg).","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"defence","text":"The second toe of each foot carried a curved claw over 2.6 in (6.5 cm) around its outer edge. From the shape of the toe joint it looks to have been held up clear of the ground while walking, keeping the point sharp.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"That claw was probably not a slashing blade. Tests on models found it punctured and gripped far better than it cut. A 2011 study argued raptors used it to pin prey to the ground. A hawk pins a rat under its feet the same way.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"build","text":"Thin bony rods grew forward from the tail bones and locked the tail into one stiff shaft. It could still bend side to side, which is exactly what you want from a counterweight when you turn at speed.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Specimen MPC-D 100/54 has the bone of a large flying reptile sitting inside its ribcage. The bone shows no pitting from stomach acid, so the meal was recent. The animal it came from was large. That points to scavenging rather than a catch.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Velociraptor means 'swift seizer'. No Velociraptor trackway has ever been found, so nobody can put a real number on how fast it ran. Every speed you have read for it is worked out from the shape of the leg bones.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"home","text":"The bones come out of the Djadochta rocks of Mongolia and the Bayan Mandahu rocks of China. Those were fields of sand dunes, occasional streams, and a lot of dry wind. Several skeletons were buried so fast that they are still curled in a lifelike pose.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"created","text":"Velociraptor walked on land, so it belongs to day six. It stood about 1 ft 6 in (0.5 m) at the hip, knee-high on a grown-up. It was given a second-toe claw over 2.6 in (6.5 cm) around its curve. That is equipment sized for a much larger animal.","more":"That claw is the whole animal in one part. Everything else about it is turkey-sized; the weapon on its foot is not. It was finished and fitted from the start, on an animal small enough to step over.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"created","text":"The animals came to Noah; he did not go collecting them. A grown Velociraptor weighed about 40 lb — worked out from its bones, not read off a scale — and ran 6 ft 8 in (2 m), most of that neck and stiff tail. Young ones would have boarded needing less room than a crate of chickens.","kind":"record"}]},{"id":"spinosaurus","name":"Spinosaurus","group":"dinosaurs","blurb":"The longest meat-eating dinosaur anyone has dug up. It had a crocodile's snout and a tall sail of bone down its back.","size":"Around 46 ft (14 m) long — longer than Tyrannosaurus rex — but far narrower, with back spines standing over 5 ft (1.6 m) tall.","homes":["freshwater"],"stats":{"length":46,"weight":16000},"wonder":"Dense bones, a paddle of a tail and a snout that can feel ripples are not a set of accidents; they are a kit, and they match the river it was put in.","facts":[{"cat":"bones","text":"Ernst Stromer described Spinosaurus in 1915 from bones dug out of Egypt in 1912. They were kept in Munich, and the museum was hit in a bombing raid on the night of 24 April 1944. The only Spinosaurus in the world was destroyed.","more":"What survived was Stromer's published paper. It held careful drawings and measurements of bones nobody would ever see again. For decades every picture of Spinosaurus was drawn from that one document.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"bones","text":"The replacement skeleton, FSAC-KK 11888, came out of the Kem Kem rocks of Morocco. Nizar Ibrahim's team described it in 2014. They published the tail separately in 2020, and the tail turned out to be the surprise.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"bones","text":"In one Moroccan bone bed a student named Thomas Beevor sorted 1,200 fossil teeth by species. Forty-five out of every hundred were Spinosaurus. Teeth from land-living dinosaurs were far rarer, which says a great deal about where this animal spent its time.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The tail bones carry tall blades of bone standing straight up. Together they make a flat fin, rather like a newt's. A plastic model of that tail was swung in a water tank. It produced over eight times the push of an ordinary meat-eater's tail.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"build","text":"The sail is built from spines growing up out of the back bones, the tallest over 5 ft 4 in (1.65 m). Nobody knows what it was for. Showing off to other Spinosaurus is the usual answer, and it is only a guess.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"The teeth are straight cones with few or no saw edges. Read that shape and it comes out poor for cutting through flesh and excellent for holding something slippery. The snout ends in a rosette of long interlocking teeth, which is what a fish trap looks like when it is made out of jaw.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"senses","text":"The tip of the snout is peppered with tiny holes that carried nerve endings. Cristiano Dal Sasso's team scanned them in 2009 and suggested Spinosaurus felt ripples the way a crocodile does. It is a good guess, not a fact — land-only hunters like Neovenator have much the same wiring.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Most big meat-eating dinosaurs had hollow, air-filled bones to save weight. Spinosaurus had dense, thick-walled ones instead. One team reads that as ballast, the trick penguins and hippos use to stop bobbing up. Another says thick bone simply braced a heavy animal walking on land.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Scientists still argue about how it hunted: swimming after fish underwater, or wading in the shallows and grabbing them. Both sides are reading the same skeleton. The argument is not settled, and saying so is more honest than picking a side.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"home","text":"The Kem Kem rocks record an enormous river system with tidal flats and mangroves. It was full of sawfish, coelacanths and crocodile-shaped hunters. The same ground today is Saharan desert.","more":"That is a lot of large predators sharing one place. One suggestion is that they were not competing at all. Some fished and some hunted on land. The river fed far more of them than dry ground ever could.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"created","text":"Someone fishing the river that ran where the Sahara is now would have seen the sail first — spines over 5 ft tall sliding above the reeds. Its tracks in the mud would have come from two feet, not four. The dragon stories many cultures keep begin at the water.","more":"Forty-six feet is longer than most houses are wide. One reading of the dense, thick-walled bones is that they kept the animal low in the water. A hippo sits the same way. From the bank you would have seen a snout, a sail, and very little in between.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"created","text":"The snout tip is drilled with tiny holes that carried nerve endings. Set that next to a crocodile's snout and it reads as a ripple detector. Holes without nerves are just holes; nerves with nothing wired to read them report to no one. A ripple detector is no use half-made, and if that is what this is, it came finished.","more":"Crocodiles have the same arrangement and use it the same way. Cristiano Dal Sasso's team described the Spinosaurus version in 2009. It is one solution, set into a jaw shaped like a crocodile's. But it was carried on an animal that walked out onto the bank on two legs.","kind":"worked"}],"art":1},{"id":"allosaurus","name":"Allosaurus","group":"dinosaurs","blurb":"The commonest big meat-eater in the Morrison rocks. It was a two-legged hunter with saw-edged teeth and a pair of blunt horns above its eyes.","size":"About 28 ft (8.5 m) nose to tail and around 3,700 lb (1.7 tonnes) — a figure worked out from the skeleton, not from a scale.","homes":["forest","freshwater"],"stats":{"length":28,"weight":3700},"wonder":"A skull light enough to swing fast and braced enough to take the impact is a hard thing to design twice; this one arrived already solved.","facts":[{"cat":"bones","text":"Big Al, catalogue number MOR 693, was found in 1991 in Big Horn County, Wyoming. It is about 95% of one skeleton. That makes it among the most complete large meat-eaters ever dug up. The bones are at the Museum of the Rockies in Bozeman, Montana.","more":"A cast of Big Al stands at the University of Wyoming Geological Museum in Laramie. You can meet the same animal in two states. A second skeleton from the same area is nicknamed Big Al Two. It is on display at the dinosaur museum in Aathal, Switzerland.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"bones","text":"Nineteen of Big Al's bones are broken or infected — five ribs, five backbones, four foot bones, plus a shoulder blade, a hand bone and a hip bone. A toe on the right foot is swollen with an infection that lasted months. A foot in that state would have made it limp, and it was not yet full grown.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"bones","text":"The Cleveland-Lloyd Dinosaur Quarry in Utah has given up thousands of bones. At least 46 Allosaurus are tangled together there, with only a handful of plant eaters. Nobody has settled why so many hunters ended up in one patch of ground.","more":"Suggestions include a bog that trapped animals one after another, or a drying waterhole where they gathered and died. The bones are jumbled rather than laid out in place, so the bodies were shifted before they were buried. On the strength of that quarry, Utah made Allosaurus its state fossil in 1988.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Each side of the upper jaw carried 14 to 17 teeth, flattened like blades with saw edges front and back. Most Allosaurus teeth people find are an inch or two long; the biggest run to about 3 in (7.6 cm).","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Worked out from a model of the skull, its bite came out weaker than a big crocodile's. The skull could still take huge force pushed straight down. One idea is that Allosaurus drove its open upper jaw into prey like a hatchet. Then it hauled back and let the teeth do the cutting.","more":"Emily Rayfield's team stress-tested a computer model of the skull in 2001. It withstood a downward load far beyond anything the jaw muscles alone could produce. So the neck and the weight of the head must have supplied the rest.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"defence","text":"One Allosaurus hip bone has a hole punched clean through it that matches a Stegosaurus tail spike. The bone never healed and a patch the size of a baseball was eaten away by infection. Nobody watched the fight; that hole is the reason for saying hunting a Stegosaurus was not a safe job.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The two ridges above the eyes are hollow-cored and differ from one skeleton to the next. Their walls are too thin to have taken a blow, so they were not made for fighting. A sunshade, or a display, or both.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"size","text":"Growth rings in the bones sit widest apart at about age 15. Turned into weight, that year comes out at roughly 326 lb (148 kg) gained. The rings crowd together and stop in its twenties: it had finished growing.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Othniel Charles Marsh named Allosaurus in 1877. The name means 'different lizard'. He chose it for the scooped-out hollows in its back bones. They were unlike anything he had catalogued before.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"home","text":"Allosaurus bones come out of the Morrison rocks of Wyoming, Utah and Colorado. Those layers point to river floodplains with a hard dry season. Bones close enough to be called the same animal also turn up in coastal rock in Portugal, on the far side of the Atlantic.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"created","text":"At Cleveland-Lloyd at least 46 Allosaurus lie tangled together, and the hollow brow ridges differ from one skull to the next. Allosaurus and its close look-alikes are one created kind, and a pair of that kind rode the Ark. The ridges vary. Nothing new is added.","more":"Marsh named it 'different lizard' for the scooped hollows in its back bones. Bones turn up in the Morrison rocks of Wyoming, Utah and Colorado. Bones from coastal rock in Portugal are taken as the same kind. That is one kind on both sides of an ocean.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"created","text":"Growth rings put its fastest year at 326 lb gained, at age fifteen, then crowd together and stop in its twenties. Twenty-eight feet was the finished size, not the size it happened to reach. The stopping was built in as surely as the growing.","more":"The 3,700 lb comes off the skeleton, worked out from bone dimensions rather than a scale. At that weight, a Stegosaurus tail spike still punched clean through one Allosaurus hip and left a hole the size of a baseball. Big did not mean safe.","kind":"worked"}],"art":1},{"id":"ankylosaurus","name":"Ankylosaurus","group":"dinosaurs","blurb":"A low, wide plant eater, armored across the whole of its back. On the end of its tail is a bone club. The club is a knob of spongy bone under a thin hard shell.","size":"About 20 to 26 ft (6 to 8 m) long and roughly 13,000 lb, but built low — only waist-high on a grown-up.","homes":["forest","freshwater"],"stats":{"length":26,"weight":13000},"wonder":"Armor, a cropping beak, a barrel of a gut and a bone club at the far end all belong to one animal, and each one only makes sense with the others.","facts":[{"cat":"bones","text":"Barnum Brown named Ankylosaurus in 1908 from AMNH 5895. That was an upper skull, two teeth, some ribs and vertebrae and over 30 armor plates. It came out of the Hell Creek rocks of Montana. Only about five decent specimens are known, and no complete skeleton exists.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"bones","text":"Specimen AMNH 5214 was collected in 1910. It produced the first Ankylosaurus tail club anyone had seen. The club is 24 in (60 cm) long, 19 in (49 cm) across and 7.5 in (19 cm) thick. A complete skull and lower jaws came with it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"bones","text":"The largest known Ankylosaurus skull, CMN 8880, was collected in 1947 and measures 25 in (64.5 cm) long by 29 in (74.5 cm) wide. It is wider than it is long, which is an odd shape for the skull of anything.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"The last seven tail bones sit hard against one another with no cartilage between them. They lock into a stiff handle for the club. A 2009 study worked out the force of a swing from the size of the club. Only the largest clubs come out hard enough to break bone. Young ones could not.","more":"Victoria Arbour built the models. She measured how far the tail could swing sideways, around 100 degrees. Then she worked out the force at impact from the mass of the club and the muscles driving it.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"defence","text":"The armor is hundreds of separate bone plates set into the skin, from under half an inch (1 cm) across up to 14 in (35.5 cm) long. They are thin-walled and hollowed underneath, so they stayed light for their size.","more":"Even the eyelids were armored. A bone called the palpebral sat over each eye like a shutter. The plates were embedded in skin rather than joined to the skeleton. That is why they scatter when a body comes apart.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"The airway inside the snout does not run straight. It loops. What that loop was for was worked out in 2011. The work used a close relative, Euoplocephalus, rather than Ankylosaurus itself. The shape comes out working as a heat-and-moisture exchanger. It is a nose that warms and dampens the air on the way in.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"food","text":"Behind a broad flat beak sit 34 or 35 tooth sockets along each upper jaw, holding tiny leaf-shaped teeth. Teeth that small point to cropping low plants and swallowing them barely chewed.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"build","text":"The species name magniventris means 'great belly', and the ribcage earns it. A wide gut is what you need to ferment tough plants slowly, so Ankylosaurus was, in effect, a walking compost heap on legs.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"weird","text":"A close relative is called Zuul crurivastator, 'Zuul, destroyer of shins'. It was found with five armor spikes along its flanks broken and healed over. The damage clusters where another ankylosaur's club would land. It is not where a hunter's teeth would land.","more":"That skeleton is at the Royal Ontario Museum in Toronto. If the pattern holds, these animals were swinging those clubs at each other as much as at anything trying to eat them.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"home","text":"Its bones come out of the Hell Creek, Lance and Scollard rocks. Those read as a warm coastal plain of rivers and forest. Ankylosaurus shared that ground with Tyrannosaurus, Triceratops and Edmontosaurus. Its bones turn up far more rarely than any of theirs.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"created","text":"The name magniventris means great belly, and a gut that wide is built to ferment low, soft plants slowly. After the Flood the world ran colder and drier, and low green feed thinned. Armor and a bone club do nothing against an empty gut, or against men who found it would not run.","more":"The plates run from under half an inch across to 14 inches long, hundreds of them. All that bone has to be grown and hauled around, and that takes steady feeding. Lean ground is hard on an animal built that heavy.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"created","text":"People walked the same ground. What they met was an animal only waist-high yet around 13,000 lb, with a head wider than it was long. The stiff tail rode clear, so its trail would show four broad footprints and no drag mark.","more":"The club itself was worth remembering. Two feet long and a foot and a half across. It swung from a tail whose last seven bones locked together into a handle. Dragon carvings and accounts turn up across many cultures, and armored ones are in the mix.","kind":"record"}],"art":1},{"id":"parasaurolophus","name":"Parasaurolophus","group":"dinosaurs","blurb":"A plant-eating dinosaur with a long hollow tube of bone curving back off the top of its skull.","size":"About 31 ft (9.5 m) nose to tail and around 11,000 lb.","homes":["freshwater","forest"],"stats":{"length":31,"weight":11000},"wonder":"A head built as a wind instrument, tubes and all, and it worked the first time it was used.","facts":[{"cat":"bones","text":"The first one found, catalogued ROM 768, came out of the ground along the Red Deer River in Alberta and was named by William Parks in 1922. Skull plus most of a skeleton. You can still go and look at it in the Royal Ontario Museum in Toronto.","more":"A University of Toronto field party collected it in 1920. Parks named it after Sir Byron Edmund Walker, who chaired the museum's board. Hence walkeri.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"size","text":"That skull measures about 5 ft 3 in (1.6 m) long with the crest included. A skull of a second species, P. tubicen, dug up in New Mexico, is over 6 ft 7 in (2 m) — a head longer than most beds.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"bones","text":"The youngest one ever found is nicknamed Joe. A high school student, Kevin Terris, spotted it in southern Utah in 2009: a skeleton about 8 ft (2.5 m) long, probably under a year old. It is at the Raymond M. Alf Museum in California.","more":"Joe's skull is only about 10 in (246 mm) long, and its crest is a low bump rather than a tube. So the tube grew in as the animal grew up — a young Parasaurolophus did not look much like an adult one.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"The crest is hollow. A tube runs from each nostril all the way up to the tip, turns around, and comes back down into the skull. Several feet of looping airway, folded up inside a bone hat.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"A nearly complete P. tubicen skull was found in New Mexico in 1995. It was run through a hospital CT scanner in Albuquerque, about 350 slices, 3 mm apart. A computer scientist at Sandia National Laboratories rebuilt the crest from those slices. Then he worked out what noise it made.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"weird","text":"An old idea said the crest was a snorkel for feeding with its head underwater. It cannot have been. There is no hole at the tip to let air in — the crest is a closed loop, not an open pipe.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"bones","text":"ROM 768 had a rough life. A close study of its bones found healed rib fractures, broken spines along its back, and a V-shaped notch in the backbone. The bone had knitted back together, so it lived on for months or years.","more":"One suggestion is that something heavy came down across its back, possibly a falling tree. The healing is the giveaway. Bone only repairs itself while the animal is still alive to repair it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Its jaws held hundreds of small teeth packed into solid grinding blocks. As the top ones wore flat, replacements pushed up from underneath, so the grinding surface never wore out.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The front legs are shorter than the back ones but sturdy. They end in a hoof-like hand. Work back from that build and you get an animal that could walk on two legs or drop onto all fours. It cropped low plants, then stood up to reach the higher ones.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"home","text":"Its bones come out of rock laid down by rivers and floodplains near an inland sea. That ground is now Alberta. Turtles, crocodiles and fish are mixed in with them.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"created","text":"The crest only works finished. A tube that stops halfway up carries no note, and a skull with the loop but no airway carries nothing either. Every part of it — nostril, tube, turn, return — has to be there at once before a single sound comes out.","more":"That is a general problem with instruments. Half a trumpet is not a quieter trumpet, it is a piece of brass. The Parasaurolophus skull is the same: the parts have no use one at a time.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"created","text":"They are not found alive anywhere now. Why not is worked out rather than dug up. The world after the Flood was colder, drier and harder than the one their bones came out of. People hunted the big ones. What is left is the skulls, and the accounts people wrote down while they still had something to describe.","kind":"record"}],"art":1},{"id":"pachycephalosaurus","name":"Pachycephalosaurus","group":"dinosaurs","blurb":"A two-legged plant-eater whose skull roof is a lump of solid bone almost nine inches thick.","size":"About 15 ft (4.5 m) long — a small car's length, and a lot of that is tail.","homes":["forest","freshwater"],"stats":{"length":15,"weight":900},"wonder":"A skull built thick on purpose, in an animal that never had to design or test it.","facts":[{"cat":"bones","text":"The dome on top of the head is solid bone up to 8.7 in (22 cm) thick — thicker than a brick is long. Around and behind it sits a fringe of bony knobs and short spikes.","more":"The name means thick-headed lizard, and it is the largest of the whole bone-headed group. Everything in that group has a reinforced skull roof; this one has the extreme version.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"bones","text":"The first named piece, USNM 12031, is a partial skull found by George Sternberg in Wyoming in 1930. It sits in the National Museum of Natural History in Washington. A better skull, AMNH 1696, is at the American Museum of Natural History in New York.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"bones","text":"Almost everything known is head. Complete skeletons are rare. The likeliest reason is the dome itself. It was thick enough to be knocked about in a river and still come through. Thin body bones broke up and washed away.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Whether they actually rammed heads is argued about. One survey of domes found that about 22 in every 100 carried bone damage of the kind that follows an injury and infection. A single specimen had 23 separate damaged spots.","more":"That is a high rate of head injuries for an animal that supposedly only used the dome for show. It does not prove head-on ramming, but it does mean the domes were taking real hits.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"The neck bones show the neck was carried in an S or U curve rather than straight out. That is one reason some argue a full head-on crash would not work, and that they shoved each other's flanks instead.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"weird","text":"A spiky, flat-headed skull from South Dakota was named Dracorex hogwartsia, dragon king of Hogwarts. It is at the Children's Museum of Indianapolis. Line the skulls up by size, though, and it looks like simply a young Pachycephalosaurus. That is before the dome swelled up.","more":"Named in 2006 by Bob Bakker and Robert Sullivan. The idea is that youngsters had flat skulls with long spikes; as they grew, the spikes shrank and rounded off while the dome grew in. Stygimoloch may be the in-between stage.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"food","text":"Its teeth are tiny, with leaf-shaped crowns and serrated edges — the shape of a plant-shredder. Oddly, some of the front teeth are sharp and blade-like, more like a meat-eater's, so it may not have been fussy.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"build","text":"Long back legs, short arms, and a tail stiffened by a basket of bony rods so it stuck out straight behind. Whatever the head was for, those long legs and stiff, straight tail add up to a runner's build.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"home","text":"Its bones come out of the Hell Creek and Lance rocks of Montana, Wyoming and the Dakotas. Those are river floodplains and forest, the same beds that give up Tyrannosaurus and Triceratops.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"For all the fuss about the head, the animal itself was not huge: roughly 820 to 990 lb (370 to 450 kg). Nobody ever weighed one - that number comes from scaled drawings of the skeleton, and body bones turn up so rarely it stays a rough guess.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"created","text":"Chinese dragon lore puts a lump on top of the dragon's skull. It is the chimu, hill-shaped, and without it the dragon cannot rise. Pachycephalosaurus carried 8.7 in (22 cm) of solid bone in that spot. It was ringed with knobs and short spikes.","more":"The detail is written down in an old Chinese miscellany called the Youyang Zazu. It lists dragon parts the way you would list an animal's. Where each one sits. What it looks like. What it is for.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"created","text":"Work forward from the Flood, and the world it left is colder, drier and much poorer in the soft growth those tiny shredding teeth needed. A pony-weight plant-eater is also about the size people hunt. What outlasted them is the dome, thick enough to come through a river intact.","more":"The Hell Creek and Lance beds preserve river floodplain and forest, wet and thick with plants. Follow that through. Not much of the world came back like that once the water went down. An animal built to run through cover does poorly where there is no cover.","kind":"worked"}],"art":1},{"id":"iguanodon","name":"Iguanodon","group":"dinosaurs","blurb":"A big plant-eater with a spike where its thumb should be, and one of the very first dinosaurs ever named.","size":"Around 30 ft (9 m) long — about seven nine-year-olds lying head to toe.","homes":["freshwater","forest"],"stats":{"length":30,"weight":7000},"wonder":"A hand with a spike, three hooves and a grasping finger — three tools on one limb, and no spare parts.","facts":[{"cat":"bones","text":"Gideon Mantell got hold of some huge worn teeth from a Sussex quarry. They looked like an iguana's teeth, only about twenty times bigger. He named the animal Iguanodon, iguana tooth, in 1825.","more":"The Royal Society brushed the teeth off at first. Georges Cuvier in Paris eventually agreed they were reptile teeth. Samuel Stutchbury made the iguana match, after preparing an actual iguana. He was an assistant at the Royal College of Surgeons.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Mantell had a cone-shaped spike bone and no idea where it belonged, so he stuck it on the nose like a rhino's horn. It is a thumb. Reconstructions carried that wrong nose horn for decades before anyone sorted it out.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"bones","text":"The slab turned up because quarry workers blew up a lump of rock with explosives. Maidstone liked the connection enough to put an Iguanodon on the town's coat of arms in 1949. Better skeletons have been found since. Comparing them has moved the animal to a close cousin of Iguanodon rather than Iguanodon itself.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"bones","text":"In 1878 miners at a coal pit in Bernissart, Belgium hit bones 1,056 ft (322 m) underground. At least 38 individuals came out of that hole. Nine stand mounted at the Royal Belgian Institute of Natural Sciences in Brussels; nineteen more are kept in the basement.","more":"Louis Dollo spent years reconstructing them. The first mount went on show in 1882. It was one of the earliest dinosaur skeletons ever put up for the public to walk around.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The thumb spike is a solid cone of bone, somewhere between 2 and 6 in long depending on the animal, set at a right angle to the other fingers. What it was for is still argued: defense, or splitting open tough plants.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The hand looks built to do three jobs at once. Three middle fingers end in blunt hooves, the thumb is the spike, and the little finger is long and bendy with an extra bone in it. Read off those shapes, the hooves are for walking on and the bendy finger for hooking branches.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"weird","text":"On New Year's Eve in 1853 the Crystal Palace Iguanodon statue was not yet finished. Its sculptor held an eight-course dinner inside the mold. Twenty-one guests came and eleven fitted inside. Richard Owen sat at the head of the table, in the skull.","more":"The statue was based on the nose-horn reconstruction, so the concrete Iguanodon at Crystal Palace still has a horn on its snout today. It is wrong, and it is a listed monument, so it stays.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"build","text":"For a long time it was mounted upright, propped on its tail like a kangaroo. Line that pose up against the Bernissart skeletons and it does not hold: the back comes out roughly level, with the tail out straight behind as a counterweight.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"food","text":"Rows of teeth in each jaw ground against each other, and worn ones were replaced from below. At the front was a toothless bony beak. A cropping beak in front of grinding teeth is what points to a diet of plants — snip at the front, grind at the back.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"home","text":"The Bernissart animals ended up in a deep natural pit in the rock that later filled with clay. The ferns, horsetails and conifers found with them are what make the surrounding landscape a low, wet one.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"created","text":"In Genesis the first man is brought the animals to name, and people never stopped. Gideon Mantell had nothing but worn teeth from a Sussex quarry. They were the same shape as an iguana's and about twenty times the size. So he called the animal Iguanodon, iguana tooth.","more":"Samuel Stutchbury made the iguana match, having just prepared one. The rest of the animal turned up looking nothing like a lizard. Hooved fingers. A cropping beak. A spike where the thumb should be. The name held anyway. A name given once tends to stay put.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"created","text":"The hand was finished work from the start. Three middle fingers end in blunt hooves. The thumb is a solid cone of a spike, set at a right angle. The little finger is long and bendy, with an extra bone in it. Read those shapes and the jobs come out. One limb, three jobs.","kind":"record"}],"art":1},{"id":"argentinosaurus","name":"Argentinosaurus","group":"dinosaurs","blurb":"An enormous long-necked plant-eater from Argentina. It is known from only a handful of gigantic bones.","size":"Somewhere between 98 and 130 ft (30 to 40 m) long — longer than a tennis court, worked out from the few bones that were found.","homes":["freshwater","forest"],"stats":{"length":115,"weight":165000},"wonder":"Something that big had to be engineered, not merely grown — every bone hollowed exactly where the weight could be spared.","facts":[{"cat":"bones","text":"A rancher named Guillermo Heredia found the first bone on his land near Plaza Huincul, Argentina, in 1987. He took it for a petrified log. José Bonaparte led the proper dig in 1989.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"bones","text":"What was actually dug up is not much. Seven back vertebrae. The underside of the hip block with some of its ribs. Part of a rib, plus a separate lower leg bone. No skull. No neck bones. No tail.","more":"Everything else about this animal is worked out by scaling up from close kin. Those kin have more complete skeletons. That is why the length estimates disagree so widely. Different scientists pick different relatives to scale from.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"bones","text":"One of those back vertebrae is incomplete, so its full height had to be reconstructed: about 5 ft 3 in (159 cm) tall and 4 ft 3 in (129 cm) wide. The lower leg bone is 5 ft 1 in (155 cm) long — taller than the person reading this.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"The original bones, catalogued MCF-PVPH 1, are at the Museo Carmen Funes in Plaza Huincul. The full skeleton standing there is a sculpted reconstruction, start to finish. The real fossil pieces sit in the same room, off to the side — a handful of them, each one enormous.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"Weight estimates run from about 130,000 to 210,000 lb (60 to 95 metric tons) depending on the method used. Every one of them is calculated from bone measurements. Nobody ever put this animal on a scale.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"speed","text":"A team at the University of Manchester laser-scanned the mounted skeleton. They ran a walking simulation on a supercomputer. Their model topped out at about 5 mph (8 km/h). That is a brisk walk for a person, and a full effort for it.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"babies","text":"It started small. That comes from its close kin, not from its own nest. A titanosaur nesting ground at Auca Mahuevo, elsewhere in Argentina, is full of eggs. They average about 5 in (13 cm) across. Thousands have been found there, laid in shallow pits scooped out of the ground. Whole layers of them are stacked one above another.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"build","text":"The backbone is riddled with air spaces. The vertebrae are honeycombed with hollows. Work out the load on those legs and that hollowing is what kept a skeleton this large light enough to carry.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"With no skull dug up, its feeding is worked out from close kin: it ate plants and did not chew. Teeth cropped leaves off, everything went down whole, and the breaking-down happened slowly in an enormous gut.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"home","text":"Its bones come out of the Huincul rocks of Patagonia. Read from the rock those bones sit in, the place was a warm, fairly dry landscape crossed by winding rivers.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"created","text":"The book of Job describes behemoth. It eats grass like an ox. It moves its tail like a cedar. Its bones are like tubes of bronze. Read off its close kin and Argentinosaurus cropped leaves and swallowed them whole. Its own vertebrae are honeycombed with air. That is a long-necked plant-eater, described by someone who had seen one.","more":"Job puts behemoth's limbs at bars of iron. The single lower leg bone dug up near Plaza Huincul is a fibula — the thinner of the two lower leg bones — and it runs 5 ft 1 in (155 cm). Bars of iron is not an exaggeration.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"created","text":"Babylon's Ishtar Gate carries rows of a dragon called the mushhushshu. It is scaly and four-footed, with a small head, a long neck and a longer tail. It walks between rows of wild bulls that everyone in the city knew on sight. That neck and tail are the exact parts of Argentinosaurus nobody has dug up.","more":"It is not a portrait — the carved beast has a horn and a forked tongue. But Babylon's artists set it in rows beside bulls they saw every day, and gave it a long neck, four legs and a heavy tail. A rebuilt section stands in Berlin's Pergamon Museum.","kind":"record"}],"art":1},{"id":"giganotosaurus","name":"Giganotosaurus","group":"dinosaurs","blurb":"A huge meat-eating dinosaur from Argentina. An amateur out driving a dune buggy found it.","size":"About 43 ft (13 m) long, with a skull roughly 6 ft (1.8 m) — a head taller than a grown man.","homes":["desert","freshwater"],"stats":{"length":43,"weight":15000},"wonder":"Teeth shaped like blades, set in a head shaped to swing them — a matched set, made together.","facts":[{"cat":"bones","text":"Rubén Carolini was out in a dune buggy near Villa El Chocón, Argentina, in 1993 when he spotted a leg bone weathering out of the ground. The species name carolinii is after him. The skeleton, MUCPv-Ch1, is at the Ernesto Bachmann museum in that same town.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"bones","text":"Roughly 70 out of every 100 bones of the skeleton were there. Most of the backbone. The shoulder and hip girdles. Both thigh bones, and the left shin and calf bone. That is unusually complete for a giant meat-eater.","more":"Big predators are rare fossils to begin with. The reason lines up with animals alive now. Hunters are far outnumbered by the plant-eaters they hunt, so there were fewer of them to bury. Getting most of one in a single spot is a genuinely lucky find.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"bones","text":"A second individual is known from a lower jaw bone, catalogued MUCPv-95. It is 24 in (62 cm) long — about 8 per cent bigger than the same bone in the first skeleton.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"Its thigh bone is 2 in (5 cm) longer than the thigh bone of Sue, the largest Tyrannosaurus, which stands in the Field Museum in Chicago. Longer leg, though, is not the same as bigger animal.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"It gets shown as flatly bigger than Tyrannosaurus. Put a tape on the two skulls, though, and they come out about the same size. Weight is a different question — nobody has weighed either animal, and the figures worked out from their bones make T. rex the heavier of the two, with Giganotosaurus a bit longer and rather leaner.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"food","text":"Its teeth are flattened side to side, oval in cross-section, with saw edges front and back - nine to twelve serrations per millimeter. That is a slicing blade, not a bone-crushing peg; you can read the job off the shape.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"senses","text":"Its braincase has been scanned and modelled. The space the brain sat in holds about 275 millilitres, a little less than a soda can, in a long narrow shape rather than a round one.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"speed","text":"One study worked out an upper running speed of about 31 mph (50 km/h) from the leg bones and how much muscle they could have carried. That is a calculation from bones, not a stopwatch reading.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"A close relative called Mapusaurus turned up in a bone bed in the same part of Argentina. At least seven individuals of different sizes were jumbled together. Some read that as a group that traveled together. Others say bodies simply piled up in one spot over time.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"home","text":"The sand layers it was buried in, the Candeleros beds, are the kind left by braided rivers and by dune fields. Read together, they give a sandy, half-desert landscape — dry country with water running through it.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"created","text":"God brought the kinds to Noah; Noah did not go out collecting them. A grown Giganotosaurus was 43 ft long, and the weight worked out from its bones comes to about 15,000 lb. But a kind can board young. The thigh bone that outmeasures Sue's, and the 6 ft skull, grew to size afterwards.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"created","text":"Adam's first task in Genesis was naming animals, and the naming never stopped. Ruben Carolini spotted a leg bone weathering out of the ground in Argentina. The skeleton ended up with three names. Giganotosaurus, giant southern lizard. Carolinii, after him. And a number on the museum label.","more":"Carolini was not a paleontologist. He was out driving a dune buggy. The bone he noticed belonged to a skeleton about 70 per cent complete, and a man's own name now sits on the species he happened to drive past.","kind":"record"}],"art":1},{"id":"carnotaurus","name":"Carnotaurus","group":"dinosaurs","blurb":"A horned meat-eater from Patagonia. It is known from a single skeleton. That skeleton came out of the ground with its skin still printed in the rock.","size":"About 26 ft (8 m) nose to tail — two small cars parked end to end — and roughly the weight of one of them.","homes":["freshwater","forest"],"stats":{"length":26,"weight":3300},"wonder":"Nobody was ever supposed to see the pattern on that skin, and it was made beautiful anyway.","facts":[{"cat":"bones","text":"Carnotaurus is known from exactly one skeleton: MACN-CH 894, dug out of a sheep farm in Chubut Province, Argentina. Skull, spine, ribs, arms and hips were still joined in the positions they held in life.","more":"The back two-thirds of the tail, the lower legs and the feet had already weathered away when it was found. Everything anyone says about Carnotaurus traces back to this one animal. It is kept at the Argentine Museum of Natural Sciences in Buenos Aires. No second skeleton has turned up anywhere in the world.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"bones","text":"That same block preserved large patches of skin along the right side — head, shoulder, ribs and tail. Rows of big cone-shaped studs run the length of the flanks, with a mosaic of small pebbly scales packed in between. It is the most complete skin ever found on a meat-eating dinosaur.","more":"The pattern is a mosaic of small pebbly scales about 0.2 to 0.5 in (5–12 mm) across. Rows of bigger cone-shaped scales are set into that mosaic. They are 1.6 to 2 in (4–5 cm) wide. They sit roughly 3 to 4 in (8–10 cm) apart along the flanks, like studs on a leather belt.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"bones","text":"Two thick horns stick out sideways above its eyes, each about 6 in (15 cm) long and solid bone all the way through. No other meat-eating dinosaur found so far has anything like them. The name means \"meat-eating bull\".","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Its arms are almost silly. The forearm is only a quarter the length of the upper arm. The elbow barely bent. The hand ends in four stubby fingers, and only the middle two have real finger bones. T. rex arms look powerful next to these.","more":"T. rex arms were short but thickly built, and the shape of the bone points to muscle that could pull hard. Carnotaurus arms were short and going nowhere. Whatever this animal did for a living, it did with its head, neck and legs.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"speed","text":"For its size it had the largest running muscle of any animal known, living or found in rock. Measured off the tail bones, that muscle works out at roughly 245 to 300 lb (111–137 kg) on each side.","more":"The muscle is the caudofemoralis. It runs from the tail to the thighbone, and every time it pulls, the leg swings back and the animal moves forward. A tail that big suggests real straight-line speed and terrible cornering. Nobody has put an honest number on its top speed, and the researchers say so plainly.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"food","text":"For a 26-ft hunter the bite was not that strong — a computer test of the skull put the front-teeth bite at about 760 lb of force. The power was in the neck instead: the size and shape of its neck bones point to muscle enough to swing the whole head like a hammer.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"senses","text":"The braincase shows large smell bulbs and small sight lobes: it tracked by nose better than by eye. The shape of the inner ear works out to hearing that was best at low, rumbling pitches, below about 3,000 vibrations a second. A footfall, not a squeak.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"weird","text":"One idea is that two Carnotaurus shoved head to head like rams. Tests of the skull disagree about whether it could survive a running crash. So the current best guess is slow pushing, skull-top against skull-top, rather than head-banging.","more":"One study calculated the neck was strong enough to absorb two animals colliding head-on at about 13 mph each. A later computer test of the skull said the bone could take hits through the teeth but not sharp frontal blows. Both papers are on the shelf; the argument is not settled.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"size","text":"Laid out and pieced together, the skeleton works out at about 25 to 26 ft (7.5–8 m). The missing tail end is filled in from the part that survives. Nobody weighed it, obviously. The weight is worked out from the thickness of the limb bones. The published answers range from roughly 3,000 to 4,600 lb.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"home","text":"The grain and layering of the rock it came from, the La Colonia Formation, point to a low coastal plain of rivers, marshes and tidal flats. Turtles are the most common fossils in it, with snakes, frogs, fish and small mammals alongside.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"created","text":"That patch of skin is the closest anyone gets to touching this animal. Studs and pebbled scales, laid out in rows. It was made on day six with the other land animals. The pattern on its side was finished before anything ever walked on it.","more":"Skin does not normally survive at all. Everything else about how a dinosaur looked is worked out from bones. Here, for one animal, on one side, there is the actual surface.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"created","text":"Bull-horned, deep-chested, short-armed beasts turn up in old carvings and dragon accounts from more than one part of the world, described plainly rather than as monsters. People wrote down what they saw while these animals were still about.","kind":"record"}]},{"id":"therizinosaurus","name":"Therizinosaurus","group":"dinosaurs","blurb":"A huge, pot-bellied, beaked plant-eater. It has the longest hand claws of any land animal ever found.","size":"About 30 ft (9–10 m) long and 13–16 ft (4–5 m) tall — it could have looked in an upstairs window without stretching.","homes":["freshwater","forest"],"stats":{"length":32,"weight":11000},"wonder":"Claws like that on an animal that ate leaves is not a mix-up; it is a choice somebody made on purpose.","facts":[{"cat":"bones","text":"What was actually dug up is mostly arms. The first find came from the Nemegt rocks of the Gobi Desert in Mongolia. It was three big claw bones, a piece of hand bone and a few rib fragments. That is specimen PIN 551-483, kept in Moscow.","more":"Later digs added a much more complete forelimb and some hindlimb and foot bones. It is still one of the most famous dinosaurs in the world despite nobody ever having found most of it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"bones","text":"The largest known claw bone measures about 20 in (52 cm) around its curve — longer than a nine-year-old's whole arm. It is the longest hand claw of any land animal ever found. What is missing is the horn sheath: every clawed animal alive grows one over the bone core, so the real claw was longer still.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"bones","text":"The best arm gives real measurements. The upper arm bone is 30 in (76 cm). The forearm bone is 24 in (62 cm). That is about 8 ft (2.4 m) of reach in total, from shoulder to claw tip.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"When those first claws were described, they were filed under \"giant turtle\". The scientist who named them pictured a 15-ft swimming reptile raking in seaweed. The species name still says so: cheloniformis means turtle-shaped. It took another look, much later, to sort it out as a dinosaur.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"food","text":"The claws did not belong to a hunter. Read the rest of the body and it comes out a plant-eater. A horny beak. A wide barrel of a gut for fermenting greenery. Hips built for standing and reaching. The best idea for the claws is that they were hooks for pulling branches down to the beak.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"build","text":"No skull of Therizinosaurus has ever been found. Every picture you have seen of its head — the beak, the small leaf-shaped teeth, the long neck — is filled in from close relatives whose skulls are known. That part is a careful guess, not a fossil.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"build","text":"Its foot puts four toes on the ground. Almost every other two-legged meat-eating-shaped dinosaur walks on three, with the first toe shrunk to a dewclaw up the leg. This one kept all four down — which works out to a wider base, the kind a heavy animal needs.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The claws were not cat-like hooks. Each ran almost straight for most of its length, flattened side to side like a blade, and curved only near the tip. The hand behind them had three fingers on stout palm bones; the largest of those bones measures 11.3 in (29 cm) on its own.","more":"A cat's claw is round in section and hooked the whole way along, built to sink in. This one is a thin blade, almost straight, turning only at the point — closer to a scythe than to a talon. That is where the name came from: Therizinosaurus means \"scythe lizard\".","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"Weight estimates run over 5 tons, worked out from the size of the limb bones rather than from any scale. That is elephant territory, balanced on two legs, with a beak on the front and 20-in claws on the hands.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"home","text":"The Nemegt rocks are river country, not dunes — channel sands and muds holding turtles, freshwater shellfish and crocodile relatives. Big rivers, standing water, plenty of plants. A good address for something that ate 5 tons' worth of leaves.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"created","text":"About 20 in around the curve. That is the longest hand claw found on any land animal, and it grew on a beaked leaf-eater. God made the land animals on day six. The biggest hooks went to one that used them to pull branches down to its beak. That is as best anyone can work out from the rest of the body.","more":"Five tons on two legs. That weight is worked out from the limb bones, not weighed. All four toes stayed flat on the ground. Most two-legged dinosaurs walk on three. A wide base is the sort of thing that gets put under a heavy animal on purpose.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"created","text":"God brought the kinds to the Ark; Noah did not go out after them. Grown, this one works out to over 5 tons from its limb bones, with an 8-ft reach from shoulder to claw tip. A young one boards as a beaked animal whose hand claws are nowhere near 20 in yet.","more":"The claws grow with the animal. What went up the ramp was a young therizinosaur, eating greenery like everything else on the deck. It was not a 5-ton adult with 8 ft of arm and a beak level with an upstairs window.","kind":"record"}]},{"id":"deinonychus","name":"Deinonychus","group":"dinosaurs","blurb":"An 11-ft hunter with a switchblade claw on each second toe. The movie 'velociraptors' were really built from this animal.","size":"About 11 ft (3.4 m) nose to tail, but half of that is tail. Around 160 lb — a big-dog weight on long legs.","homes":["freshwater","forest"],"stats":{"length":11,"weight":160},"wonder":"The hook, the folding toe and the counterweight tail only make sense together — parts drawn to one plan.","facts":[{"cat":"bones","text":"John Ostrom named it from bones dug near Bridger, Montana. The type specimen, YPM 5205 at the Yale Peabody Museum, is a complete left foot plus part of the right — including the claw that gave the animal its name. Deinonychus means \"terrible claw\".","kind":"record"},{"cat":"bones","text":"That sickle claw on the second toe is over 4.7 in (12 cm) measured round the outer curve. The other toes carried the weight; this one rode up off the ground, out of the dirt, staying sharp.","more":"Ostrom worked out what the claw looked like in life. He studied the claws of crocodiles and birds, where the horn sheath is still attached to the bone core. The sheath always adds length. So the living claw was bigger than the fossil bone.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"bones","text":"In one Yale quarry, four adult Deinonychus and one youngster lay together with a single Tenontosaurus. That Tenontosaurus was a plant-eater several times their size. Shed Deinonychus teeth turn up beside Tenontosaurus skeletons again and again.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"The movie 'velociraptors' are Deinonychus in everything but the name. The film's model makers built them at Deinonychus size. The real Velociraptor, from Mongolia, was much smaller.","more":"Velociraptor is a genuinely small animal. It is roughly 6 ft long including a long thin tail, and light enough to pick up. If you want a raptor that really was bigger than the film's, look up Utahraptor. It ran to about 20 ft.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"The pack-hunting raptors of the films have not held up well. Chemical traces locked in Deinonychus teeth were tested. The young ones carry a different signature from the adults, which reads as the two eating different food. That is not what you would expect if a family hunted together and shared a kill.","more":"Ostrom's original idea came from those several Deinonychus found with one Tenontosaurus. Others read the same bones as a crowd mobbing a carcass. They sometimes killed each other over it, the way Komodo dragons do. Parallel trackways elsewhere hint at animals traveling together. Nobody has settled it, and honest write-ups say so.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"build","text":"Bony rods ran back along the tail in bundles, like the shafts of a broom, locking the back half stiff. That stiffness reads as a counterweight — which is what its species name antirrhopus means: counterbalancing.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"build","text":"The skull is about 16 in (41 cm) long. It carries around 70 curved, blade-like teeth, edged like steak knives. The hands had three fingers with big claws. That shape reads as built for grabbing and holding. They could not fold up against the body the way a bird's wing does.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Two studies of its bite disagree. One put it at about 160 lb of force at the tooth — modest, a holding bite. An earlier one, working backwards from tooth punctures in Tenontosaurus bone, came out several times higher. Both are still on the shelf.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"build","text":"Its arms were long. Ken Carpenter worked out in 2002 that they gave a longer reach than most other meat-eaters had. The palms could not turn to face the ground either. Push the arms forward and they swing palm-up. So prey was held between both hands at once, like a bear hug.","more":"Alan Gishlick tested what the hand could actually do in 2001. The grab worked across the arm rather than along it. Both hands closed on the same animal at the same time. It is a very different action from a person picking something up. It is the one the shoulder, elbow and wrist joints allow.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"record","text":"Ostrom's study of these bones changed how everybody pictured dinosaurs. The standard dinosaur before it was a slow, tail-dragging swamp lizard. A fast, sharp, bird-like hunter forced the whole picture to be redrawn.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"created","text":"Anyone sharing the ground with it would have known it by its prints: two toe marks, not three, since the 4.7-inch second-toe claw rode clear of the dirt. It stood about kitchen-counter height at the hip. The 160-lb figure is worked out from the skeleton — nobody weighed one.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"created","text":"Bony rods run back along the tail in bundles, like broom shafts, locking the rear half stiff. Work out what that stiffness is for and you land on a counterweight — something to swing against when the animal turns on one foot. A rod spanning one joint stiffens nothing; the bracing only works once the bundles overlap.","kind":"worked"}]},{"id":"compsognathus","name":"Compsognathus","group":"dinosaurs","blurb":"A small meat-eater known from just two skeletons — a chicken-sized youngster from Germany and a bigger one from France. Both of them still have a last meal inside the ribcage.","size":"The German skeleton is about 2 ft 6 in (75 cm) nose to tail-tip and weighed around a pound. The French one is bigger: roughly 4 ft (1.25 m) and 6 lb.","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"length":4,"weight":6},"wonder":"Even the smallest of them was finished properly — hollow bones, long legs, a full set of teeth, in an animal you could hold.","facts":[{"cat":"bones","text":"Only two Compsognathus skeletons have ever been found. One came from the limestone at Solnhofen in Bavaria, Germany; the other from Canjuers, near Nice in France. Both are nearly complete and lie flattened on the slab like a pressed flower.","more":"The German one, specimen BSP AS I 563, is in Munich. The French one is in the National Museum of Natural History in Paris. Fine-grained limestone is what makes them look like that. The rock recorded the skeleton without crushing the detail out of it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"bones","text":"Inside the German skeleton's ribs is a lizard, in several pieces. The eating is plain. The method is worked out from how a hawk handles the same job. Compsognathus held the lizard down with hands and teeth, tore it apart, and swallowed the chunks. It is one of the few dinosaurs whose last meal we know.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"That lizard was a long-toed, fast-running animal. Compsognathus swallowed one whole and undamaged. So it had to be quicker than the lizard, at least over the short distances between rocks.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"record","text":"For a long time Compsognathus was called the smallest dinosaur. That title has since gone elsewhere, to animals like Microraptor. It is still one of the smallest — and the German skeleton is now read as a youngster rather than a full-grown adult.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"build","text":"Small teeth, and a lot of them. The German skull carries three in each front bone of the upper jaw, fifteen or sixteen more behind those, and eighteen in the lower jaw. The French one has a few more of each.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"How many working fingers it had is still argued over. The German hand clearly preserves bones from the first two fingers, with a smaller third that may not have done much. Two or three, depending which paper you read.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"bones","text":"Round marks about 0.4 in (10 mm) across near the German skeleton were once read as eggs inside the body. Later work doubts it. Bumpy marks on the tail, once called skin, were re-read in a recent review as odd bony growths instead.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Read from the rock itself, the Solnhofen limestone was a still, salty lagoon between low islands. Anything that sank into it landed in airless mud. Nothing came along to scatter the bones. That is why these fossils are flat, whole and famous.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"home","text":"That same rock was quarried for printing. It is so fine and so flat that pictures could be drawn straight onto a slab and printed from it. The whole method is named after the stone: lithography, from the Greek for \"stone writing\".","kind":"record"},{"cat":"weird","text":"The little dinosaurs that swarm people in The Lost World: Jurassic Park are Compsognathus — \"compys\" in the film. The swarming is invented. Two skeletons, found in different countries, tell you nothing whatsoever about group behavior.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"created","text":"Part of that gap is simply age. Read from its bones, the German animal was not full-grown. The rest is the ordinary spread found within one kind. A little longer, a little heavier, a tooth or two either way. All of it works from the same original design.","more":"Part of that gap is simply age. The German animal was not full-grown. The rest is the ordinary spread found within one kind. A little longer, a little heavier, a tooth or two either way. All of it works from the same original design.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"created","text":"Two feet six inches nose to tail-tip, and about a pound in weight. That weight is taken from the skeleton, not from any scale. It still has a lower jaw carrying eighteen teeth, hollow bones and long running legs. Nothing was skipped for being small. That size was the specification, not a shortfall.","more":"The whole animal would sit in cupped hands, and most of its length is tail. Everything a hunter needs is there at full quality, just scaled down. The teeth. The light bones. Legs quick enough to run down a lizard between rocks.","kind":"worked"}],"art":1},{"id":"protoceratops","name":"Protoceratops","group":"dinosaurs","blurb":"A sheep-sized, beaked plant-eater with a bony frill. It is one half of the most famous fossil ever dug up.","size":"About 6 to 8 ft (2–2.5 m) long and, worked out from the bones, 140 to 230 lb — a large sheep with a beak.","homes":["desert"],"stats":{"length":7,"weight":180},"wonder":"Two animals locked together in the sand, kept exactly as they fell — nothing that happens gets lost.","facts":[{"cat":"bones","text":"The \"Fighting Dinosaurs\" specimen was found at Tugrikin Shire in Mongolia's Gobi Desert. It is a Protoceratops and a Velociraptor buried together in the middle of a fight. The raptor's sickle claw is at the Protoceratops's throat. The Protoceratops's beak has clamped the raptor's right arm.","more":"The two are catalogued separately, as Protoceratops MPC-D 100/512 and Velociraptor MPC-D 100/25. The Protoceratops has broken shoulder bones and its right forelimb torn away. The raptor's trapped arm is broken. A joint Polish-Mongolian team dug them out. The usual explanation is that a dune collapsed, or a sand-laden storm buried them where they stood. It is a national treasure of Mongolia, and casts of it travel to museums abroad.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"bones","text":"Protoceratops is not rare. An expedition's photographer spotted the first skull at the Flaming Cliffs. Within a few years more than a hundred specimens were known. They run every size from hatchling to adult. Few dinosaurs are known this well.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"bones","text":"One block from Tugrikin Shire holds fifteen young Protoceratops together, bones still in order. It is specimen MPC-D 100/530. Fifteen youngsters in one spot, all buried at once. That looks like a nursery.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Eggs found near Protoceratops in the Gobi were labeled Protoceratops eggs for decades. A small dinosaur was found lying on a clutch of them and named Oviraptor, \"egg thief\". Then an embryo was found inside one of those eggs. It was an Oviraptor. The thief was sitting on its own nest.","more":"The name stuck anyway. That is how scientific naming works. Once a name is published it does not get swapped out for being unfair. Real Protoceratops nests were identified later. One clutch held at least twelve eggs with embryos still inside.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"build","text":"Skulls of the main species run to about 20 in (50 cm) long, and a good share of that is frill. The frills vary enormously between individuals — some short and compact, some nearly half the length of the whole skull.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Because so many were found, you can lay them out youngest to oldest. A hatchling's frill is small, flat and almost round. An adult's is a broad fan. Line the series up and the frill turns out to grow faster than the rest of the head does.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"weird","text":"The name means \"first horned face\", which rather oversells it. Protoceratops had no brow horns at all — just a low bump on the snout. The properly horned relatives, like Triceratops, are a separate story.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"food","text":"A parrot-like beak at the front, and rows of leaf-shaped teeth set behind it. Read those shapes and you get the job: the beak crops, the teeth behind shear tough plants. The main species also carries two little peg-shaped teeth tucked in the upper beak.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"weird","text":"A popular idea says Protoceratops skulls gave the world the griffin. Nomads prospecting for gold supposedly found them. A recent review pushed back hard. The fossil sites sit hundreds of miles from any gold. Griffin art turns up where nobody was digging skulls.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"home","text":"Whole Protoceratops are sometimes found upright in the sandstone. Their legs are folded under them, as though caught standing. One suggestion is that they dug shelters against the desert heat. Their broad, shovel-shaped foot claws would do it. Then the sand came down on them.","more":"The other explanation is simpler. A sand-carrying storm buried them where they stood. Either way, that upright pose is why so many Gobi skeletons are complete. The sand arrived before anything could scatter the bones.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"created","text":"The world after the Flood was harder, colder and drier. Protoceratops sheared tough plants with rows of leaf-shaped teeth and had nothing to fight with — no brow horns, only a bump on the snout. Put those together: a plant-eater of about 180 lb, worked out from the bones, with no weapons on it, was worth a hunter's trouble.","more":"Its broad, shovel-shaped foot claws may have dug shelters against the desert heat. That works while there is still something to crop. A beak and rows of shearing teeth need plants to shear, and where the plants thin out an animal built that narrowly runs short first.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"created","text":"The Ark carried the land kinds, and this one made no difficulty: an animal of about 180 lb does not take up much room. The Gobi block holding fifteen young together points to numbers, so a pair aboard was plenty.","more":"The Ark carried the land kinds, and this one made no difficulty. An animal of about 180 lb does not take up much room. The Gobi block holding fifteen young together shows they came in numbers. So a pair aboard was plenty.","kind":"record"}],"art":1},{"id":"oviraptor","name":"Oviraptor","group":"dinosaurs","blurb":"A parrot-beaked dinosaur, roughly ostrich-sized. It was named egg thief by mistake and later cleared.","size":"About 6 ft (2 m) from beak to tail tip and around 80 lb (36 kg) — a big dog's weight stretched out on an ostrich's frame.","homes":["desert"],"stats":{"length":6,"weight":80},"wonder":"A beak strong enough to crush hard food and arms wide enough to shelter a nest, fitted to the same animal on purpose.","facts":[{"cat":"bones","text":"Oviraptor is known from a single partial skeleton, AMNH 6517, dug up at the Flaming Cliffs in Mongolia in 1923 and named by Henry Fairfield Osborn in 1924. The American Museum of Natural History in New York still holds it. No second skeleton has ever turned up.","more":"The skull is badly crushed and missing its back end. What survives measures about 7 in (17.9 cm). Rough patches on the roof bones show a tall crest once sat up there, but the crushing means nobody can say exactly what shape it was.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"bones","text":"The skeleton was lying on a nest of about fifteen eggs, catalogued separately as AMNH 6508. Each egg is roughly 5.5 in (14 cm) long. Oviraptorid nests like it are laid out as a ring of paired eggs, not a random pile.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"The name means \"egg thief.\" Osborn chose it because he thought the animal had died raiding a Protoceratops nest. In the same paper he warned that the name might turn out to be wrong. It was.","more":"He went further and made the species name philoceratops, \"lover of ceratopsians\". It was a joke about a taste for Protoceratops eggs. Both halves of the name record a mistake. The rules of animal naming mean it can never be swapped for a better one.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"bones","text":"In 1993 an expedition to Ukhaa Tolgod in Mongolia found an egg of the same type. A tiny skeleton was still inside it, specimen IGM 100/971. The skeleton was an unhatched oviraptorid, not a Protoceratops. Match the shell type across the two sites. The Flaming Cliffs eggs had been oviraptor eggs all along.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Close relatives have been dug up crouched on top of their nests, arms folded back over the eggs the way a hen covers a clutch. The best known is IGM 100/979, nicknamed Big Mama — a cousin called Citipati, buried in that pose. Nobody watched the sitting; it is worked out from the way the bones lie.","more":"Line those two ideas up and the story flips completely. The best reading of the Flaming Cliffs skeleton is no longer an animal eating the eggs but one sitting on them. A hundred textbooks had to be corrected, and the mistaken name stayed. Being wrong in public is how the correction got made.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"food","text":"A 1995 report found the broken-up remains of a lizard inside the holotype's body cavity. That is where the stomach had been. So Oviraptor did eat meat. Just not eggs, on the evidence anybody has.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"No teeth anywhere. The jaws ended in a deep, hooked beak of horn, closer to a parrot's than a lizard's, and that depth and hook point to crushing rather than slicing. The hands carried three fingers, each tipped with a curved claw flattened side to side.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"One idea is that the pairing is also how the nest got built. The mother stood in the middle and turned as she worked. She added a pair at each stop until the ring closed. Crocodiles have two tubes as well. Each of theirs sends several eggs at a time rather than one.","more":"That pairing may also be how the nest got built. One idea is that the mother stood in the middle and turned as she worked, adding a pair at each stop until the ring closed. Crocodiles have two tubes as well, but each of theirs sends several eggs at a time rather than one.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"created","text":"A toothless crushing beak, and a nest of about fifteen eggs set out in a ring. Oviraptor was a land animal, made on day six of the creation week. That is the same day as the people who could have stood and watched it at that nest.","more":"A crushed lizard sat in the holotype's body cavity, where the stomach had been, so this was a meat-eater with no teeth anywhere. The beak had to do the whole job, and it was there from the day the land animals were made.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"created","text":"Oviraptor's own crest is known only from rough patches on the skull roof of AMNH 6517. Crests differ across its close look-alikes, Citipati included. The toothless horn beak, three clawed fingers and fused tail fan hold steady. Read that pattern across the group. It comes out as one created kind reshuffling what it already had.","more":"The ring-of-paired-eggs nest layout turns up right across the group, from the Flaming Cliffs clutch of about fifteen to Big Mama's at Ukhaa Tolgod. Same habit, same body plan, different crests and sizes. That is what variation inside one kind looks like.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"home","text":"The Flaming Cliffs are red sandstone in the Gobi Desert, old dune sand. Skeletons there often come out whole and still articulated, in lifelike poses. That points to sand arriving faster than the animal could get out of it.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"The 1923 Mongolian finds are the ones that made dinosaur eggs famous around the world. They were announced as Protoceratops eggs. They stayed labeled that way for about seventy years. Then the embryo inside one settled the question.","kind":"record"}]},{"id":"gallimimus","name":"Gallimimus","group":"dinosaurs","blurb":"A long-legged, completely toothless runner, built like an ostrich with a tail added. It is the largest ostrich-mimic dinosaur known from good skeletons.","size":"About 20 ft (6 m) long, with the hips a little over 6 ft (1.9 m) off the ground — a grown-up could stand underneath them.","homes":["freshwater","forest"],"stats":{"length":20,"weight":1000},"wonder":"Short thigh, long shin, long foot — the leg is arranged for one job, and it came arranged that way.","facts":[{"cat":"bones","text":"Gallimimus was named in 1972 by Halszka Osmólska, Ewa Roniewicz and Rinchen Barsbold. Polish-Mongolian expeditions collected the material in the Gobi Desert in the 1960s. The holotype is IGM 100/11. Its skull measures about 13 in (330 mm) and its thigh bone 26 in (660 mm).","kind":"record"},{"cat":"bones","text":"It is known from many individuals at different sizes. Young ones stand about 1.5 ft (0.5 m) at the hip. Adults reach near 6 ft (1.9 m) at the hip. Having both ends of the growth range from one region is unusual and very useful.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"bones","text":"One skull, IGM 100/1133, kept part of its horny beak. Inside the upper beak run fine vertical ridges, like the comb of plates inside a duck's bill. That is a beak preserved, not a beak guessed at.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Those ridges made researchers wonder whether Gallimimus strained food out of water the way a duck does. Paul Barrett looked again and argued the ridges suit cropping and shearing tough plants instead. The argument is still open; both sides are reading the same beak.","more":"This is normal science, not a scandal. One team saw duck-like plates and proposed filter feeding. Another measured them, compared them with more living birds, and got a different answer. Nobody faked anything — the beak is simply hard to read, and it is the only one anybody has.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"build","text":"No teeth at all. The name means \"chicken mimic,\" chosen because the neck bones reminded the team of a chicken's, not because of its size. The second half, bullatus, refers to a hollow bulb of bone at the base of the skull, named after a locket Roman boys wore.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"speed","text":"Nobody has ever timed a Gallimimus. The often-quoted 26–35 mph (42–56 km/h) comes from comparing the proportions of its leg bones with living runners, not from trackways. Treat it as reasoning from bones, not a stopwatch reading.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"senses","text":"The eyes were large and set high on the sides of the head. That layout points to an animal catching movement all around it rather than judging distance straight ahead — good for spotting something coming, less good for aiming at it.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"build","text":"Three fingers on each hand, all about the same length, tipped with curved claws. Measured against its relatives, Gallimimus had the shortest hands for its body size. The arms were not the point of this animal. The legs were.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"home","text":"Gallimimus comes out of the Nemegt Formation in Mongolia — river channel sand and floodplain mud. That is different ground from the dune sand at the Flaming Cliffs, even though both are in the same country.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"created","text":"Medieval English bestiaries and church carvings show the cockatrice — a cock-headed creature on two long legs with a long tail behind. The King James translators used the word in Isaiah. Gallimimus means chicken mimic: toothless beak, hips over 6 ft off the ground, tail trailing.","more":"The carvings differ in the details, but the pairing holds: bird at the front, reptile behind. That is a strange thing to keep inventing and a simple thing to describe if a man had watched a toothless, long-shinned runner cross open ground.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"created","text":"The Nemegt rock that holds Gallimimus is river-channel sand and floodplain mud. That same ground is Gobi desert now. Put those together and one idea follows. A toothless animal with the shortest hands among its relatives had running and nothing else. A herd out on open, drying floodplain would be easy for hunters to track.","more":"Speed answers one predator at a time. It does not answer water pulling back into a few channels, or people who work in groups and simply wait where the herd has to come to drink.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"copied","text":"The stampeding Gallimimus herd in the movie was one of the first crowds of computer-made animals ever put on screen. The shape they animated is close to the real skeleton.","kind":"record"}]},{"id":"dilophosaurus","name":"Dilophosaurus","group":"dinosaurs","blurb":"A 20-ft meat-eater carrying two thin bony crests along the top of its skull. It is nothing like the small, frilled, venom-spitting version in the film.","size":"The best skeleton measures 19 ft 9 in (6.03 m) nose to tail. The largest individual known reaches about 23 ft (7 m).","homes":["freshwater","desert"],"stats":{"length":20,"weight":620},"wonder":"Two thin crests, no use in a fight and a cost to carry, on a head that already worked — some things are made to be looked at.","facts":[{"cat":"bones","text":"A Navajo man named Jesse Williams found the bones in northern Arizona in 1940. He led Charles Camp's crew to them in 1942. The holotype, UCMP 37302, is at the University of California Museum of Paleontology. Samuel Welles named the species wetherilli in 1954.","more":"Welles first filed it under Megalosaurus. That was a catch-all name for large meat-eaters from all over the world. Then a better specimen showed the twin crests clearly. Only in 1970 did he give it a genus of its own. Dilophosaurus means \"two-crested lizard.\"","kind":"record"},{"cat":"bones","text":"Only one specimen, UCMP 77270, preserves much of the crests. They are thin plates of bone standing up along the top of the snout, arched like a pair of sails. Bone that thin snaps, so they could never have been used to hit anything.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"bones","text":"The holotype had been badly hurt. Phil Senter and Sara Juengst counted injuries in eight bones of its shoulders and arms in 2016. A cracked shoulder blade. A bent forearm. An infected bone. Three lumps on one arm. All partly healed.","more":"That is the highest number of forelimb injuries recorded in any meat-eating dinosaur. Bone heals slowly and leaves a record while it does. The pattern says this animal survived a very bad stretch, months at least, with arms it could not use properly.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"copied","text":"The film Jurassic Park gave Dilophosaurus a fan-shaped neck frill and venom it spat into people's eyes. Neither shows up in any fossil. Both were invented for the film, and the film's animal was shrunk well below the real size.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Nobody knows what the crests were for. They are too thin for fighting, they carry no muscle, and they sit on top of the head where the animal itself could never see them. That points at being seen rather than doing.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"build","text":"There is a notch in the upper jaw just behind the front teeth, called the subnarial gap, with a matching break in the tooth row. The front teeth jut ahead of it. It is the easiest way to recognize this skull at a glance.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Counted straight off the skull: four teeth in each premaxilla at the front, twelve in each maxilla behind it, seventeen in each side of the lower jaw. The teeth are long, thin and curved backwards, with more than thirty fine saw-teeth along the front edge.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The 2020 redescription by Adam Marsh and Timothy Rowe found pockets of air running through the skull bones and the backbone, the same lightening trick a bird's skeleton uses. A large head that weighs less than it looks is easier to swing.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"At the St. George Dinosaur Discovery Site in Utah there is a resting trace, SGDS 18.T1, where a big meat-eater sat down and left tail, foot and hand marks. The hands faced inward, palms toward each other. No name comes with a track, so what it says about Dilophosaurus is read across from a similar animal.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"home","text":"The bones come from the Kayenta Formation in northern Arizona. The spot is about 20 miles (32 km) north of Cameron, near Tuba City. The rock there was laid down by rivers and streams running through otherwise dry country.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"created","text":"In Genesis the first man is brought the animals to name, and the job never ended. Samuel Welles first filed these bones under Megalosaurus, a catch-all for big meat-eaters. Then a better skull showed the twin crests. He renamed the animal Dilophosaurus, two-crested lizard.","more":"A Navajo man, Jesse Williams, found the bones in northern Arizona. He led Charles Camp's crew to them. Names get corrected as people look harder. The one that stuck describes what is actually there. Two ridges of bone standing along the snout.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"created","text":"It walked, so it belongs to day six with the other land animals. It arrived complete. Backward-curving teeth for meat. A notch in the upper jaw behind the four front teeth. Two thin bone crests arched like sails. They were thin enough from the start that they could never have served in a fight.","more":"Marsh and Rowe found pockets of air running through the skull bones and the backbone, the same lightening trick a bird's skeleton uses. A head that size weighs less than it looks and swings more easily, so the crests cost less to carry than their span suggests.","kind":"record"}],"art":1},{"id":"styracosaurus","name":"Styracosaurus","group":"dinosaurs","blurb":"A rhino-sized horned dinosaur with one long nose horn. Its bony neck frill is rimmed with a crown of spikes.","size":"About 18 ft (5.5 m) long and somewhere near 4,000–6,000 lb (1.8–2.7 tonnes), worked out from the skeleton — a white rhinoceros wearing a spiked collar.","homes":["forest","freshwater"],"stats":{"length":18,"weight":5000},"wonder":"Nothing on that frill helps the animal eat, run or hide. It is decoration — and decoration means somebody had an eye for it.","facts":[{"cat":"bones","text":"Charles H. Sternberg collected the type skull along the Red Deer River in Alberta in 1913, and Lawrence Lambe named Styracosaurus albertensis from it the same year. It is catalogued as CMN 344 at the Canadian Museum of Nature.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"bones","text":"The rest of that same animal stayed in the ground until 1935. Sternberg's son Levi went back to the quarry with a Royal Ontario Museum crew. They took out the lower jaw and most of the skeleton. Head and body, dug up 22 years apart.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"bones","text":"The nose horn measures about 22 in (57 cm). Four to six spikes stand out from the rim of the frill, and the four longest run 20–22 in (50–55 cm). Those are measurements off actual bone, not reconstruction.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"The spikes point up and back, away from whatever is standing in front of the animal. That is an awkward layout for stabbing anything. Reading backwards from the direction they face, the frill comes out as something built to be looked at — a signal, not a shield.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"build","text":"The frill is not a slab of solid bone. Two large windows open right through it. That leaves a bony frame, which would have been covered over with skin in life. It is enormous to look at and far lighter than it appears.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"Bonebed 42 in Dinosaur Provincial Park, Alberta, is packed with Styracosaurus pieces. Nose horns, jaws, frill fragments. Counting the horn cores gives at least ten adults in one spot. Ten animals stacked into one bed reads as a group that went down together. It does not read as one animal at a time.","more":"From 46 square meters of the bed, diggers pulled 373 bones, about eight per square meter. Over half the material is horned dinosaur. A herd caught trying to cross a river in flood is the usual explanation for a bed built like this.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"A skull found in Alberta is nicknamed Hannah, UALVP 55900, and it is lopsided. Seven spikes run along the right side of the frill and eight along the left. Several are different sizes and point different ways. Real animals are not symmetrical the way drawings are.","more":"Robert Holmes and colleagues described it in 2019. Hannah stretched the known range of Styracosaurus a long way. A second animal, once named Rubeosaurus, now looks like nothing more than a Styracosaurus with an unusual frill. One skull deleted a whole genus.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A young Styracosaurus has small bumps where an adult has a 22-in horn. Setting small skulls beside big ones is how the horn and the spikes are known to have grown in with age — nobody watched one grow up. It is also one reason young horned dinosaurs have sometimes been named as separate species by mistake.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"food","text":"The front of the mouth is a narrow, toothless hook shaped like a parrot's beak; further back sit tightly packed rows of teeth. Shapes like that do the cropping at the front and the cutting and grinding behind, going by animals that eat plants today. One head, two tools, arranged front to back.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"home","text":"The bones come out of the Dinosaur Park Formation in Alberta. The rock around them is river channel sand and floodplain mud. That is what the landscape is read from. Low, wet, wooded country, not far from a shoreline.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"created","text":"Behemoth in Job moves a tail like a cedar; leviathan belongs to the sea. Styracosaurus is neither. It runs 18 ft long and roughly 5,000 lb. Everything remarkable — the 22-in nose horn, the crown of spikes — sits in front of the shoulders.","more":"A cedar-sized tail suits a long-necked giant. It does not suit a rhino-sized animal on four short legs. Job's list is not a catalogue of every creature. Behemoth is one animal, named for what stood out about it.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"created","text":"Pliny the Elder described the monoceros. A deep bellow, elephant feet, one long horn, a heavy four-legged body. Medieval bestiaries copied him almost word for word. A 22-in nose horn on an animal of roughly 5,000 lb fits that better than any horse does.","more":"The Aberdeen Bestiary puts the monoceros horn on the forehead and stretches it to four feet. A description picks up that sort of drift when it is passed along by people who never saw the animal. The build survives the retelling. One horn, four heavy legs, no wings.","kind":"record"}],"art":1},{"id":"edmontosaurus","name":"Edmontosaurus","group":"dinosaurs","blurb":"A big duck-billed plant-eater. It is one of the very few dinosaurs known from specimens that kept their skin.","size":"Around 39 ft (12 m) long and roughly 12,000 lb (5.6 tonnes), worked out from the skeleton.","homes":["freshwater","forest"],"stats":{"length":39,"weight":12000},"wonder":"Over a thousand teeth, replaced on a schedule so the grinding surface never wears out — that is machinery, and machinery gets designed.","facts":[{"cat":"bones","text":"In 1908 Charles H. Sternberg and his sons dug up a specimen near Lusk, Wyoming, that had kept its skin. It is AMNH 5060, known as the Trachodon mummy, and it is still at the American Museum of Natural History in New York.","more":"What survives is not soft skin but a hardened impression of it. It shows the pattern of small pebbly scales across the body. One reading is that the animal dried out and shrunk down onto its bones before burial. That would be part of why the shape held.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"bones","text":"The Sternbergs found a second one in 1910, now SM 4036 at the Senckenberg Museum in Frankfurt. It kept the horny covering of the beak, which extends at least 3 in (8 cm) past the bone. The real bill was bigger than the skull alone suggests.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"bones","text":"In 1999 a high-school student named Tyler Lyson found another mummy near Marmarth, North Dakota. It was on his family's land. Nicknamed Dakota, specimen NDGS 2000 preserves skin. It has a row of spikes down the midline of the tail, and hoof-like nails on the front feet.","more":"A 2022 study led by Stephanie Drumheller found bite marks and punctures in Dakota's skin. Read from those marks, scavengers opened the carcass. It deflated and dried out instead of bloating and bursting. That would be part of why so much skin survived.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"One specimen, UALVP 53722, came out of the ground with a fleshy comb on top of its head. It is a soft dome about 8 in (20 cm) tall, with no bone inside it, covered in tiny scales. Nothing like it had been seen on a dinosaur before.","more":"Phil Bell's team described it in 2013 and compared it to a rooster's comb. In 2025 Henry Sharpe and colleagues argued the specimen cannot safely be pinned to a named species. The comb is real; whose head it sat on is still being argued about.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"The jaws hold roughly a hundred columns of teeth on each side of the head. About fifty above, about forty-eight below. Up to six teeth are stacked in every column. That is more than a thousand teeth in one mouth, working as a single grinding surface.","more":"Worn teeth were pushed out and replaced from underneath. In Edmontosaurus a tooth took roughly fifty days to come through, so the grinding surface renewed itself continuously and never wore down. No living animal has a mouth built this way.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"defence","text":"Stood up on its legs, the top of that tail comes out at least 9.5 ft (2.9 m) off the ground. Only one meat-eater in those rocks was tall enough to reach it. Tyrannosaurus. Kenneth Carpenter studied the specimen. He also found an older, better-healed break in the left hip.","more":"The top of that tail stood at least 9.5 ft (2.9 m) off the ground. Only one meat-eater in those rocks was tall enough to reach it. Tyrannosaurus. Kenneth Carpenter studied the specimen. He also found an older, better-healed break in the left hip.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"teamwork","text":"Some Edmontosaurus bone beds are enormous. One site in the Lance Formation of Wyoming spreads remains over more than a third of a square mile. That works out to somewhere between 10,000 and 25,000 animals. The thickest part is packed into about 100 acres.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"The front feet were not hands. The middle fingers were bound together inside a fleshy pad ending in hoof-like nails, which you can see on Dakota. A front foot built like that points to an animal that could walk on all fours to feed low and rear up on two legs to move.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"record","text":"Most adults come in around 39 ft (12 m). A few unusually large individuals work out to close to 49 ft (15 m), which makes Edmontosaurus one of the biggest duck-bills anyone has dug up.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"home","text":"It turns up in the Horseshoe Canyon Formation in Alberta and the Hell Creek and Lance formations further south — low, wet, coastal floodplain country crossed by rivers. Lawrence Lambe named E. regalis in 1917; Othniel Marsh had already named E. annectens in 1892.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"created","text":"Skin only survives if an animal is buried fast and deep. A body left out is picked apart in days. The Flood moved water and sediment on a scale nothing since has matched, which is the kind of burial these mummies would need.","more":"AMNH 5060 and Dakota both kept skin. That is the puzzle worth sitting with: whatever covered them did it quickly enough that scavengers and rot never got started.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"created","text":"Edmontosaurus, Parasaurolophus and the other duck-bills look like variations on one design. Same beak, same tooth batteries, same build, different heads. Genesis has animals made as kinds, each producing more of its own kind. That is what a group like this looks like.","kind":"record"}],"art":1},{"id":"pteranodon","name":"Pteranodon","group":"dinosaurs","blurb":"A large flying reptile with a toothless beak and a long backswept crest — not a dinosaur, and not a bird either.","size":"Wings about 20 ft (6 m) from tip to tip, on a body only about 6 ft (1.8 m) from beak to tail.","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"length":6,"weight":45},"facts":[{"cat":"bones","text":"About 1,200 Pteranodon specimens have been dug up, more than any other flying reptile, nearly all of them from the chalk of western Kansas. Othniel Marsh found the first pieces there in 1870 or 1871.","more":"Samuel Williston found the first skull on 2 May 1876, along the Smoky Hill River. George Sternberg later spent years working the same chalk. He kept the best of his material close to home. You can go and see it at the Sternberg Museum of Natural History in Hays, Kansas.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"bones","text":"Specimen AMNH 5098 was found with a wad of fish bones packed between its jaws — the last meal, still in the beak. Other specimens have fish scales and fish backbones preserved where the stomach used to be.","more":"That is about as direct as diet evidence gets. Most of the time what an animal ate has to be guessed from tooth shape; here it is sitting in the mouth.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"bones","text":"One Pteranodon neck bone came out of the chalk with a shark tooth still stuck in it, from Cretoxyrhina, a big shark of the same water. Whether the pterosaur was caught alive or scavenged afterwards, the bone cannot say.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Pteranodon is not a dinosaur. It is a pterosaur — a flying reptile — and that is a separate kind altogether. Nearly every book cover that calls it a flying dinosaur has it wrong.","more":"Dinosaurs are land animals that stand with their legs straight underneath them. No dinosaur ever grew a wing of skin, and no pterosaur ever had a dinosaur's hips. They lived side by side without being the same sort of creature.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"build","text":"Each wing was one sheet of skin, stretched from the body out along a single enormously long finger — the fourth. The finger is right there in the bones; the shape of the skin comes from other pterosaurs found with the wing preserved. A bat spreads four fingers through its wing. This used one.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"weird","text":"The name means wing without tooth. The beak is completely empty: two long pointed jaws with nothing in them. The lower jaw of one large specimen measures 4 ft 1 in (1.25 m) on its own.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"size","text":"The specimens fall into two clear size classes. The big ones, taken to be males, spanned 18 to 25 ft (5.6 to 7.6 m) and carried tall backswept crests. The smaller ones averaged about 12 ft (3.8 m), with small rounded crests.","more":"With 1,200 specimens to compare, the two classes are hard to argue with. The larger group also has narrower hips; the smaller group's hips are wide, which is what you would expect of a female.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"build","text":"Nobody has ever put one on a scale. Worked out from the bones, weight estimates run from about 45 lb (20 kg) up to 200 lb (90 kg) — either way, remarkably light for an animal with a 20-ft wingspan.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Films enjoy showing a Pteranodon snatching a person off the ground. Its feet were flat walking feet, not gripping talons, and there is nothing in the skeleton built for lifting and carrying a struggling load.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"home","text":"The chalk holding these bones was laid down under water that once covered Kansas. A flying reptile buried out in the middle of it died a long way from any shore, which says something about how far it could travel.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"created","text":"One finger — the fourth — holds the whole wing, grown longer than the rest of the arm, stretching a sheet of skin 20 ft (6 m) across. The finger is measured bone; the sheet's reach comes from other pterosaurs found with the wing preserved. Shorten the finger and the sheet hangs slack. Half a finger is not half a wing.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"created","text":"Behemoth has a tail like a cedar and leviathan belongs to the sea. Pteranodon is neither: a 6 ft (1.8 m) body carrying a weight worked out from the bones at about 45 lb (20 kg), all of its size in span. What it matches instead are the old accounts of flying serpents.","more":"The Ark carried the flying creatures along with the land kinds, so a place on it was needed. A 20 ft (6 m) span sounds impossible to house until the weight is worked out from the bones: about 45 lb, and a young one a good deal less.","kind":"record"}],"wonder":"One finger, drawn out long enough to hold up a wing 20 ft across, and it flew.","_units":"imperial"},{"id":"quetzalcoatlus","name":"Quetzalcoatlus","group":"dinosaurs","blurb":"The largest flying animal ever found. It was a toothless flying reptile that stalked river shallows on all fours. It launched itself into the air with a jump.","size":"Wings about 33 to 36 ft (10 to 11 m) across, the wingspan of a small airplane. Standing on the ground it held its head more than 13 ft (4 m) up — higher than a basketball hoop.","homes":["freshwater"],"stats":{"weight":450},"facts":[{"cat":"bones","text":"Douglas Lawson, a geology student, found the first bones in 1971 in Big Bend National Park, Texas. The specimen, TMM 41450-3, is part of a left wing. The giant species is still known from only about a dozen bones.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"bones","text":"A smaller relative from the same park is called Quetzalcoatlus lawsoni. It is known from more than 300 bones belonging to over 200 individuals. That is more material than for any other pterosaur. It was named in 2021, fifty years after Lawson's first find.","more":"Its wings spanned about 15 to 20 ft (4.5 to 6 m). Its neck alone is 4 ft 11 in (1.5 m) long and its skull about 3 ft (0.95 m). Most of what is known about how the giant walked, fed and took off comes from this smaller one. So much more of it survived.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"No flying animal found anywhere is bigger. Its wingspan is scaled up from the more complete smaller relative. It works out to about 36 ft (11 m). That is about the same as a Cessna 172, the little four-seater plane you see at small airfields.","more":"Early guesses were wilder still. One ran to 85 ft, which nobody defends now. The figure came down as more of the smaller relative was found. The giant's proportions could then be scaled from a complete animal rather than a few wing pieces.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"build","text":"Its weight has to be worked out from the bones, not measured. Most estimates land between 440 and 550 lb (200 to 250 kg), though one 2021 study argued for as little as 330 lb (150 kg).","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"speed","text":"To get airborne it jumped. Researchers who modelled the skeleton in 2021 reckon it leaped at least 8 ft (2.4 m) straight up from a standing start, then swept its wings down on the way.","more":"A bird that size would need a runway. Jumping first means the wings never have to beat close to the ground. Down there they would strike it. The animal is already clear of the mud before the first downstroke.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"build","text":"On the ground it walked on all fours with the wings folded, putting the knuckle of the wing finger down as a front foot. That pose is read off pterosaur trackways, which preserve exactly that: a pair of footprints with knuckle prints beside them, step after step.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"food","text":"The jaws are long, thin, straight and toothless. The 2021 study argues it worked the shallows the way a stork does. It picked crabs, worms and clams out of river mud and lake edges. It did not snatch fish in flight.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Quetzalcoatlus is not a dinosaur. It was a pterosaur, a flying reptile, and its bones came out of an old river floodplain in Texas rather than a sea bed.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"The name comes from Quetzalcoatl, the serpent god of wind and sky in Aztec accounts. The second half, northropi, honours Jack Northrop, who built airplanes with no tail — which is roughly what this animal was.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Cultures that never met each other tell of enormous flying serpents and winged creatures. Aztec, Chinese, European, and more. An animal standing taller than a man and opening 36 ft of wing is exactly what those accounts describe.","more":"People generally describe what they have seen. Carvings and stories of winged dragons turn up on separate continents. The people who made them had no way of comparing notes. The descriptions keep landing on the same handful of features.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"created","text":"Thirty-six feet of wing on an animal weighing roughly 450 pounds. Nothing found anywhere flew bigger. Day five was given to the flying creatures. The largest of them was made toothless and four-footed on the ground. It could jump 8 feet up before its first wingbeat.","more":"The second half of its name, northropi, honours Jack Northrop, who built airplanes with no tail. The animal already had that layout: no tail, no teeth, no engine, just a standing leap and a downstroke off a Texas floodplain. Northrop reached the same shape by drawing it.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"created","text":"God brought the kinds to Noah. Nobody had to go out and catch a 36-foot wingspan. Quetzalcoatlus starts in an egg, and a young one folds small. Wings shut, it walks aboard on all fours. The knuckle of the wing finger serves as a front foot.","more":"Feeding the adult would mean crabs, worms and clams by the barrowload; a young one probing the same shallows needs a fraction of that. Room and food both scale to the animal that walks up the ramp, not to the one it will grow into afterwards.","kind":"record"}],"wonder":"An animal of about 450 lb (200 kg), built light enough to jump into the sky.","_units":"imperial"},{"id":"plesiosaurus","name":"Plesiosaurus","group":"dinosaurs","blurb":"A swimming reptile with a long neck, a short tail and four big flippers. It is not a dinosaur. Nothing alive swims the way it did.","size":"About 10 ft (3 m) from nose to tail — and roughly a third of that is neck.","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"length":10},"facts":[{"cat":"bones","text":"On 10 December 1823, Mary Anning dug the first complete Plesiosaurus out of the cliffs at Lyme Regis in Dorset, England. That skeleton is NHMUK PV OR 22656. It is on display at the Natural History Museum in London.","more":"Anning was 24. She had been collecting from those cliffs since she was a girl, selling fossils to keep her family fed. The Geological Society of London discussed her finds at length. It did not allow women to join it, or even attend as guests.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"bones","text":"Georges Cuvier was the most respected anatomist in Europe. He saw the drawings and declared the neck too long to be real. He called it a fake, bones from different animals stuck together. The Geological Society examined the specimen in 1824 and found it genuine. Cuvier admitted he had judged too fast.","more":"It was a serious accusation. Anning made her living selling fossils, and a reputation for fakery would have ended that. The specimen survived the inspection because it was exactly what she said it was: one animal, in one piece, in the rock.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"bones","text":"The neck alone holds about 40 separate bones — different skeletons preserve between 38 and 42 of them. A giraffe manages its whole neck with seven.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Four flippers, all much the same size, and a short tail. Work back from that build and the swimming comes out as underwater flight — all four beating like wings, the short tail steering instead of pushing. Nothing alive swims that way now: sea turtles and sea lions pull with two.","more":"Working out how the four were timed is still an argument among people who build models of it. Front pair and back pair beating together gives power; beating out of step gives control.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"food","text":"The teeth are thin needle-shaped cones that lean forward — about 20 to 25 in each upper row, and 24 in each lower one. Nobody ever watched one feed, so the shape has to do the arguing: cones like that make a fish trap, not a cutting tool. Shut the jaws and nothing slips back out.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Plesiosaur skeletons often turn up with a heap of smooth stones in the belly. One had 95 of them, 15 lb (6.8 kg) of stone altogether, the biggest a 2 lb (1 kg) lump. Ballast for diving, or a grinder for food — the argument continues.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"A plesiosaur from Kansas sat in storage in a Los Angeles museum for years. In 2011 somebody looked at it properly and found an unborn baby inside. Just one, and already about a third of its mother's length.","more":"One big calf rather than a clutch of eggs makes sense for an animal that could not haul itself up a beach. It also matches how whales and dolphins do it today.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Not a dinosaur. Plesiosaurus was a swimming reptile that came up to breathe air, and it was named in 1821 — twenty-one years before Richard Owen invented the word dinosaur at all.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Sailors have reported sea serpents for centuries. On 6 August 1848 the officers of HMS Daedalus reported one in the South Atlantic. The head and neck were held about 4 ft above the water. Some 60 ft of animal was in view. A long-necked swimmer fits that better than anything alive today.","more":"Richard Owen tried to explain it away as an elephant seal. Captain Peter M'Quhae stood by what his officers saw and had drawings published in the newspapers so people could judge for themselves.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"size","text":"Plesiosaurus itself reached about 9 to 11 ft (2.9 to 3.5 m). Later relatives took the same body plan much further. Elasmosaurus carried 72 bones in its neck and grew longer than a bus.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"created","text":"Plesiosaurus was made on day five with the great sea creatures, a day before the first land animals. About a third of its 10 ft was neck — forty bones — built for reaching after fish in open water, and the fish were made that same day.","more":"It breathed air and had to surface, but it was never a land animal and never needed a boat. When the Flood came, the water it already lived in rose with it. People and Plesiosaurus were on the earth together, and long-necked swimmers keep turning up in sailors' reports and old carvings.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"created","text":"The teeth are forward-leaning needle cones, 20 to 25 in each upper row against 24 below. They never meet point to point — they slide past each other and shut into a cage. Half-meshed teeth leave gaps, and a fish leaves through a gap.","more":"The four flippers work the same way. Underwater flight needs all four shaped like wings and timed against each other. One wing and three stubs is not a slower version of the stroke; it is a spin.","kind":"worked"}],"wonder":"Four wings, beating under water, on an animal that still had to come up for air.","_units":"imperial","art":1},{"id":"mosasaurus","name":"Mosasaurus","group":"dinosaurs","blurb":"An enormous sea-going lizard with paddles and a shark-like tail. It gave the world its first great sea-monster fossil.","size":"Around 40 ft (12 m) long, worked out from the skull.","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"length":40},"facts":[{"cat":"bones","text":"The famous skull came out of a chalk mine under Maastricht, in the Netherlands, in the 1770s. In 1794 French troops took the town and carried it off to Paris. It is still there, in the Muséum national d'Histoire naturelle, catalogued MNHN AC 9648.","more":"A favorite version of the story has the soldiers handing over 600 bottles of wine for it. Historians who went back through the records could not find support for that part; the taking of it, though, is thoroughly documented. An earlier skull had been found nearby in 1764.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"bones","text":"William Conybeare named it Mosasaurus in 1822. The name means lizard of the Meuse, after the river at Maastricht. That is twenty years before Richard Owen invented the word dinosaur in 1842. This animal had a scientific name before the dinosaurs did.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"bones","text":"Skulls of Mosasaurus hoffmannii measure over 4 ft 9 in (145 cm). The length of the whole animal has to be scaled up from the skull, which is why published figures range from about 39 ft (12 m) to 56 ft (17 m).","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"Mosasaurus was a lizard. Line its skeleton up against animals alive today. Monitor lizards and snakes come out the closest match. So picture a monitor lizard 40 ft (12 m) long, with paddles where the feet should be.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Not a dinosaur, and not a fish. It was a reptile that swam. The skeleton is a lizard's. A reptile breathes air, so it had to come to the surface for a breath. A whale does the same.","more":"The confusion is understandable — it turns up in dinosaur books, dinosaur films and dinosaur toy sets. But dinosaurs walked on land. This one never left the water.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"food","text":"The jaws carry 14 to 16 teeth along each upper edge, and eight more on the roof of the mouth. That second set points backwards down the throat. Once a fish was in there, reversing out was not an option.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"A specimen nicknamed Lars was found with the bones of a young sea turtle inside it, pitted and etched by stomach acid. Shell included.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"build","text":"For a long time mosasaurs were drawn with straight, eel-like tails. Then one from Harrana in Jordan turned up with the outline of its skin preserved, showing a two-lobed fin at the tail tip like a shark's. Every picture had to be redrawn.","more":"Soft tissue almost never survives. The shape of a tail is usually guesswork from the bones. The Harrana specimen preserves the flipper outlines too. Those are real edges, not reconstructions.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Mosasaurs gave birth at sea. Bones of newborn-sized mosasaurs turn up in rock that formed far from any shore, which is not somewhere an animal goes to lay eggs.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"weird","text":"The mosasaur in the dinosaur films is drawn far larger than any Mosasaurus ever dug up. The real animal works out at around 40 ft (12 m). That is enormous, and it could take a sea turtle whole. It is still nowhere near the film's version.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"created","text":"Mosasaurus, Tylosaurus, Platecarpus. The same paddles instead of feet. The same backward-pointing teeth on the roof of the mouth. The same shark-like tail. One created kind, made on day five with the great sea creatures. Then it was sorted into different sizes and snouts. Rearranged, never added to.","more":"Published lengths for Mosasaurus alone run from about 39 ft to 56 ft, because the whole animal is scaled up from the skull. Size shifts easily inside a kind. What never shifts is the kit — no mosasaur has ever turned up with a leg on it.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"created","text":"A monitor lizard is an armload of animal. Mosasaurus ran about 40 ft, school-bus length, its skull alone over 4 ft 9 in, paddles where the feet should be. That was the design from the start: an ocean lizard at ocean scale, swimming before any land animal existed.","more":"Sea-monster accounts turn up in the old records of many nations — carved, painted, retold. A 40-ft lizard with a shark's tail, surfacing to breathe, is the sort of thing people would remember and pass down long after the last one was gone.","kind":"record"}],"wonder":"A lizard fitted out for the open ocean, right down to the shark's tail.","_units":"imperial"},{"id":"ichthyosaurus","name":"Ichthyosaurus","group":"dinosaurs","blurb":"A dolphin-shaped swimming reptile from the cliffs of the Dorset coast. It is shaped like a fish and breathes air. It is not a dinosaur.","size":"About 6 to 7 ft (2 m) long. The largest one ever found reached nearly 11 ft (3.3 m).","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"length":7},"facts":[{"cat":"bones","text":"In 1811 Joseph Anning found a 4-ft skull in the cliffs at Lyme Regis. About a year later his sister Mary, then 12, dug out the rest of the skeleton — over 15 ft (4.6 m) of it. The skull is at the Natural History Museum in London.","more":"That animal is now put in a group of its own. But it was the find that made the world take these sea reptiles seriously. It also began thirty years of collecting by Mary Anning, who found most of what we know from that coast.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"bones","text":"Anning noticed odd stones lying where the belly had been in ichthyosaur skeletons. She broke them open and found fish scales, fish bones, sometimes bones of small ichthyosaurs. William Buckland worked out what they were. He named them coprolites in 1829. Fossil dung.","more":"It is a genuinely clever piece of detective work. A stone tells you nothing; a stone full of scales tells you what the animal ate, how it chewed and how quickly it digested. Buckland credited Anning by name.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"bones","text":"The largest Ichthyosaurus known sat unstudied in a museum in Hannover, Germany. Someone looked at it properly in 2016. It is 10 to 11 ft (3 to 3.3 m) long. It also has an unborn baby inside it. That is 2.75 in (7 cm) of tiny backbone, a small fin, some ribs.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"babies","text":"Ichthyosaurs gave birth in the water, and the best reading of the fossils is that the baby came out tail first, so it did not drown part-way. Only three pregnant Ichthyosaurus have ever been found.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Not a dinosaur, and not a fish. Ichthyosaurus was a reptile that swam. Bone for bone it is built like a reptile, so it had to come up for air the way every reptile does. And the pregnant specimens show it bore live young instead of laying eggs on a beach.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"build","text":"It has the same shape as a dolphin. Pointed snout, back fin, crescent tail. A back fin contains no bones. We only know it was there because of specimens from Holzmaden in Germany. Those preserve the outline of the whole body as a dark film in the rock.","more":"Two animals built to the same plan for the same job, one a reptile and one a mammal. Engineers call that convergence; it is the same reason a submarine and a shark end up looking alike.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"senses","text":"The eye is huge, and holds a ring of bony plates inside it. A ring like that would have propped the eyeball against the push of the water. The record for the largest eye of any animal belongs to Temnodontosaurus. It is a bigger relative from the same Dorset cliffs, at 10 in (26 cm) across.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"food","text":"Stomach contents show fish and belemnites, squid-like animals with a hard internal shell. Buckland thought some of the coprolites were black because of ink swallowed along with them.","kind":"found"},{"cat":"record","text":"In February 2021 Joe Davis was draining a lagoon at Rutland Water in England. He spotted bones in the mud. They belonged to a 33-ft (10 m) sea dragon, a Temnodontosaurus. It is the largest and most complete ichthyosaur skeleton ever found in Britain.","more":"The dig took most of August and September that year, and the block holding the skull alone weighed about a ton. The find was announced in January 2022. Fossils like this are still turning up in ordinary places, found by people who were doing something else entirely.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"weird","text":"Collecting on that coast is dangerous work. The cliffs give up bones after a winter storm and then slide. In October 1833 a landslide came down on Mary Anning, missed her, and buried her dog Tray.","kind":"record"},{"cat":"created","text":"The cold is the harder problem. It bore its young alive in open water. Nothing in its build fits coming ashore. So it could not haul out onto a warm rock the way a lizard does.","more":"The cold is the harder problem. It bore its young alive in open water and never came ashore, so it could not haul out onto a warm rock the way a lizard does.","kind":"worked"},{"cat":"created","text":"A fisherman would have taken the fin for a dolphin's, until the head lifted: a long snout of small teeth, a great round eye. Nothing in its build fits coming ashore, so there are no tracks to find. The men who dug them from those cliffs called them sea dragons.","more":"A boat crew could have seen one thing we only ever meet in stone. A birth in open water, tail first. The baby swims up for its first breath on its own.","kind":"worked"}],"wonder":"Built like a dolphin, breathing like us, bringing its young into the world alive in open water.","_units":"imperial","art":1}];
